window.GIQ={"meta":{"lede":"Clicker, interactive and TA-review questions made for the GI biochemistry lectures (2018–2026), in the order this year's lectures teach them. Similar questions sit together in a shaded box. Tap an answer to see why it is right, why the others are wrong, and the slides that teach it."},"lectures":[{"id":"BIO-L31","t":"Heme metabolism & bilirubin"},{"id":"BIO-P14","t":"Primer: nutrition"},{"id":"BIO-L32","t":"Macronutrients"},{"id":"BIO-L33","t":"Insulin & glucagon"},{"id":"BIO-L34","t":"Fat-soluble vitamins"},{"id":"BIO-L35","t":"Water-soluble vitamins"},{"id":"BIO-L36","t":"Iron metabolism"}],"groups":[{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Heme proteins and their jobs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-4.jpg","cap":"Heme roles: cytochrome electron carrier, catalase peroxide breakdown, hemoglobin and myoglobin oxygen binding","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-48-1","s":"Which of the following is a correct function of heme in biological systems?","o":["A. Acts as an energy-storage molecule in adipose tissue","B. Serves as an electron carrier in cytochromes","C. Catalyzes peptide bond formation in ribosomes","D. Stores iron in hepatocytes"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Serves as an electron carrier in cytochromes","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Heme is the <b>electron carrier in cytochromes</b>. It also binds oxygen in hemoglobin and myoglobin and breaks down hydrogen peroxide in catalase.<br>A. Energy storage in fat: that is triglyceride, not heme.<br>C. Peptide bond formation: done by ribosomal RNA (ribonucleic acid), not heme.<br>D. Iron storage in liver cells: that is ferritin, not heme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 4 (Heme proteins:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":48,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":48,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0133-19-1","s":"Which of the following correctly matches the heme protein with its biological role?","o":["A. Myoglobin – electron transport","B. Catalase – reversible oxygen binding","C. Cytochromes – electron transport","D. Hemoglobin – breakdown of hydrogen peroxide"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cytochromes – electron transport","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In <b>cytochromes</b>, heme is an <b>electron carrier</b>.<br>A. Myoglobin: its heme binds oxygen reversibly; it does not carry electrons.<br>B. Catalase: its heme sits in the active site that breaks down hydrogen peroxide; it does not bind oxygen reversibly.<br>D. Hemoglobin: its heme binds oxygen reversibly; catalase is the protein that breaks down hydrogen peroxide.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 4 (Heme proteins:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":19,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":19,"f":"F0890"}]},{"id":"F2438-3-1","s":"What statement is accurate about heme found in biological systems?","o":["A. Heme proteins are a group of specialized proteins that contain heme as a tightly bound cofactor","B. The heme group functions as a proton carrier in cytochromes","C. The heme group is part of the active site of catalase which catalyzes the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide","D. The heme group serves to bind oxygen reversibly in hemoglobin and irreversibly in myoglobin","E. The role of the heme group is dictated by the 2-dimensional structure of the amino acid sequence of the protein"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The heme group is part of the active site of catalase which catalyzes the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide","a":"C","why":"<b>A and C are both true.</b> Heme proteins hold heme as a <b>tightly bound prosthetic group</b> (a prosthetic group is a tightly bound cofactor), and heme is part of the <b>active site of catalase</b>, which breaks down hydrogen peroxide.<br>B. False: heme carries <b>electrons</b>, not protons, in cytochromes.<br>D. False: oxygen binds <b>reversibly</b> in both hemoglobin and myoglobin.<br>E. False: the role is set by the <b>three-dimensional</b> environment the protein creates.","ex":"","fl":["Statement A is also true: G026:4 says heme proteins are 'a group of specialized proteins that contain heme as a tightly bound prosthetic group' (a prosthetic group is a tightly bound cofactor). B is false (heme carries electrons, not protons), D is false (O2 binding is reversible in both Hb and Mb), and E is false (the role depends on the 3-D protein environment). The question has two correct statements."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 4 (Heme proteins:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":3,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":147,"f":"F3882"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Heme a, b, c: which proteins use them","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0028-14.jpg","cap":"Heme a, b, c structures: heme b in hemoglobin and myoglobin; c in cytochromes","where":"2020 Harlem TA review, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G026-5.jpg","cap":"Hemoglobin's heme is ferrous iron plus protoporphyrin IX (that is, heme b)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-49-1","s":"Which heme group is found in both hemoglobin and myoglobin?","o":["A. Heme A","B. Heme B","C. Heme C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme B","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Heme b</b> (iron protoporphyrin IX) is the heme of <b>hemoglobin and myoglobin</b>. It is also in catalase and b-type cytochromes.<br>A. Heme A: has a long side chain and sits in a-type cytochromes (cytochrome c oxidase).<br>C. Heme C: covalently bound in c-type cytochromes such as cytochrome c.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 4 (Heme proteins:)","L31 Heme slide 5 (Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":49,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 review","n":"old Exam 3 review: heme part only","sl":214,"f":"F0044"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":157,"f":"F0048"}]},{"id":"F0028-12-1","s":"Which heme groups is not found in hemoglobin and myoglobin? (more than one answer)","o":["A. Heme A","B. Heme B","C. Heme C"],"g":["A","C"],"k":["A","C"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme A and Heme C (heme B is the one in hemoglobin and myoglobin)","a":"A,C","why":"<b>A and C.</b> Hemoglobin and myoglobin both use <b>heme b</b> (iron protoporphyrin IX). <b>Heme a</b> is in a-type cytochromes (cytochrome c oxidase), and <b>heme c</b> is covalently bound in c-type cytochromes.<br>B. Heme B: this is the one that IS in hemoglobin and myoglobin, so it is not an answer here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 4 (Heme proteins:)","L31 Heme slide 5 (Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":12,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0257-46-1","s":"Which of the following correctly matches a type of heme with its major biological function?","o":["A. Heme a – oxygen-binding transport in hemoglobin","B. Heme b – found in cytochrome c oxidase","C. Heme c – covalently bound in cytochromes of the electron transport chain","D. Heme o – prosthetic group used in catalase for hydrogen peroxide breakdown"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme c – covalently bound in cytochromes of the electron transport chain","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Heme c</b> is <b>covalently bound</b> (through links to cysteine) in c-type cytochromes, such as cytochrome c of the electron transport chain.<br>A. Heme a: found in cytochrome a/a3 (cytochrome c oxidase); hemoglobin uses heme b.<br>B. Heme b: found in hemoglobin, myoglobin and catalase; cytochrome c oxidase uses heme a.<br>D. Heme o: a step on the way to heme a, and the heme of some bacterial oxidases; catalase uses heme b.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 4 (Heme proteins:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":46,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":46,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Type III porphyrin is the human one","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-6.jpg","cap":"Uroporphyrin I symmetric vs III with ring D reversed; type III used in humans","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F1061-159-1","s":"Which type of Uroporphyrin is physiologically important in humans?","o":["A. Type I","B. Type II","C. Type III","D. Type IV"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Type III","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Type III</b> porphyrins (side chains asymmetric, reversed in ring D) are the ones used to make heme in humans. <b>Type I</b> (symmetric) porphyrins are useless and <b>build up in porphyrias</b>.<br>A. Type I: made only by a slow spontaneous side reaction; it accumulates in porphyrias.<br>B and D. Types II and IV: not made in the body.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 6 (Heme- Porphyrin Structure)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":159,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":3,"f":"F2433"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"ALA synthase: rate-limiting first step","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-9.jpg","cap":"Step one: glycine plus succinyl-CoA to aminolevulinic acid by mitochondrial ALA synthase, rate-limiting","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G026-14.jpg","cap":"Pathway map: rate-limiting step one and last three steps in mitochondria; middle steps cytoplasmic","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-5-1","s":"What is the rate-limiting enzyme in the synthesis of heme?","o":["A. Ferrochelatase","B. ALA synthase","C. Porphobilinogen deaminase","D. ALA dehydratase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ALA synthase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. ALA synthase</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase) is the <b>rate-limiting enzyme</b>. It runs the first step, glycine plus succinyl coenzyme A to aminolevulinic acid, in the mitochondria.<br>A. Ferrochelatase: the last step, adding iron; inhibited by lead.<br>C. Porphobilinogen deaminase: step three; deficient in acute intermittent porphyria.<br>D. ALA dehydratase: step two; inhibited by lead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":5,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":5,"f":"F0890"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":33,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":177,"f":"F3882"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":161,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":5,"f":"F2433"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":7,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-51-1","s":"Which of the following is the rate-limiting step in the heme biosynthesis pathway?","o":["A. Porphobilinogen -> Hydroxymethylbilane","B. Protoporphyrin IX -> Heme","C. Aminolevulinic Acid -> Porphobilinogen","D. Glycine + Succinyl-CoA -> Aminolevulinic Acid"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Glycine + Succinyl-CoA -> Aminolevulinic Acid","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Glycine plus succinyl coenzyme A to aminolevulinic acid</b> is the first, rate-limiting and most regulated step. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase) runs it in the mitochondria, using vitamin B6.<br>A. Porphobilinogen to hydroxymethylbilane: step three (porphobilinogen deaminase); deficient in acute intermittent porphyria.<br>B. Protoporphyrin IX to heme: the last step (ferrochelatase); inhibited by lead.<br>C. Aminolevulinic acid to porphobilinogen: step two (ALA dehydratase); inhibited by lead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":51,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 review","n":"old Exam 3 review: heme part only","sl":216,"f":"F0044"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":3,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":109,"f":"F2304"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme & biotin TA review","sl":3,"f":"F2441"}]},{"id":"F0028-15-1","s":"Which of the following is the rate-limiting step in the heme biosynthesis pathway? Which is the regulated step?","o":["A. Aminolevulinic acid—> Porphobilinogen; Gly + succinyl-coA—>Aminolevulinic acid","B. Gly + succinyl-coA—>Aminolevulinic acid; Gly + succinyl-coA—>Aminolevulinic acid","C. Protoporphyrin IX—> Heme; Aminolevulinic acid—> Porphobilinogen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Gly + succinyl-coA—>Aminolevulinic acid; Gly + succinyl-coA—>Aminolevulinic acid","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> One step is both: <b>glycine plus succinyl coenzyme A to aminolevulinic acid</b>, run by ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase). It is the rate-limiting step and the regulated step (heme feedback, inducing drugs, iron in red-cell precursors).<br>A. Aminolevulinic acid to porphobilinogen: step two (ALA dehydratase); not rate-limiting.<br>C. Protoporphyrin IX to heme: the last step (ferrochelatase); neither rate-limiting nor the regulated step.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":15,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":160,"f":"F0048"}]},{"id":"F2438-6-1","s":"Heme biosynthesis occurs in both the mitochondria and cytosol. It is a complicated process involving numerous enzymes and intermediates. What is the rate limiting step enzyme and where in the heme biosynthesis process does it occur? (bonus if you can say which cell it does not occur in!)","o":["A. ALA dehydratase; 1st step","B. ALA synthase; 1st step","C. ALA synthase; 2nd step","D. HBM synthase; 1st step","E. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase; 2nd step"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ALA synthase; 1st step","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>ALA synthase</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase) runs the <b>first step</b>, glycine plus succinyl coenzyme A to aminolevulinic acid, in the mitochondria. It is the rate-limiting step. (Bonus: heme is not made in mature red blood cells.)<br>A. ALA dehydratase: the second step, not rate-limiting.<br>C. ALA synthase is step one, not step two.<br>D. HMB synthase (hydroxymethylbilane synthase): the third step.<br>E. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase: the fourth step.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 8 (Glycine +Succinyl CoA)","L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":6,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":150,"f":"F3882"}]},{"id":"F0144-5-1","s":"What is the rate-limiting enzyme of the heme biosynthesis pathway, and does it take place in the mitochondria or cytosol?","o":["A. Ferrochelatase, cytosol","B. Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase, cytosol","C. Porphobilinogen deaminase, mitochondria","D. Aminolevulinic acid synthase, mitochondria","E. Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase, cytosol","F. Ferrochelatase, mitochondria","G. Aminolevulinic acid synthase, mitochondria"],"g":["D","G"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Aminolevulinic acid synthase, mitochondria","a":"D","why":"<b>D and G</b> (the same text): <b>aminolevulinic acid synthase</b> (ALA synthase) is the rate-limiting enzyme, and it works in the <b>mitochondria</b>.<br>A and F. Ferrochelatase: the last step (adds iron); it is mitochondrial but not rate-limiting.<br>B and E. ALA dehydratase: the second step, in the cytosol; inhibited by lead, not rate-limiting.<br>C. Porphobilinogen deaminase: a cytosolic step; its deficiency causes acute intermittent porphyria.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only D, but G has the identical text (aminolevulinic acid synthase, mitochondria), so both are correct."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 8 (Glycine +Succinyl CoA)","L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":5,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":5,"f":"F1048"}]},{"id":"F0065-2-1","s":"A patient with acute intermittent porphyria has recurrent attacks despite avoiding known precipitating drugs. A new therapy effectively prevents attacks by reducing transcription of hepatic ALA synthase 1. Which direct effect of this therapy would you expect in hepatocytes?","o":["A. Decreased conversion of heme to biliverdin","B. Decreased formation of ALA from glycine and succinyl-CoA","C. Decreased conjugation of bilirubin with glucuronic acid","D. Decreased transport of conjugated bilirubin into bile canaliculi","E. Decreased synthesis of albumin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased formation of ALA from glycine and succinyl-CoA","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> ALA synthase 1 (aminolevulinic acid synthase 1) makes <b>aminolevulinic acid from glycine and succinyl coenzyme A</b>. Less enzyme means less aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen piling up in acute intermittent porphyria.<br>A. Heme to biliverdin: heme oxygenase, in macrophages.<br>C. Bilirubin conjugation: UDP-glucuronyl transferase (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase).<br>D. Pumping conjugated bilirubin into bile: a canalicular transporter, not this enzyme.<br>E. Albumin synthesis: unrelated to heme synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":2,"f":"F0065"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"No heme synthesis in mature red cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-8.jpg","cap":"Heme synthesis in mitochondria and cytosol; all cells except mature erythrocytes","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G026-14.jpg","cap":"Steps one, six, seven, eight happen inside the mitochondrion, which mature red cells lack","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-7-1","s":"Which of the following best explains why heme biosynthesis does not occur in mature erythrocytes?","o":["A. Mature erythrocytes lack ribosomes","B. Mature erythrocytes lack mitochondrial enzymes","C. Mature erythrocytes receive their heme from erythroid precursor cells in the bone marrow","D. Mature erythrocytes receive heme from the liver’s heme synthesis pathway"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mature erythrocytes lack mitochondrial enzymes","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The first step and the last three steps of heme synthesis happen in <b>mitochondria</b>. <b>Mature red blood cells have no mitochondria</b>, so they cannot make heme.<br>A. They do lack ribosomes, but the missing mitochondria are what stop heme synthesis.<br>C. Marrow precursors make heme before they mature; mature cells receive no new heme.<br>D. The liver makes heme for its own cytochrome P450 enzymes; it does not supply red cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 8 (Glycine +Succinyl CoA)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":7,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":7,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"ALA synthase needs vitamin B6","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-9.jpg","cap":"Step one: ALA synthase requires pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G026-13.jpg","cap":"B6 deficiency (isoniazid) blocks ALA synthase: low aminolevulinic acid, microcytic anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-7-1","s":"Which cofactor is required for the enzyme ALA synthase?","o":["A. Thiamine (Vitamin B1)","B. Pyridoxal phosphate (Vitamin B6)","C. Biotin","D. Folic acid"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxal phosphate (Vitamin B6)","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase) needs <b>pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)</b>. That is why B6 deficiency, for example from isoniazid, lowers aminolevulinic acid and causes a microcytic anemia.<br>A. Thiamine (vitamin B1): cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase and transketolase.<br>C. Biotin: cofactor for carboxylases.<br>D. Folic acid: carries one-carbon units for making DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":7,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":7,"f":"F0890"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":162,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":6,"f":"F2433"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0257-8-1","s":"Which enzyme catalyzes the rate-limiting step of heme biosynthesis and requires which cofactor?","o":["A. ALA synthase requiring pyridoxal phosphate","B. Ferrochelatase requiring NADPH and oxygen","C. ALA dehydratase requiring pyridoxal phosphate","D. PBG deaminase requiring UDP-glucuronic acid"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"ALA synthase requiring pyridoxal phosphate","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>ALA synthase</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase) is the rate-limiting first step and needs <b>pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)</b>.<br>B. Ferrochelatase: the last step; it adds ferrous iron and is inhibited by lead. Oxygen and NADPH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate) are used by heme oxygenase in heme breakdown.<br>C. ALA dehydratase: a zinc enzyme inhibited by lead; it does not use B6 and is not rate-limiting.<br>D. PBG deaminase (porphobilinogen deaminase): does not use UDP-glucuronic acid (uridine diphosphate glucuronic acid), the donor for bilirubin conjugation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":8,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":8,"f":"F0938"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Heme feedback-represses ALA synthase 1","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-10.jpg","cap":"Low heme raises, high heme lowers ALA synthase 1 synthesis; barbiturates induce it","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/G026-9.jpg","cap":"Diagram: heme inhibits ALA synthase 1; intracellular iron stimulates erythroid ALA synthase 2","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G026-24.jpg","cap":"Glucose loading or hemin represses ALA synthase","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-16-1","s":"How does heme regulate its own biosynthesis in the liver and other tissues?","o":["A. Heme inhibits ALA synthase through negative feedback","B. Heme enhances ferrochelatase activity","C. Heme promotes PBG deaminase expression","D. Heme activates ALA synthase through positive feedback"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme inhibits ALA synthase through negative feedback","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Heme <b>inhibits ALA synthase</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase), the rate-limiting first enzyme, by <b>negative feedback</b>. More heme lowers the enzyme; less heme raises it.<br>B. Heme does not enhance ferrochelatase.<br>C. Heme does not promote porphobilinogen deaminase.<br>D. Heme is a negative regulator, not a positive one.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":16,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":16,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0065-3-1","s":"In hepatic cells, which situation most strongly increases expression of ALA synthase 1?","o":["A. High intracellular free heme concentration","B. Low intracellular free heme concentration","C. High bilirubin diglucuronide concentration","D. High ferritin iron content","E. High hepcidin levels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Low intracellular free heme concentration","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Heme <b>represses</b> ALA synthase 1 (aminolevulinic acid synthase 1). <b>Low free heme</b> lifts that brake, so the liver makes more enzyme.<br>A. High heme does the opposite: it lowers ALA synthase 1.<br>C. Bilirubin diglucuronide: a breakdown product; it does not control heme synthesis.<br>D and E. Ferritin iron and hepcidin: control iron handling. Iron regulates the red-cell form, ALA synthase 2, not the liver form.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":3,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F0257-50-1","s":"Which statement best describes the rate-limiting enzyme of heme biosynthesis and its regulation in the liver?","o":["A. ALA dehydratase is rate-limiting and activated by heme","B. Ferrochelatase is rate-limiting and inhibited by heme","C. ALA synthase is rate-limiting and inhibited by heme via feedback regulation","D. Coproporphyrinogen oxidase is rate-limiting and induced by barbiturates"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"ALA synthase is rate-limiting and inhibited by heme via feedback regulation","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>ALA synthase</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase) is rate-limiting. In the liver, <b>heme feedback-inhibits ALA synthase 1</b>: low heme raises its synthesis, high heme lowers it.<br>A. ALA dehydratase: not rate-limiting; inhibited by lead, not activated by heme.<br>B. Ferrochelatase: the last step, not rate-limiting.<br>D. Coproporphyrinogen oxidase: not rate-limiting. Barbiturates induce ALA synthase 1, not this enzyme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":50,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":50,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0112-4-1","s":"What best describes the rate-limiting step of heme biosynthesis?","o":["A. Inhibited by lead","B. Catalyzed by δ- aminolevulinate","C. Requires cofactor vitamin B12","D. Occurs in mature erythrocytes","E. Is upregulated in the presence of heme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Catalyzed by δ- aminolevulinate","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The rate-limiting step is run by <b>δ-aminolevulinate synthase</b> (ALA synthase; the option leaves off the word synthase).<br>A. Inhibited by lead: that is ALA dehydratase (and ferrochelatase).<br>C. ALA synthase needs <b>vitamin B6</b> (pyridoxal phosphate), not B12.<br>D. Mature red blood cells have no mitochondria and make no heme.<br>E. Heme <b>represses</b> ALA synthase (feedback inhibition); it does not upregulate it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":4,"f":"F0112"}]},{"id":"F0048-163-1","s":"Which enzyme is involved in the following reaction and how is it regulated? Gly + Succinyl-coA—> Aminolevulinic acid","o":["A. Ferrochelatase; lead","B. Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase; lead","C. Aminolevulinic acid synthase; glucose","D. Aminolevulinic acid synthase; iron"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"ALA synthase; glucose (source key)","a":"C","why":"<b>C and D are both true.</b> <b>ALA synthase</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase) runs this step. The liver form is repressed by heme and by <b>glucose loading</b>; the red-cell form, ALA synthase 2, is controlled by <b>intracellular iron</b> (and erythropoietin).<br>A. Ferrochelatase: the last step (adds iron); lead inhibits it, but it does not run this reaction.<br>B. ALA dehydratase: the next step (aminolevulinic acid to porphobilinogen); lead inhibits it, but it does not run this reaction.","ex":"","fl":["D is also correct. The erythroid isoform (ALAS2/ALAS-E) is regulated by intracellular iron through an iron-responsive element (G026:10; the source's own slide 166). Glucose loading represses the hepatic isoform (G026:24). Both statements are true, so the question has two correct answers."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)","L31 Heme slide 24 (Porphyrias)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":163,"f":"F0048"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Heme synthesis compartments; zinc ALA dehydratase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-14.jpg","cap":"Steps one and six to eight in mitochondrion; two to five in cytoplasm","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 14"},{"src":"sl/G026-9.jpg","cap":"ALA dehydratase joins two aminolevulinic acids into porphobilinogen; heavy metals inhibit it","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-95-1","s":"Which of the following answer choices best describes the synthesis of heme?","o":["A. The first and last steps of heme synthesis take place in the cytosol","B. Steps 2-5 of heme synthesis take place in the mitochondria","C. The rate limiting step of heme formation occurs in the cytosol","D. Lead toxicity can be partially attributed to the rate limiting step of heme synthesis","E. Formation of the pyrrole ring in heme synthesis is catalyzed by a zinc-containing enzyme"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Formation of the pyrrole ring in heme synthesis is catalyzed by a zinc-containing enzyme","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>ALA dehydratase</b> (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase) joins two aminolevulinic acids into porphobilinogen, the first <b>pyrrole ring</b>. It is a <b>zinc-containing</b> enzyme, and lead displaces the zinc.<br>A. The first step and the last three steps are in the <b>mitochondria</b>, not the cytosol.<br>B. Steps two to five are in the <b>cytosol</b>.<br>C. The rate-limiting step (ALA synthase) is <b>mitochondrial</b>.<br>D. Lead blocks ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, not the rate-limiting ALA synthase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 14 (d-aminolevulinic acid synthase (ALA synthase))"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":95,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":178,"f":"F1047"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Lead inactivates ALA dehydratase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-12.jpg","cap":"Lead binds sulfhydryl groups of ferrochelatase and ALA dehydratase; aminolevulinic acid, protoporphyrin rise","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G026-9.jpg","cap":"Step two: ALA dehydratase, extremely sensitive to heavy-metal (lead) inhibition","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-10-1","s":"Which of the following clinical presentations can inactivate ALA Dehydratase?","o":["A. Iron poisoning","B. Cu poisoning","C. Lead poisoning","D. Al poisoning"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Lead poisoning","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Lead</b> binds the sulfhydryl groups of <b>ALA dehydratase</b> (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase) and displaces its zinc. It also blocks ferrochelatase. Aminolevulinic acid and zinc protoporphyrin rise, and a microcytic anemia follows.<br>A. Iron: the metal ferrochelatase inserts; iron poisoning does not block ALA dehydratase.<br>B. Copper and D. aluminum: not the heavy metal that inhibits this enzyme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)","L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":10,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Barbiturates induce hepatic ALA synthase 1","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-10.jpg","cap":"ALA synthase 1 synthesis induced by liver-metabolized drugs such as barbiturates","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/G026-20.jpg","cap":"Acute intermittent porphyria attacks triggered by barbiturates, anesthetics, alcohol via cytochrome P450","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-3-1","s":"A 37 year-old male with history of seizure disorder presents to the ED after being found unconscious by patient’s wife, who reports that patient was found next to an empty bottle of barbiturate medication, for which he takes for his seizure disorder. Assuming the patient overdosed on barbiturates, what enzyme in his body is being overly synthesized?","o":["A. Phospholipase A2","B. 7α-hydroxylase","C. δ-aminolevulinate synthase","D. ALA dehydratase","E. Ferrochelatase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"δ-aminolevulinate synthase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The liver breaks down barbiturates with <b>cytochrome P450</b>, a heme protein. Barbiturates induce it, use up heme, and so <b>induce δ-aminolevulinate synthase</b> (ALA synthase 1).<br>A. Phospholipase A2: releases arachidonic acid; not induced this way.<br>B. 7α-hydroxylase: the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis, not heme synthesis.<br>D. ALA dehydratase (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase): step two, inhibited by lead; not the induced enzyme.<br>E. Ferrochelatase: the last step; not induced by barbiturates.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":3,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":167,"f":"F1070"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":37,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":181,"f":"F3882"}]},{"id":"F0065-1-1","s":"A 24-year-old woman has a history of intermittent episodes of severe abdominal pain, anxiety, and darkening of the urine on standing. Workup during a prior episode showed elevated aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen, and she was diagnosed with acute intermittent porphyria due to porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency. At a follow-up visit, she is advised to avoid drugs that markedly induce hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes. Which mechanism best explains why such drugs can precipitate acute attacks in this patient?","o":["A. Increased hepatic demand for heme, upregulating δ-aminolevulinate (ALA) synthase 1 and increasing ALA and porphobilinogen production","B. Direct inhibition of porphobilinogen deaminase by these drugs","C. Decreased activity of ferrochelatase, impairing insertion of iron into protoporphyrin IX","D. Acceleration of heme breakdown to bilirubin in macrophages of the reticuloendothelial system","E. Reduced hepatic albumin synthesis, diminishing bilirubin transport in plasma"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased hepatic demand for heme, upregulating δ-aminolevulinate (ALA) synthase 1 and increasing ALA and porphobilinogen production","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> These drugs <b>induce cytochrome P450</b>, a heme protein, so the liver uses up heme. Low free heme <b>lifts feedback inhibition of ALA synthase 1</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase 1). More aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen then pile up above the porphobilinogen deaminase block.<br>B. The drugs do not inhibit porphobilinogen deaminase directly.<br>C. Ferrochelatase: blocked by lead, not these drugs.<br>D. Heme breakdown in macrophages does not make aminolevulinic acid or porphobilinogen.<br>E. Albumin and bilirubin transport play no part in the attack.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)","L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":1,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Lead: high ALA and zinc protoporphyrin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-13.jpg","cap":"Lead: high aminolevulinic acid and zinc protoporphyrin; B6 deficiency: low aminolevulinic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G026-12.jpg","cap":"Lead blocks ferrochelatase (iron insertion) and ALA dehydratase; protoporphyrin rises in red cells","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-22-1","s":"A factory worker has microcytic anemia with elevated ALA and elevated zinc protoporphyrin. The most likely cause is:","o":["A. Iron deficiency","B. Lead poisoning","C. B₆ deficiency","D. Sideroblastic anemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lead poisoning","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Microcytic anemia with <b>high aminolevulinic acid and high zinc protoporphyrin</b> is <b>lead poisoning</b>. Lead blocks ALA dehydratase (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase) and ferrochelatase, so zinc goes in where iron should.<br>A. Iron deficiency: microcytic, but aminolevulinic acid is not raised.<br>C. Vitamin B6 deficiency: microcytic with <b>low</b> aminolevulinic acid.<br>D. Sideroblastic anemia: a type of anemia, not a cause; lead is one of its causes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)","L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":22,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":22,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0144-9-1","s":"A 23 year old art student presents with significant fatigue. A blood count reveals a microcytic anemia and urinalysis revealed high ALA. Which one of the following may account for this type of anemia?","o":["A. Vitamin B6 deficiency","B. Lead poisoning","C. Barbiturate overdose","D. Iron deficiency","E. Folate deficiency"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lead poisoning","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> An art student handles paints. Microcytic anemia with <b>high urine aminolevulinic acid</b> means <b>lead poisoning</b>: lead blocks ALA dehydratase (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase), so its substrate builds up.<br>A. Vitamin B6 deficiency: microcytic, but aminolevulinic acid is <b>low</b>.<br>C. Barbiturate overdose: induces heme synthesis; it does not cause anemia.<br>D. Iron deficiency: microcytic, but aminolevulinic acid is not high.<br>E. Folate deficiency: a <b>macrocytic</b> anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)","L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":9,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":9,"f":"F1048"}]},{"id":"F2438-39-1","s":"From the previous question, the patient’s wife was so distraught that she fainted as the patient was being treated. The wife is admitted and upon regaining consciousness, she states that she has been feeling lightheaded for the past few months, ever since she’s been taking up pottery as a hobby. Upon hearing this, you run a blood smear which reveals microcytic anemia, as well as a urinalysis which reveals presence of ALA and protoporphyrin. From what is this patient suffering?","o":["A. Lead poisoning","B. B6 deficiency","C. Zinc poisoning","D. B9 deficiency"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lead poisoning","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Pottery glazes contain lead. Microcytic anemia with <b>aminolevulinic acid and protoporphyrin</b> building up is <b>lead poisoning</b>. Lead blocks ALA dehydratase (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase) and ferrochelatase.<br>B. Vitamin B6 deficiency: microcytic, but aminolevulinic acid is low.<br>C. Zinc poisoning: not the cause; zinc protoporphyrin rises in lead poisoning because zinc takes iron's place.<br>D. Vitamin B9 (folate) deficiency: a macrocytic anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)","L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":39,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":183,"f":"F3882"},{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":10,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":174,"f":"F1070"}]},{"id":"F2438-8-1","s":"A 45 year old male comes into your office with abdominal pain, recent neurological changes, irritability and joint pain. From your history taking, you learn he works with old paint and suspect he could have lead poisoning. What findings would confirm your suspicion?","o":["A. Microcytic anemia, low ALA, high zinc protoporphyrin","B. Macrocytic anemia, low ALA, high zinc protoporphyrin","C. Microcytic anemia, high ALA, high zinc protoporphyrin","D. Microcytic anemia, high ALA, low zinc protoporphyrin","E. Microcytic anemia, high ALA, high zinc protoporphyrin"],"g":["C","E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic anemia, high ALA, high zinc protoporphyrin","a":"E","why":"<b>C and E</b> (the same text): lead poisoning gives <b>microcytic anemia, high aminolevulinic acid and high zinc protoporphyrin</b>. Lead blocks ALA dehydratase (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase), so aminolevulinic acid rises, and blocks ferrochelatase, so zinc goes in place of iron.<br>A. Low aminolevulinic acid: that is vitamin B6 deficiency.<br>B. Lead gives a microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia, and aminolevulinic acid is high.<br>D. Zinc protoporphyrin is high, not low.","ex":"","fl":["Options C and E are word-for-word identical (both 'Microcytic anemia, high ALA, high zinc protoporphyrin'), so C is equally correct; the key marks only E."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)","L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":8,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":152,"f":"F3882"}]},{"id":"F0179-20-1","s":"A 31-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with a 4 month history of fatigue and shortness of breath. She is vegan, drinks 2 glasses of wine per night, and lives in an old house. Physical exam reveals a thin, disheveled woman with conjunctival pallor. Selected laboratory tests showed: Hemoglobin: 9.5 g/dL (12 to 16 g/dL); Mean corpuscular volume: 73 fl (81 to 99 fl); Ferritin: 130 ng/ml (10 to 120 ng/mL). (Normal range given in the parenthesis). Which of the following is the best explanation of this patient's presentation?","o":["A. Ferrochelatase inhibition","B. Decreased hepcidin expression","C. Increased hepcidin expression","D. Inherited hemoglobinopathy","E. Iron deficiency"],"g":["A"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased hepcidin expression","a":"C","why":"<b>A.</b> An <b>old house</b> means lead paint. Lead <b>inhibits ferrochelatase</b>, so iron cannot be put into protoporphyrin. The result is a <b>microcytic anemia with normal or high ferritin</b>: iron is there but unused.<br>B. Decreased hepcidin: iron overload, not anemia.<br>C. Increased hepcidin: anemia of chronic disease, but no inflammatory disease is given.<br>D. Inherited hemoglobinopathy: nothing in the history points to it.<br>E. Iron deficiency (vegan diet): ferritin would be low, not high.","ex":"","fl":["The stem names no chronic inflammatory disease (the source's explanation refers to rheumatoid arthritis, which is not in this stem). 'Lives in an old house' = lead paint; lead inhibits ferrochelatase (and ALA dehydratase) → microcytic (sideroblastic) anemia with normal/high ferritin (G026:12–13). Vegan and wine are distractors; normal/high ferritin rules out iron deficiency."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)","L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":20,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":4,"f":"F0293"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"B6 deficiency: low ALA, microcytic anemia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-13.jpg","cap":"B6 deficiency from isoniazid blocks ALA synthase: low aminolevulinic acid, microcytic anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F2438-9-1","s":"What would be the findings for B6 deficiency?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Low ALA and microcytic anemia (ALA synthase cannot work without pyridoxal phosphate)","a":null,"why":"<b>Low aminolevulinic acid and microcytic anemia.</b> ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase) needs <b>pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)</b>. Without it, the first step slows, so little aminolevulinic acid and little heme are made. Zinc protoporphyrin is not raised.<br>Common mix-up: lead poisoning is also microcytic, but its aminolevulinic acid and zinc protoporphyrin are <b>high</b>. A classic cause of B6 deficiency is isoniazid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":9,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":153,"f":"F3882"}]},{"id":"F2438-35-1","s":"Match the disease to the appropriate outcomes:","o":["A. B6 deficiency → high ALA, microcytic anemia","B. B6 deficiency → low ALA, microcytic anemia","C. Lead poisoning → low ALA, low zinc protoporphyrin, microcytic anemia","D. Lead poisoning → increased heme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"B6 deficiency → low ALA, microcytic anemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Vitamin B6 deficiency gives <b>low aminolevulinic acid and microcytic anemia</b>, because ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase) needs B6.<br>A. B6 deficiency lowers aminolevulinic acid; it does not raise it.<br>C. Lead poisoning raises aminolevulinic acid and zinc protoporphyrin; it does not lower them.<br>D. Lead poisoning lowers heme; that is why there is anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":35,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":179,"f":"F3882"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":164,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":8,"f":"F2433"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0112-7-1","s":"A patient presents with fatigue, shortness of breath, mucosal and conjunctival pallor, and lightheadedness. If ALA levels are low, what would you suspect is causing the patient’s symptoms?","o":["A. Ingestion of lead paint","B. Treatment for tuberculosis","C. High animal protein diet, lacking leafy green vegetables","D. Chronic alcohol consumption"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Treatment for tuberculosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Low aminolevulinic acid</b> means ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase) is not working, as in <b>vitamin B6 deficiency</b>. The lecture's cause is <b>isoniazid</b>, the tuberculosis drug.<br>A. Lead paint: blocks later steps, so aminolevulinic acid is <b>high</b>.<br>C. A diet low in leafy greens: folate deficiency, a macrocytic anemia; animal protein is rich in B6.<br>D. Chronic alcohol use: usually a macrocytic anemia; it is not the cause taught for low aminolevulinic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)","L31 Heme slide 9 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":7,"f":"F0112"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Porphyrias: definition, inheritance, classification","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-18.jpg","cap":"Enzyme-disease chart; all porphyrias autosomal dominant except congenital erythropoietic porphyria (recessive)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/G026-17.jpg","cap":"Symptoms: product deficiency beyond the block or metabolite build-up above it","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G026-16.jpg","cap":"Porphyrias: inherited defects of heme biosynthesis enzymes","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F1061-166-1","s":"Which of the following statements is true regarding porphyrias?","o":["A. All porphyrias are inherited autosomal dominant with no exception","B. Signs and symptoms result from either a deficiency of metabolic products above the enzymatic block or an accumulation of metabolites beyond the block","C. Group of inherited metabolic disorders due to the defect in heme biosynthesis","D. Classified on the basis of organs or cells that are least affected"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Group of inherited metabolic disorders due to the defect in heme biosynthesis","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Porphyrias are <b>inherited metabolic disorders of heme biosynthesis</b>.<br>A. False: all are autosomal dominant <b>except congenital erythropoietic porphyria</b>, which is autosomal recessive.<br>B. Reversed: symptoms come from a deficiency of products <b>beyond</b> the block or a build-up of metabolites <b>above</b> it.<br>D. They are classified by the organs or cells <b>most</b> affected (erythropoietic or hepatic), not least.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 16 (Porphyrias)","L31 Heme slide 17 (Signs and symptoms result from either a deficiency of the metabolic products bey)","L31 Heme slide 18 (Porphyrias)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":166,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":10,"f":"F2433"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Hepatic porphyrias: AIP acute, PCT chronic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-19.jpg","cap":"Erythropoietic vs hepatic: acute intermittent porphyria (acute), porphyria cutanea tarda (chronic)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G026-23.jpg","cap":"Table comparing acute intermittent porphyria with porphyria cutanea tarda","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F1061-168-1","s":"Which type of porphyria is chronic vs. acute?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Chronic porphyria → Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT); Acute porphyria → acute intermittent porphyria (AIP); both hepatic as opposed to erythropoietic","a":null,"why":"<b>Acute = acute intermittent porphyria; chronic = porphyria cutanea tarda.</b> Both are <b>hepatic</b> porphyrias. Acute intermittent porphyria gives attacks of abdominal pain and neuropsychiatric symptoms, set off by barbiturates, alcohol and anesthetics. Porphyria cutanea tarda is a slow skin disease with photosensitivity that starts in the forties or fifties.<br>Common mix-up: congenital erythropoietic porphyria and erythropoietic protoporphyria are <b>erythropoietic</b>, with skin blisters from childhood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"slide shows the heme-synthesis pathway chart with a box for each porphyria (lead poisoning, AIP, EPP, VP, HCP, PCT, CEP); not needed to answer","tg":["L31 Heme slide 19 (Porphyrias)","L31 Heme slide 23 (| AIP | PCT |)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":168,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":12,"f":"F2433"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"AIP: ALA and PBG cause neuro symptoms","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-20.jpg","cap":"Porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency: aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen accumulate, causing abdominal, neuropsychiatric symptoms","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G026-17.jpg","cap":"Flowchart: precursor build-up gives neuropsychiatric signs; porphyrinogen build-up gives photosensitivity","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-25-1","s":"A patient with severe abdominal pain, anxiety, and hallucinations is suspected of having Acute Intermittent Porphyria (AIP). What is responsible for these symptoms?","o":["A. Elevated heme","B. Toxic buildup of ALA and PBG","C. Lack of cytochrome oxidase","D. Accumulation of bilirubin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Toxic buildup of ALA and PBG","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> In acute intermittent porphyria, porphobilinogen deaminase is deficient. <b>Aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen</b> build up and are <b>toxic to abdominal nerves and the brain</b>, giving pain, anxiety and hallucinations.<br>A. Heme is low, not high.<br>C. Cytochrome oxidase is not the problem.<br>D. Bilirubin comes from heme breakdown; it is not raised in this disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":25,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":25,"f":"F0890"}]},{"id":"F0257-34-1","s":"Which metabolites accumulate in acute intermittent porphyria and cause neuropsychiatric symptoms?","o":["A. Uroporphyrinogens and coproporphyrinogens accumulate in tissues","B. Delta-aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen accumulate in tissues","C. Protoporphyrin IX and heme accumulate in tissues","D. Bilirubin and biliverdin accumulate in tissues"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Delta-aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen accumulate in tissues","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Porphobilinogen deaminase is blocked early, so its substrates, <b>δ-aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen</b>, build up. They are neurotoxic: abdominal pain and neuropsychiatric symptoms.<br>A. Uroporphyrinogens and coproporphyrinogens: build up with later blocks, such as porphyria cutanea tarda, and cause photosensitivity.<br>C. Protoporphyrin IX builds up in erythropoietic protoporphyria; heme is low, not high.<br>D. Bilirubin and biliverdin: heme breakdown products, not raised here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":34,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":34,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"AIP = porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-20.jpg","cap":"Acute intermittent porphyria: porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency; aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen accumulate","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G026-18.jpg","cap":"Chart pairing each heme-pathway enzyme with its porphyria","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-40-1","s":"Which condition results from deficiency of porphobilinogen deaminase?","o":["A. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria","B. Erythropoietic protoporphyria","C. Acute intermittent porphyria","D. Porphyria cutanea tarda"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute intermittent porphyria","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Porphobilinogen deaminase</b> (also called hydroxymethylbilane synthase) deficiency causes <b>acute intermittent porphyria</b>.<br>A. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria: uroporphyrinogen III synthase deficiency.<br>B. Erythropoietic protoporphyria: ferrochelatase deficiency.<br>D. Porphyria cutanea tarda: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":40,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":40,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0257-52-1","s":"A patient presents with abdominal pain and neuropsychiatric symptoms. Lab studies show elevated ALA and PBG. Which enzyme deficiency is most likely?","o":["A. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","B. Ferrochelatase","C. Porphobilinogen deaminase","D. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphobilinogen deaminase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Abdominal pain, neuropsychiatric symptoms and high <b>aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen</b> mean acute intermittent porphyria: <b>porphobilinogen deaminase</b> deficiency.<br>A. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase: porphyria cutanea tarda; skin photosensitivity, not neurological signs.<br>B. Ferrochelatase: erythropoietic protoporphyria; photosensitivity.<br>D. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase: congenital erythropoietic porphyria; skin blisters from childhood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":52,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":52,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0144-14-1","s":"A 34-year-old female has a history of sudden onset episodes of severe abdominal pain. Her urine appears dark during an attack and gets even darker if exposed to sunlight. There was an accumulation of ALA and PBG in her blood as well. This patient most likely has a defect in which enzyme?","o":["A. δ-Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthase","B. Porphobilinogen (PBG) deaminase","C. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase","D. δ-Aminolevulinic acid (ALA) dehydratase","E. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphobilinogen (PBG) deaminase","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Attacks of abdominal pain, dark urine that <b>darkens further in light</b>, and high <b>aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen</b> mean acute intermittent porphyria: <b>porphobilinogen deaminase</b> deficiency.<br>A. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase): the first step; a block here would lower aminolevulinic acid.<br>C. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase: congenital erythropoietic porphyria.<br>D. ALA dehydratase: a block here raises aminolevulinic acid but not porphobilinogen.<br>E. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase: porphyria cutanea tarda.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":14,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":14,"f":"F1048"}]},{"id":"F0179-8-1","s":"A 25-year-old female who is an outdoors enthusiast presents to the emergency department with vague abdominal pain. Physical exam is remarkable only for diffuse abdominal pain with no rebound or localizing signs. A urine sample is obtained and is shown below (the tube on the left contains water for comparison). It also tests positive for aminolevulinic acid (ALA) and porphobilinogen. A deficiency in which of the following enzymes is most likely responsible for this patient's symptoms?","o":["A. ALA Dehydratase","B. Porphobilinogen deaminase","C. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","D. Ferrochelatase","E. Hemoglobin beta chain"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphobilinogen deaminase","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Vague abdominal pain, port-wine colored urine, and urine positive for <b>aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen</b> mean acute intermittent porphyria, from <b>porphobilinogen deaminase</b> deficiency.<br>A. ALA dehydratase (aminolevulinic acid dehydratase): its block raises aminolevulinic acid only, not porphobilinogen.<br>C. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase: porphyria cutanea tarda, with skin lesions and uroporphyrins.<br>D. Ferrochelatase: erythropoietic protoporphyria (and lead poisoning).<br>E. Hemoglobin beta chain: hemoglobinopathies, not porphyria.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))","L31 Heme slide 11 (Heme Biosynthesis steps)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":8,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":5,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":5,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":6,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":8,"f":"F2421"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":2,"f":"F0293"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-13-1","s":"By the time the patient’s wife’s tests are being run, the initial patient regains consciousness and is referred outpatient to run a battery of tests. By the time the patient undergoes these tests, he began to complain of abdominal pain, chest tightness, and some tingling to his fingertips. Given his prior barbiturate overdose, from what disease is this patient suffering, and what is the deficient enzyme?","o":["A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria; Ferrochelatase","B. Porphyria cutanea tarda; Uroporphyrinogen Decarboxylase","C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria; Uroporphyrinogen Cosynthase","D. Acute intermittent porphyria; PBG Deaminase","E. Variegate porphyria; protoporphyrinogen Oxidase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute intermittent porphyria; PBG Deaminase","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> After barbiturates, abdominal pain, chest tightness and tingling fingers (neuropathy) mean <b>acute intermittent porphyria</b>, from <b>porphobilinogen deaminase</b> deficiency.<br>A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria (ferrochelatase): photosensitivity, no attacks.<br>B. Porphyria cutanea tarda (uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase): chronic skin blisters.<br>C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria (uroporphyrinogen III cosynthase): skin disease from childhood.<br>E. Variegate porphyria (protoporphyrinogen oxidase): can have attacks, but usually with skin photosensitivity, which is absent here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":13,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":177,"f":"F1070"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Barbiturates and alcohol trigger AIP","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-20.jpg","cap":"Attacks precipitated by barbiturates, anesthetics and alcohol (cytochrome P450 heme demand)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G026-23.jpg","cap":"Table: acute intermittent porphyria drug-precipitated and neurological; porphyria cutanea tarda cutaneous","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-24-1","s":"Which type of porphyria is precipitated by drugs such as barbiturates and alcohol?","o":["A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria is precipitated by these drugs","B. Porphyria cutanea tarda is precipitated by these drugs","C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria is precipitated by these drugs","D. Acute intermittent porphyria is precipitated by these drugs"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute intermittent porphyria is precipitated by these drugs","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Acute intermittent porphyria</b> attacks are set off by <b>barbiturates, alcohol and anesthetics</b>. These drugs induce liver cytochrome P450, use up heme, and raise ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase).<br>A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria: photosensitivity from childhood; no drug-triggered attacks.<br>B. Porphyria cutanea tarda: a chronic skin disease worsened by iron, sunlight and hepatitis; no acute drug-triggered attacks.<br>C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria: autosomal recessive skin disease from childhood; not drug-triggered.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))","L31 Heme slide 23 (| AIP | PCT |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":24,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":24,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0359-5-1","s":"A stuporous male presents to the emergency department complaining of abdominal pain and hallucinations. The paramedics state he was found on the streets reeking of alcohol. What is the most likely cause of his symptoms?","o":["A. Porphyria cutanea tarda","B. Acute intermittent porphyria","C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria","D. Erythropoietic protoporphyria"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute intermittent porphyria","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Alcohol</b> can set off an attack of <b>acute intermittent porphyria</b>: abdominal pain plus neuropsychiatric signs such as hallucinations. The defect is porphobilinogen deaminase deficiency.<br>A. Porphyria cutanea tarda: chronic blistering skin; no acute neurological attacks.<br>C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria: skin blisters from early childhood.<br>D. Erythropoietic protoporphyria: photosensitivity from childhood; no neurological attacks.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))","L31 Heme slide 23 (| AIP | PCT |)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":5,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":111,"f":"F2304"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme & biotin TA review","sl":5,"f":"F2441"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Porphyria profiles: lead, AIP, PCT, EPP","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-25.jpg","cap":"Overview chart: each porphyria's enzyme, accumulated product, and photosensitivity","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G026-23.jpg","cap":"Table: acute intermittent porphyria raises aminolevulinic acid, porphobilinogen; cutanea tarda raises uroporphyrins","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-14-1","s":"Match the characteristics to the correct porphyria. Characteristics: Protoporphyrin and ALA accumulate in urine / Urine darkens on exposure to light and air. Patients NOT photosensitive. / Uroporphyrin accumulates in the urine. Patients are photosensitive. / Protoporphyrin accumulates in erythrocytes. Patients are photosensitive. — Porphyrias: 1 Porphyria cutanea tarda, 2 Lead poisoning, 3 Acute intermittent porphyria, 4 Erythropoietic protoporphyria","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Protoporphyrin + ALA in urine → 2 lead poisoning; urine darkens, not photosensitive → 3 AIP; uroporphyrin in urine, photosensitive → 1 PCT; protoporphyrin in erythrocytes, photosensitive → 4 EPP","a":null,"why":"<b>Lead poisoning</b>: protoporphyrin and aminolevulinic acid accumulate. <b>Acute intermittent porphyria</b>: aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen in urine, which darkens in light and air; no photosensitivity. <b>Porphyria cutanea tarda</b>: uroporphyrin in urine; photosensitive. <b>Erythropoietic protoporphyria</b>: protoporphyrin in red cells; photosensitive.<br>Rule: blocks <b>after</b> porphyrinogens form cause <b>photosensitivity</b>; early blocks (before any porphyrin ring) cause neurological symptoms instead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 20 (Acute intermittent porphyria(AIP))","L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 13 (| B6 deficiency Intake of anti tubercle drug Isoniazid(INH) Glycine +Succinyl Co)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":14,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Photosensitivity + uroporphyrins → PCT","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-21.jpg","cap":"Porphyria cutanea tarda: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency, blistered sun-exposed skin, red urine","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G026-23.jpg","cap":"Table: porphyria cutanea tarda cutaneous with high uroporphyrins; acute intermittent porphyria neurological","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-9-1","s":"A patient presents with chronic blistering on sun-exposed areas. Laboratory tests reveal elevated uroporphyrins. Which type of porphyria is most likely?","o":["A. Acute intermittent porphyria (AIP)","B. Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT)","C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria","D. Variegate porphyria"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphyria cutanea tarda (PCT)","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Chronic blistering on sun-exposed skin</b> with <b>high uroporphyrins</b> is <b>porphyria cutanea tarda</b> (uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency), the chronic hepatic porphyria.<br>A. Acute intermittent porphyria: neurovisceral attacks with high aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen; no skin lesions.<br>C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria: severe blistering from early childhood, autosomal recessive.<br>D. Variegate porphyria: photosensitivity plus acute attacks; protoporphyrin and coproporphyrin rise, not mainly uroporphyrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":9,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":9,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0928-13-1","s":"A 40-year old single, Caucasian female presents in the emergency room in an agitated state, complaining of severe abdominal pain and a rash. The patient’s history reveals similar past episodes with the severe skin rashes when exposed to sunlight. Her physical examination and laboratory tests appear largely normal, with the exception of high levels of uroporphyrin III in the urine and a photosensitive skin. What is the likely diagnosis of this patient based on her clinical presentation?","o":["A. Porphyria Cutanea Tarda","B. Acute Intermittent Porphyria","C. Phenylketonuria","D. Acute lead poisoning","E. Alkaptonuria"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphyria Cutanea Tarda","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> A rash on sun exposure with <b>high urine uroporphyrin</b> means <b>porphyria cutanea tarda</b>.<br>B. Acute intermittent porphyria: pain and agitation fit, but there is <b>no photosensitivity</b>, and aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen rise, not uroporphyrin.<br>C. Phenylketonuria: phenylalanine build-up, intellectual disability, musty odor.<br>D. Acute lead poisoning: high aminolevulinic acid and zinc protoporphyrin; no uroporphyrin rise.<br>E. Alkaptonuria: homogentisic acid; urine darkens, cartilage turns dark.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )","L31 Heme slide 23 (| AIP | PCT |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":13,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2019 clicker","n":"old Exam 3 clicker questions: heme part only","sl":22,"f":"F0034"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"PCT: porphyrins → photosensitive skin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-22.jpg","cap":"Late blocks: porphyrins accumulate; light-excited porphyrins form radicals that damage skin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G026-21.jpg","cap":"Porphyria cutanea tarda hands with blisters and scarring; red-brown urine","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-54-1","s":"A patient comes to your office complaining of wine-red colored urine. Upon physical examination you observe blisters on his hands and face. You diagnose this patient with the most common porphyria. Which symptom is most commonly seen?","o":["A. Kernicterus","B. Psychotic episodes","C. Photosensitive skin","D. Hepatitis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Photosensitive skin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Wine-red urine and blisters on the hands and face mean <b>porphyria cutanea tarda</b>, the most common porphyria. Its main sign is <b>photosensitive skin</b>: porphyrins in the skin react to light.<br>A. Kernicterus: brain damage from unconjugated bilirubin in newborns.<br>B. Psychotic episodes: acute intermittent porphyria.<br>D. Hepatitis: can trigger porphyria cutanea tarda, but it is not the main sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":54,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 review","n":"old Exam 3 review: heme part only","sl":219,"f":"F0044"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":21,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0257-12-1","s":"What is the primary consequence of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency in porphyria cutanea tarda?","o":["A. Defective bilirubin conjugation causing jaundice","B. Elevated protoporphyrin in RBCs causing hemolytic anemia","C. Accumulation of ALA and PBG causing neurological symptoms","D. Accumulation of porphyrins causing photosensitivity and skin damage"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Accumulation of porphyrins causing photosensitivity and skin damage","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase is a <b>late</b> step. Porphyrinogens pile up and oxidize to <b>porphyrins</b>, which make the skin <b>photosensitive</b>: blisters and scarring.<br>A. Defective bilirubin conjugation: Crigler-Najjar syndromes, not a porphyria.<br>B. Protoporphyrin in red cells: erythropoietic protoporphyria or lead poisoning.<br>C. Aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen with neurological symptoms: acute intermittent porphyria.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":12,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":12,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"PCT = uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-21.jpg","cap":"Porphyria cutanea tarda: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency; onset in forties to fifties","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G026-18.jpg","cap":"Enzyme-disease chart: uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase → porphyria cutanea tarda","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-12-1","s":"Which enzyme deficiency leads to Porphyria Cutanea Tarda?","o":["A. ALA Synthetase","B. Ferrochelatase (FeCH)","C. ALA Dehydratase","D. Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase (UroIIID)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase (UroIIID)","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Porphyria cutanea tarda</b> is <b>uroporphyrinogen (III) decarboxylase</b> deficiency. Uroporphyrins build up and make the skin photosensitive.<br>A. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase): its deficiency gives X-linked sideroblastic anemia.<br>B. Ferrochelatase: erythropoietic protoporphyria; also inhibited by lead.<br>C. ALA dehydratase: deficiency gives the rare ALA-dehydratase-deficient porphyria; inhibited by lead.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":12,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F0035-16-1","s":"A 30 year old male comes to your office and presents with photosensitivity and high concentration of uroporphyrin in his urine. This disorder is caused by which enzyme deficiency?","o":["A. ALA synthase","B. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase","C. Ferrochelatase","D. Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Photosensitivity with high urine uroporphyrin</b> means <b>porphyria cutanea tarda</b>, from <b>uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase</b> deficiency.<br>A. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase): deficiency gives X-linked sideroblastic anemia, not porphyrin build-up.<br>B. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase: congenital erythropoietic porphyria; severe disease from early childhood.<br>C. Ferrochelatase: erythropoietic protoporphyria; protoporphyrin builds up in red cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":16,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F0048-167-1","s":"A patient comes to your office complaining of wine-red colored urine. Upon physical examination you observe blisters on his hand and face. You diagnose the patient with the most common porphyria. This disorder is caused by which enzyme deficiency?","o":["A. ALA synthase","B. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase","C. Ferrochelatase","D. Uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Uroporphyrinogen (III) decarboxylase - porphyria cutanea tarda","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The most common porphyria, with wine-red urine and blisters on the hands and face, is <b>porphyria cutanea tarda</b>: <b>uroporphyrinogen III decarboxylase</b> deficiency.<br>A. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase): deficiency gives X-linked sideroblastic anemia.<br>B. Uroporphyrinogen III synthase: congenital erythropoietic porphyria, rare and from childhood.<br>C. Ferrochelatase: erythropoietic protoporphyria, painful photosensitivity from childhood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":167,"f":"F0048"}]},{"id":"F0112-9-1","s":"A 55 year old man presents with reddish brown urine and skin photosensitivity and blistering. Which of the following describes his condition?","o":["A. Accumulation of ALA and PBG","B. Deficiency of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","C. Neurological symptoms are also likely to be present","D. Caused by treatment for HIV infection"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Deficiency of uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Reddish-brown urine with <b>photosensitive, blistering skin</b> in a middle-aged man is <b>porphyria cutanea tarda</b>: <b>uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase</b> deficiency.<br>A. Aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen build-up: acute intermittent porphyria.<br>C. Neurological symptoms: acute intermittent porphyria, not porphyria cutanea tarda.<br>D. <b>HIV infection</b> (human immunodeficiency virus), like hepatitis B or C, can bring it out; the treatment is not the cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 23 (| AIP | PCT |)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":9,"f":"F0112"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-15-1","s":"Upon hearing of his diagnosis, the patient recalls of his grandfather who suffered from a similar disease. According to the patient, his grandfather began to suffer symptoms of this disease in his 50s, when he retired early and spent his time at the beach near his vacation home. His symptoms included itches and burns across his skin as well as his urine appearing brown. Based on this description, from what was the patient’s grandfather suffering, and what was the deficient enzyme?","o":["A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria; Ferrochelatase","B. Porphyria cutanea tarda; Uroporphyrinogen Decarboxylase","C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria; Uroporphyrinogen Cosynthase","D. Acute intermittent porphyria; PBG Deaminase","E. Variegate porphyria; protoporphyrinogen Oxidase"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphyria cutanea tarda; Uroporphyrinogen Decarboxylase","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Onset in the <b>fifties</b>, <b>skin itching and burning in the sun</b>, and brown urine mean <b>porphyria cutanea tarda</b>: <b>uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase</b> deficiency.<br>A. Erythropoietic protoporphyria (ferrochelatase): photosensitivity from childhood.<br>C. Congenital erythropoietic porphyria (uroporphyrinogen III cosynthase): severe skin disease from early childhood; the only autosomal recessive porphyria.<br>D. Acute intermittent porphyria (porphobilinogen deaminase): attacks, no skin signs.<br>E. Variegate porphyria (protoporphyrinogen oxidase): skin signs plus acute attacks.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)","L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":15,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":179,"f":"F1070"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Light-excited porphyrins make oxygen radicals","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-22.jpg","cap":"Light excites porphyrins → oxygen radicals → lysosome damage → skin scarring","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-26-1","s":"What is the mechanism by which porphyrins cause photosensitivity in chronic porphyrias?","o":["A. Porphyrins become excited by light and react with oxygen to form radicals","B. Porphyrins absorb UV radiation and directly damage DNA","C. Porphyrins increase blood flow to superficial skin layers","D. Porphyrins block melanin production in the skin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Porphyrins become excited by light and react with oxygen to form radicals","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Porphyrins in the skin absorb visible light (about four hundred nanometers) and become <b>excited</b>. They react with oxygen to form <b>oxygen radicals</b>. The radicals damage lysosomes, which release their enzymes and injure the skin, causing scarring.<br>B. The damage comes from radicals made with visible light, not direct damage to DNA (deoxyribonucleic acid) by ultraviolet light.<br>C. Porphyrins do not raise skin blood flow.<br>D. Porphyrins do not block melanin production.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 22 (Enzyme blocks later in the pathway Leads to the accumulation of porphyrinogens )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":26,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":26,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"AIP treatment: hemin, glucose repress ALA synthase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-24.jpg","cap":"Glucose loading or hemin represses ALA synthase; avoid barbiturates, alcohol, anesthetics","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G026-23.jpg","cap":"Table: heme relieves symptoms in acute intermittent porphyria and porphyria cutanea tarda","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-11-1","s":"A 25-year-old woman presents with severe abdominal pain and neuropsychiatric symptoms. Her urine darkens on standing and is positive for ALA and porphobilinogen. Apart from avoiding precipitating drugs, which biochemical step would be most effective to target therapeutically?","o":["A. Inhibition of heme oxygenase in the spleen","B. Inhibition of bilirubin conjugation in hepatocytes","C. Suppression of hepatic ALA synthase activity","D. Inhibition of intestinal bacterial conversion of bilirubin","E. Stimulation of albumin synthesis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Suppression of hepatic ALA synthase activity","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In acute intermittent porphyria, the goal is to <b>shut down hepatic ALA synthase</b> (aminolevulinic acid synthase) so less aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen are made. <b>Hemin (heme)</b> and <b>glucose loading</b> both repress it.<br>A. Heme oxygenase: heme breakdown; blocking it does not lower the precursors.<br>B. Bilirubin conjugation: unrelated to porphyrin precursors.<br>D. Gut bacterial conversion of bilirubin: unrelated.<br>E. Albumin synthesis: unrelated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 24 (Porphyrias)","L31 Heme slide 10 (Regulation of Heme Biosynthesis)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":11,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"85% of degraded heme from old RBCs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-27.jpg","cap":"Eighty-five percent of degraded heme from senescent red cells; fifteen percent elsewhere","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-4-1","s":"Approximately what fraction of daily heme catabolism in adults originates from senescent circulating red blood cells?","o":["A. 15%","B. 25%","C. 50%","D. 85%","E. 100%"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"85%","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> About <b>eighty-five percent</b> of the heme broken down each day comes from <b>old (senescent) red blood cells</b>. The other fifteen percent comes from immature red cells and from cytochromes in other tissues.<br>A. Fifteen percent: the share from immature red cells and cytochromes.<br>B and C. Twenty-five and fifty percent: not the taught value.<br>E. One hundred percent: ignores the fifteen percent from other sources.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 27 (Catabolism  of  Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":4,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F0257-32-1","s":"What percentage of heme destined for degradation comes from senescent red blood cells?","o":["A. Approximately 50% from senescent RBCs and 50% from other sources","B. Approximately 95% from senescent RBCs and 5% from other sources","C. Approximately 70% from senescent RBCs and 30% from other sources","D. Approximately 85% from senescent RBCs and 15% from other sources"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Approximately 85% from senescent RBCs and 15% from other sources","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> About <b>eighty-five percent</b> comes from <b>senescent red blood cells</b>. About <b>fifteen percent</b> comes from immature red cells and from cytochromes in other tissues.<br>A. Fifty and fifty: underestimates old red cells.<br>B. Ninety-five and five: overestimates them.<br>C. Seventy and thirty: not the taught split.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 27 (Catabolism  of  Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":32,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":32,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Heme degraded in reticuloendothelial cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-27.jpg","cap":"Heme catabolism occurs in reticuloendothelial cells, mainly liver and spleen","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G026-29.jpg","cap":"Macrophage diagram: heme oxygenase makes biliverdin; biliverdin reductase makes bilirubin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-18-1","s":"A patient is found to have mild anemia. Laboratory studies indicate premature destruction of red blood cells, and the heme breakdown products are being converted into biliverdin and then bilirubin. Catabolism of heme primarily takes place in which of the following cell types?","o":["A. Erythroid progenitor cells","B. Reticuloendothelial cells","C. Liver parenchymal cells","D. Renal tubular epithelial cells","E. Splenic fibroblasts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Reticuloendothelial cells","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Heme is broken down in <b>reticuloendothelial cells</b> (macrophages), mainly in the <b>spleen and liver</b>. Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin; biliverdin reductase makes bilirubin.<br>A. Erythroid progenitor cells make heme; they do not break it down.<br>C. Liver parenchymal cells (hepatocytes) take up and conjugate bilirubin; they do not degrade the heme.<br>D. Renal tubular cells: not the site.<br>E. Splenic fibroblasts: the spleen's macrophages do this, not its fibroblasts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 27 (Catabolism  of  Heme)","L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":18,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":18,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Heme breakdown order: biliverdin, bilirubin, conjugation","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-28.jpg","cap":"Step map: heme, biliverdin, bilirubin, albumin, liver conjugation, gut urobilinogen, stercobilin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G026-29.jpg","cap":"Macrophage: heme oxygenase makes biliverdin, biliverdin reductase makes bilirubin, leaves on albumin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G026-35.jpg","cap":"Overview of heme catabolism from senescent red cells to urine and feces","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-57-1","s":"In what order does heme catabolism occur?","o":["A. Heme -> Biliverdin-> Bilirubin","B. Heme -> Bilirubin -> Biliverdin","C. Biliverdin -> Bilirubin -> Heme","D. Bilirubin -> Biliverdin -> Heme"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme -> Biliverdin-> Bilirubin","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Heme → biliverdin → bilirubin.</b> Heme oxygenase makes biliverdin (green); biliverdin reductase makes bilirubin (yellow). That is why a bruise goes from purple to green to yellow.<br>B. Biliverdin comes before bilirubin, not after.<br>C and D. These run backward; heme is the starting point.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)","L31 Heme slide 28 (Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":57,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 review","n":"old Exam 3 review: heme part only","sl":222,"f":"F0044"}]},{"id":"F0035-19-1","s":"What is the sequence of heme catabolism?","o":["A. Heme → bilirubin → bilverdin → bilirubin digluronide → bile","B. Heme → bilverdin → bilirubin digluronide → bilirubin → bile","C. Heme → bilverdin → bilirubin digluronide → bile → bilirubin","D. Heme → biliverdin → bilirubin → bilirubin digluronide → bile"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme → biliverdin → bilirubin → bilirubin digluronide → bile","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Heme → biliverdin</b> (heme oxygenase) → <b>bilirubin</b> (biliverdin reductase) → <b>bilirubin diglucuronide</b> (conjugated in the liver) → <b>bile</b>.<br>A. Bilirubin comes after biliverdin, not before.<br>B. Biliverdin must become bilirubin before it can be conjugated.<br>C. Also skips bilirubin before conjugation; biliverdin is never conjugated directly.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 28 (Heme)","L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)","L31 Heme slide 32 (Addition of glucuronic acid to bilirubin converts the non-polar bilirubin to mor)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":19,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F0112-11-1","s":"Which of the following sequences is correct?","o":["A. Heme→ bilirubin→ conjugated bilirubin→ urobilinogen→ urobilin","B. Heme→ bilirubin→ stercobilin→ urobilinogen","C. Heme→ unconjugated bilirubin→ urobilinogen→ stercobilin","D. Heme→ urobilinogen→ stercobilin→ urobilinogen"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme→ bilirubin→ conjugated bilirubin→ urobilinogen→ urobilin","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Heme → bilirubin (by way of biliverdin) → <b>conjugated bilirubin</b> in the liver → <b>urobilinogen</b> made by gut bacteria → <b>urobilin</b> in urine. It is the only choice in the right order that includes liver conjugation.<br>B. Stercobilin is made from urobilinogen, so it cannot come before it.<br>C. Skips conjugation: bilirubin must be conjugated and secreted in bile before gut bacteria can make urobilinogen.<br>D. Heme does not turn straight into urobilinogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 28 (Heme)","L31 Heme slide 34 (Bilirubin diglucuronide)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":11,"f":"F0112"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0048-171-1","s":"Fill in the blanks in the Heme catabolism pathway [diagram: RBC → (blank) in macrophage → heme oxygenase (O2, NADPH + H+ → NADP+; releases Fe3+, CO) → (blank) → biliverdin reductase (NADPH + H+ → NADP+) → (blank) → leaves cell as Bil → 'Bilirubin-(blank) Complex' in blood]","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Heme → Biliverdin → Bilirubin; Bilirubin-Albumin complex","a":null,"why":"<b>Heme → biliverdin → bilirubin, then the bilirubin–albumin complex.</b> In the macrophage, <b>heme oxygenase</b> (uses oxygen and NADPH, reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate; releases iron and carbon monoxide) makes biliverdin. <b>Biliverdin reductase</b> (uses NADPH) makes bilirubin. Bilirubin does not dissolve in water, so it rides in blood on <b>albumin</b>.<br>Common mix-up: conjugation with glucuronic acid happens later, in the liver, not in the macrophage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0048-171.jpg","in":"Diagram of a macrophage with three blank boxes (the heme catabolism intermediates) and a blank after 'Bilirubin-' in the 'Bilirubin-___ Complex' label in the blood.","tg":["L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)","L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":171,"f":"F0048"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Bruise colors: red, green, yellow","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-29.jpg","cap":"Biliverdin labeled green, then bilirubin; bruise color changes track heme breakdown","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-12-1","s":"Which sequence correctly describes the main color changes observed as heme is catabolized and excreted?","o":["A. Yellow → green → blue-red","B. Green → blue-red → yellow","C. Blue-red → green → yellow","D. Yellow → blue-red → green","E. Blue-red → yellow → green"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Blue-red → green → yellow","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Heme is blue-red, biliverdin is green, bilirubin is yellow.</b> That is why a bruise turns purple, then green, then yellow as its heme is broken down.<br>A, B, D and E put the colors in the wrong order. Green (biliverdin) must come between blue-red (heme) and yellow (bilirubin).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":12,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Heme oxygenase, then biliverdin reductase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-29.jpg","cap":"Heme oxygenase needs oxygen and NADPH, makes biliverdin, iron, carbon monoxide; then biliverdin reductase","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-42-1","s":"Which enzyme in heme catabolism requires oxygen and NADPH and produces carbon monoxide?","o":["A. Ferrochelatase catalyzes this reaction","B. Glucuronyl transferase catalyzes this reaction","C. Biliverdin reductase catalyzes this reaction","D. Heme oxygenase catalyzes this reaction"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme oxygenase catalyzes this reaction","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Heme oxygenase</b> uses <b>oxygen and NADPH</b> (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate) to open the heme ring. It makes biliverdin, iron and <b>carbon monoxide</b>.<br>A. Ferrochelatase: puts iron into protoporphyrin during heme synthesis.<br>B. Glucuronyl transferase: conjugates bilirubin in the liver, using UDP-glucuronic acid (uridine diphosphate glucuronic acid).<br>C. Biliverdin reductase: uses NADPH to make bilirubin from biliverdin; it makes no carbon monoxide and uses no oxygen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":42,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":42,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2438-41-1","s":"Senescent RBCs contribute to the majority of heme degradation, which occurs in reticuloendothelial cells. What enzyme begins this initial step in heme degradation? What enzyme then reduces this product into indirect bilirubin?","o":["A. β-glucuronidase; bilirubin glucuronyl transferase","B. ALA synthase; ferrochelatase","C. Biliverdin reductase; heme oxygenase","D. Bilirubin glucuronyl transferase; β-glucuronidase","E. Heme oxygenase; biliverdin reductase"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme oxygenase; biliverdin reductase","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> <b>Heme oxygenase</b> opens heme to <b>biliverdin</b> (releasing iron and carbon monoxide). <b>Biliverdin reductase</b> then reduces biliverdin to <b>bilirubin</b>, the indirect (unconjugated) form.<br>A. β-glucuronidase works in the gut; glucuronyl transferase works in the liver.<br>B. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase) and ferrochelatase make heme; they do not break it down.<br>C. Right enzymes, wrong order.<br>D. These two act later, in the liver and the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme TA review","sl":41,"f":"F2438"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme/bilirubin part only","sl":185,"f":"F3882"},{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":18,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":182,"f":"F1070"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Albumin carries unconjugated bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-30.jpg","cap":"Unconjugated bilirubin travels on albumin, which has high- and low-affinity binding sites","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G026-37.jpg","cap":"Table: unconjugated bilirubin absent in urine; conjugated can be present","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-10-1","s":"In which compartment of the body does unconjugated bilirubin circulate immediately after being produced from biliverdin?","o":["A. Free in plasma as a water-soluble molecule","B. Bound to albumin in plasma","C. Inside hepatocytes, stored in ferritin","D. Within bile canaliculi as bilirubin diglucuronide","E. In the intestinal lumen as urobilinogen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bound to albumin in plasma","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Unconjugated bilirubin is <b>hydrophobic</b>, so it travels in plasma <b>bound to albumin</b> to the liver.<br>A. It is not water-soluble; only conjugated bilirubin is.<br>C. Ferritin stores iron, not bilirubin.<br>D. Bilirubin diglucuronide in bile canaliculi comes later, after liver conjugation.<br>E. Urobilinogen forms even later, in the gut.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":10,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F0257-36-1","s":"What role does albumin play in bilirubin transport in the blood?","o":["A. It conjugates bilirubin to make it water-soluble","B. It converts bilirubin to urobilinogen in circulation","C. It prevents bilirubin from being filtered by the kidneys","D. It has two binding sites for transporting bilirubin to the liver"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"It has two binding sites for transporting bilirubin to the liver","a":"D","why":"<b>C and D are both true.</b> Albumin carries unconjugated bilirubin to the liver on <b>two binding sites</b> (one high-affinity, one low-affinity). Because the complex is too big to filter, albumin also <b>keeps this bilirubin out of the urine</b>.<br>A. Conjugation happens in liver cells, by UDP-glucuronyl transferase (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase), not by albumin.<br>B. Urobilinogen is made by gut bacteria, not in the blood.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only D, but C is also true: albumin-bound bilirubin is too large to be filtered, which is why unconjugated bilirubin never appears in urine (the source's own explanation says so)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":36,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":36,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Free bilirubin enters the brain: kernicterus","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-30.jpg","cap":"Sulfonamides and salicylates displace bilirubin from albumin; hydrophobic bilirubin enters brain: kernicterus","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G026-43.jpg","cap":"Loosely bound bilirubin crosses the blood-brain barrier, causing kernicterus in newborns","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-30-1","s":"Why can unconjugated bilirubin cause kernicterus in newborns?","o":["A. It blocks glucose transport to the brain","B. It is hydrophobic and can penetrate the blood-brain barrier","C. It is water-soluble and accumulates in cerebrospinal fluid","D. It causes direct oxidative damage to neurons"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"It is hydrophobic and can penetrate the blood-brain barrier","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Unconjugated bilirubin is <b>hydrophobic (lipid-soluble)</b>. When it is not held by albumin, it <b>crosses the blood-brain barrier</b> and deposits in the brain (kernicterus).<br>A. It does not block glucose transport.<br>C. It is not water-soluble; conjugated bilirubin is.<br>D. The key point taught is entry: it reaches neurons only because it is lipid-soluble and crosses the barrier.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)","L31 Heme slide 43 (Pathological Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":30,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":30,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0928-17-1","s":"A 26-year old woman in her third trimester of pregnancy self medicated herself with sulfamethoxazole for urinary tract infection. Sulfamethoxazole is contraindicated in late pregnancy because it binds to albumin and can lead to kernicterus in the fetus. The plasma concentration of which of the following unbound intermediates of heme degradation would be affected by sulfamethoxazole administration?","o":["A. Biliverdin","B. Bilirubin","C. Bilirubin diglucuronide","D. Urobilin","E. Urobilinogen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Bilirubin","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Sulfonamides such as sulfamethoxazole <b>displace unconjugated bilirubin from albumin</b>. Free bilirubin rises and, being hydrophobic, can enter the fetal brain (<b>kernicterus</b>).<br>A. Biliverdin: reduced to bilirubin inside macrophages; it does not circulate on albumin.<br>C. Bilirubin diglucuronide: water-soluble and goes into bile; it does not depend on albumin.<br>D and E. Urobilin and urobilinogen: made from gut urobilinogen; not displaced from albumin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":17,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2019 clicker","n":"old Exam 3 clicker questions: heme part only","sl":24,"f":"F0034"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Inherited jaundice syndromes and their defects","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-31.jpg","cap":"Gilbert syndrome: inherited defective bilirubin uptake, mild unconjugated jaundice","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G026-32.jpg","cap":"Crigler-Najjar I complete vs II partial UDP-glucuronyl transferase deficiency","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G026-33.jpg","cap":"Dubin-Johnson (black liver) vs Rotor (no pigment): bile excretion defects","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-20-1","s":"Match the disorder with the dysfunction. Disorders: Gilbert’s Syndrome; Crigler-Najjar I; Crigler-Najjar II; Dubin-Johnson Syndrome; Rotor’s Syndrome","o":["A. Defective uptake of bilirubin into liver & no conjugation","B. Defective excretion of bilirubin into bile & normal liver color","C. Complete UDP Glucuronyl Transferase deficiency","D. Defective excretion of bilirubin into bile & black pigmented liver","E. Partial UDP Glucuronyl Transferase Deficiency"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gilbert’s → 1; Crigler-Najjar I → 3; Crigler-Najjar II → 5; Dubin-Johnson → 4; Rotor’s → 2","a":null,"why":"<b>Gilbert → A</b> (defective uptake; mild unconjugated jaundice). <b>Crigler-Najjar I → C</b> (complete UDP-glucuronyl transferase deficiency; uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase; poor survival). <b>Crigler-Najjar II → E</b> (partial deficiency; compatible with life). <b>Dubin-Johnson → D</b> (excretion defect, black liver). <b>Rotor → B</b> (excretion defect, normal liver color).<br>Common mix-up: Gilbert and Crigler-Najjar raise <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin; Dubin-Johnson and Rotor raise <b>conjugated</b> bilirubin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 31 (Uptake of Indirect bilirubin by liver parenchymal cells)","L31 Heme slide 32 (Addition of glucuronic acid to bilirubin converts the non-polar bilirubin to mor)","L31 Heme slide 33 (Secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":20,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":20,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1061-170-2","s":"What disorders are the other answer choice options? (Defect in excretion of bilirubin in the bile; UDP glucuronyl transferase deficiency; Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Defect in excretion of bilirubin in the bile → Dubin-Johnson syndrome (with black pigmentation of liver) or Rotors syndrome (no pigmentation); UDP glucuronyl transferase deficiency → Crigler Najjar I (complete) or II (partial); Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency → porphyria cutanea tarda","a":null,"why":"<b>Excretion defect into bile → Dubin-Johnson</b> (black pigmented liver) <b>or Rotor</b> (liver not pigmented); both raise conjugated bilirubin. <b>UDP-glucuronyl transferase</b> (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) <b>deficiency → Crigler-Najjar</b> I (complete, poor survival) or II (partial). <b>Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency → porphyria cutanea tarda</b>.<br>Common mix-up: Gilbert syndrome is defective uptake, not an excretion defect or a full enzyme deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 32 (Addition of glucuronic acid to bilirubin converts the non-polar bilirubin to mor)","L31 Heme slide 33 (Secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile)","L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":170,"f":"F1061"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Gilbert = defective hepatic uptake","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-31.jpg","cap":"Gilbert syndrome box: defective uptake, no conjugation, mild unconjugated jaundice","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G026-32.jpg","cap":"Contrast: Crigler-Najjar is complete or partial conjugating-enzyme deficiency","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-38-1","s":"What is the primary defect in Gilbert syndrome?","o":["A. Excessive hemolysis of red blood cells","B. Obstruction of the bile duct","C. Deficiency of heme oxygenase enzyme","D. Defective uptake of bilirubin by hepatocytes"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Defective uptake of bilirubin by hepatocytes","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The lecture defines <b>Gilbert syndrome</b> as an inherited <b>defect in uptake of bilirubin by hepatocytes</b>, giving mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia. (Its conjugating enzyme activity is also mildly low.)<br>A. Excessive hemolysis: hemolytic (pre-hepatic) jaundice.<br>B. Bile duct obstruction: post-hepatic jaundice with conjugated bilirubin.<br>C. Heme oxygenase deficiency: would lower bilirubin production, not cause jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 31 (Uptake of Indirect bilirubin by liver parenchymal cells)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":38,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":38,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-20-1","s":"Indirect bilirubin is then bound to albumin in order to be released into the blood, where it’ll then be uptaken by hepatocytes. What disorder is characterized by defective uptake of the bilirubin and causes mild jaundice?","o":["A. Crigler-Najjar syndrome I","B. Crigler-Najjar syndrome II","C. Gilbert's syndrome","D. Dubin-Johnson syndrome","E. Rotors syndrome"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Gilbert's syndrome","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Gilbert syndrome</b>: <b>defective hepatic uptake</b> of unconjugated bilirubin, giving mild jaundice.<br>A. Crigler-Najjar I: <b>complete</b> UDP-glucuronyl transferase (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) deficiency; poor survival.<br>B. Crigler-Najjar II: <b>partial</b> deficiency of the same enzyme.<br>D. Dubin-Johnson: defect in excretion into bile; black liver.<br>E. Rotor: defect in excretion into bile; liver not pigmented.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 31 (Uptake of Indirect bilirubin by liver parenchymal cells)","L31 Heme slide 32 (Addition of glucuronic acid to bilirubin converts the non-polar bilirubin to mor)","L31 Heme slide 33 (Secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":20,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":184,"f":"F1070"}]},{"id":"F1061-169-1","s":"What causes Gilbert's syndrome?","o":["A. Defect in excretion of bilirubin in the bile","B. UDP glucuronyl transferase deficiency","C. Defective uptake of bilirubin → no conjugation of bilirubin","D. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Defective uptake of bilirubin → no conjugation of bilirubin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The lecture defines <b>Gilbert syndrome</b> as <b>defective uptake</b> of bilirubin into hepatocytes, so less gets conjugated. Result: mild, inherited unconjugated jaundice.<br>A. Defect in excretion into bile: Dubin-Johnson or Rotor syndrome.<br>B. UDP-glucuronyl transferase (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) deficiency: Crigler-Najjar I (complete) or II (partial). Gilbert has only a mild drop in this enzyme.<br>D. Uroporphyrinogen decarboxylase deficiency: porphyria cutanea tarda.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 31 (Uptake of Indirect bilirubin by liver parenchymal cells)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":169,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":13,"f":"F2433"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Liver conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-32.jpg","cap":"Microsomal bilirubin glucuronyl transferase adds glucuronic acid from UDP-glucuronic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G026-31.jpg","cap":"Liver diagram: bilirubin–albumin in, bilirubin diglucuronide out to bile","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-10-1","s":"During heme catabolism, which organ system is primarily responsible for converting indirect bilirubin to conjugated bilirubin?","o":["A. Reticuloendothelial system in spleen","B. Kidney tubules filtering unconjugated bilirubin","C. Hepatocytes in the liver using UDP-glucuronic acid","D. Intestinal bacteria producing urobilinogen"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatocytes in the liver using UDP-glucuronic acid","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Hepatocytes</b> conjugate bilirubin. Microsomal <b>bilirubin glucuronyl transferase</b> uses <b>UDP-glucuronic acid</b> (uridine diphosphate glucuronic acid) as the donor, making water-soluble bilirubin diglucuronide.<br>A. Reticuloendothelial cells in the spleen make unconjugated bilirubin from heme.<br>B. The kidney does not conjugate bilirubin, and it cannot filter albumin-bound bilirubin.<br>D. Gut bacteria turn bilirubin into urobilinogen, a later step.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 32 (Addition of glucuronic acid to bilirubin converts the non-polar bilirubin to mor)","L31 Heme slide 28 (Heme)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":10,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":10,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-98-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the transport  of bilirubin?","o":["A. Bilirubin is conjugated to glucouronate for excretion in bile","B. Bacterial oxidation in the intestine results in urobilinogen formation","C. Bilirubin is complexed to stercobilin for transport to the liver","D. Unconjugated bilirubin is soluble and is and is classified as indirect bilirubin","E. Bilirubin is always excreted in the urine in the unconjugated form"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Bilirubin is conjugated to glucouronate for excretion in bile","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> In the liver, bilirubin is <b>conjugated to glucuronate</b> (glucuronic acid) so it becomes water-soluble and can be <b>excreted in bile</b>.<br>B. Gut bacteria <b>reduce</b> bilirubin to urobilinogen; oxidation comes later, turning urobilinogen into stercobilin.<br>C. Bilirubin travels to the liver on <b>albumin</b>, not stercobilin.<br>D. Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is <b>insoluble</b> in water.<br>E. Unconjugated bilirubin <b>never</b> appears in urine; only conjugated bilirubin can.","ex":"","fl":["Key A is correct; explanation-only error: the notes for (d) say unconjugated bilirubin 'is classified as direct bilirubin' — unconjugated = INDIRECT (G026:37-38); (d) is false because unconjugated bilirubin is insoluble in water. Also (b) is false because bacteria REDUCE bilirubin to urobilinogen (G026:34)."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 32 (Addition of glucuronic acid to bilirubin converts the non-polar bilirubin to mor)","L31 Heme slide 34 (Bilirubin diglucuronide)","L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":98,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":181,"f":"F1047"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Crigler-Najjar I complete, II partial","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-32.jpg","cap":"Table: Crigler-Najjar I complete deficiency, poor survival; II partial, compatible","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-22-1","s":"What distinguishes Crigler-Najjar syndrome type I from type ll?","o":["A. Type I has partial enzyme deficiency with better prognosis","B. Type I has complete enzyme deficiency with poor survival","C. Type I presents with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","D. Type I affects bilirubin excretion rather than conjugation"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Type I has complete enzyme deficiency with poor survival","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Crigler-Najjar I</b> is a <b>complete</b> UDP-glucuronyl transferase (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) deficiency, with <b>poor survival</b>. Type II is a <b>partial</b> deficiency, compatible with life.<br>A. That describes type II.<br>C. Both types raise <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin, since conjugation fails.<br>D. Both are conjugation defects; excretion defects are Dubin-Johnson and Rotor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 32 (Addition of glucuronic acid to bilirubin converts the non-polar bilirubin to mor)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":22,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":22,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Bile secretion of bilirubin is active","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-33.jpg","cap":"Secretion into bile by active transport; rate-limiting for hepatic bilirubin handling","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-22-1","s":"How does conjugated bilirubin become excreted into bile?","o":["A. Facilitated diffusion","B. Passive diffusion","C. Active transport","D. Electrochemical gradient"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Active transport","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Conjugated bilirubin enters bile by <b>active transport</b>. This is the <b>rate-limiting</b> step of liver bilirubin handling, and the drugs that induce conjugation also induce it.<br>A. Facilitated diffusion: how unconjugated bilirubin <b>enters</b> the hepatocyte from the sinusoid.<br>B. Passive diffusion: conjugated bilirubin is charged and needs a pump.<br>D. It is pumped against its gradient, not carried down an electrochemical gradient.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 33 (Secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":22,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":22,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Dubin-Johnson: black pigmented liver","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-33.jpg","cap":"Dubin-Johnson: excretion defect, black liver; Rotor: excretion defect, no pigment","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-44-1","s":"Which inherited syndrome is characterized by defective excretion of bilirubin and a black pigmented liver?","o":["A. Dubin-Johnson syndrome with pigmented liver","B. Rotor syndrome with melanin deposition","C. Gilbert syndrome with hepatic pigmentation","D. Crigler-Najjar syndrome with liver damage"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome with pigmented liver","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Dubin-Johnson syndrome</b>: a defect in <b>excretion</b> of conjugated bilirubin into bile, with a <b>black pigmented liver</b>.<br>B. Rotor syndrome: the same excretion defect, but the liver is <b>not</b> pigmented.<br>C. Gilbert syndrome: defective uptake; mild unconjugated jaundice; no pigment.<br>D. Crigler-Najjar syndrome: conjugating-enzyme deficiency; unconjugated bilirubin; no black liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 33 (Secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":44,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":44,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Gut bacteria: urobilinogen → stercobilin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-34.jpg","cap":"β-glucuronidases free bilirubin; bacteria make urobilinogen, oxidized to stercobilin (brown stool)","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/G026-35.jpg","cap":"Overview: urobilinogen to urine urobilin or fecal stercobilin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-8-1","s":"A researcher blocks the action of intestinal bacteria in a lab animal, but all other hepatic functions remain intact. Which pigment in the feces will decrease most as a direct consequence?","o":["A. Bilirubin diglucuronide","B. Biliverdin","C. Urobilin","D. Stercobilin","E. Hemoglobin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Stercobilin","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Gut <b>bacteria</b> remove glucuronic acid (β-glucuronidases) and reduce bilirubin to <b>urobilinogen</b>. Urobilinogen is oxidized to <b>stercobilin</b>, the brown stool pigment. No bacteria, little stercobilin.<br>A. Bilirubin diglucuronide: would rise, since bacteria no longer break it down.<br>B. Biliverdin: made in macrophages, not in the gut.<br>C. Urobilin: the urine pigment, made from reabsorbed urobilinogen.<br>E. Hemoglobin: not a normal stool pigment.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 34 (Bilirubin diglucuronide)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":8,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F0382-6-1","s":"Selena is a 19-year-old female who consulted for episodes of headache, weakness, and easy fatigability. As the attending physician, you examined her and noticed that she looked pale. You ordered a serum hemoglobin level which turned out to be low. In your mind, you know that the prosthetic group of hemoglobin is heme. Which of the following is true regarding heme metabolism?","o":["A. ALA Dehydratase requires B6 for its activity.","B. PCT is heme synthesis defect leading to accumulation of ALA and PBG.","C. Massive hemolysis may lead to an increase conjugated bilirubin.","D. Bilirubin Glucuronidase is essential for urobilinogen synthesis in the intestine.","E. Lead decreases the synthesis of heme by binding with aminolevulinic acid (ALA) synthetase."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Bilirubin Glucuronidase is essential for urobilinogen synthesis in the intestine.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Bacterial <b>β-glucuronidase</b> (the option calls it bilirubin glucuronidase) strips glucuronic acid off bilirubin diglucuronide. Bacteria then reduce the freed bilirubin to <b>urobilinogen</b>.<br>A. Vitamin B6 is needed by ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase), not ALA dehydratase.<br>B. Aminolevulinic acid and porphobilinogen build up in acute intermittent porphyria; porphyria cutanea tarda builds up uroporphyrins.<br>C. Massive hemolysis raises <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin.<br>E. Lead binds ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, not ALA synthase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 34 (Bilirubin diglucuronide)","L31 Heme slide 12 (Step 8:  Formation of heme)","L31 Heme slide 21 (Chronic Porphyria)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh recap 2 heme & iron","sl":6,"f":"F0382"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":18,"f":"F2421"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble, enters urine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-37.jpg","cap":"Conjugated water-soluble and in urine; unconjugated absent from urine; normal ranges","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-12-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents with yellowing of the eyes and dark urine. Laboratory results show a total serum bilirubin of 3.0 mg/dL, with a conjugated bilirubin of 2.2 mg/dL. Which of the following statements best explains this patient’s condition?","o":["A. The bilirubin present is water-insoluble and cannot appear in the urine.","B. The elevated bilirubin is due to overproduction of unconjugated bilirubin that the liver cannot handle.","C. The bilirubin present is water-soluble and excreted in the urine, indicating conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","D. The bilirubin level is within the normal range and does not indicate jaundice."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The bilirubin present is water-soluble and excreted in the urine, indicating conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Most of the bilirubin is <b>conjugated</b> (two point two of three milligrams per deciliter). Conjugated bilirubin is <b>water-soluble</b>, so it passes into urine and darkens it. This is conjugated hyperbilirubinemia.<br>A. That describes unconjugated bilirubin, which never reaches urine.<br>B. Overproduction (hemolysis) raises mainly unconjugated bilirubin.<br>D. Normal total bilirubin is under one milligram per deciliter; above two, jaundice shows.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 37 (Hyperbilirubinemia)","L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":12,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":12,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Three causes of hyperbilirubinemia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-37.jpg","cap":"Causes: overproduction, damaged liver, duct obstruction; hyperbilirubinemia above one, jaundice above two","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F1061-172-1","s":"Which of the following does NOT cause hyperbilirubinemia?","o":["A. Production of more bilirubin than the normal liver can handle","B. Failure of a damaged liver to conjugate and excrete bilirubin in normal amounts","C. Obstruction to excretory ducts of the liver","D. All the above are correct"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"All the above are correct","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> All three listed <b>do</b> cause hyperbilirubinemia, so none is the exception: <b>too much bilirubin made</b> (hemolysis), a <b>damaged liver</b> that cannot conjugate and excrete it, and <b>blocked bile ducts</b>.<br>A. Overproduction: pre-hepatic (hemolytic) jaundice.<br>B. Damaged liver: hepatocellular jaundice.<br>C. Duct obstruction: post-hepatic (obstructive) jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 37 (Hyperbilirubinemia)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":172,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":16,"f":"F2433"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Hyperbilirubinemia >1, jaundice >2 mg/dL","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-37.jpg","cap":"Total bilirubin under one mg/dL normal; above one hyperbilirubinemia; above two jaundice","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F1061-173-2","s":"What amount of bilirubin in the blood is considered hyperbilirubinemia?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":">1 mg/dl; when serum bilirubin >2 mg/dl → jaundice","a":null,"why":"<b>Above one milligram per deciliter</b> of total bilirubin is hyperbilirubinemia. <b>Above two milligrams per deciliter</b>, bilirubin deposits in skin and sclera and <b>jaundice</b> shows.<br>Common mix-up: the normal total is under one milligram per deciliter. Normal conjugated bilirubin is up to two-tenths, and unconjugated two- to six-tenths, of a milligram per deciliter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 37 (Hyperbilirubinemia)"],"src":[{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":173,"f":"F1061"},{"t":"? Montana TA review","n":"Lecture 29 (heme) TA review","sl":17,"f":"F2433"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Hemolysis speeds heme → bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-39.jpg","cap":"Hemolytic jaundice: bilirubin produced faster than conjugated; unconjugated rises","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 39"},{"src":"sl/G026-28.jpg","cap":"Step map: heme to biliverdin to bilirubin in the reticuloendothelial system","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-7-1","s":"A 40-year-old man presents with severe anemia and scleral icterus after consuming large amounts of oxidant drugs. Laboratory testing shows markedly increased unconjugated bilirubin. Which step of heme catabolism is primarily increased in this setting?","o":["A. Hepatic conjugation of bilirubin with glucuronic acid","B. Intestinal conversion of conjugated bilirubin to stercobilin","C. RES conversion of heme to biliverdin and bilirubin","D. Renal conversion of urobilinogen to urobilin","E. Hepatic secretion of conjugated bilirubin into bile"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"RES conversion of heme to biliverdin and bilirubin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Oxidant drugs cause <b>hemolysis</b>. More red cells are destroyed in the <b>reticuloendothelial system</b> (RES), so more heme is turned into biliverdin and <b>unconjugated bilirubin</b>. That rise is the cause of the jaundice.<br>A. Hepatic conjugation: normal capacity; it is swamped, not increased as the primary change.<br>B. Gut stercobilin formation rises only as a downstream result.<br>D. Urobilin forms when urobilinogen is oxidized; not the primary change.<br>E. Hepatic secretion: not the step that drives this jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 29 (Reticuloendothelial system)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":7,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Pre-hepatic jaundice: no urine bilirubin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-39.jpg","cap":"Hemolytic jaundice: high unconjugated bilirubin, high urine urobilinogen, no urine bilirubin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 39"},{"src":"sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Jaundice table: pre-hepatic, hepatic, post-hepatic bilirubin, urine, stool and enzyme findings","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-26-1","s":"A patient with G6PD deficiency presents to your office with significant yellowing of his skin. Which of the following blood results would indicate massive hemolysis resulting in prehepatic jaundice?","o":["A. Elevated serum ALT & AST levels","B. Elevated conjugated bilirubin levels","C. Decreased albumin levels","D. Bilirubin absent in the urine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Bilirubin absent in the urine","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> In hemolytic (pre-hepatic) jaundice, the extra bilirubin is <b>unconjugated</b> and albumin-bound, so <b>bilirubin is absent from urine</b>.<br>A. High ALT and AST (alanine and aspartate transaminases): liver-cell damage (hepatic jaundice).<br>B. High conjugated bilirubin: hepatic or post-hepatic jaundice.<br>C. Low albumin: chronic liver disease, not hemolysis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":26,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":26,"f":"F1048"}]},{"id":"F0065-9-1","s":"Fifteen-month-old Myko with G6PD deficiency ingests naphthalene balls and develops pallor and jaundice. Urine analysis shows increased urobilinogen and no detectable bilirubin. Which type of hyperbilirubinemia best describes his condition?","o":["A. Pure conjugated hyperbilirubinemia","B. Mixed conjugated and unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","C. Pre-hepatic unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","D. Hepatic conjugation defect","E. Post-hepatic obstructive hyperbilirubinemia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pre-hepatic unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In G6PD (glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase) deficiency, naphthalene causes <b>hemolysis</b>. Much unconjugated bilirubin is made; it is albumin-bound, so <b>no bilirubin in urine</b>. More bilirubin reaches the gut, so <b>urine urobilinogen rises</b>. This is pre-hepatic jaundice.<br>A. Pure conjugated: bilirubin would appear in urine.<br>B. Mixed: hepatocellular jaundice, with bilirubin in urine.<br>D. Conjugation defect (Crigler-Najjar): no hemolysis trigger.<br>E. Post-hepatic: bilirubin in urine, urobilinogen absent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":9,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F0179-11-1","s":"Fifteen-month-old Myko, known to have glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency, was brought to your clinic for severe progressive pallor and jaundice. Prior to the onset of jaundice, Myko was seen playing with several naphthalene balls, with some seen inside his mouth. Suspecting that his jaundice is due to massive hemolysis, which of the following will be observed on urine analysis?","o":["A. Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen 0","B. Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen +2","C. Urine bilirubin +2; urine urobilinogen 0","D. Urine bilirubin +2; urine urobilinogen +2","E. Urine bilirubin +4; urine urobilinogen +4"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Urine bilirubin 0; urine urobilinogen +2","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Hemolysis raises <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin, which is albumin-bound and water-insoluble, so <b>urine bilirubin is zero</b>. The liver conjugates more, more reaches the gut, and more urobilinogen is reabsorbed, so <b>urine urobilinogen rises</b>.<br>A. Both zero: no extra urobilinogen; does not fit hemolysis.<br>C. Bilirubin up, urobilinogen zero: complete bile duct obstruction.<br>D and E. Both up: the hepatocellular jaundice pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":11,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":3,"f":"F0293"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":12,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":12,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":16,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":19,"f":"F2421"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0257-14-1","s":"What distinguishes hemolytic jaundice from hepatocellular jaundice in terms of bilirubin metabolism?","o":["A. Hepatocellular jaundice shows absent urobilinogen in urine","B. Hemolytic jaundice shows elevated conjugated bilirubin with normal liver function","C. Hepatocellular jaundice shows normal ALT and AST enzyme levels","D. Hemolytic jaundice shows elevated unconjugated bilirubin with normal liver conjugation capacity"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemolytic jaundice shows elevated unconjugated bilirubin with normal liver conjugation capacity","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> In <b>hemolytic</b> jaundice, red cells break down faster than a <b>normal liver</b> can conjugate, so <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin rises; liver enzymes stay normal.<br>A. Hepatocellular jaundice still has urobilinogen in urine; it is absent only with complete obstruction.<br>B. Hemolytic jaundice raises unconjugated, not conjugated, bilirubin.<br>C. Hepatocellular jaundice has <b>high</b> ALT and AST (alanine and aspartate transaminases).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 40 (Damage to the liver cells leading to decreased conjugation of bilirubin in the l)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":14,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":14,"f":"F0938"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Liver capacity: 3000 mg bilirubin a day","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-39.jpg","cap":"Liver capacity three thousand mg/day versus normal production three hundred mg/day","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 39"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-28-1","s":"What is the normal daily capacity of the liver to conjugate and excrete bilirubin?","o":["A. 5000 mg/day","B. 1000 mg/day","C. 300 mg/day","D. 3000 mg/day"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"3000 mg/day","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> A normal liver can conjugate and excrete about <b>three thousand milligrams of bilirubin a day</b>. Normal production is about three hundred milligrams a day, so hemolytic jaundice needs massive red-cell breakdown.<br>A. Five thousand: too high.<br>B. One thousand: too low.<br>C. Three hundred: that is daily production, not liver capacity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 39 (Hemolytic /Pre Hepatic Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":28,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":28,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Hepatocellular jaundice: both bilirubins, high ALT/AST","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-40.jpg","cap":"Hepatocellular jaundice: unconjugated and conjugated rise, urine urobilinogen present, transaminases high","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Jaundice table: hepatic column shows mixed bilirubin and raised ALT/AST","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-17-1","s":"A patient with hepatitis has yellowing of the eyes and dark urine. Lab tests show high ALT and AST levels and increased bilirubin in the blood. Which type of bilirubin mainly increases because of liver cell damage?","o":["A. Conjugated bilirubin","B. Unconjugated bilirubin","C. Both conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin","D. No change in bilirubin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Both conjugated and unconjugated bilirubin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In hepatitis, damaged liver cells <b>conjugate less</b>, so <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin rises. What they do conjugate is poorly secreted (intrahepatic obstruction), so <b>conjugated</b> bilirubin rises too and the urine turns dark.<br>A. Conjugated only: post-hepatic obstruction.<br>B. Unconjugated only: hemolysis or a conjugation defect.<br>D. Bilirubin is clearly raised.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 40 (Damage to the liver cells leading to decreased conjugation of bilirubin in the l)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":17,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":17,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-24-1","s":"A patient presents to the ED after being found unconscious by EMS. Prior charts of the patient reveal that he is a chronic alcoholic. Physical Exam reveals yellowing of the skin, and labs reveal elevated ALT and AST. Which diagnosis is consistent with the above findings?","o":["A. Prehepatic jaundice","B. (Intra)Hepatic jaundice","C. Posthepatic jaundice","D. Dubin-Johnson syndrome","E. Gilbert’s syndrome"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"(Intra)Hepatic jaundice","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> A chronic alcoholic with jaundice and <b>high ALT and AST</b> (alanine and aspartate transaminases) has <b>hepatic (hepatocellular) jaundice</b> from liver-cell damage such as cirrhosis.<br>A. Pre-hepatic: hemolysis, with normal liver enzymes.<br>C. Post-hepatic: obstruction, with high ALP (alkaline phosphatase).<br>D. Dubin-Johnson: inherited excretion defect; enzymes usually normal.<br>E. Gilbert: mild inherited uptake defect; enzymes normal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 40 (Damage to the liver cells leading to decreased conjugation of bilirubin in the l)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":24,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":188,"f":"F1070"}]},{"id":"F0208-92-1","s":"A 40 year old intravenous drug using male patient with a history of IV drug use presents to an emergency room with jaundice.  Liver function tests are conducted and reveal an elevated serum ALT/AST ratio as well as elevated serum bilirubin.  Given the patient’s presentation and lab values, which of the following answer choices best describes the patient’s defect.","o":["A. Increased bilirubin production","B. Defective bilirubin metabolism","C. Obstruction of the bile duct","D. Deficiency in bilirubin excretion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Defective bilirubin metabolism","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> IV (intravenous) drug use spreads viral hepatitis. Jaundice with <b>high ALT and AST</b> (alanine and aspartate transaminases) means damaged liver cells that cannot process bilirubin: <b>defective bilirubin metabolism</b> (hepatic jaundice).<br>A. Increased production: hemolysis, with normal enzymes.<br>C. Bile duct obstruction: high ALP (alkaline phosphatase) and clay stools.<br>D. An excretion defect alone: Dubin-Johnson or Rotor, with normal enzymes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 40 (Damage to the liver cells leading to decreased conjugation of bilirubin in the l)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":92,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":175,"f":"F1047"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Obstructive jaundice: direct bilirubin, clay stools","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-41.jpg","cap":"Obstruction: conjugated bilirubin up, bilirubin in urine, no urobilinogen, clay stools","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Post-hepatic column: conjugated bilirubin, absent urobilinogen and stercobilin, high alkaline phosphatase","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-28-1","s":"A patient presents with a complaint of clay colored stools ongoing for 3 days. The attending tells you that he most likely has obstructive jaundice. The attending asks you, what would you expect his laboratory results to look like?","o":["A. Elevated albumin","B. Elevated unconjugated bilirubin","C. Elevated conjugated bilirubin","D. Elevated indirect bilirubin","E. Low albumin","F. Low unconjugated bilirubin","G. Low conjugated bilirubin","H. Low indirect bilirubin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Elevated conjugated bilirubin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> In obstructive (post-hepatic) jaundice the liver conjugates normally, but bile cannot drain, so <b>conjugated (direct) bilirubin rises</b>. Stools are clay-colored and bilirubin appears in urine.<br>A and E. Albumin: not the key change.<br>B and D. Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin is the hemolysis marker; it rises only late in long obstruction.<br>F, G and H. Bilirubin is raised, not low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":28,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":28,"f":"F1048"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":7,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":113,"f":"F2304"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme & biotin TA review","sl":7,"f":"F2441"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0257-20-1","s":"Which laboratory finding is characteristic of complete bile duct obstruction?","o":["A. Absence of urobilinogen in urine and pale clay-colored stools","B. Elevated unconjugated bilirubin and normal stool color","C. Normal bilirubin levels with elevated liver enzymes","D. Elevated urobilinogen in urine and dark stools"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Absence of urobilinogen in urine and pale clay-colored stools","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> With <b>complete</b> obstruction, no bilirubin reaches the gut. No urobilinogen forms, so <b>none is in urine</b>; no stercobilin forms, so stools are <b>pale and clay-colored</b>.<br>B. High unconjugated bilirubin with normal stool: hemolysis.<br>C. Bilirubin is high, not normal, in obstruction.<br>D. High urine urobilinogen and dark stools: hemolysis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":20,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":20,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0208-101-1","s":"Which of the following lab values would be expected in a patient with a bile duct obstruction?","o":["A. Decreased serum ALP","B. Normal serum direct bilirubin","C. Absent urine direct bilirubin","D. Absent urine urobilnogen & stercobilin","E. Elevated serum ALT/AST"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Absent urine urobilnogen & stercobilin","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> A blocked bile duct stops bilirubin reaching the gut, so there is <b>no urobilinogen in urine and no stercobilin in stool</b> (stercobilin is a stool pigment, never a urine one).<br>A. ALP (alkaline phosphatase) <b>rises</b> in obstruction.<br>B. Direct (conjugated) bilirubin <b>rises</b>.<br>C. Direct bilirubin <b>appears</b> in urine.<br>E. High ALT and AST (alanine and aspartate transaminases) mark hepatocellular damage; ALP is the obstruction enzyme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":101,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":184,"f":"F1047"}]},{"id":"F0065-5-1","s":"A 70-year-old man with obstructing pancreatic carcinoma presents with progressive jaundice and pruritus. Laboratory studies show markedly elevated conjugated (direct) bilirubin. Which additional finding is most consistent with his condition?","o":["A. Pale (clay-colored) stools and dark urine positive for bilirubin","B. Normal stool color and absence of urine bilirubin","C. Markedly increased unconjugated bilirubin bound to albumin","D. Decreased formation of urobilinogen in the intestine","E. Increased conversion of hemoglobin to unconjugated bilirubin in the RES"],"g":["A","D"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Pale (clay-colored) stools and dark urine positive for bilirubin","a":"A","why":"<b>A and D are both true.</b> A pancreatic tumor <b>blocks the bile duct</b>. Conjugated bilirubin backs up into blood and spills into urine (<b>dark urine</b>). Little bilirubin reaches the gut, so <b>less urobilinogen</b> forms and stools are <b>pale (clay-colored)</b>.<br>B. Normal stool and no urine bilirubin: pre-hepatic jaundice.<br>C. Markedly high unconjugated bilirubin: hemolysis.<br>E. More heme breakdown in the RES (reticuloendothelial system): hemolysis, not obstruction.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only A, but D is also true: with the duct blocked, little bilirubin reaches the gut, so bacteria make less urobilinogen (the source's own explanation says so)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":5,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F0928-15-1","s":"A 40-year old overweight female presents at the clinic with yellow sclerae, intolerance to fatty foods, pain in the upper right quadrant of the abdomen and passage of clay colored stools. Serum total bilirubin was 20 mg/dL and direct bilirubin was 16 mg/dL. LFT showed elevated ALP. Her urine was dark in color. Which of the following diagnoses is consistent with the above findings? Normal values: Total = < 1.0 mg/dL; Direct = 0.1 - 0.2 mg/dL; Indirect = 0.2 - 0.6 mg/dL","o":["A. Crigler-Najjar syndrome, Type 2","B. Posthepatic jaundice","C. Intrahepatic jaundice","D. Gilbert’s syndrome","E. Prehepatic jaundice"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Posthepatic jaundice","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Fatty-food intolerance and right upper quadrant pain (gallstones), clay stools, dark urine, <b>mostly direct bilirubin</b> (sixteen of twenty) and <b>high ALP</b> (alkaline phosphatase) mean <b>post-hepatic (obstructive)</b> jaundice.<br>A. Crigler-Najjar type 2: unconjugated bilirubin; normal stools.<br>C. Intrahepatic: high ALT and AST (alanine and aspartate transaminases); stool stercobilin still present.<br>D. Gilbert: mild unconjugated jaundice.<br>E. Pre-hepatic: unconjugated bilirubin, no urine bilirubin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 41 (Obstructive jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":15,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2019 clicker","n":"old Exam 3 clicker questions: heme part only","sl":23,"f":"F0034"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Newborn jaundice: low UDP-glucuronyl transferase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-42.jpg","cap":"Physiological jaundice day two to three; graph of low conjugating-enzyme activity in newborns","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 42"},{"src":"sl/G026-43.jpg","cap":"Pathological jaundice: Rh/ABO incompatibility, unconjugated above twenty-five, kernicterus risk","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-6-1","s":"A newborn develops jaundice on day 3 of life. Laboratory tests show elevated unconjugated bilirubin with normal liver enzymes and no evidence of hemolysis. Which mechanism best explains the jaundice?","o":["A. Excessive heme breakdown in macrophages","B. Immaturity of hepatic conjugating enzyme activity","C. Autoimmune destruction of bile canaliculi","D. Complete obstruction of the common bile duct","E. Inhibition of albumin synthesis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Immaturity of hepatic conjugating enzyme activity","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Jaundice on day two to three, with high unconjugated bilirubin and no hemolysis, is <b>physiological jaundice</b>: the newborn liver's <b>UDP-glucuronyl transferase</b> (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) activity is still low.<br>A. Excess heme breakdown: hemolysis, which the stem rules out.<br>C. Destroyed bile canaliculi: conjugated (cholestatic) jaundice.<br>D. Duct obstruction: conjugated bilirubin, pale stools.<br>E. Albumin synthesis: not the cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 42 (Physiological Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":6,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F0133-14-1","s":"A 2-day-old premature newborn develops yellow discoloration of the skin and sclera. Laboratory tests reveal elevated total bilirubin, predominantly in the unconjugated form. The infant is otherwise healthy, feeding well, and has no signs of infection or hemolytic disease. Which of the following best explains this newborn’s condition?","o":["A. Biliary atresia leading to obstruction of bile flow","B. Inherited deficiency of conjugated bilirubin transporters","C. Excessive conjugated bilirubin due to increased hepatic metabolism","D. Immature activity of UDP-glucuronyl transferase in the liver"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Immature activity of UDP-glucuronyl transferase in the liver","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> A healthy premature baby with day-two jaundice and mostly <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin has <b>physiological jaundice</b>. <b>UDP-glucuronyl transferase</b> (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) activity is low in newborns, especially premature ones. It is transient.<br>A. Biliary atresia: obstruction, with conjugated bilirubin and pale stools.<br>B. Transporter defects (Dubin-Johnson, Rotor): conjugated bilirubin.<br>C. The rise here is unconjugated, not conjugated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 42 (Physiological Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":14,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":14,"f":"F0890"}]},{"id":"F0144-30-1","s":"A mother presents to the emergency department complaining of her newborn having yellow skin and extremely poor feeding. She notes that it only began to appear after they were released from the hospital and returned home. What is causing the newborn’s symptoms?","o":["A. Decreased activity of ALA synthase","B. Accumulation of conjugated albumin","C. Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronyl transferase","D. Accumulation of conjugated bilirubin","E. Decreased activity of biliverdin reductase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased activity of UDP-glucuronyl transferase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A newborn turning yellow a few days after birth has <b>physiological jaundice</b>, from <b>low UDP-glucuronyl transferase</b> (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) activity in the immature liver. Unconjugated bilirubin builds up.<br>A. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase): heme synthesis; not a cause of jaundice.<br>B. Albumin is not conjugated.<br>D. The bilirubin that builds up is unconjugated.<br>E. Biliverdin reductase works normally; low activity would lower bilirubin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 42 (Physiological Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":30,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":30,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0359-9-1","s":"A mother presents to the emergency department complaining of her newborn having yellow skin and extremely poor feeding. She notes having a massive hemorrhage and had been given the wrong blood type during her pregnancy. What is causing the newborn’s symptoms?","o":["A. Low activity of ALA synthase","B. Accumulation of conjugated albumin","C. Low activity of UDP-glucuronyl transferase","D. Accumulation of conjugated bilirubin","E. Low activity of biliverdin reductase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Low activity of UDP-glucuronyl transferase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Blood-group (Rh or ABO) mismatch causes <b>hemolysis</b> in the baby. The newborn liver's <b>UDP-glucuronyl transferase</b> (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase) activity is low and cannot keep up, so <b>unconjugated</b> bilirubin rises (pathological jaundice).<br>A. ALA synthase (aminolevulinic acid synthase): heme synthesis; unrelated.<br>B. Albumin is not conjugated.<br>D. The bilirubin that rises is unconjugated.<br>E. Biliverdin reductase works; low activity would lower bilirubin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 42 (Physiological Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 43 (Pathological Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":9,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":115,"f":"F2304"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 29 heme & biotin TA review","sl":9,"f":"F2441"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Blue light makes bilirubin water-soluble","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-44.jpg","cap":"Blue fluorescent phototherapy turns bilirubin into water-soluble isomers excreted without conjugation","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/G026-42.jpg","cap":"Newborn conjugating enzyme (UDP-glucuronyl transferase) activity is low, especially in premature babies","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 42"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-18-1","s":"What treatment is used for neonatal jaundice to convert bilirubin to water-soluble isomers?","o":["A. Blue fluorescent light phototherapy","B. Exchange blood transfusion","C. Intravenous administration of albumin","D. Phenobarbital enzyme induction"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Blue fluorescent light phototherapy","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Blue fluorescent light</b> (phototherapy) changes bilirubin into more polar, <b>water-soluble isomers</b> that are excreted without conjugation to glucuronic acid.<br>B. Exchange transfusion removes bilirubin in severe cases; it does not convert it.<br>C. IV (intravenous) albumin binds bilirubin; it does not convert it.<br>D. Phenobarbital induces the conjugating enzyme; it does not make isomers.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 44 (Jaundice of the newborn)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":18,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":18,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-104-1","s":"A preterm neonate presents with toxic encephalopathy due to excess unconjugated bilirubin levels.  Which of the following treatment options would minimize the likelihood of the patient developing serious complications from kernicterus?","o":["A. Blood transfusion","B. Plasmapheresis","C. Blue fluorescent light exposure","D. Liver transplantation","E. IV saline infusion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Blue fluorescent light exposure","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Blue fluorescent light</b> turns unconjugated bilirubin into <b>water-soluble isomers</b> that are excreted without conjugation. This lowers the free bilirubin that damages the brain.<br>A. A simple blood transfusion does not remove bilirubin (an exchange transfusion is a different procedure).<br>B. Plasmapheresis: not the treatment taught.<br>D. Liver transplantation: for end-stage liver disease, not newborn jaundice.<br>E. IV (intravenous) saline: does not change bilirubin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 44 (Jaundice of the newborn)","L31 Heme slide 43 (Pathological Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":104,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":187,"f":"F1047"}]},{"id":"F0048-179-1","s":"Neonatal hyperbilirubinemia is caused by reduced levels of bilirubin glucuronyltransferase. What is the treatment and what can result if this is left untreated?","o":["A. Dialysis; Rotor syndrome","B. Blue-fluorescent light; kernicterus","C. Blue-fluorescent light; Gilbert’s syndrome","D. Dialysis; Crigler Najjar syndrome"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Blue fluorescent light (phototherapy); kernicterus","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Treat with <b>blue fluorescent light</b> (phototherapy). Untreated, high unconjugated bilirubin can cross the blood-brain barrier and cause <b>kernicterus</b> (toxic encephalopathy).<br>A and D. Dialysis does not remove albumin-bound bilirubin and is not the treatment.<br>A. Rotor syndrome: an inherited excretion defect, not a result of newborn jaundice.<br>C and D. Gilbert and Crigler-Najjar syndromes are inherited; they are not complications.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 42 (Physiological Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 43 (Pathological Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 44 (Jaundice of the newborn)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":179,"f":"F0048"}]},{"id":"F0208-26-1","s":"A 3-day old patient born 30 weeks gestation (premature) is brought to the ED by mother for concerns of jaundice and neurological deficits. Highly suspicious on your differential diagnosis is kernicterus secondary to jaundice of the newborn. If this is the case, then what enzyme is deficient in the patient, and what is the appropriate treatment?","o":["A. UDP glucuronyl transferase; blue fluorescent light","B. β glucuronidase; blue fluorescent light","C. UDP glucuronyl transferase; β-carotene","D. β glucuronidase; β-carotene"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"UDP glucuronyl transferase; blue fluorescent light","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Newborns, especially premature ones, have low <b>UDP-glucuronyl transferase</b> (uridine diphosphate glucuronyl transferase). The treatment is <b>blue fluorescent light</b>, which turns bilirubin into water-soluble isomers excreted without conjugation.<br>B and D. β-glucuronidase removes glucuronic acid in the gut; it is not the deficient enzyme.<br>C and D. β-carotene treats photosensitivity in porphyrias, not newborn jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 42 (Physiological Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 43 (Pathological Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 44 (Jaundice of the newborn)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":26,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"old Exam 3 TA review: heme part only","sl":190,"f":"F1070"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Kernicterus = toxic encephalopathy","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-43.jpg","cap":"Unconjugated bilirubin crosses blood-brain barrier and deposits in brain: kernicterus","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G026-30.jpg","cap":"Hydrophobic bilirubin displaced from albumin diffuses into the brain","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-62-1","s":"Babies born with low levels of conjugating enzyme are at risk for which of the following complications?","o":["A. Muscle Weakness","B. Brain Hemorrhage","C. Toxic Encephalopathy","D. Gaze Paralysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Toxic Encephalopathy","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Low conjugating enzyme lets <b>unconjugated bilirubin</b> rise. It crosses the blood-brain barrier and deposits in the brain: <b>kernicterus</b>, a toxic encephalopathy.<br>A. Muscle weakness: not the named complication.<br>B. Brain hemorrhage: bilirubin deposits; it does not cause bleeding.<br>D. Gaze paralysis: can be a late sign of kernicterus, but the complication itself is the encephalopathy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 43 (Pathological Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 42 (Physiological Jaundice)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":62,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 review","n":"old Exam 3 review: heme part only","sl":227,"f":"F0044"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0028-18-1","s":"If there is excess unconjugated billrubin that diffuses into the basal ganglia, what is caused?","o":["A. Muscle ataxia","B. Hemorrhage","C. Left sided brain atrophy","D. Toxic encephalopathy"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Toxic encephalopathy","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Unconjugated bilirubin that diffuses into the <b>basal ganglia</b> causes <b>kernicterus</b>, a <b>toxic encephalopathy</b>.<br>A. Muscle ataxia: not the named result.<br>B. Hemorrhage: bilirubin deposits; it does not cause bleeding.<br>C. Left-sided brain atrophy: kernicterus hits the basal ganglia on both sides.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 43 (Pathological Jaundice)","L31 Heme slide 30 (Release of Indirect Bilirubin in the Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":18,"f":"F0028"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Hepatic vs post-hepatic: stool stercobilin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-45.jpg","cap":"Table: hepatic has urine bilirubin and fecal stercobilin; post-hepatic lacks stercobilin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-59-1","s":"How would you differentiate between intrahepatic and post-hepatic jaundice?","o":["A. Intrahepatic jaundice will only have conjugated bilirubin in the urine","B. Post-hepatic jaundice will have urobilinogen in the urine","C. Post-hepatic jaundice will have conjugated bilirubin in the urine and stercobilin in fecal excretion","D. Intrahepatic jaundice will have both conjugated bilirubin in the urine and stercobilin in fecal excretion"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Intrahepatic jaundice will have both conjugated bilirubin in the urine and stercobilin in fecal excretion","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> In <b>intrahepatic</b> jaundice, some conjugated bilirubin leaks into blood and <b>urine</b>, but bile still reaches the gut, so <b>stercobilin is present</b> in stool. In complete <b>post-hepatic</b> obstruction, stercobilin is absent (clay stool).<br>A. Intrahepatic urine also has urobilinogen, not only conjugated bilirubin.<br>B. Post-hepatic: urobilinogen is absent from urine.<br>C. Post-hepatic: urine bilirubin yes, but stool stercobilin is absent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 45 (|  | Pre Hepatic  | Hepatic | Post hepatic  |)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":59,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 review","n":"old Exam 3 review: heme part only","sl":224,"f":"F0044"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":24,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":174,"f":"F0048"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":22,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L31","label":"Hemoglobin synthesis: prorubricyte to reticulocyte","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0035-6.jpg","cap":"Hemoglobin synthesis begins in prorubricyte, two-thirds by metarubricyte, completes in reticulocyte","where":"2020 TA review, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-46-1","s":"At what stage in erythrocyte development does hemoglobin synthesis begin?","o":["A. Rubriblast","B. Prorubricyte","C. Metarubricyte","D. Reticulocyte","E. Erythrocyte"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Prorubricyte","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Hemoglobin synthesis <b>begins in the prorubricyte</b> (basophilic normoblast). About two-thirds is done by the metarubricyte, and it is <b>completed in the reticulocyte</b>.<br>A. Rubriblast: too early; no hemoglobin made yet.<br>C. Metarubricyte: synthesis is about two-thirds done here.<br>D. Reticulocyte: where synthesis finishes.<br>E. Erythrocyte: the mature cell makes no new hemoglobin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":46,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2021 review","n":"old Exam 3 review: heme part only","sl":211,"f":"F0044"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-4-1","s":"At which stage does the completion of hemoglobin synthesis occur?","o":["A. Erythrocyte","B. Prorubricyte","C. Metarubricyte","D. Reticulocyte"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Reticulocyte","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Hemoglobin synthesis starts in the prorubricyte, is about two-thirds done by the metarubricyte, and is <b>completed in the reticulocyte</b>.<br>A. Erythrocyte: has no mitochondria or ribosomes, so it makes no new hemoglobin.<br>B. Prorubricyte: where synthesis begins.<br>C. Metarubricyte: only about two-thirds done.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":4,"f":"F0035"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"old Exam III TA review: heme part only","sl":154,"f":"F0048"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":9,"f":"F0028"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"Adipose is about 15% water; muscle 80%","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-5.jpg","cap":"Fuel stores of a 70 kg man; adipose about 15% water, muscle about 80%","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F0120-19-1","s":"How much water does adipose tissue have","o":["A. 80%","B. 20%","C. 15%","D. 15.5%"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"15%","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 15%.</b> <b>Adipose tissue is only about 15% water</b>, so stored triacylglycerol (TAG) packs a lot of energy into little weight. That is why fat is the body's efficient energy reserve.<br>A. 80%: that is <b>muscle</b>, which is about 80% water.<br>B. 20%: too high; adipose is about 15%.<br>D. 15.5%: a decoy decimal; the lecture number is about 15%.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 5 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"2025 TA review macronutrients, nutrition","sl":19,"f":"F0120"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"Liver glycogen keeps blood glucose between meals","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-6.jpg","cap":"Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose between meals; muscle glycogen fuels contraction","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-142-1","s":"Stanley eats his breakfast pretzel at 10 AM and his next meal will be at 5 PM, after his work day ends. What is responsible for maintaining blood glucose between his meals?","o":["A. Fat breakdown","B. Liver glycogen","C. Protein breakdown","D. Muscle glycogen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Liver glycogen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Liver glycogen.</b> <b>Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose between meals</b> (about 200 g after a meal, about 80 g between meals).<br>A. Fat breakdown: fatty acids fuel tissues but cannot be turned into glucose.<br>C. Protein breakdown: the fuel of last resort.<br>D. Muscle glycogen: muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so it keeps its glucose for its own contraction.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":142,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F0120-21-1","s":"A track runner is starving because he just ran a 5 mile race without eating breakfast. In order to keep him from going into hypoglycemic shock, what does the body most likely do first to compensate for the sugar loss?","o":["A. Break down his protein stores","B. Break down liver glycogen","C. Break down nucleic acid","D. Break down Muscle glycogen"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Break down liver glycogen","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Break down liver glycogen.</b> <b>Liver glycogen</b> keeps blood glucose up between meals, so it is the first store tapped when blood sugar falls.<br>A. Protein: the fuel of <b>last resort</b>, used after glycogen and fat.<br>C. Nucleic acid: not a fuel store.<br>D. Muscle glycogen: used by the muscle itself for contraction. Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase, so it cannot release free glucose into the blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"2025 TA review macronutrients, nutrition","sl":21,"f":"F0120"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"Protein is the fuel of last resort","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-6.jpg","cap":"Proteins listed as fuel of last resort, with many non-fuel functions","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/G027-5.jpg","cap":"Protein is 6 kg of stored fuel, behind 15 kg of fat","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-164-1","s":"Which of the following regarding fuel stores in humans is true?","o":["A. Proteins are the first fuel source after glucose stores are used up","B. Glycogen from the liver is the last resort for fuel sources","C. Triglycerides from muscle are the first fuel source after glucose is used up","D. Proteins are a last resort for fuel sources"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Proteins are a last resort for fuel sources","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Proteins are a last resort.</b> Proteins have <b>many jobs besides fuel</b> (enzymes, structure, transport), so the body spares them.<br>A. Proteins first after glucose: no; glycogen and then fat come first.<br>B. Liver glycogen last resort: no; it is the <b>first</b> store used between meals.<br>C. Muscle triglycerides first: no; liver glycogen comes first, then fatty acids from <b>adipose tissue</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":164,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2296-161-2","s":"Proteins are a LAST RESORT for fuel. Why?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"They have many other important roles in the body - so we do not want to use them if we don't have to","a":null,"why":"<b>Protein is the fuel of last resort</b> because it has <b>many functions other than fuel</b>: enzymes, muscle and structural proteins, transporters, antibodies and hormones. Burning it means losing working tissue.<br>Common mix-up: the body holds about 6 kg of protein, but it is not a storage depot like glycogen or fat; it is spared and broken down mainly in prolonged starvation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":161,"f":"F2296"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"Glycogen stores are small; fat is the reserve","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-5.jpg","cap":"Fat is 15 kg (85%) of fuel stores; glycogen under 1%","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G027-6.jpg","cap":"Glycogen stores are small: liver about 80 to 200 g, muscle 400 g","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-161-1","s":"Why do we use fat stores and not glycogen stores?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Because glycogen stores are SMALL and therefore will not provide a sufficient energy supply","a":null,"why":"<b>Glycogen stores are small</b>: under 1% of stored fuel in a 70 kg man (liver about 80 to 200 g, muscle about 400 g). <b>Fat is about 15 kg, about 85% of stored fuel</b>, and adipose holds only about 15% water, so it is a dense store.<br>Common mix-up: liver glycogen is used first between meals, but it is nearly gone after about a day of fasting, so fat carries longer fasts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":161,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"BMI = weight ÷ height squared","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-8.jpg","cap":"BMI formula as kg/m² and 703 × lb/in², with height-weight chart","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-14-1","s":"During the fourth year of medical school, many students go on away rotations. And you oh-so-luckily are doing an away away rotation -- in Italy! Thus (and once again) a 35 year old female patient comes into the office for an annual check up. Her metrics are as follows: Height: 1.53m Weight: 90 kg. OH NO! You forgot in Europe they use the metric system. But no stress, you still know how to calculate the BMI and find that the patient’s is:","o":["A. 32.6","B. 29.9","C. 30.1","D. 38.5","E. 24.9"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"38.5","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 38.5.</b> In metric units, body mass index (BMI) is weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared. 1.53² = 2.34, and 90 ÷ 2.34 = <b>38.5</b>, which is obese.<br>A. 32.6: too low for 90 kg at 1.53 m.<br>B. 29.9: too low; the top of the overweight range.<br>C. 30.1: too low.<br>E. 24.9: the top of the normal range, far too low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":14,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":11,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F0577-10-1","s":"A 35 year old female patient comes into your office for their annual check up. During the visit she asks what her BMI is. Consulting her chart you see the following: Height: 5’5”, Weight: 140 lbs. What is her BMI?","o":["A. 27.3","B. 32.2","C. 23.3","D. 24.9","E. 19.4"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"23.3","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 23.3.</b> Body mass index (BMI) is weight divided by height squared; in US units, multiply pounds by 703, then divide by inches squared. 5 ft 5 in = 65 in, so 703 × 140 ÷ 65² = 98,420 ÷ 4,225 = <b>23.3</b>.<br>A. 27.3: too high; would be overweight.<br>B. 32.2: too high; would be obese.<br>D. 24.9: the top of the normal range, not her value.<br>E. 19.4: too low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":10,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":7,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F0621-55-1","s":"A 40 year old female comes into your office for their annual check up. Height: 5’6”, weight: 135lbs. What is her BMI","o":["A. 19.4","B. 21.3","C. 22.5","D. 25.2","E. 20.8"],"g":["B"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"22.5","a":"C","why":"<b>B. 21.3</b> is the closest choice. Body mass index (BMI) is weight divided by height squared; in US units, 703 × pounds ÷ inches squared. 5 ft 6 in = 66 in, so 703 × 135 ÷ 66² = 94,905 ÷ 4,356 = <b>21.8</b>, normal range. No choice is exact; 21.3 is nearest.<br>A. 19.4: too low.<br>C. 22.5: the old key; it comes from using 65 in instead of 66 in.<br>D. 25.2: too high; overweight range.<br>E. 20.8: further off than 21.3.","ex":"","fl":["703 x 135 / 66^2 = 94,905 / 4,356 = 21.8 kg/m2, not 22.5; no option is exact, B (21.3) is the nearest"],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":55,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F2303-145-1","s":"Moana takes Keke to the local Motunui clinic for a physical. His current weight is 97 Kg and his measured height is 5’10. What is his BMI and what range of BMI does he fall under? (1 Kg is 2.2 lb) (1 inch = 0.0254 m)","o":["A. 30.6 & overweight","B. 30.6 & obese","C. 23.8 & healthy","D. 18.5 & underweight"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"30.6 & obese","a":"B","why":"<b>B. 30.6, obese.</b> Body mass index (BMI) is weight divided by height squared; in pounds and inches, multiply by 703. 97 kg × 2.2 = 213.4 lb; 5 ft 10 in = 70 in. BMI = 703 × 213.4 ÷ 70² = 150,020 ÷ 4,900 = <b>30.6</b>. <b>30 or more is obese</b>.<br>A. 30.6 and overweight: right number, wrong range; overweight is 25.0 to 29.9.<br>C. 23.8 and healthy: arithmetic error.<br>D. 18.5 and underweight: arithmetic error; 18.5 is only the underweight cutoff.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":145,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"BMI ranges: normal 18.5–24.9, obese ≥30","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-8.jpg","cap":"BMI cutoffs: under 18.5, 18.5–24.9, 25–29.9, 30 or more, with chart","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G027-9.jpg","cap":"Starred rough estimate: BMR = 24 × weight in kg per day","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-12-1","s":"After the calculation from the last question, you tell the patient her BMI is 23.3. “Ok great,” she says, “What BMI range is that in?” You answer:","o":["A. Emaciated","B. Underweight","C. Normal","D. Overweight","E. Obese"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Normal","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Normal.</b> A body mass index (BMI) of 23.3 sits in the <b>normal range, 18.5 to 24.9</b>. BMI is weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared.<br>A. Emaciated: not a BMI category.<br>B. Underweight: below 18.5.<br>D. Overweight: 25.0 to 29.9.<br>E. Obese: 30 or more.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":12,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":9,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3881-83-1","s":"Explain the BMI ranges and provide the BMR equation.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Underweight < 18.5; Normal 18.5-24.9; Overweight 25.0-29.9; Obese ≥ 30.0. BMR (rough estimate) = 24 x weight in kg (kcal/day)","a":null,"why":"<b>Body mass index (BMI)</b> is weight in kilograms divided by height in meters squared. <b>Under 18.5 underweight, 18.5 to 24.9 normal, 25.0 to 29.9 overweight, 30 or more obese.</b><br>The rough <b>basal metabolic rate (BMR)</b> is 24 times weight in kilograms, in kilocalories (kcal) per day.<br>Common mix-up: BMR uses kilograms, not pounds, and BMI plays no part in the BMR formula.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 8 (Definition of BMI)","P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":83,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"Rough BMR = 24 × weight in kg","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-9.jpg","cap":"Starred rough BMR = 24 × kg; sex factors 0.9 women, 1.0 men","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F1200-106-3","s":"BMR equation?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"BMR (rough estimate) = 24 x weight in kg (kcal/day)","a":null,"why":"<b>Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) = 24 times weight in kilograms</b>, in kilocalories (kcal) per day; about one kilocalorie per kilogram per hour. The sex-specific version multiplies by 0.9 for women and 1.0 for men. The Owen and Harris-Benedict equations also exist; Harris-Benedict overestimates by about 5%.<br>Common mix-up: plug in kilograms, not pounds; pounds give a value 2.2 times too high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":106,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":98,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":106,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0621-58-1","s":"A 50 year old male came into your office today for a general check up. Weight: 80kg. What is their estimated BMR of the day?","o":["A. 2000","B. 3205","C. 1740","D. 1920","E. 1840"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"1920","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 1920.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 times weight in kilograms: 24 × 80 = <b>1920</b> kilocalories (kcal) per day. Age does not enter the rough estimate.<br>A. 2000: a round guess.<br>B. 3205: too high.<br>C. 1740: too low.<br>E. 1840: about 24 × 77 kg; arithmetic slip.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":58,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F1010-11-1","s":"Mr. Harris is 6 ft 4 in (1.93 m) tall and weighs 220 lb (100 Kg). His basal metabolic rate (BMR) over 24 hours may be roughly estimated to be:","o":["A. 1930 Kcal","B. 4800 Kcal","C. 3000 Kcal","D. 2400 Kcal","E. 5300 Kcal"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"2400 Kcal","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 2400 kcal.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 times weight in kilograms per day: 24 × 100 = <b>2400</b> kilocalories (kcal). Height does not enter the rough estimate.<br>A. 1930: built from the height (1.93 m), not the weight.<br>B. 4800: double the answer.<br>C. 3000: too high.<br>E. 5300: about 24 × 220; uses pounds instead of kilograms.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)","L32 Macronutrients slide 9 (Methods for estimating BMR)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 16 quiz","sl":11,"f":"F1010"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":30,"f":"D006"}]},{"id":"F0577-16-1","s":"After a full day of studying a 79 kg (174.2 lbs) medical student takes a break to go make dinner. But alas, they did not go grocery shopping and have no food. Sad, tired, and hungry, they go to sleep. As usual, their brain will not shut off before going to bed and asks them one last question: what was their estimated BMR of the day?","o":["A. 4176","B. 2000","C. 1748","D. 1896","E. 2400"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"1896","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 1896.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 times weight in kilograms: 24 × 79 = <b>1896</b> kilocalories (kcal) per day.<br>A. 4176: 24 × 174.2; uses pounds instead of kilograms.<br>B. 2000: a round guess.<br>C. 1748: too low.<br>E. 2400: the value for 100 kg.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":16,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":13,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F0120-23-1","s":"Calculate the basal metabolic rate of a 100 kg male","o":["A. 3000 kcal/day","B. 2000 kcal/day","C. 2370 kcal/day","D. 2400 kcal/day","E. 2160 kcal/day"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"2400 kcal/day","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 2400 kcal/day.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 kilocalories (kcal) per kilogram per day. For a man the sex factor is 1.0: 24 × 1.0 × 100 = <b>2400</b>.<br>A. 3000: too high.<br>B. 2000: too low.<br>C. 2370: does not come from 24 × weight in kilograms.<br>E. 2160: the women's factor, 24 × 0.9 × 100.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)","L32 Macronutrients slide 9 (Methods for estimating BMR)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"2025 TA review macronutrients, nutrition","sl":23,"f":"F0120"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2303-147-1","s":"Glinda is getting ready for her trek to the Land of Oz. Before starting her journey, she wants to calculate her basal metabolic rate to figure out how much energy her body uses to maintain life sustaining functions. Her weight is 99 lb and her height is 5’5 (1 kg = 2.2 lb). Calculate her rough estimate.","o":["A. 1808.7 kcal/day","B. 1770.3 kcal/day","C. 1080.0 kcal/day","D. 2450.2 kcal/day"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"1080.0 kcal/day","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 1080.0 kcal/day.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 times weight in kilograms, in kilocalories (kcal) per day. 99 lb ÷ 2.2 = 45 kg, and 24 × 45 = <b>1080</b>. Height is not used.<br>A. 1808.7: not 24 × 45.<br>B. 1770.3: not 24 × 45.<br>D. 2450.2: far too high for 45 kg.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":147,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F0998-163-1","s":"You have a 27 year old female patient who comes into the office. She weighs 115 lbs. What is her approximate BMR? (Basal Metabolic Rate?","o":["A. 12","B. 124","C. 1248","D. 12480"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"1248 (kcal/day): 115 lb / 2.2 = ~52 kg x 24","a":"C","why":"<b>C. 1248 kcal/day.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 times weight in kilograms. 115 lb ÷ 2.2 ≈ 52 kg, and 24 × 52 = <b>1248</b> kilocalories (kcal) per day. The women's estimate (× 0.9) would give about 1123.<br>A. 12: off by a factor of 100.<br>B. 124: off by a factor of 10.<br>D. 12480: ten times too high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":163,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Lecture 40 (iron) TA review","sl":16,"f":"F2432"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"RDA meets needs of 98% of population","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-11.jpg","cap":"Risk curve: EAR at 50% inadequacy, RDA 2 SD higher; definitions listed","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-103-1","s":"What is the Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)?","o":["A. Average daily intake estimated to meet the requirement of 50% of a population","B. Average daily intake estimated to meet the requirements of 98% of a population","C. The maximum amount of a nutrient that can be taken by almost all individuals without adverse effects"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Average daily intake estimated to meet the requirements of 98% of a population","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Meets the requirements of 98% of a population.</b> The <b>Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)</b> is the Estimated Average Requirement plus 2 standard deviations, so it covers 98% of people.<br>A. Meets 50%: the Estimated Average Requirement (EAR).<br>C. Maximum amount without adverse effects: the Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":103,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F3881-85-1","s":"A patient comes to your office asking information about portion sizes and recommended dietary intake. During the course of your interaction, you explain the concept of recommended dietary allowance (RDA). Which statement correctly explains this concept?","o":["A. Recommended to be adequate for almost all in a population and used if data is insufficient for other calculations","B. Average daily intake estimated to meet requirements for 98% of population","C. Maximum amount of a nutrient that can be taken by almost everyone without adverse effects","D. Average daily intake estimated to meet the requirement of 50% of the population"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Average daily intake estimated to meet requirements for 98% of population","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Meets requirements of 98% of a population.</b> That is the <b>Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)</b>, set 2 standard deviations above the average requirement.<br>A. Adequate for almost all, used when data are short: the Adequate Intake (AI).<br>C. Maximum amount without adverse effects: the Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL).<br>D. Meets 50%: the Estimated Average Requirement (EAR).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":85,"f":"F3881"}]},{"id":"F2303-149-1","s":"Marcello, during his weekend update, states that his daily nutrition intake is sufficient to meet the need of 98% of the population. What Dietary Reference Intakes (DRI) concept did he describe?","o":["A. Adequate Intake","B. Recommended dietary allowance","C. Tolerable Upper intake level","D. Estimated average requirement"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Recommended dietary allowance (RDA)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Recommended dietary allowance.</b> Meeting the needs of <b>98% of a population</b> defines the <b>Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)</b>, set 2 standard deviations above the average requirement.<br>A. Adequate Intake (AI): a best guess when data are too thin for an RDA.<br>C. Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL): the safe upper limit.<br>D. Estimated Average Requirement (EAR): meets 50% of a population.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":149,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F1013-166-1","s":"Which of the following dietary reference intake definitions is correct?","o":["A. Estimated average requirement - average daily intake estimated to meet the requirements of 75% of the population","B. Recommended daily allowance - average daily intake estimated to meet the requirements of 98% of the population","C. Adequate intake - estimated to be adequate for at least 50% of the population","D. Total upper intake level - the maximum amount of a nutrient that can be taken by 75% of the population without adverse effects"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Recommended daily allowance - average daily intake estimated to meet the requirements of 98% of the population","a":"B","why":"<b>B. RDA meets the needs of 98%.</b> The <b>Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)</b> is the average daily intake that meets the requirement of <b>98% of a population</b>.<br>A. Estimated Average Requirement (EAR): meets 50%, not 75%.<br>C. Adequate Intake (AI): adequate for almost all, used when data are too thin for an RDA; not 'at least 50%'.<br>D. The Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL) is the most almost all people can take without harm, not a 75% figure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":166,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"Above the UL, adverse-effect risk rises","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-11.jpg","cap":"Risk curve: adverse-effect risk climbs once intake passes the UL","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-151-1","s":"Which of the following will occur if an individual consumes nutrients beyond the tolerable upper intake level (UL)?","o":["A. The risk of inadequacy will become 50%","B. The risk of inadequacy will become 3%","C. Proper dietary intake will be met","D. The risk of adverse effects will increase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The risk of adverse effects will increase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Risk of adverse effects increases.</b> The <b>Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL)</b> is the most almost everyone can take without harm; <b>above it, the risk of adverse effects rises</b>.<br>A. 50% risk of inadequacy: that is at the Estimated Average Requirement (EAR).<br>B. 2 to 3% risk of inadequacy: that is at the Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA).<br>C. Proper intake is met below the UL, not by going above it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":151,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-P14","label":"EAR, RDA, AI, UL definitions","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-11.jpg","cap":"DRI tree and definitions: EAR 50%, RDA 98%, AI, UL","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-165-1","s":"Match the Following! 1. The maximum amount of nutrient that can be taken by ALL individuals without adverse effects; 2. Average daily requirement met by 98% of individuals.; 3. Average daily requirement met by 50% of individuals.; 4. Estimated to be adequate for almost all individuals.","o":["A. EAR (estimated required requirement)","B. RDA (recommended daily allowance)","C. UL (Tolerable Upper Intake Level)","D. AI (adequate intake)"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1-C (UL), 2-B (RDA), 3-A (EAR), 4-D (AI)","a":"1-D, 2-B, 3-A, 4-C","why":"<b>1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D.</b> <b>Maximum intake without adverse effects = Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL).</b> 98% = Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA). 50% = Estimated Average Requirement (EAR). <b>Adequate for almost all = Adequate Intake (AI)</b>, the best guess when there are too few data for an RDA.<br>Common mix-up: the old key swapped UL and AI. Think 'upper limit' for UL and 'enough' for AI.","ex":"","fl":["The key swaps UL and AI. UL (C) is the maximum intake that almost all individuals can take without adverse effects. AI (D) is the intake estimated to be adequate for almost all individuals (G027:11).","The source keyed 1-D and 4-C, swapping UL and AI; the maximum intake without adverse effects is the UL (C) and the intake adequate for almost all is the AI (D)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":165,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Lecture 40 (iron) TA review","sl":18,"f":"F2432"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1200-110-1","s":"Definitions of EAR, RDA, AI, UL?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"EAR: average daily intake that meets the requirement of 50% of a population. RDA: meets the requirement of 98% of a population (EAR + 2 SD). AI: used when there is not enough data for an RDA; estimated adequate for almost all of a population. UL: maximum amount that almost all individuals can take without adverse effects.","a":null,"why":"<b>Estimated Average Requirement (EAR)</b>: meets the needs of 50% of a population. <b>Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)</b>: meets 98%; it is the EAR plus 2 standard deviations. <b>Adequate Intake (AI)</b>: a best guess, adequate for almost all, used when there are too few data for an RDA. <b>Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL)</b>: the most almost all people can take without adverse effects.<br>Common mix-up: AI and UL both say 'almost all'; AI is about enough, UL is about too much.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":110,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":102,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":110,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Energy expenditure: BMR, TEF, physical activity","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-4.jpg","cap":"Pie chart: resting metabolic rate 60%, activity 30%, thermic effect 10%","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-85-1","s":"Energy balance is achieved when energy intake equals energy expenditure. What 3 factors make up energy expenditure?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Basal (resting) metabolic rate; thermic effect of food; physical activity","a":null,"why":"Energy expenditure has three parts: <b>basal metabolic rate (BMR)</b>, about 60%; <b>thermic effect of food (TEF)</b>, the cost of digesting, absorbing and storing food, about 10%; and <b>physical activity</b>, about 30% and the most variable part.<br>Common mix-up: TEF is highest for protein (20 to 30% of its calories), then carbohydrate (5 to 10%), then fat (0 to 3%).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 4 (Daily Energy Expenditure)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":85,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"BMR: resting energy, awake, post-absorptive","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-5.jpg","cap":"BMR definition and test conditions: rest, awake, warm, 12 hours fasted","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-146-1","s":"What is basal metabolic rate?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"The energy expenditure necessary to maintain basic physiologic functions while at rest in a post-absorptive state. It is the amount of calories your body would need if you were to stay in bed awake all day","a":null,"why":"<b>Basal metabolic rate (BMR)</b> is the energy needed for <b>basic body functions at rest</b>: heart pumping, lung and kidney work, ion gradients across membranes, and biochemical reactions. It is measured <b>awake, at rest, warm, and at least 12 hours after a meal</b> (post-absorptive), by indirect calorimetry of oxygen use.<br>Common mix-up: BMR is measured awake, not asleep, and it is the largest share (about 60%) of daily energy use.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 5 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)","P14 Nutrition slide 9 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":146,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Race or ethnicity does not set BMR","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-7.jpg","cap":"Factors that change BMR: sex, body and room temperature, age, hormones","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G028-6.jpg","cap":"BMR proportional to lean body mass and surface area","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-87-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT a factor affecting BMR?","o":["A. Age","B. Body temperature","C. Gender","D. Race","E. Hormone levels"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Race","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Race.</b> Race is not on the list of what changes <b>basal metabolic rate (BMR)</b>: lean body mass, surface area, sex, age, body temperature, ambient temperature, and hormones.<br>A. Age: children have a higher BMR than adults.<br>B. Body temperature: BMR rises 12% per 1 °C.<br>C. Gender: women are lower, with relatively more fat.<br>E. Hormones: thyroid hormone, growth hormone, epinephrine and cortisol raise BMR.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 6 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate (BMR/RMR))","L32 Macronutrients slide 7 (How Do These Factors Affect BMR?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":87,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":121,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":117,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":97,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-148-1","s":"2. Which of the following does not significantly  affect basal metabolic rate","o":["A. Ethnicity","B. Body temperature","C. Ambient temperature","D. Growth hormones","E. Thyroxine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ethnicity","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ethnicity.</b> Ethnicity is not among the factors that set <b>basal metabolic rate (BMR)</b>.<br>B. Body temperature: BMR rises 12% per 1 °C.<br>C. Ambient temperature: BMR is lowest at 26 °C and rises in the cold.<br>D. Growth hormone: raises BMR.<br>E. Thyroxine (T4, a thyroid hormone): raises BMR; it is high in Graves disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 7 (How Do These Factors Affect BMR?)","L32 Macronutrients slide 6 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate (BMR/RMR))"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":148,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Factors that change BMR","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-7.jpg","cap":"Sex, body temperature, ambient temperature, age, hormones and their BMR effects","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G028-6.jpg","cap":"BMR proportional to lean body mass and surface area","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-98-1","s":"Which 5 factors affect BMR?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Gender (BMR lower for women — relatively more adipose tissue); body temperature (BMR rises ~12% per 1°C increase); ambient temperature (lowest BMR at 26°C, slightly higher in cold climates from shivering thermogenesis); age (children higher than adults); other factors — thyroid hormones, growth hormone, epinephrine, cortisol can elevate BMR","a":null,"why":"The five factors on the lecture slide: <b>sex</b> (women lower, more fat), <b>body temperature</b> (up 12% per 1 °C), <b>ambient temperature</b> (lowest at 26 °C, higher in cold from shivering), <b>age</b> (children higher than adults), and <b>hormones</b> (thyroid hormone, growth hormone, epinephrine and cortisol raise basal metabolic rate, BMR).<br>Common mix-up: BMR rises with fever; it does not fall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 7 (How Do These Factors Affect BMR?)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":98,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":106,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":106,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F3881-135-1","s":"What factors can influence BMR?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Lean body mass and body surface area (directly proportional), gender, age, body temperature, ambient temperature, and hormones (thyroid hormone, growth hormone, epinephrine, cortisol)","a":null,"why":"<b>Basal metabolic rate (BMR) is proportional to lean body mass and surface area.</b> It is also set by <b>sex</b> (lower in women), <b>age</b> (higher in children), <b>body temperature</b> (up 12% per 1 °C), <b>ambient temperature</b> (lowest at 26 °C, higher in cold), and <b>hormones</b> (thyroid hormone, growth hormone, epinephrine and cortisol raise it).<br>Common mix-up: race and ethnicity are not on the list.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 6 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate (BMR/RMR))","L32 Macronutrients slide 7 (How Do These Factors Affect BMR?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":135,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"BMR tracks lean body mass and surface area","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-6.jpg","cap":"Lean mass and surface area set BMR; taller, thinner person runs higher","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-98-2","s":"BMR is proportional to what? (2 things)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Lean body mass (adipose is much less metabolically active) and surface area (heat loss ∝ surface area; of two people with comparable body mass, the taller, thinner one has the higher BMR)","a":null,"why":"<b>Basal metabolic rate (BMR) is proportional to lean body mass and surface area.</b> Fat is much less metabolically active than muscle, so more muscle means a higher BMR. Heat loss grows with surface area, so of two people with the same mass, the <b>taller, thinner one has the higher BMR</b>.<br>Common mix-up: BMR follows lean mass, not total weight or body mass index.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 6 (Basal/Resting Metabolic Rate (BMR/RMR))"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":98,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":106,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":106,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"BMR lowest at 26°C; +12% per °C","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-7.jpg","cap":"Body temperature adds 12% per °C; lowest BMR at 26 °C ambient","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 7"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-102-1","s":"A 25-year-old medical student is studying factors that influence basal metabolic rate (BMR). Which of the following statements about BMR is correct?","o":["A) Women generally have higher BMRs than men due to increased muscle mass.","B) BMR decreases by 12% with each 1°C increase in body temperature.","C) BMR is lowest at an ambient temperature of 26°C.","D) Thyroid hormones and cortisol lower BMR by reducing metabolic activity."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"BMR is lowest at an ambient temperature of 26°C","a":"C","why":"<b>C. BMR is lowest at 26 °C.</b> At about 26 °C the body spends nothing extra on warming, so <b>basal metabolic rate (BMR)</b> is lowest; cold raises it slightly through shivering.<br>A. Women have a <b>lower</b> BMR, because they have relatively more fat.<br>B. BMR <b>increases</b> 12% per 1 °C rise in body temperature.<br>D. Thyroid hormone and cortisol <b>raise</b> BMR.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 7 (How Do These Factors Affect BMR?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":102,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Highest BMR: hyperthyroid, young, tall, cold","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-8.jpg","cap":"Graves disease raises BMR; Hashimoto thyroiditis lowers it","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G028-7.jpg","cap":"Cold climate, youth and male sex raise BMR","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G028-6.jpg","cap":"Taller, thinner body has higher BMR from greater surface area","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-110-1","s":"Who of the following is expected to have the highest basal metabolic rate (BMR)?","o":["A. An elderly woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis","B. An average height, slightly obese female medical student","C. A tall, thin male teenager diagnosed with Graves disease living in Alaska","D. A tall, thin male teenager diagnosed with Graves disease living in Hawaii"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"A tall, thin male teenager diagnosed with Graves disease living in Alaska","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Tall, thin teen with Graves disease in Alaska.</b> Every factor pushes <b>basal metabolic rate (BMR)</b> up: excess thyroid hormone, youth, male sex, large surface area for his mass, and a <b>cold climate</b> (shivering).<br>A. Elderly woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis: low thyroid hormone, older, female; low BMR.<br>B. Slightly obese woman: more fat, less lean mass; lower BMR.<br>D. Same teen in Hawaii: warm climate near 26 °C, where BMR is lowest, so a bit lower than in Alaska.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 7 (How Do These Factors Affect BMR?)","L32 Macronutrients slide 8 (Thyroid Disorders and BMR)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":110,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Hyperthyroidism raises BMR","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-8.jpg","cap":"Hyperthyroidism (Graves) raises BMR; hypothyroidism (Hashimoto) lowers it","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-89-1","s":"True or False: Hyperthyroidism decreases BMR","o":["A. TRUE","B. FALSE"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"FALSE","a":"B","why":"<b>B. False.</b> <b>Hyperthyroidism increases basal metabolic rate (BMR).</b> In Graves disease, antibodies switch on the thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) receptor, thyroid hormone rises, and BMR goes up: weight loss, heat intolerance, more bowel movements.<br>Common mix-up: it is <b>hypothyroidism</b> (Hashimoto thyroiditis) that lowers BMR: weight gain, cold intolerance, constipation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 8 (Thyroid Disorders and BMR)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":89,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":123,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":119,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":99,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Rough BMR: convert pounds, then 24 × kg","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-9.jpg","cap":"Starred rough BMR = 24 × kg, plus Owen and Harris-Benedict equations","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-112-1","s":"The teenager diagnosed with Graves disease from the previous question presents to your office for a wellness check. The nurse checks his vital signs and records his height at 6’3” and his weight at 145 lb. What is his roughly estimated BMR? (1 kg = 2.2 lb)","o":["A. 1580","B. 3480","C. 1800","D. 820"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"1580","a":"A","why":"<b>A. 1580.</b> Rough basal metabolic rate (BMR) is 24 times weight in kilograms. 145 lb ÷ 2.2 = 65.9 kg, and 24 × 65.9 ≈ <b>1580</b> kilocalories per day. The rough formula ignores height and thyroid status.<br>B. 3480: 24 × 145; uses pounds, not kilograms.<br>C. 1800: too high for 65.9 kg.<br>D. 820: about half the right value.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 9 (Methods for estimating BMR)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":112,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"TEE = BMR × activity factor","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-12.jpg","cap":"TEE = BMR × activity factor; worked example 1400 × 1.6 = 2240","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0120-3-1","s":"A physician is trying to calculate the total energy expenditure(TEE) of one of his patients. He forgets the formula needed to do so and asks you. You answer by saying:","o":["A. TEE= BMR * activity factor","B. TEE= Activity factor/ BMR","C. TEE= BMI * activity factor","D. TEE/BMR= BMI","E. TEE= BMR * BMI"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"TEE= BMR * activity factor","a":"A","why":"<b>A. TEE = BMR × activity factor.</b> <b>Total energy expenditure (TEE)</b> is the <b>basal metabolic rate (BMR) multiplied by an activity factor</b> (about 1.2 sedentary up to 2.5 very active). Example: 1400 × 1.6 = 2240 kilocalories per day.<br>B. Activity factor ÷ BMR: upside down.<br>C. BMI × activity factor: body mass index (BMI) is weight for height, not energy.<br>D. TEE ÷ BMR is the activity factor, not BMI.<br>E. BMR × BMI: BMI is not a multiplier.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 12 (Estimating Total Energy Expenditure (TEE))"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"2025 TA review macronutrients, nutrition","sl":3,"f":"F0120"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Match EAR, AI, RDA, UL to definitions","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-15.jpg","cap":"Risk curve with EAR, RDA, AI, UL and one-line definitions","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-91-1","s":"MATCH THE FOLLOWING: (1) Average daily intake estimated to meet the requirement of 50% of a population. (2) Used if there is insufficient data to calculate an RDA. Estimated to be adequate for almost all of a population (3) Average daily intake estimated to meet the requirements of 98% of a population. (4) The maximum amount of a nutrient that can be taken by almost all individuals without adverse effects. — RDA / EAR / UL / AI","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"(1) EAR; (2) AI; (3) RDA; (4) UL","a":null,"why":"<b>(1) Estimated Average Requirement (EAR)</b>: meets 50%. <b>(2) Adequate Intake (AI)</b>: used when there are too few data for a Recommended Dietary Allowance; adequate for almost all. <b>(3) Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA)</b>: meets 98%, the EAR plus 2 standard deviations. <b>(4) Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL)</b>: the most almost all people can take without harm.<br>Common mix-up: AI is about getting enough; UL is about too much.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 15 (Dietary Reference Intakes (DRIs): the nutrient requirement 2 SD above EAR)","P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":91,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"EAR is the lowest DRI value","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-15.jpg","cap":"EAR sits 2 SD left of RDA; UL far right on intake axis","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-115-1","s":"Which of these values is relatively lowest?","o":["A. RDA (Recommended Dietary Allowance)","B. EAR (Estimated Average Requirement)","C. UL (Tolerable Upper Intake Level)","D. Adequate Intake (AI)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"EAR (Estimated Average Requirement)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. EAR.</b> The <b>Estimated Average Requirement (EAR)</b> meets only 50% of people, so it is the lowest value. The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) sits 2 standard deviations above it.<br>A. RDA: EAR plus 2 standard deviations, so higher.<br>C. Tolerable Upper Intake Level (UL): the highest, the safe upper limit.<br>D. Adequate Intake (AI): set to cover almost everyone, so above the EAR; it has no fixed link to the EAR or RDA.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 15 (Dietary Reference Intakes (DRIs): the nutrient requirement 2 SD above EAR)","P14 Nutrition slide 11 (Dietary Reference Intakes)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":115,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Low carbohydrate → ketones and protein breakdown","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-19.jpg","cap":"Carbohydrate deficiency raises ketone bodies and muscle protein breakdown","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G028-20.jpg","cap":"Simple, complex and non-digestible (fiber) carbohydrates defined","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-151-1","s":"3. Which of the following statements about carbohydrates is true?","o":["A. Carbohydrate is an  essential nutrient","B. In the absence of carbohydrates, the body may degrade proteins as an energy source","C. Simple carbohydrates include slowly hydrolyzed, oligo- and polysaccharides","D. Complex carbohydrates include monosaccharides, and rapidly hydrolyzed oligosaccharides and polysaccharides"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"In the absence of carbohydrates, the body may degrade proteins as an energy source","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Without carbohydrate, the body may degrade protein.</b> Glucogenic amino acids from muscle protein are turned into glucose to keep blood sugar up, and ketone body production rises.<br>A. False: carbohydrate is not essential.<br>C. False: slowly digested oligo- and polysaccharides are <b>complex</b> carbohydrates.<br>D. False: monosaccharides and rapidly digested oligosaccharides are <b>simple</b> carbohydrates.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)","L32 Macronutrients slide 20 (Types of Dietary Carbohydrates and Their Digestibility)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":151,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F1013-93-1","s":"True or False regarding Carbohydrates: (mark each statement)","o":["A. Carbohydrate is an essential nutrient","B. Absence may lead to ketone body production","C. Absence may lead to protein degradation","D. Simple carbohydrates include slowly hydrolyzed, oligo- and polysaccharides","E. Non-digestible carbohydrates are often components of fiber"],"g":["B","C","E"],"k":["B","C","E"],"rv":false,"at":"TRUE: B, C, E | FALSE: A (carbohydrate is not essential), D (simple carbohydrates are not the slowly hydrolyzed oligo-/polysaccharides)","a":"B,C,E","why":"<b>True: B, C, E.</b> Carbohydrate is <b>not essential</b>, but without it the body makes <b>ketone bodies</b> and breaks down <b>muscle protein</b> (glucogenic amino acids become glucose).<br>A. False: carbohydrate is not biochemically essential.<br>B. True: low carbohydrate raises ketone body production.<br>C. True: low carbohydrate raises muscle protein breakdown.<br>D. False: slowly digested oligo- and polysaccharides are <b>complex</b> carbohydrates.<br>E. True: fiber is non-digestible carbohydrate with β-linkages.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)","L32 Macronutrients slide 20 (Types of Dietary Carbohydrates and Their Digestibility)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":93,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Carbohydrate is not an essential nutrient","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-19.jpg","cap":"Carbohydrate not biochemically essential; no specific type required","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G028-21.jpg","cap":"Glycemic index curves: high (over 70) versus low (under 55) foods","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 21"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-137-1","s":"True or False: Carbohydrates are an essential macronutrient.","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>B. False.</b> Carbohydrate is <b>not biochemically essential</b>: the liver can make glucose from <b>glucogenic amino acids</b>. Low intake still has costs: more <b>ketone bodies</b> and more <b>muscle protein breakdown</b>.<br>Common mix-up: essential nutrients are ones we cannot make, such as essential amino acids and the essential fatty acids linoleic and α-linolenic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":137,"f":"F3881"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":150,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-118-1","s":"The teenager with Graves disease complains of fatigue for the last few weeks. He informs you that he has been eating 2 pounds of pasta a day in attempt to “bulk up” and in order to meet his recommended carbohydrate requirement. What do you need to tell this patient?","o":["A. Ensure that the patient is meeting his protein and fat requirements in addition to the carbohydrate requirement","B. If he continues to consume this level of carbohydrates, then his body will undergo ketone body production","C. There is no specific requirement type for carbohydrate intake because it is not an essential nutrient; it’s better to consume foods with a low glycemic index"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"There is no specific requirement type for carbohydrate intake because it is not an essential nutrient; it’s better to consume foods with a low glycemic index","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> There is <b>no specific carbohydrate requirement</b> because carbohydrate is <b>not essential</b>; what matters is quality, so favor <b>low glycemic index</b> foods that raise blood glucose slowly. Eating huge amounts of pasta to meet a 'requirement' is unnecessary.<br>A. Implies a carbohydrate requirement exists; protein and fat have essential parts, carbohydrate does not.<br>B. Ketone bodies rise when carbohydrate is <b>lacking</b>, not when it is eaten in excess.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":118,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Fiber delays, not speeds, gastric emptying","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-23.jpg","cap":"Fiber benefits: delays gastric emptying, bulks stool, butyrate, lowers cholesterol","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 23"},{"src":"sl/G028-25.jpg","cap":"Soluble fiber slows gastric emptying and blunts after-meal glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-139-1","s":"What is not a health effect of dietary fiber?","o":["A. Speeds up gastric emptying and generates a sensation of fullness","B. Reduces constipation and hemorrhoid formation","C. Decreases absorption of dietary fat and cholesterol","D. Increases bowel motility, thus reducing exposure of gut to carcinogens","E. Metabolized to butyrate by intestinal flora"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Speeds up gastric emptying and generates a sensation of fullness","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Fiber does not speed gastric emptying.</b> Fiber, especially soluble fiber, <b>delays gastric emptying</b>; that is what gives fullness and better glucose control.<br>B. True effect: fiber bulks stool and cuts constipation and hemorrhoids.<br>C. True effect: fiber binds bile acids and raises fecal cholesterol loss.<br>D. True effect: faster transit cuts the gut's exposure to carcinogens.<br>E. True effect: gut bacteria ferment soluble fiber to butyrate, fuel for colon cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 23 (Health Benefits of Dietary Fiber)","L32 Macronutrients slide 24 (Insoluble Fiber: Gastrointestinal Benefits)","L32 Macronutrients slide 25 (Soluble Fiber: Metabolic and Colonic Benefits)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":139,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Excess soluble fiber blocks fat-soluble vitamins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-25.jpg","cap":"Soluble fiber: excess may block fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, K","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G028-26.jpg","cap":"Soluble versus insoluble table; soluble excess impairs fat-soluble vitamin uptake","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F0120-9-1","s":"A 62 year old Asian American man is brought in to the emergency room for some digestive issues. After a blood test, the physician on call notices that he is having trouble absorbing fat soluble vitamins. What might the man have eaten in excess to cause this? What vitamin might have been malabsorbed?","o":["A. Soluble fibers; vitamin B12","B. Insoluble fibers; vitamin k","C. protein ; vitamin D","D. Soluble fibers; vitamin E","E. Carbohydrates; vitamin A"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Soluble fibers; vitamin E","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Soluble fibers; vitamin E.</b> <b>Excess soluble fiber</b> can impair absorption of the <b>fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K</b>.<br>A. Vitamin B12 is water-soluble.<br>B. Vitamin K is fat-soluble, but the fiber that interferes is <b>soluble</b>, not insoluble.<br>C. Protein does not block fat-soluble vitamins.<br>E. Carbohydrate does not block vitamin A absorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 25 (Soluble Fiber: Metabolic and Colonic Benefits)","L32 Macronutrients slide 26 (| Feature | Soluble Fiber | Insoluble Fiber |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"2025 TA review macronutrients, nutrition","sl":9,"f":"F0120"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Soluble fiber → butyrate fuels colonocytes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-26.jpg","cap":"Table: soluble fermented to butyrate; insoluble bulks stool, colon cancer link","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G028-25.jpg","cap":"Soluble fiber fermented in colon to butyrate","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-121-1","s":"You inform the patient from the previous question that althought while he should probably lower his carbohydrate intake, he should maintain a high-fiber diet. Which of the following statements is true?","o":["A. Soluble fibers, such as gums and pectin, may reduce the risk of colon cancer","B. Insoluble fibers, such as cellulose and lignin, may affect the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins","C. Soluble fibers, such as gums and pectin, are converted to butyrate and serve as fuel for colonocytes","D. Insoluble fibers, such as cellulose and lignin, attenuates the postprandial rise in blood sugar"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Soluble fibers, such as gums and pectin, are converted to butyrate and serve as fuel for colonocytes","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Soluble fibers become butyrate, fuel for colonocytes.</b> Gut bacteria ferment <b>soluble fiber</b> (gums, pectin) to the short-chain fatty acid <b>butyrate</b>, which feeds colon cells and calms inflammation.<br>A. Lower colon cancer risk is linked to <b>insoluble</b> fiber in the lecture.<br>B. Interference with fat-soluble vitamins is a <b>soluble</b> fiber effect.<br>D. Blunting the after-meal sugar rise is a <b>soluble</b> fiber effect; insoluble fiber barely affects glucose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 25 (Soluble Fiber: Metabolic and Colonic Benefits)","L32 Macronutrients slide 26 (| Feature | Soluble Fiber | Insoluble Fiber |)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":121,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"PDCAAS: milk and egg score 1.00","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-31.jpg","cap":"PDCAAS table: egg, milk, soy 1.00; kidney beans 0.68; gelatin 0.08","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G028-33.jpg","cap":"Beans plus wheat complement lysine and methionine plus cysteine","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-123-1","s":"You continue to council the patient on proper nutrition and inform him of the Protein Digestibility-Corrected Amino Acid Score. Which of the following has the highest profile of essential amino acids?","o":["A. Milk protein (PDCAAS = 1.00)","B. Kidney beans (PDCAAS = 0.68)","C. Beans + Wheat together (PDCAAS is approximately 0.75)","D. Collagen (PDCAAS = 0.08)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Milk protein (PDCAAS = 1.00)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Milk protein.</b> The <b>Protein Digestibility-Corrected Amino Acid Score (PDCAAS)</b> rates essential amino acid profile and digestibility; <b>1.00 is the top</b>. Milk, egg and soy score 1.00.<br>B. Kidney beans, 0.68: short in methionine and cysteine.<br>C. Beans plus wheat, about 0.75: complementary proteins improve the mix but still score below milk.<br>D. Collagen (gelatin), 0.08: lacks tryptophan.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 31 (PDCAAS: A Previous Standard for Protein Quality)","L32 Macronutrients slide 33 (Complementary Protein Sources)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":123,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Swollen belly, poor healing → kwashiorkor","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-35.jpg","cap":"Labeled kwashiorkor child: prominent belly, rash, poor wound healing","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0120-6-1","s":"A 5 year old child is brought to the hospital due to severe influenza symptoms. His vitals are: 102.5F, 99 BPM, 90% SpO2, 101/75 mmHg. On physical examination the doctors noticed that the boy had very slim arms, a prominent belly and poor wound healing. The physical exam findings most likely describes which of the following conditions","o":["A. Marasmus","B. Kwashiorkor","C. Tabes dorsalis","D. Obesity","E. Hypoalbuminemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Kwashiorkor","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Kwashiorkor.</b> A <b>prominent belly</b> (edema from low albumin) and <b>poor wound healing</b>, set off by an infection that raises protein needs, point to <b>kwashiorkor</b>: too little protein with enough calories.<br>A. Marasmus: wasting with no edema or swollen belly.<br>C. Tabes dorsalis: a late nerve complication of syphilis.<br>D. Obesity: no; the arms are thin.<br>E. Hypoalbuminemia: the lab finding behind the edema, not the disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TA review","n":"2025 TA review macronutrients, nutrition","sl":6,"f":"F0120"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Kwashiorkor: low protein, adequate calories","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-35.jpg","cap":"Marasmus lacks protein and energy; kwashiorkor lacks protein with adequate calories","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-99-1","s":"Which of the following diseases is characterized by intake of adequate calories but lack of protein intake?","o":["A. Kwashiorkor","B. Marasmus","C. Rickets","D. Wet Beriberi","E. Tetralogy of Fallot"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Kwashiorkor","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Kwashiorkor.</b> <b>Too little protein with generally adequate calories</b> is kwashiorkor.<br>B. Marasmus: too little of <b>both</b> protein and energy; general starvation.<br>C. Rickets: vitamin D deficiency.<br>D. Wet beriberi: thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency with heart failure.<br>E. Tetralogy of Fallot: a congenital heart defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":99,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":125,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":121,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":101,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1010-13-1","s":"A 3-year old male is brought by his mother to the pediatrician's office. The mother and child are refugees from Somalia and arrived in the United States one week ago. Physical examination reveals a listless child with a rotund abdomen and positive fluid wave. Notable lower extremity edema is present. This patient's condition is most likely caused by a deficiency in which of the following?","o":["A. Protein intake","B. Vitamin intake","C. Fat intake","D. Total caloric intake","E. Carbohydrate intake"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Protein intake","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Protein intake.</b> A swollen belly with a <b>fluid wave</b> and <b>leg edema</b> is <b>kwashiorkor</b>: low protein, low albumin, low oncotic pressure.<br>B. Vitamin intake: does not cause this edema pattern.<br>C. Fat intake: not the cause.<br>D. Total caloric intake: that is marasmus, wasting without edema.<br>E. Carbohydrate intake: not the cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 16 quiz","sl":13,"f":"F1010"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":32,"f":"D006"}]},{"id":"F2303-105-1","s":"A 3-year-old boy is brought to the clinic in a resource-limited area. On examination, he appears severely underweight, with prominent ribs and muscle wasting. Another child in the same household has a swollen abdomen and puffy limbs despite appearing better nourished. Which of the following correctly differentiates kwashiorkor from marasmus?","o":["A) Marasmus is caused by inadequate protein intake, while kwashiorkor is caused by general starvation.","B) Marasmus is associated with edema due to low oncotic pressure, while kwashiorkor is not.","C) Kwashiorkor results from protein insufficiency despite adequate caloric intake, while marasmus results from inadequate intake of both protein and calories.","D) Kwashiorkor primarily leads to long-term stunted growth, while marasmus does not."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Kwashiorkor = protein lack with adequate calories; marasmus = lack of both protein and calories","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Kwashiorkor</b> = too little protein despite enough calories. <b>Marasmus</b> = too little protein <b>and</b> energy. So the wasted child has marasmus and the swollen child has kwashiorkor.<br>A. Reversed: marasmus is general starvation.<br>B. Reversed: edema from low oncotic pressure is kwashiorkor.<br>D. Reversed: stunted growth is the long-term effect of marasmus; kwashiorkor leaves poor immunity and mental development problems.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":105,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F1201-104-1","s":"Difference b/t Kwashiorkor & Marasmus?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Marasmus = inadequate intake of both protein and energy (general starvation): prominent bones, decreased subcutaneous fat, loose skin. Kwashiorkor = insufficient protein with generally adequate calories, often precipitated by infection; plump appearance from edema due to loss of oncotic pressure (low albumin), prominent belly, itchy rash, xerosis, poor wound healing. Long-term: stunted growth, poor mental development and immune response.","a":null,"why":"<b>Marasmus</b> = too little <b>protein and energy</b>: wasted, prominent bones, little fat, loose skin, <b>no edema</b>; long term, stunted growth. <b>Kwashiorkor</b> = too little <b>protein</b> with enough calories, often triggered by infection: <b>edema from low albumin</b>, plump look, swollen belly, rash, dry skin (xerosis), poor wound healing; long term, poor immunity and mental development.<br>Common mix-up: the edema belongs to kwashiorkor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":104,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":112,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":112,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Marasmus: wasting, no distended belly","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-35.jpg","cap":"Marasmic child labeled: prominent bones, less subcutaneous fat, loose skin","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-101-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT a clinical presentation of a child diagnosed with Marasmus?","o":["A. Decrease in subcutaneous fat","B. Distended belly","C. Prominent bones","D. Loose skin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Distended belly","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Distended belly.</b> A swollen belly belongs to <b>kwashiorkor</b> (edema from low albumin). <b>Marasmus</b> is general starvation, so the child is wasted with no edema.<br>A. Less subcutaneous fat: a marasmus sign.<br>C. Prominent bones: a marasmus sign.<br>D. Loose skin: a marasmus sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":101,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":127,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":123,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":103,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-157-1","s":"5. A 7 year old child is found to have prominent bone, decrease in subcutaneous fat and loose skin. The child does not have a distended belly. Which of the following malnutrition diseases is the child most likely suffering from?","o":["A. Kwashiorkor","B. Goitre","C. Marasmus","D. Dry Beriberi","E. Rickets"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Marasmus","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Marasmus.</b> <b>Prominent bones, less subcutaneous fat and loose skin, with no swollen belly</b>, is marasmus: too little protein and energy.<br>A. Kwashiorkor: swollen belly from edema.<br>B. Goiter: enlarged thyroid, usually from iodine deficiency.<br>D. Dry beriberi: thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency with nerve damage.<br>E. Rickets: soft bones from vitamin D deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":157,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Kwashiorkor edema: low albumin, low oncotic pressure","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-35.jpg","cap":"Plump kwashiorkor look from edema: low albumin, lost oncotic pressure","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-135-1","s":"What causes the loss of oncotic pressure and edema in Kwashiorkor?","o":["A. Loss of sodium","B. Loss of water","C. Loss of albumin","D. Loss of calcium"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Loss of albumin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Loss of albumin.</b> Low protein intake lowers <b>albumin</b> synthesis. Albumin supplies most of plasma <b>oncotic pressure</b>, so fluid leaks into tissues, giving edema and the swollen belly.<br>A. Sodium: not the cause of kwashiorkor edema.<br>B. Water: the edema is extra fluid in tissues, not water loss.<br>D. Calcium: not linked to oncotic pressure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":135,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0621-125-1","s":"You encourage the patient to increase his protein intake, and inform him that if he does not, he may develop a more plump appearance. What is this condition known as, and what is responsible for the plump appearance?","o":["A. Kwashiorkor; edema from loss of oncotic pressure","B. Marasmus; general starvation","C. Kwashiorkor; inadequate intake of both protein and energy","D. Marasmus; insufficient protein intake"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Kwashiorkor; edema from loss of oncotic pressure","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Kwashiorkor; edema from loss of oncotic pressure.</b> Low protein intake lowers <b>albumin</b>, plasma oncotic pressure falls, and fluid leaks into tissues, giving the plump look.<br>B. Marasmus is general starvation; it causes wasting, not plumpness.<br>C. Kwashiorkor is right, but 'too little protein and energy' defines marasmus.<br>D. Marasmus is a lack of both protein and energy, not protein alone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":125,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3881-141-1","s":"Kwashiorkor’s [blank] appearance is a result of decreased [blank] while Marasmus is the inadequate intake of [blank].","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"plump; oncotic pressure (from albumin loss); protein and energy","a":null,"why":"Kwashiorkor's <b>plump</b> look is <b>edema</b>: low albumin lowers plasma <b>oncotic pressure</b>, so fluid leaks into tissues. Marasmus is inadequate intake of <b>both protein and energy</b> (general starvation).<br>Common mix-up: the kwashiorkor child looks well fed, but the fullness is fluid, not fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 35 (Protein-Energy Malnutrition: Marasmus vs Kwashiorkor)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":141,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Monounsaturated fat lowers LDL, raises HDL","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-40.jpg","cap":"Fat-type table: monounsaturated (olive oil) lowers LDL, raises HDL; saturated raises LDL","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F0114-17-1","s":"Which of the following is associated with lower plasma cholesterol and LDL, higher HDL and decreased risk of coronary heart disease?","o":["A. Butyric acid (contained in butter)","B. Lauric acid (contained in coconut oil)","C. Palmitic acid (contained in palm oil)","D. Stearic acid (contained in cocoa butter)","E. Oleic acid (contained in olive oil)"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Oleic acid (contained in olive oil)","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Oleic acid (olive oil).</b> Oleic acid is a <b>monounsaturated</b> fatty acid (18 carbons, one double bond). Monounsaturated fats <b>lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)</b>, linked to less coronary heart disease.<br>A. Butyric acid: saturated, 4 carbons.<br>B. Lauric acid: saturated, 12 carbons; coconut oil raises LDL.<br>C. Palmitic acid: saturated, 16 carbons; palm oil raises LDL.<br>D. Stearic acid: saturated, 18 carbons, no double bond.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)","L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Dr. Pap macronutrients interactive","sl":17,"f":"F0114"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"2025 IS macronutrients","sl":3,"f":"F0118"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"post-discussion carb digestion & macronutrients IS: macronutrient part only","sl":9,"f":"F0125"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 16 IS","sl":23,"f":"F0172"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":19,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 16 IA session","sl":21,"f":"F0140"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session 16 Fall 2024","sl":29,"f":"F2100"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1201-106-1","s":"Which are considered the “good fats” Saturated fats or Monounsaturated fats?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Monounsaturated fats — associated with lower plasma cholesterol and LDL, higher HDL and decreased risk of CHD (saturated fats raise cholesterol/LDL and CHD risk)","a":null,"why":"<b>Monounsaturated fats</b> (olive oil, canola oil, avocados, nuts) are the good fats: they <b>lower LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and raise HDL (high-density lipoprotein)</b>, linked to lower coronary heart disease risk. <b>Saturated fats</b> (meat, dairy, coconut and palm oil) raise LDL with little HDL effect.<br>Common mix-up: trans fats are the worst; they raise LDL and also lower HDL.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":106,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":114,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":114,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Essential fatty acids: linoleic and α-linolenic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-39.jpg","cap":"Essential fatty acids: linoleic (18:2, ω-6) and α-linolenic (18:3, ω-3)","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 39"},{"src":"sl/G028-45.jpg","cap":"Essential fatty acid intakes: ω-6 2–4 g/day, ω-3 0.2–0.4 g/day","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 45"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-104-1","s":"What are the two essential fatty acids found in the mediterranean diet:","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Linoleic acid (18:2, N-6/ω-6) and α-Linolenic acid (18:3, N-3/ω-3)","a":null,"why":"The two <b>essential fatty acids</b> are <b>linoleic acid</b> (18 carbons, 2 double bonds, omega-6) and <b>α-linolenic acid</b> (18 carbons, 3 double bonds, omega-3). Humans lack the enzymes that add double bonds beyond carbon 9, so both must come from food.<br>Common mix-up: oleic acid (olive oil) is healthy but not essential; arachidonic acid is made from linoleic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 41 (Polyunsaturated Fatty Acids: ω-6 vs. ω-3)","L32 Macronutrients slide 45 (Essential Fatty Acid Requirements)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":104,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":160,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"ω-6 lowers LDL and HDL; ω-3 lowers triglycerides","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-41.jpg","cap":"ω-6 lowers LDL and HDL; ω-3 lowers triglycerides, thrombosis, blood pressure","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G028-40.jpg","cap":"Lipid effects of saturated, monounsaturated, polyunsaturated and trans fats","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-143-1","s":"N-6 fatty acids (ω-6) are associated with:","o":["A. Lower serum triglycerides and decreased thrombosis","B. Lower plasma LDL and higher HDL","C. High plasma cholesterol and LDL","D. Elevated LDL and no increase HDL","E. Lower LDL and HDL"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Lower LDL and HDL","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Lower LDL and HDL.</b> Omega-6 (ω-6) fatty acids such as linoleic acid <b>lower both LDL (low-density lipoprotein) and HDL (high-density lipoprotein)</b>; too much relative to omega-3 may promote inflammation.<br>A. Lower triglycerides and less thrombosis: omega-3 fatty acids.<br>B. Lower LDL, higher HDL: monounsaturated fats.<br>C. High cholesterol and LDL: saturated fats.<br>D. Higher LDL with no HDL rise: saturated fats; trans fats also lower HDL.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 41 (Polyunsaturated Fatty Acids: ω-6 vs. ω-3)","L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)","L32 Macronutrients slide 42 (Trans Fats: Structure, Sources, and Health Risks)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":143,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1201-106-2","s":"Why are N-3 fatty acids (ω-3) better than N-6 fatty acids (ω-6)?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"ω-3 (α-linolenic, EPA, DHA) lowers serum triglycerides, decreases thrombosis, blood pressure and CHD with little effect on LDL or HDL, whereas ω-6 (linoleic) lowers both LDL and HDL","a":null,"why":"<b>Omega-3 (ω-3)</b> fatty acids (α-linolenic acid; EPA, eicosapentaenoic acid; DHA, docosahexaenoic acid) <b>lower triglycerides and reduce thrombosis, blood pressure and coronary heart disease</b>, with little effect on LDL (low-density lipoprotein) or HDL (high-density lipoprotein). <b>Omega-6 (ω-6)</b> fatty acids lower LDL but also lower protective HDL, and excess ω-6 promotes inflammation.<br>Common mix-up: aim for an ω-6 to ω-3 ratio of about 4 to 1 or lower.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 41 (Polyunsaturated Fatty Acids: ω-6 vs. ω-3)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":106,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":114,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":114,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Mediterranean diet: more monounsaturated fat","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-43.jpg","cap":"Bars: same 38% fat; Mediterranean mostly monounsaturated, Western mostly saturated","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G028-44.jpg","cap":"Resveratrol and ω-3 fatty acids as protective Mediterranean components","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F1010-15-1","s":"A typical Mediterranean diet has the same distribution of calories among carbohydrates, protein and fat as a typical Western diet. Which of the following best explains why the Mediterranean diet is considered healthier than the Western diet?","o":["A. It contains more monounsaturated fat","B. It contains lots of red wine","C. It contains lot of bread and butter","D. It contains more polyunsaturated fat","E. It contains more protein"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It contains more monounsaturated fat","a":"A","why":"<b>A. More monounsaturated fat.</b> Both diets get about 38% of calories from fat, 42% from carbohydrate and 20% from protein. The difference is fat <b>type</b>: Mediterranean fat is mostly <b>monounsaturated (olive oil)</b>; Western fat is mostly saturated (animal fat).<br>B. Red wine polyphenols help, but they are not the main reason.<br>C. Bread and butter add saturated fat.<br>D. Polyunsaturated fat is a small, similar share in both.<br>E. Protein is the same, 20%.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)","L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 16 quiz","sl":15,"f":"F1010"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":34,"f":"D006"}]},{"id":"F2303-107-1","s":"Which of the following statements best describes the fat composition in a typical Western diet compared to a Mediterranean diet?","o":["B) The Western diet emphasizes monounsaturated fatty acids from olive oil, while the Mediterranean diet contains a higher proportion of omega-6 fatty acids from nuts and seeds.","C) The Mediterranean diet is high in omega-3 fatty acids from fish and monounsaturated fats from olive oil, while the Western diet has a higher intake of saturated fats and trans fats from processed foods.","D) Both the Mediterranean and Western diets are equally high in omega-6 fatty acids from nuts and seeds but differ in their intake of monounsaturated fats."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mediterranean = omega-3 (fish) + monounsaturated (olive oil); Western = more saturated and trans fat","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>Mediterranean</b> diet gets its fat from <b>omega-3 fatty acids (fish)</b> and <b>monounsaturated fat (olive oil)</b>; the <b>Western</b> diet has more <b>saturated and trans fat</b> from animal fat and processed food. Total fat is the same, about 38% of calories.<br>B. Reversed: olive oil monounsaturated fat is the Mediterranean hallmark.<br>D. The diets differ in fat <b>type</b>: Mediterranean is mainly monounsaturated, Western mainly saturated.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)","L32 Macronutrients slide 40 (Dietary Fats: Saturated vs. Monounsaturated)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":107,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-128-1","s":"Which diet follows more closely the dietary guidelines for a prudent diet?","o":["A. Mediterranean diets which are typically high in saturated fats and trans fats","B. Mediterranean diets which are typically rich in PUFAs, monounsaturated fats, and polyphenols","C. Western diets which are typically high in saturated fats and trans fats","D. Western diets which are typically rich in monounsaturated fats and polyphenols"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Mediterranean diets which are typically rich in PUFAs, monounsaturated fats, and polyphenols","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Mediterranean diets, rich in polyunsaturated and monounsaturated fats and polyphenols.</b> A prudent diet swaps saturated and trans fats for <b>monounsaturated</b> (olive oil) and <b>polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs)</b> such as fish omega-3, plus polyphenols from red wine.<br>A. Mediterranean diets are <b>low</b> in saturated and trans fats.<br>C. Western diets are high in saturated and trans fats, which raise LDL (low-density lipoprotein); that is not prudent.<br>D. Western diets are not rich in monounsaturated fats and polyphenols.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)","L32 Macronutrients slide 44 (Key Protective Components of the Mediterranean Diet)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":128,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F1201-106-3","s":"What is mediterranean diet rich in?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Fish (ω-3 fatty acids), olive oil (monounsaturated fatty acids), nuts (ω-6 fatty acids) and red wine (polyphenols = antioxidants); same total fat as a Western diet but more monounsaturated fat","a":null,"why":"The <b>Mediterranean diet</b> is rich in <b>fish (omega-3 fatty acids)</b>, <b>olive oil (monounsaturated fat)</b>, <b>nuts (omega-6 fatty acids)</b> and <b>red wine (polyphenols such as resveratrol)</b>. It has the same total fat as a Western diet but much more monounsaturated and less saturated fat; it is linked to less cardiovascular disease and better insulin sensitivity.<br>Common mix-up: it is not a low-fat diet.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 43 (Mediterranean Diet: Fat Quality Matters)","L32 Macronutrients slide 44 (Key Protective Components of the Mediterranean Diet)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":106,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":114,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":114,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Resveratrol: anti-inflammatory red wine polyphenol","slides":[{"src":"sl/G028-44.jpg","cap":"Resveratrol structure; red wine polyphenol, antioxidant and anti-inflammatory","where":"2026 lecture: L32 Macronutrients, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-140-1","s":"What component of red wine is an anti-inflammatory?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Resveratrol (a polyphenol)","a":null,"why":"<b>Resveratrol</b> is the <b>polyphenol in red wine</b> with <b>antioxidant and anti-inflammatory</b> effects. It is one of the protective parts of the Mediterranean diet, alongside omega-3 fatty acids from fish, nuts and seeds.<br>Common mix-up: the benefit credited to red wine comes from its polyphenols, not from the alcohol.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 44 (Key Protective Components of the Mediterranean Diet)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":140,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F1013-104-2","s":"Which component of the mediterranean diet is known to have anti-inflammatory properties?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Resveratrol (a polyphenol in red wine)","a":null,"why":"<b>Resveratrol</b>, a <b>polyphenol in red wine</b>, has <b>antioxidant and anti-inflammatory</b> effects. Omega-3 fatty acids from fish, nuts and seeds add heart, nerve and retina protection.<br>Common mix-up: olive oil is the diet's monounsaturated fat source; the named anti-inflammatory polyphenol is resveratrol from red wine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 44 (Key Protective Components of the Mediterranean Diet)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":104,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":160,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Arginine → nitric oxide","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0035-156.jpg","cap":"TA slide: arginine makes nitric oxide; cysteine makes taurine and glutathione","where":"2020 TA review, slide 156"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-97-1","s":"Which amino acid is needed for the production of nitric oxide?","o":["A. Cysteine","B. Glutamine","C. Arginine","D. Tryptophan","E. Any amino acid may be used"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Arginine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Arginine.</b> <b>Nitric oxide (NO) is made from arginine</b> by nitric oxide synthase, which releases citrulline. NO causes vasodilation; arginine also stimulates immunity.<br>A. Cysteine: used for glutathione and for <b>taurine</b>.<br>B. Glutamine: the main fuel for small-intestine and immune cells.<br>D. Tryptophan: makes serotonin, melatonin and niacin.<br>E. Any amino acid: no; only arginine feeds nitric oxide synthase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":97,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":154,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"Cysteine → taurine","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0035-156.jpg","cap":"TA slide: cysteine used for glutathione and taurine, needed for bile salts","where":"2020 TA review, slide 156"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-129-1","s":"Which of the following AA is needed for taurine production?","o":["A. Arginine","B. Glutamine","C. Cysteine","D. Proline","E. Aspartate"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Cysteine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Cysteine.</b> <b>Taurine is made from cysteine.</b> Taurine conjugates bile acids to form bile salts. Cysteine also helps make glutathione (GSH), a detoxifier.<br>A. Arginine: makes nitric oxide and creatine.<br>B. Glutamine: fuel for enterocytes and immune cells.<br>D. Proline: a major part of collagen.<br>E. Aspartate: feeds the urea cycle and nucleotide synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":129,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":125,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":105,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L32","label":"GLUT2: liver and intestine glucose transporter","slides":[{"src":"sl/G045-20.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte: SGLT1 and GLUT5 on brush border, GLUT2 on basolateral side","where":"2026 lecture: L55 Carb & Protein Absorption, slide 20"},{"src":"sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Beta cell takes up glucose through GLUT2, without insulin","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-25-1","s":"Which transporter allows for glucose uptake from the liver and small intestine directly after a meal?","o":["A. GLUT 3","B. GLUT 4","C. GLUT 1","D. GLUT 2"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"GLUT 2","a":"D","why":"<b>D. GLUT 2.</b> <b>Glucose transporter 2 (GLUT2)</b> sits in the <b>liver</b>, on the <b>blood side of small-intestine cells</b>, and in pancreatic beta cells. It is high-capacity, insulin-independent and two-way, so it handles the glucose surge after a meal.<br>A. GLUT3: neurons; high affinity.<br>B. GLUT4: muscle and fat; needs insulin.<br>C. GLUT1: red blood cells and blood-brain barrier; basal uptake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L55 slide 19 (Absorption of Sugars)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":25,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":21,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin anabolic, glucagon catabolic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Tug-of-war: insulin's anabolic arrows versus glucagon and epinephrine's catabolic arrows","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-28-1","s":"4. True or False The metabolic effects of insulin are anabolic while the metabolic effects of glucagon are catabolic.","o":["A. TRUE","B. FALSE"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"TRUE","a":"A","why":"<b>True.</b> <b>Insulin is anabolic</b>: it builds glycogen, fat and protein. <b>Glucagon is catabolic</b>: it breaks down glycogen, fat and protein to raise blood glucose. They are counter-regulatory hormones that pull blood glucose in opposite directions.<br>Common mix-up: epinephrine and cortisol are catabolic too and side with glucagon; insulin is the only hormone that lowers blood glucose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 5 (Insulin and Glucagon are)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":28,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Islet cells: β insulin, α glucagon, δ somatostatin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-8.jpg","cap":"Islet cell legend: α 10–20%, β 60–80%, δ 5%, PP under 1%","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 8"},{"src":"sl/G029-27.jpg","cap":"Islet section: α cells glucagon, β cells insulin, δ cells somatostatin","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-5-1","s":"What Cells Make Somatostatin?","o":["A. Alpha cells","B. Beta Cells","C. Gamma Cells","D. Delta Cells","E. Theta Cells"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Delta Cells","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Delta (δ) cells</b> make <b>somatostatin</b>. It inhibits release of both insulin and glucagon.<br>A. Alpha cells: make glucagon.<br>B. Beta cells: make insulin.<br>C. Gamma cells: another name for PP (pancreatic polypeptide) cells; they make pancreatic polypeptide.<br>E. Theta cells: not a pancreatic cell type.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 8 (The Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":5,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":204,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F2303-33-1","s":"A histological examination of the pancreas reveals clusters of endocrine cells organized into islets of Langerhans. A specific hormone is identified, somatostatin, that inhibits the release of both insulin and glucagon. Which of the following pancreatic cell types is responsible for secreting this hormone?","o":["A. Alpha (α) cells","B. Beta (β) cells","C. Delta (δ) cells","D. PP (pancreatic polypeptide) cells","E. Acinar cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Delta (δ) cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Delta (δ) cells</b> make <b>somatostatin</b>, which inhibits release of both insulin and glucagon. Delta cells are about 5% of islet cells.<br>A. Alpha cells: make glucagon.<br>B. Beta cells: make insulin.<br>D. PP cells: make pancreatic polypeptide.<br>E. Acinar cells: exocrine; they make digestive enzymes, not hormones.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 8 (The Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":33,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F0035-65-1","s":"Name the site of production of the following pancreatic hormones: Insulin – Glucagon – Somatostatin","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Insulin – beta (β) cells; Glucagon – alpha (α) cells; Somatostatin – delta (δ) cells","a":null,"why":"<b>Insulin = beta (β) cells</b> (60–80% of the islet). <b>Glucagon = alpha (α) cells</b> (10–20%). <b>Somatostatin = delta (δ) cells</b> (about 5%). All sit in the islets of Langerhans, the endocrine pancreas.<br>Common mix-up: swapping alpha and beta. Beta cells are the big majority, and they make insulin, the main islet hormone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 8 (The Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":65,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F3881-33-1","s":"Which of the following correctly matches the pancreatic cell type with the hormone it secretes and its primary function?","o":["A. Beta cells - Glucagon - Stimulates gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis","B. Alpha cells - Insulin - Promotes glucose uptake and storage","C. Beta cells - Insulin - Lowers blood glucose levels by promoting glucose uptake","D. Alpha cells - Somatostatin - Inhibits digestive enzyme secretion","E. Beta cells - Somatostatin - Stimulates glycogen synthesis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Beta cells - Insulin - Lowers blood glucose levels by promoting glucose uptake","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Beta cells → insulin → lowers blood glucose</b> by promoting glucose uptake and storage.<br>A. Beta cells make insulin, not glucagon. Glucagon (from alpha cells) drives gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis.<br>B. Alpha cells make glucagon, not insulin.<br>D. Somatostatin comes from delta cells, not alpha cells.<br>E. Somatostatin comes from delta cells, and it inhibits hormone release; it doesn't build glycogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 8 (The Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":33,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin: 51 amino acids, two chains","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-10.jpg","cap":"Insulin A and B chains with disulfide bonds; proinsulin cut into insulin, C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-35-1","s":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the structure and synthesis of insulin?","o":["A. Insulin consists of 51 amino acids, organized into two polypeptide chains linked by two disulfide bonds.","B. The A chain of insulin contains no disulfide bonds.","C. C-peptide is not required for the proper folding of insulin.","D. Insulin synthesis does not involve any post-translational modifications.","E. Insulin is a single-chain polypeptide with no disulfide bonds."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Insulin consists of 51 amino acids, organized into two polypeptide chains linked by two disulfide bonds.","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Insulin = <b>51 amino acids in two chains (A and B) joined by two disulfide bonds</b>, plus one more disulfide bond inside the A chain.<br>B. False: the A chain has its own internal disulfide bond.<br>C. False: <b>C-peptide is needed for proper folding</b> of proinsulin.<br>D. False: it needs signal-sequence removal, disulfide bonds and cleavage of proinsulin.<br>E. False: insulin has two chains. Glucagon is the single-chain hormone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 10 (51 amino acids; 2 polypeptide chains linked  by 2 disulfide bonds; disulfide bon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":35,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin and C-peptide released together","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-11.jpg","cap":"Steps 5–7: Golgi cuts proinsulin; insulin and C-peptide share granules, exocytosed together","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Ca2+ entry triggers insulin granule exocytosis from the beta cell","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-39-1","s":"Which of the following describes the final step in insulin synthesis and secretion?","o":["A. Cleavage of proinsulin into insulin and glucagon in the Golgi complex","B. Cleavage of proinsulin into insulin and C-peptide, which are released via exocytosis","C. Conversion of preproinsulin to proinsulin in the RER","D. Secretion of insulin alone without any other byproducts"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cleavage of proinsulin into insulin and C-peptide, released together by exocytosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Final step: <b>proinsulin is cleaved into insulin + C-peptide</b> (starting in the Golgi). Both are stored in granules and <b>released together by exocytosis</b>.<br>A. Proinsulin never yields glucagon. Glucagon is cut from proglucagon in alpha cells.<br>C. Preproinsulin → proinsulin in the RER (rough endoplasmic reticulum) is an earlier step.<br>D. Insulin is always released with an equal amount of C-peptide.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 11 (Synthesis of Insulin)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":39,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-67-1","s":"Which of the following statements about insulin is true?","o":["A. Insulin consists 4 polypeptide chains linked  by 3 disulfide bonds","B. C- peptide and insulin are co-secreted from the same vesicles","C. Increased intracellular Ca2+ inhibits insulin secretion","D. Glucose in GI tract causes the release of incretins which  inhibit  insulin release"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"C- peptide and insulin are co-secreted from the same vesicles","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Proinsulin is cut into insulin and <b>C-peptide</b>. Both sit in the <b>same secretory granules</b> and are released together, one to one.<br>A. Insulin has two chains linked by two disulfide bonds, not four chains.<br>C. A rise in intracellular Ca2+ (calcium) <b>triggers</b> vesicle fusion and insulin release.<br>D. Gut glucose releases incretins, GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) and GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1), which <b>boost</b> insulin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 10 (51 amino acids; 2 polypeptide chains linked  by 2 disulfide bonds; disulfide bon)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 14 (Observation: Oral glucose stimulates insulin release sooner than IV glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":67,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Signal sequence → RER → proinsulin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-11.jpg","cap":"Signal sequence steers ribosomes to RER; cleaved there to give proinsulin","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G029-10.jpg","cap":"Preproinsulin loses signal sequence in ER, then proinsulin cut in Golgi","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-35-1","s":"What is the primary function of the N-terminal signal sequence during insulin synthesis?","o":["A. To bind mRNA to the ribosome in the cytoplasm","B. To direct the mRNA-ribosome complex to the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER)","C. To convert preproinsulin into proinsulin in the Golgi","D. To facilitate the secretion of insulin from the cell"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"To direct the mRNA-ribosome complex to the RER","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The <b>N-terminal hydrophobic signal sequence</b> is made first on cytosolic ribosomes. It steers the mRNA (messenger RNA)–ribosome complex to the <b>RER (rough endoplasmic reticulum)</b>.<br>A. Ribosomes bind mRNA without any signal sequence.<br>C. Preproinsulin becomes proinsulin in the RER lumen, not the Golgi.<br>D. Secretion is exocytosis of granules triggered by Ca2+; the signal sequence was removed long before.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 11 (Synthesis of Insulin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":35,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2303-37-1","s":"At which stage in insulin synthesis is the signal sequence cleaved, and what molecule is produced?","o":["A. After translation, producing insulin directly","B. Upon entry into the Golgi, producing proinsulin","C. After preproinsulin enters the RER, producing proinsulin","D. During packaging into secretory granules, producing insulin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"After preproinsulin enters the RER, producing proinsulin","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Once preproinsulin enters the lumen of the <b>RER (rough endoplasmic reticulum)</b>, the <b>signal sequence is cleaved, leaving proinsulin</b>.<br>A. Translation gives preproinsulin, not insulin.<br>B. In the Golgi, proinsulin is cut into insulin plus C-peptide; the signal sequence is already gone.<br>D. Granule packaging comes later and doesn't remove the signal sequence.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 11 (Synthesis of Insulin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":37,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F3881-37-1","s":"Which of the following correctly describes a step in the synthesis and processing of insulin as illustrated in the image?","o":["A. Insulin is transcribed in the cytoplasm and directly translated on free ribosomes.","B. Preproinsulin is synthesized on ribosomes and directed into the rough endoplasmic reticulum by an N-terminal signal sequence.","C. The signal sequence is cleaved in the Golgi apparatus to form active insulin.","D. Insulin and C-peptide are packaged together in vesicles immediately after transcription.","E. Proinsulin is cleaved in the rough endoplasmic reticulum to form insulin and C-peptide."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Preproinsulin is synthesized on ribosomes and directed into the rough endoplasmic reticulum by an N-terminal signal sequence.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Preproinsulin is made on ribosomes, and its <b>N-terminal signal sequence</b> directs it into the <b>RER (rough endoplasmic reticulum)</b>.<br>A. The gene is transcribed in the nucleus, and translation finishes on RER-bound ribosomes, not free ones.<br>C. The signal sequence is cut in the RER, and the product is proinsulin, not active insulin.<br>D. Packaging into granules happens after Golgi processing, long after transcription.<br>E. Proinsulin is cut into insulin and C-peptide in the Golgi, not the RER.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Referenced insulin-synthesis figure is not in the text dump (blank page 39); not needed to answer","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 11 (Synthesis of Insulin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":37,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Sulfonylureas close beta-cell K-ATP channels","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Sulfonylureas block the beta-cell K-ATP channel, causing depolarization and insulin release","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"},{"src":"sl/G029-13.jpg","cap":"Tolbutamide and glibenclamide (sulfonylureas) listed as initiators of insulin secretion","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0088-11-1","s":"A 57-year-old man with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus, presents to doctor's office for routine screening. His serum glucose is 260 mg/dL. His doctor decided to start the patient on a medication that triggers insulin release. What is the mechanism of action of this drug?","o":["A. Closes potassium channels in β-cells","B. Increases insulin sensitivity in myocytes","C. Inhibits intestinal brush border enzymes","D. Delays gastric emptying","E. Decreases ATP generation in pancreas"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Closes potassium channels in β-cells","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> The drug is a <b>sulfonylurea</b> (for example tolbutamide, glibenclamide). It <b>closes ATP (adenosine triphosphate)-sensitive K+ (potassium) channels</b> in beta cells → the cell depolarizes → voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open → insulin is released.<br>B. Raising insulin sensitivity is how thiazolidinediones work; it doesn't release insulin.<br>C. Blocking brush-border enzymes (acarbose) slows carbohydrate absorption.<br>D. Slowing gastric emptying is a GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) agonist effect.<br>E. Less ATP would open K+ channels and stop insulin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":11,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":10,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":10,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":13,"f":"F2030"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":1,"f":"F0997"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Beta cell: ATP closes K+, Ca2+ releases insulin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-12.jpg","cap":"Beta cell: GLUT2, glucokinase, ATP closes K-ATP, Ca2+ enters, insulin released","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-9-1","s":"You are sitting in Room 238 and someone asks you to explain to them the mechanism of how insulin is secreted from the pancreatic cells. You correctly respond:","o":["A. Glucose levels go up-glycolysis yields ATP- ATP closes K+ causing Ca+2 channels to open- Increased Ca+2 promotes insulin release","B. Increased glucose levels cause Glut 4 to migrate to the surface and promotes glycolysis which which creates energy to fuse insulin to the surface of the cell where it can be released.","C. Glucose levels go up-glycolysis yields ATP- ATP closes K+ causing Ca+2 channels to close- decreased Ca+2 promotes insulin release","D. Na+ channels cause Ca+2 channels to open which promotes the release of insulin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose levels go up-glycolysis yields ATP- ATP closes K+ causing Ca+2 channels to open- Increased Ca+2 promotes insulin release","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Glucose rises → glycolysis makes ATP (adenosine triphosphate) → ATP <b>closes K+ channels</b> → <b>Ca2+ channels open</b> → more Ca2+ inside → insulin release.<br>B. GLUT4 moving to the surface is insulin's effect on muscle and fat. Beta cells use GLUT2 (glucose transporter 2).<br>C. Ca2+ channels open, and rising (not falling) Ca2+ triggers release.<br>D. The trigger is K+ channel closure, not Na+ (sodium) channels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":9,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":208,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F3881-40-1","s":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which glucose stimulates insulin secretion in pancreatic β-cells?","o":["A. Glucose enters β-cells through GLUT4 transporters, leading to ATP production and K+ channel opening.","B. ATP generated from glucose metabolism closes K+ channels, causing depolarization and opening of Ca2+ channels.","C. GLUT2 transporters mediate glucose entry, which directly triggers insulin release without involving ATP or ion channels.","D. Increased intracellular Ca2+ reduces ATP production, inhibiting insulin secretion.","E. K+ channel closure decreases intracellular Ca2+, which promotes insulin secretion."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"ATP generated from glucose metabolism closes K+ channels, causing depolarization and opening of Ca2+ channels.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Glucose enters by GLUT2 (glucose transporter 2) and is metabolized. The <b>ATP (adenosine triphosphate) closes K+ channels</b> → depolarization → <b>Ca2+ channels open</b> → insulin exocytosis.<br>A. Beta cells use GLUT2, and ATP closes (not opens) K+ channels.<br>C. Glucose entry alone releases nothing; ATP and the channels are the trigger.<br>D. A rise in Ca2+ promotes, not inhibits, insulin secretion.<br>E. K+ channel closure raises Ca2+ through voltage-gated channels.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":40,"f":"F3881"}]},{"id":"F2303-51-1","s":"Which of the following accurately describes the mechanism of insulin secretion from beta cells in the pancreas?","o":["A. The alpha cells express GLUT4, which causes K+ channels to close and Ca2+ channels to open, leading to insulin release","B. The beta cells express GLUT4, which causes K+ channels to close and Ca2+ channels to open, leading to insulin release.","C. The alpha cells express GLUT2, which causes K+ channels to close and Ca2+ channels to open, leading to insulin release.","D. The beta cells express GLUT2, which causes K+ channels to close and Ca2+ channels to open, leading to insulin release."],"g":["D"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Source key: A (alpha cells, GLUT4)","a":"A","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Beta cells</b> take up glucose by <b>GLUT2</b> (glucose transporter 2). Its metabolism makes ATP (adenosine triphosphate), which <b>closes K+ channels</b>; the cell depolarizes, Ca2+ channels open, and insulin is released.<br>A. Wrong cell and wrong transporter: alpha cells make glucagon, and GLUT4 is the insulin-regulated transporter of muscle and fat.<br>B. Beta cells use GLUT2, not GLUT4.<br>C. Alpha cells make glucagon, not insulin.","ex":"","fl":["Insulin is made by beta cells, which take up glucose by GLUT2; glucose metabolism makes ATP, which closes K+ channels, and then Ca2+ channels open (G029:12). Option A (alpha cells, GLUT4) is wrong on both points. The 2023 version of this question (F0998 slide 29) keys the beta-cell GLUT2 option."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":51,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-7-1","s":"Q3. After a large carbohydrate meal, high glucose serves as a signal to secrete insulin into the blood. Which of the following is the correct pathway?","o":["A. Glucose enters pancreatic cell via GLUT4 transporter → metabolized via glycolysis & TCA → ↑ ATP opens K+ channels → ↓ [intracellular K+] leads to membrane depolarization → opens voltage gated Ca2+ channel → ↑ [intracellular Ca2+ ] → secretory vesicles of insulin fuse with plasma membrane → secretion of insulin.","B. Glucose enters pancreatic cell via GLUT2 transporter → metabolized via glycolysis & TCA → ↑ ATP opens K+ channels → ↓ [intracellular K+] leads to membrane depolarization → opens voltage gated Ca2+ channel → ↑ [intracellular Ca2+ ] → secretory vesicles of insulin fuse with plasma membrane → secretion of insulin.","C. Glucose enters pancreatic cell via GLUT4 transporter → metabolized via glycolysis & TCA → ↑ ATP closes K+ channels → ↑ [intracellular K+] leads to membrane depolarization → opens voltage gated Ca2+ channel → ↑ [intracellular Ca2+ ] → secretory vesicles of insulin fuse with plasma membrane → secretion of insulin.","D. Glucose enters pancreatic cell via GLUT2 transporter → metabolized via glycolysis & TCA → ↑ ATP closes K+ channels → ↑ [intracellular K+] leads to membrane depolarization → opens voltage gated Ca2+ channel → ↑ [intracellular Ca2+ ] → secretory vesicles of insulin fuse with plasma membrane → secretion of insulin."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose enters pancreatic cell via GLUT2 transporter → metabolized via glycolysis & TCA → ↑ ATP closes K+ channels → ↑ [intracellular K+] leads to membrane depolarization → opens voltage gated Ca2+ channel → ↑ [intracellular Ca2+ ] → secretory vesicles of insulin fuse with plasma membrane → secretion of insulin.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>GLUT2</b> (glucose transporter 2) → glycolysis and TCA (tricarboxylic acid) cycle → more ATP (adenosine triphosphate) <b>closes K+ channels</b> → K+ stays inside → depolarization → voltage-gated Ca2+ channels open → granules fuse → insulin out.<br>A. Two errors: beta cells use GLUT2, and ATP closes K+ channels.<br>B. Right transporter, but ATP closes K+ channels. Open channels would let K+ out and stop depolarization.<br>C. Right channel steps, but beta cells use GLUT2, not GLUT4.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":7,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"GLUT2 insulin-independent liver; GLUT4 fat/muscle","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-18.jpg","cap":"Insulin's protein kinase B branch increases glucose uptake by GLUT4","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/F0577-38.jpg","cap":"GLUT table: GLUT2 liver and beta cells, insulin-insensitive; GLUT4 fat and muscle","where":"2022 TA review, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/F0106-6.jpg","cap":"Fed liver: insulin-independent GLUT2 uptake driven by rising blood glucose","where":"2024 discussion, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F0997-5-1","s":"Which of the following glucose transporters is matched with the appropriate organ, is insulin-dependent, and most active in the fed state?","o":["A. Intestine: GLUT-3","B. Brain: GLUT-2","C. Muscle: GLUT-3","D. Adipose: GLUT-4","E. Liver: GLUT-4"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Adipose: GLUT-4","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Adipose: GLUT4</b> (glucose transporter 4) is the <b>insulin-dependent</b> transporter of fat and muscle. Insulin moves it to the cell membrane after a meal.<br>A. The intestine uses SGLT1 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 1), GLUT2 and GLUT5, not GLUT3.<br>B. The brain uses GLUT1 and GLUT3, not GLUT2.<br>C. Muscle uses GLUT4, not GLUT3.<br>E. The liver uses GLUT2, which is insulin-independent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":5,"f":"F0997"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0028-92-1","s":"Choose the correct answer","o":["A. Fat is the preferred energy source for the brain.","B. Fat metabolism yields more ATP than glucose, and therefore it is the preferred energy source throughout the body.","C. Skeletal muscle uses branched-chain amino acids for energy.","D. Glucose is taken up by the liver regardless of insulin levels."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose is taken up by the liver regardless of insulin levels.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> The liver takes up glucose by <b>GLUT2</b> (glucose transporter 2), which is <b>insulin-independent</b>. Uptake is driven by the rise in blood glucose itself.<br>A. The brain prefers glucose; fatty acids barely cross the blood-brain barrier.<br>B. The brain and red cells need glucose, so fat can't be everyone's fuel despite its higher ATP (adenosine triphosphate) yield.<br>C. Muscle does break down some branched-chain amino acids, but they are a minor fuel. Its main fuels are glucose (fed) and fatty acids and ketone bodies (fasting).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 12 (Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":92,"f":"F0028"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Somatostatin inhibits insulin secretion","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-13.jpg","cap":"Secretion wheel: inhibitors somatostatin, galanin, alpha-2 agonists such as epinephrine","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F1218-49-3","s":"What are some examples of inhibitors of insulin?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Somatostatin, galanin, alpha2-adrenergic agonists","a":null,"why":"<b>Inhibitors</b> of insulin secretion: <b>somatostatin</b> (from delta cells), <b>galanin</b>, and <b>alpha-2-adrenergic agonists such as epinephrine</b>. This is why stress lowers insulin.<br>Common mix-up: epinephrine is not a potentiator. It raises blood glucose partly by blocking insulin release through alpha-2 receptors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":49,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F1013-22-1","s":"Which of the following is NOT an initiator of insulin secretion?","o":["A. Glucose","B. Leucine","C. Somatostatin","D. Glyceraldehyde"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Somatostatin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Somatostatin</b> (from delta cells) is an <b>inhibitor</b> of insulin secretion, not an initiator.<br>A. Glucose: the most important initiator.<br>B. Leucine: an initiator; the beta cell metabolizes it.<br>D. Glyceraldehyde: an initiator; it enters glycolysis and makes ATP (adenosine triphosphate).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 8 (The Pancreas)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":22,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Glucose is the main insulin initiator","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-13.jpg","cap":"Initiators glucose, glyceraldehyde, leucine, sulfonylureas; 'glucose is the most important initiator'","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F1218-49-1","s":"What is the most potent initiator of insulin release?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Glucose (the most important initiator)","a":null,"why":"<b>Glucose</b> is the <b>most important initiator</b> of insulin release. Other initiators: glyceraldehyde, leucine, ketoisocaproate, and sulfonylurea drugs.<br>Common mix-up: the incretins GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) are potentiators. They boost release only when glucose is present.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":49,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F2303-42-1","s":"A 45-year-old male presents for evaluation of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus. He admits to dietary non-compliance and limited physical activity. You discuss the role of glucose in insulin secretion. Laboratory findings reveal normal pancreatic beta-cell function. Which of the following is the most critical initiator of insulin secretion in this patient?","o":["A. Acetylcholine","B. Glucose","C. Leucine","D. Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1)","E. Tolbutamide"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Glucose</b> is the <b>most important initiator</b> of insulin secretion.<br>A. Acetylcholine: a potentiator; it boosts release but doesn't start it.<br>C. Leucine: an initiator, but a minor one.<br>D. GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1): an incretin potentiator, released when glucose is in the gut.<br>E. Tolbutamide: a sulfonylurea drug that initiates release, not the body's own main signal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":42,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin release: protein meal yes, stress no","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-13.jpg","cap":"Leucine initiates and arginine potentiates insulin release; stress inhibits it","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G029-27.jpg","cap":"Insulin inhibits glucagon release; alpha cells receive high local insulin","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G029-14.jpg","cap":"IV glucose still raises plasma insulin, just less and later than oral","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-72-1","s":"Choose the correct answer","o":["A. Insulin is released in response to stress.","B. Insulin is secreted when a hypoglycemic patient is given IV glucose in the ED.","C. Insulin directly inhibits glucagon release.","D. Insulin is released in response to eating a meal high in protein."],"g":["B","C","D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Insulin is released in response to eating a meal high in protein.","a":"D","why":"<b>B, C and D are true; only A is false.</b> <b>Stress inhibits insulin secretion</b>: epinephrine acts on alpha-2 receptors on beta cells.<br>A. False: stress lowers insulin release.<br>B. True: IV (intravenous) glucose still raises insulin. The rise is just <b>smaller and later</b> than with oral glucose, because no incretins are released.<br>C. True: insulin <b>inhibits glucagon release</b>; alpha cells get high local insulin.<br>D. True: amino acids from protein release insulin (leucine initiates, arginine potentiates).","ex":"","fl":["Not the only true statement: C ('Insulin directly inhibits glucagon release') is also true - the 2026 deck lists insulin as an inhibitor of glucagon release (G029:27) - and B is arguably true (IV glucose still triggers insulin secretion, only less than oral glucose, G029:14). D is keyed.","The source keyed only D; insulin also directly inhibits glucagon release (G029:27) and IV glucose still triggers insulin secretion (G029:14 graph), so B, C and D are all true and only A is false."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 27 (Stimulation of Release)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":72,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin potentiators: incretins, acetylcholine, arginine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-13.jpg","cap":"Potentiator arc: acetylcholine, arginine, glucagon, GLP, GIP, VIP","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F1218-49-2","s":"What are some examples of potentiators of insulin?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Acetylcholine, arginine, glucagon, GIP, VIP (potentiators don't initiate secretion but enhance the effects of initiators)","a":null,"why":"<b>Potentiators</b> boost insulin release but don't start it: <b>acetylcholine, arginine, glucagon, GLP-1</b> (glucagon-like peptide-1), <b>GIP</b> (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) and <b>VIP</b> (vasoactive intestinal peptide).<br>Common mix-up: glucagon opposes insulin's effects, yet on the beta cell it is a potentiator. Leucine and glyceraldehyde are initiators, not potentiators.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)"],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":49,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Carbohydrate snack → insulin rises, glucagon falls","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-4.jpg","cap":"High blood glucose → beta cells release insulin → cells take up glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 4"},{"src":"sl/G029-14.jpg","cap":"Plasma insulin rises fast after oral glucose; GIP and GLP-1 boost it","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F0106-3-1","s":"Which hormone(s) will spike after consumption of the chips?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Insulin (potentiated by the incretins GIP and GLP-1 from the gut); glucagon falls","a":null,"why":"Chips are a <b>carbohydrate (plus fat) load</b>, so blood glucose rises and <b>insulin spikes</b>. Glucose in the gut also releases the incretins <b>GIP</b> (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) and <b>GLP-1</b> (glucagon-like peptide-1), which boost the insulin rise. Glucose and insulin both <b>suppress glucagon</b>.<br>Common mix-up: glucagon doesn't spike after a carbohydrate meal; it rises in fasting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 14 (Observation: Oral glucose stimulates insulin release sooner than IV glucose)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 13 (Regulation of Insulin Secretion)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 discussion","n":"W15 discussion","sl":3,"f":"F0106"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Oral glucose → incretins → more insulin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-14.jpg","cap":"Graph: insulin higher and earlier after oral than IV glucose; GIP, GLP-1 incretins","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F1218-51-1","s":"Why is oral glucose better for insulin release than IV glucose?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Oral glucose stimulates the release of incretins (GIP & GLP-1) which increase insulin secretion","a":null,"why":"Only <b>oral glucose</b> passes through the gut, where it releases the <b>incretins GIP</b> (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) <b>and GLP-1</b> (glucagon-like peptide-1) from small-intestine endocrine cells. They potentiate insulin release, so insulin rises <b>sooner and higher</b> than with IV (intravenous) glucose.<br>Common mix-up: IV glucose still raises insulin; the rise is just smaller and slower.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 14 (Observation: Oral glucose stimulates insulin release sooner than IV glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":51,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0028-88-1","s":"How does oral and IV glucose differ physiologically?","o":["A. Oral glucose is more easily converted to fat and stored in adipose tissue.","B. Oral glucose is broken down primarily in the mouth, whereas IV glucose is broken down elsewhere.","C. IV glucose stimulates incretins such as GLP which lead to insulin release.","D. Oral glucose stimulates incretins such as GLP which lead to insulin release.","E. Patients given IV glucose will have elevated C-peptide in their serum."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Oral glucose stimulates incretins such as GLP which lead to insulin release.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Only <b>oral glucose</b> releases <b>incretins</b> such as GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) and GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide), so insulin rises sooner and higher.<br>A. Whether glucose is stored as fat doesn't depend on the route.<br>B. Glucose isn't broken down in the mouth; salivary amylase digests starch.<br>C. IV (intravenous) glucose bypasses the gut, so it releases no incretins.<br>E. Both routes raise C-peptide along with insulin, so it is not a difference.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 14 (Observation: Oral glucose stimulates insulin release sooner than IV glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":88,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F2303-44-1","s":"A study is conducted to compare plasma insulin levels in response to oral glucose administration versus intravenous glucose infusion. The results demonstrate a significantly greater rise in plasma insulin following oral glucose administration. Which of the following best explains this observation?","o":["A. Oral glucose is absorbed more rapidly than intravenous glucose.","B. Oral glucose triggers glucagon release, which stimulates insulin secretion.","C. Glucose in the gastrointestinal tract stimulates the release of incretins, which enhance insulin secretion.","D. Intravenous glucose bypasses the pancreas, limiting insulin secretion.","E. Oral glucose leads to slower metabolism of glucose, allowing sustained insulin release."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose in the GI tract releases incretins (GIP, GLP-1) that enhance insulin secretion","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Glucose in the gut makes intestinal endocrine cells release <b>incretins</b>, GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) and GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1). They <b>potentiate insulin release</b>.<br>A. IV (intravenous) glucose enters blood at once; oral glucose isn't faster.<br>B. Glucose suppresses glucagon release.<br>D. IV glucose reaches the pancreas fine; it just lacks the incretin boost.<br>E. Glucose metabolism isn't slower; the gut hormones explain the difference.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 14 (Observation: Oral glucose stimulates insulin release sooner than IV glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":44,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F3881-42-1","s":"A 45-year-old patient with type 2 diabetes undergoes an oral glucose tolerance test. The physician notes that oral glucose administration stimulates a greater and faster insulin release compared to intravenous glucose. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this observation?","o":["A. GLUT2 transporters in the pancreas are more active following oral glucose administration.","B. Oral glucose stimulates the release of incretin hormones, such as GIP and GLP-1, from the small intestine epithelium.","C. Oral glucose bypasses the liver, leading to direct stimulation of insulin secretion by pancreatic β-cells.","D. Intravenous glucose suppresses the activity of Ca2+ channels in pancreatic β-cells, reducing insulin secretion.","E. Oral glucose directly depolarizes pancreatic β-cells, enhancing insulin secretion independently of incretins."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Oral glucose stimulates the release of incretin hormones, such as GIP and GLP-1, from the small intestine epithelium.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Oral glucose releases the <b>incretins GIP</b> (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) <b>and GLP-1</b> (glucagon-like peptide-1) from endocrine cells of the <b>small-intestine epithelium</b>. They boost insulin release.<br>A. Beta-cell GLUT2 (glucose transporter 2) works the same with either route.<br>C. Oral glucose goes through the portal vein to the liver; it doesn't bypass it.<br>D. IV (intravenous) glucose doesn't block Ca2+ channels.<br>E. Glucose acts on beta cells the same way by either route; the extra insulin needs incretins.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 14 (Observation: Oral glucose stimulates insulin release sooner than IV glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":42,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1201-11-1","s":"EMT’S are taught to give glucose orally when given the chance as opposed to giving it intravenously. Why?","o":["A. Incretins decrease insulin release so blood glucose levels remain higher for longer","B. GLP-1 increases insulin so it will drive glucose into cells faster","C. GIP will increase renal clearance so the glucose will be eliminated from the body faster","D. Incretins Decrease renal clearance so glucose will stay elevated","E. Somatostatin will decrease the uptake of insulin by alpha cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"GLP-1 increases insulin so it will drive glucose into cells faster","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Oral glucose releases <b>GLP-1</b> (glucagon-like peptide-1), an incretin that <b>increases insulin release</b>, so glucose is driven into cells faster.<br>A. Incretins increase, not decrease, insulin release.<br>C. GIP (glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) boosts insulin; it doesn't change renal clearance.<br>D. Incretins don't act on renal clearance.<br>E. Somatostatin inhibits insulin release; alpha cells don't take up insulin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 14 (Observation: Oral glucose stimulates insulin release sooner than IV glucose)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":11,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":210,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Low C-peptide = injected (exogenous) insulin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-23.jpg","cap":"Red line: insulin preparations contain only mature insulin, no C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 23"},{"src":"sl/G029-11.jpg","cap":"Own beta cells release insulin and C-peptide together from the same granules","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-23-1","s":"A 24 year old diabetic male presents to the ED, on arrival he is diaphoretic, fatigued, and pale. His pulse oximeter reads HR 120, SpO2 99%. You suspect hypoglycemia due to an insulin overdose, which marker in the blood would support this?","o":["A. High C-peptide","B. Low C-peptide"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Low C-peptide","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Low C-peptide.</b> Insulin preparations contain <b>only mature insulin, no C-peptide</b>. The injected insulin also suppresses his own beta cells, so C-peptide falls while insulin is high.<br>A. High C-peptide means his own beta cells are secreting: insulinoma or a sulfonylurea.<br>Low C-peptide alone also occurs in starvation; <b>high insulin with low C-peptide</b> is what proves exogenous insulin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":23,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":19,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F1201-7-1","s":"A 42 year old male patient was brought to the emergency room with a glucose level of 37. He was found unconscious on the street. The physician suspects an exogenous insulin overdose. What lab value would confirm his suspicion","o":["A. Blood glucose Levels below 60","B. High glucagon Level","C. High C-Peptide level","D. Low C-Peptide Level","E. Increased S-Protein"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Low C-Peptide Level","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Low C-peptide</b> with hypoglycemia confirms <b>exogenous insulin</b>. Preparations contain no C-peptide, and the injected insulin suppresses his own beta cells.<br>A. Glucose below 60 only restates the hypoglycemia; it doesn't show the cause.<br>B. Glucagon rises in any hypoglycemia.<br>C. High C-peptide means the body's own insulin (insulinoma, sulfonylurea).<br>E. 'S-protein' has no role in insulin processing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":7,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":206,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F1218-51-2","s":"How do you know if a patient’s hyperinsulinemia is because of endogenous or exogenous source?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Endogenous insulin secretion will have high levels of BOTH insulin & C-peptide; high insulin with low C-peptide = EXOGENOUS","a":null,"why":"Beta cells release insulin and <b>C-peptide in equal amounts</b>, so <b>endogenous</b> excess (insulinoma, sulfonylurea drugs) raises <b>both</b>. Insulin preparations contain <b>no C-peptide</b>, so <b>exogenous</b> insulin gives <b>high insulin with low C-peptide</b>.<br>Common mix-up: a sulfonylurea overdose looks like insulinoma (both high), not like an insulin injection.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":51,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F2303-47-1","s":"A 12-year-old child is brought to the emergency department after being found with an empty bottle of pills at school. The child reports being bullied and feeling overwhelmed. Laboratory results show a blood glucose level of 45 mg/dL (normal range: 70–100 mg/dL) and a C-peptide level of 0.2 ng/mL (normal range: 0.5–2.0 ng/mL). Which of the following is the most likely cause of this child’s hypoglycemia?","o":["A. Insulin overdose from exogenous insulin administration","B. Insulinoma causing excessive endogenous insulin production","C. Adrenal insufficiency leading to impaired cortisol secretion","D. Type 1 diabetes with inadequate insulin dosing","E. Starvation-induced hypoglycemia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Exogenous insulin overdose (hypoglycemia with low C-peptide)","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Hypoglycemia + low C-peptide = exogenous insulin.</b> Injected insulin contains <b>no C-peptide</b> and shuts down the child's own beta cells, so C-peptide falls. (Insulin is injected, so the 'pills' wording is loose.)<br>B. Insulinoma: high insulin and <b>high</b> C-peptide.<br>C. Adrenal insufficiency: C-peptide is low too, but nothing points to it.<br>D. Too little insulin in type 1 diabetes causes hyperglycemia.<br>E. Starvation: low C-peptide too, but there is no fasting story; the overdose scene fits A.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 24 (Question)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":47,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F0088-17-1","s":"A medical examiner was called to investigate the death of a 75-year-old type 1 diabetic Caucasian male who was a retired physician. His caretaker discovered his body in the bedroom with an empty syringe and a small bottle of lispro lying on the nightstand. She explains that his wife of 50 years passed away six months ago and that he had no children or family. He had become extremely depressed and did not want to live anymore. Which of the following values would be most consistent with his blood chemistries if a blood sample was taken?","o":["A. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and high C-peptide levels","B. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and normal C-peptide levels","C. Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and absent C-peptide levels","D. Glucose: 95 mg/dL, low insulin and low C-peptide levels","E. Glucose: 95 mg/dL, high insulin and C-peptide levels"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose: 25 mg/dL, high insulin and absent C-peptide levels","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A lispro overdose gives <b>hypoglycemia (25 mg/dL), high insulin and absent C-peptide</b>. Insulin preparations contain <b>no C-peptide</b>, and his type 1 diabetes leaves no beta cells to make any.<br>A. High C-peptide means the body's own insulin (insulinoma, sulfonylurea).<br>B. Normal C-peptide doesn't fit a type 1 diabetic.<br>D. Glucose 95 mg/dL is normal; an overdose drops glucose.<br>E. Normal glucose with high C-peptide doesn't fit an insulin overdose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":17,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":11,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":15,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":20,"f":"F2030"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":3,"f":"F0997"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulinoma: high insulin and high C-peptide","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-15.jpg","cap":"Insulinoma: unregulated insulin secretion, hypoglycemia, elevated C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G029-34.jpg","cap":"Fasting hypoglycemia causes include insulinoma (increased glucose use)","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-44-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman presents with recurrent episodes of confusion, sweating, and palpitations, especially after prolonged fasting. Blood tests during an episode reveal hypoglycemia, elevated insulin levels, and elevated C-peptide levels. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Type 1 diabetes mellitus","B. Insulinoma","C. Factitious hypoglycemia due to exogenous insulin use","D. Glucagonoma","E. Addison's disease"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Insulinoma","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Insulinoma</b>: a neuroendocrine tumor of beta cells with <b>unregulated insulin secretion</b>. It causes fasting hypoglycemia with <b>high insulin and high C-peptide</b>.<br>A. Type 1 diabetes: low insulin and C-peptide, with hyperglycemia.<br>C. Injected insulin: high insulin but <b>low</b> C-peptide.<br>D. Glucagonoma: excess glucagon causes hyperglycemia.<br>E. Addison's disease (adrenal insufficiency): hypoglycemia with low insulin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":44,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-3-1","s":"Q1. A 35 year old female was found unresponsive in her room. You notice an open jar of pills on her dresser. Labs include a fingerstick glucose 20 mg/mL and elevated C-peptides. She regains conscious after receiving IV dextrose. What would best explain what happened?","o":["A. Insulin overdose","B. Uncontrolled Type I Diabetes Mellitus","C. A tumor in the beta cells of the pancreas","D. A tumor in the alpha cells of the pancreas","E. Overactivity of insulinase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"A tumor in the beta cells of the pancreas","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Hypoglycemia with <b>elevated C-peptide</b> means her own beta cells are pouring out insulin. A <b>beta-cell tumor (insulinoma)</b> is the only choice that fits. (A sulfonylurea pill overdose looks the same, but it isn't offered.)<br>A. Injected insulin: low C-peptide.<br>B. Uncontrolled type 1 diabetes: hyperglycemia, low C-peptide.<br>D. Alpha-cell tumor (glucagonoma): hyperglycemia.<br>E. More insulinase would destroy insulin and raise glucose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 15 (Insulin is degraded by Insulinase)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":3,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin receptor → IRS-1 → PI3K/PKB → GLUT4","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-18.jpg","cap":"Insulin receptor, IRS-1: Ras–MAP kinase to genes; protein kinase B to enzymes, GLUT4","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-27-1","s":"Which of the following describes the mechanism of action of insulin when it binds to its receptor?","o":["A. Gs → adenylate cyclase → cAMP → PKA","B. Gq → PLC → IP3 + DAG → PKC","C. Tyrosine Kinase Receptor → IRS-1 → Ras → MAP Kinase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Tyrosine Kinase Receptor → IRS-1 → Ras → MAP Kinase","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Insulin binds a <b>tyrosine kinase receptor</b> that phosphorylates <b>IRS-1</b> (insulin receptor substrate-1). One branch runs <b>Ras → MAP kinase</b> (mitogen-activated protein kinase) for gene expression. The other runs PI3K (phosphoinositide 3-kinase) → protein kinase B for GLUT4.<br>A. Gs (stimulatory G protein) → adenylate cyclase → cAMP (cyclic AMP) → PKA (protein kinase A) is the glucagon and epinephrine pathway.<br>B. Gq → PLC (phospholipase C) → IP3 and DAG → PKC (protein kinase C) is used by Gq receptors such as alpha-1, not insulin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 18 (Mechanism of Insulin Action)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":27,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":29,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":23,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":25,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F3881-46-1","s":"Which of the following mechanisms best explains how insulin signaling promotes glucose uptake into target tissues?","o":["A. Insulin binds to its receptor, activating a kinase cascade that increases GLUT2 expression in skeletal muscle.","B. Activation of the insulin receptor leads to phosphorylation of IRS-1, which activates Protein Kinase B (Akt), promoting GLUT4 translocation to the cell membrane.","C. Insulin signaling directly increases transcription of GLUT4 via MAP kinase without post-translational modifications.","D. Insulin binding to its receptor stimulates G-protein activation, which enhances glucose uptake by GLUT1.","E. The insulin receptor activates MAP kinase to phosphorylate GLUT4, increasing its transport activity."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of the insulin receptor leads to phosphorylation of IRS-1, which activates Protein Kinase B (Akt), promoting GLUT4 translocation to the cell membrane.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The insulin receptor (a tyrosine kinase) phosphorylates <b>IRS-1</b> (insulin receptor substrate-1) → PI3K (phosphoinositide 3-kinase) → <b>protein kinase B (Akt)</b> → <b>GLUT4 moves to the membrane</b> of muscle and fat.<br>A. Insulin acts on GLUT4, not GLUT2, in muscle.<br>C. GLUT4 effect is moving existing transporters (PI3K branch), not new transcription by MAP (mitogen-activated protein) kinase.<br>D. The insulin receptor isn't G-protein coupled, and GLUT1 is basal.<br>E. MAP kinase drives gene expression; it doesn't phosphorylate GLUT4.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 18 (Mechanism of Insulin Action)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":46,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-5-1","s":"Q2. Which signaling pathway does insulin use to induce translocation of a glucose transporter located on the cell surface of myocytes and adipocytes?","o":["A. MAP kinase pathway to activate gene expression and synthesis of enzymes, occurring within hours to days.","B. MAP kinase pathway to activate gene expression and synthesis of enzymes, occurring within minutes.","C. PI 3- kinase pathway, to activate PKB which regulates enzyme activity, occurring within hours to days.","D. PI 3- kinase pathway, to activate PKB which regulates enzyme activity, occurring within minutes.","E. GPCR pathway, where Gs activates AC → AC converts ATP into cAMP → cAMP activates PKA which phosphorylates proteins such as glycogen phosphorylase."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"PI 3- kinase pathway, to activate PKB which regulates enzyme activity, occurring within minutes.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Insulin moves <b>GLUT4</b> to the surface of muscle and fat cells through the <b>PI3K</b> (phosphoinositide 3-kinase) → <b>PKB</b> (protein kinase B) branch. It changes enzyme activity and works <b>within minutes</b>.<br>A. MAP (mitogen-activated protein) kinase changes gene expression over hours to days; it doesn't move GLUT4.<br>B. MAP kinase is slow (hours to days), not minutes.<br>C. Right pathway, wrong speed: PKB acts within minutes.<br>E. Gs → cAMP (cyclic AMP) → PKA (protein kinase A) is glucagon's pathway.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 18 (Mechanism of Insulin Action)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":5,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F0950-15-1","s":"A 32-year-old woman with obesity and metabolic syndrome is found to have fasting hyperglycemia. Insulin signaling studies reveal impaired phosphoinositide-3 kinase (PI3K) activation. Which metabolic process is most directly affected?","o":["A. Glycogen breakdown in liver","B. Glucose uptake in skeletal muscle","C. Gluconeogenesis in the liver","D. Ketogenesis in the liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucose uptake in skeletal muscle","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>PI3K</b> (phosphoinositide 3-kinase) activates protein kinase B, which moves <b>GLUT4</b> (glucose transporter 4) <b>to the membrane</b>. Without it, <b>skeletal muscle can't take up glucose</b> after a meal, so blood glucose stays high.<br>A. Liver glycogen breakdown: driven by glucagon through PKA (protein kinase A); it rises only indirectly when insulin fails.<br>C. Liver gluconeogenesis: also an indirect rise, not the direct PI3K target.<br>D. Ketogenesis: a fasting liver pathway, not a direct PI3K effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 18 (Mechanism of Insulin Action)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":15,"f":"F0950"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Phosphorylation switches = covalent modification","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"PKA phosphorylates liver enzymes: phosphorylase on, glycogen synthase and pyruvate kinase off","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-84-1","s":"PKB dephosphorylating pyruvate kinase is an example of which type of enzyme modification?","o":["A. Substrate","B. Allosteric","C. Covalent","D. Induction"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Covalent","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Covalent modification</b>: adding or removing a phosphate group changes an enzyme's activity. Insulin <b>dephosphorylates</b> (activates) liver pyruvate kinase; glucagon, through PKA (protein kinase A), phosphorylates (inactivates) it. Strictly, protein phosphatases that insulin turns on remove the phosphate; PKB (protein kinase B) is a kinase.<br>A. Substrate level: changing substrate supply, no change to the enzyme.<br>B. Allosteric: a molecule binds non-covalently at a regulatory site.<br>D. Induction: changing how much enzyme is made (hours to days).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 18 (Mechanism of Insulin Action)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":84,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Glucagon Gs receptor vs insulin tyrosine kinase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"Glucagon (and epinephrine) receptor with Gs, adenylate cyclase, cAMP, protein kinase A","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G029-18.jpg","cap":"Insulin receptor is a membrane tyrosine kinase that phosphorylates IRS-1","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-86-1","s":"How does signaling between insulin & glucagon differ?","o":["A. Glucagon binds alpha-1 receptors, whereas insulin binds beta-1 receptors.","B. Insulin binds alpha-1 receptors, whereas glucagon binds beta-1 receptors.","C. Glucagon binds an adrenergic receptor, whereas insulin does not.","D. The receptor for glucagon is on the receptor of target cells, whereas insulin receptors are intracellular.","E. The receptor for insulin is on the receptor of target cells, whereas glucagon receptors are intracellular."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucagon binds an adrenergic receptor, whereas insulin does not.","a":"C","why":"<b>C</b> is the intended answer. Glucagon binds its own <b>Gs-coupled receptor</b> (a G-protein-coupled receptor working like the beta-adrenergic receptor: cAMP, cyclic AMP → PKA, protein kinase A), the same pathway epinephrine uses. Insulin binds a <b>tyrosine kinase receptor</b>. The glucagon receptor itself is not an adrenergic receptor.<br>A, B. Neither hormone binds alpha-1 or beta-1 receptors.<br>D. The insulin receptor is on the cell surface, not inside.<br>E. The glucagon receptor is on the cell surface, not inside.","ex":"","fl":["Keyed C is inaccurate: glucagon binds its own Gs-coupled GPCR (the glucagon receptor), not an adrenergic receptor; the real difference is GPCR → cAMP/PKA (glucagon) vs receptor tyrosine kinase → PI3K/PKB (insulin) (G029:18, G029:29). No option is fully correct; C is the intended answer (GPCR 'like' adrenergic receptors), and both receptors are on the cell surface, so D and E are false."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 18 (Mechanism of Insulin Action)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 29 (Signaling by Glucagon)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":86,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Fed liver: glucose → acetyl-CoA → fatty acids","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Insulin side: increased glucose uptake, glycogen, protein and fat synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/F0106-6.jpg","cap":"Fed liver: pyruvate dehydrogenase, citrate, acetyl-CoA carboxylase to fatty acids; TCA slowed","where":"2024 discussion, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F0997-7-1","s":"Two hours after a large carbohydrate meal, which three pathways would predominate in the hepatocyte?","o":["A. 2,3,5","B. 1,2,4","C. 1,3,5","D. 2,3,6","E. 1,3,4"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"1,3,5","a":"C","why":"<b>C (1, 3, 5).</b> After a big carbohydrate meal the liver turns extra glucose into fat: <b>pyruvate → acetyl-CoA</b> (acetyl coenzyme A; pyruvate dehydrogenase on), <b>acetyl-CoA → citrate</b> (citrate carries it to the cytosol), <b>citrate → fatty acid</b> (acetyl-CoA carboxylase on).<br>Step 2 (fatty acid → acetyl-CoA) is β-oxidation: off, blocked by malonyl-CoA.<br>Step 4 (acetyl-CoA → acetoacetyl-CoA) is ketogenesis: a fasting pathway.<br>Step 6 (citrate → CO2): the TCA (tricarboxylic acid) cycle is slowed, so citrate goes to fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0997-7.jpg","in":"Pathway diagram: 1 pyruvate→acetyl-CoA; 2 fatty acid→acetyl-CoA; 3 acetyl-CoA→citrate; 4 acetyl-CoA→acetoacetyl-CoA; 5 citrate→fatty acid; 6 citrate→CO2","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":7,"f":"F0997"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0106-3-2","s":"For carbs and fats: List the pathways/cycles that are active during the 1-2 hours after consumption of the chips. For each active pathway/cycle list the main regulated enzyme","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Carbs: glycolysis (glucokinase/PFK-1), PDH, TCA (isocitrate dehydrogenase), glycogen synthesis (glycogen synthase), pentose phosphate pathway (G6P dehydrogenase). Fats: fatty acid synthesis (acetyl-CoA carboxylase), TAG synthesis/VLDL export in liver, TAG uptake and storage in adipose (lipoprotein lipase); β-oxidation is off (malonyl-CoA inhibits CPT-1).","a":null,"why":"Fed state, insulin high. <b>Carbs</b>: glycolysis (glucokinase, PFK-1 = phosphofructokinase-1), PDH (pyruvate dehydrogenase), glycogen synthesis (glycogen synthase), pentose phosphate pathway (glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase). <b>Fats</b>: fatty acid synthesis (acetyl-CoA carboxylase), TAG (triacylglycerol) made and exported as VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein), fat storage in adipose (lipoprotein lipase).<br>Common mix-up: β-oxidation is off (malonyl-CoA blocks CPT-1, carnitine palmitoyltransferase-1), and the TCA (tricarboxylic acid) cycle slows so citrate goes to fat.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 discussion","n":"W15 discussion","sl":3,"f":"F0106"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin raises glycogen, fat, protein synthesis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Insulin arrows up: glucose uptake, glycogen, protein and fat synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G029-19.jpg","cap":"Insulin effects list: glucose uptake, glycogen, fatty acid/TAG, protein synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-31-1","s":"Which of the following processes is positively regulated by insulin, a key anabolic hormone?","o":["A. Ketogenesis","B. Gluconeogenesis","C. Glycogen synthesis","D. Lipolysis","E. Glycogenolysis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glycogen synthesis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Glycogen synthesis.</b> Insulin is anabolic: it raises glucose uptake and glycogen, fat and protein synthesis.<br>A. Ketogenesis: catabolic, raised by glucagon.<br>B. Gluconeogenesis: raised by glucagon, suppressed by insulin.<br>D. Lipolysis: insulin inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase.<br>E. Glycogenolysis: raised by glucagon and epinephrine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":31,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-69-1","s":"What is the effect of insulin on: i. Glucose uptake by muscles ii. glycogen synthesis iii. fatty acid and triacylglycerol synthesis iv. protein synthesis","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"All increased: ↑ glucose uptake (muscle), ↑ glycogen synthesis, ↑ fatty acid & TAG synthesis, ↑ amino acid uptake & protein synthesis","a":null,"why":"Insulin is <b>anabolic</b>, so all four <b>increase</b>: glucose uptake by muscle (GLUT4, glucose transporter 4, moves to the membrane), glycogen synthesis, fatty acid and TAG (triacylglycerol) synthesis, and amino acid uptake with protein synthesis.<br>Common mix-up: insulin doesn't control glucose entry into liver or brain; those use insulin-independent transporters (GLUT2 in liver, GLUT1 and GLUT3 in brain).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":69,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin activates LPL, inhibits HSL","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0577-37.jpg","cap":"Adipose fed state: insulin upregulates lipoprotein lipase, inhibits hormone-sensitive lipase","where":"2022 TA review, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-98-1","s":"What is the connection between glucose and cholesterol?","o":["A. Glucose is converted to cholesterol for storage after normal metabolic needs are met.","B. Cholesterol and glucose are carried together via chylomicrons to different tissue.","C. Insulin upregulates lipoprotein lipase in fat and liver cells, which releases TAGs to be used for energy.","D. Insulin activates LPL but inactivates HSL."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Insulin activates LPL but inactivates HSL.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Insulin <b>activates LPL</b> (lipoprotein lipase), which frees fatty acids from chylomicrons and VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) for storage in fat. It <b>inactivates HSL</b> (hormone-sensitive lipase) by dephosphorylation, so stored fat isn't broken down.<br>A. Extra glucose is stored as glycogen and fat, not cholesterol.<br>B. Chylomicrons carry dietary fat and cholesterol, not glucose.<br>C. LPL sits on capillaries of fat and muscle, not liver cells, and in fat its products are stored, not burned.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 19 (Major effects are anabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":98,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Insulin analogs: rapid vs long-acting","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-23.jpg","cap":"Analogs: lispro/aspart rapid, glargine/detemir long, degludec ultra-long; no C-peptide","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-49-1","s":"Which of the following insulin types is classified as rapid-acting?","o":["A. Glargine","B. Aspart","C. Detemir","D. Degludec","E. Regular"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Aspart","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Aspart</b> is <b>rapid-acting</b> (onset under 15 minutes), like lispro and glulisine.<br>A. Glargine: long-acting.<br>C. Detemir: long-acting (C14 fatty acid at B29).<br>D. Degludec: ultra-long-acting (C16 fatty acid at B29).<br>E. Regular: short-acting human insulin, onset about 30 minutes, lasting 6–12 hours.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":49,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0577-31-1","s":"Which of the insulin types below are considered “rapid acting” and are very readily absorbed?","o":["A. Sitagliptin","B. Lispro and Aspart","C. Glargine and Detemir","D. Degludec","E. Metformin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Lispro and Aspart","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Lispro and aspart</b> are <b>rapid-acting</b>: more readily absorbed, onset under 30 minutes.<br>A. Sitagliptin: a DPP-4 (dipeptidyl peptidase-4) inhibitor pill, not insulin.<br>C. Glargine and detemir: long-acting.<br>D. Degludec: ultra-long-acting.<br>E. Metformin: an oral drug that lowers liver glucose output, not insulin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":31,"f":"F0577"}]},{"id":"F1013-24-1","s":"2. Several insulin analogs have been created over the past years ranging from rapid-acting, short-acting, long-acting, and ultra-long acting. Which of the following pairs of insulin analogs are both long-acting?","o":["A. Lispro & Aspart","B. Glargine & Detemir","C. Detemir & Degludec","D. Aspart & Glulisine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Glargine & Detemir","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Glargine and detemir</b> are the <b>long-acting</b> analogs (onset about 1 hour, lasting about 24 hours). They keep a steady basal insulin level.<br>A. Lispro and aspart: rapid-acting.<br>C. Detemir is long-acting, but degludec is <b>ultra-long</b>-acting.<br>D. Aspart and glulisine: rapid-acting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":24,"f":"F1013"}]},{"id":"F0621-11-1","s":"Q5. Which of the following applies to rapid-acting insulin? (more than 1 answer)","o":["A. It can have Proline at position 28 and Lysine at position 29 of the B-chain.","B. It can have Lysine at position 28 and Proline at position 29 of the B-chain.","C. It describes Lispro and Aspart","D. It describes Glargine and Detemir","E. It has an onset of <30 minutes and duration of <6 hours."],"g":["B","C","E"],"k":["B","C","E"],"rv":false,"at":"It can have Lysine at position 28 and Proline at position 29 of the B-chain. | It describes Lispro and Aspart | It has an onset of <30 minutes and duration of <6 hours.","a":"B,C,E","why":"<b>B, C, E.</b> <b>Lispro</b> swaps the B-chain residues of human insulin to <b>lysine (Lys) at B28 and proline (Pro) at B29</b>. Lispro and <b>aspart</b> are rapid-acting, with <b>onset under 30 minutes and duration under 6 hours</b>.<br>A. Proline at B28 and lysine at B29 is normal human insulin.<br>D. Glargine and detemir are long-acting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":11,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F3881-49-1","s":"Matching Time! 1. Normal Human Insulin 2. Lispro 3. Aspart 4. Glulisine 5. Technosphere (Inhaled Insulin) 6. Glargine 7. Detemir 8. Degludec — A. Pro at position 28 and Lys at 29 of B-chain; B. Rapid-acting insulin; C. Glu at B29 and Lys at B3; D. Inhaled insulin preparation; E. Long-acting insulin; maintains basal insulin levels; F. B30 deleted and C14 fatty acid at B29; G. Ultra-long-acting insulin; B30 deleted and C16 fatty acid at B29; H. Only mature insulin, no C-peptide in preparations. Which one has C-peptide?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1-A, 2-B, 3-B, 4-C, 5-D, 6-E, 7-F, 8-G (H applies to all preparations). Only insulin made by the person's own β-cells comes with C-peptide; every insulin preparation contains mature insulin only.","a":null,"why":"<b>No insulin preparation has C-peptide.</b> All products, even recombinant 'human' insulin, contain <b>only mature insulin</b>; only insulin from the person's own beta cells comes with C-peptide. Matches: human insulin = Pro (proline) B28, Lys (lysine) B29; lispro, aspart, glulisine and inhaled Technosphere = rapid; glargine = long; detemir = B30 deleted, C14 fatty acid; degludec = ultra-long, C16 fatty acid.<br>Common mix-up: thinking vial 'human insulin' carries C-peptide.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 23 (Insulin Analogs)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":49,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Glucagon: α cells, 29 amino acids, one chain","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-26.jpg","cap":"Glucagon: alpha cells, 29 amino acids, single chain, conserved, from prohormone","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G029-4.jpg","cap":"Low blood glucose → alpha cells release glucagon → liver releases glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"F1218-46-1","s":"What hormone is secreted by alpha cells of pancreas?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Glucagon","a":null,"why":"<b>Alpha (α) cells</b> (10–20% of islet cells) secrete <b>glucagon</b>, a 29-amino-acid, single-chain hormone. It raises blood glucose by acting mainly on the liver.<br>Common mix-up: beta cells make insulin; delta cells make somatostatin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 26 (Glucagon)"],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":46,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F3881-51-1","s":"A 45-year-old patient presents with symptoms of hypoglycemia. Laboratory results show elevated levels of a hormone that is produced by the α cells of the Islets of Langerhans in the pancreas. This hormone contains 29 amino acids and is synthesized from a prohormone through proteolytic cleavage, similar to insulin. Which of the following hormones is most likely elevated in this patient?","o":["A. Glucagon","B. Insulin","C. Somatostatin","D. Cortisol","E. Epinephrine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucagon","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Glucagon</b>: made by <b>alpha cells</b>, <b>29 amino acids</b> in one chain, cut from a prohormone (proglucagon) like insulin. It rises in hypoglycemia as the counter-regulatory response.<br>B. Insulin: beta cells, 51 amino acids in two chains.<br>C. Somatostatin: delta cells.<br>D. Cortisol: a steroid from the adrenal cortex.<br>E. Epinephrine: a catecholamine from the adrenal medulla.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 26 (Glucagon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":51,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0577-21-1","s":"Which hormone can increase blood glucose under normal fasting conditions, and where is this hormone made?","o":["A. Insulin, pancreatic Beta cells","B. Somatostatin, pancreatic delta cells","C. Glucagon, pancreatic alpha cells","D. Cortisol, adrenal cortex"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Glucagon, pancreatic alpha cells","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Glucagon, from alpha cells</b>, raises blood glucose in <b>ordinary fasting</b> by liver glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis.<br>A. Insulin lowers glucose.<br>B. Somatostatin inhibits both insulin and glucagon; it doesn't raise fasting glucose.<br>D. Cortisol also raises glucose, but it is the slower stress hormone and comes from the adrenal cortex, not the pancreas.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 26 (Glucagon)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 27 (Stimulation of Release)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":21,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":17,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F1201-13-1","s":"What Is true of glucagon?","o":["A. It is secreted from Beta cells","B. It is made from exocrine pancreas","C. It consists of 2 polypeptide chains","D. It is secreted in an inactive state","E. It has a conserved sequence in all mammals"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"It has a conserved sequence in all mammals","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Glucagon's <b>sequence is conserved in all mammals</b>.<br>A. Glucagon comes from alpha cells; beta cells make insulin.<br>B. It is an endocrine (islet) hormone, not an exocrine product.<br>C. It is a <b>single</b> 29-amino-acid chain; insulin has two chains.<br>D. It is cut from proglucagon before release and is secreted active.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 26 (Glucagon)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":13,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":212,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Glucagon release: epinephrine up; glucose, insulin down","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-27.jpg","cap":"Glucagon release: stimulated by low glucose, arginine, epinephrine; inhibited by glucose, insulin","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-71-1","s":"Identify which of the following statements about glucagon is false:","o":["A. It is produced by α cells of Islets of Langerhans in the pancreas","B. Low blood glucose stimulates release of glucagon","C. Epinephrine and Norepinephrine inhibit release of glucagon","D. Glucose and Insulin inhibit release of insulin"],"g":["C","D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Epinephrine and Norepinephrine inhibit release of glucagon","a":"C","why":"<b>C (and D as typed) are false.</b> <b>Epinephrine and norepinephrine stimulate</b> glucagon release, as do low glucose and amino acids such as arginine.<br>A. True: alpha cells of the islets make glucagon.<br>B. True: low glucose stimulates release.<br>D. False as written ('inhibit release of insulin'): glucose stimulates insulin. It was meant to say glucose and insulin inhibit <b>glucagon</b> release, which is true.","ex":"","fl":["Option D has a typo: as written ('Glucose and Insulin inhibit release of insulin') it is ALSO false (glucose stimulates insulin release). It was meant to read 'release of glucagon', which is true (G029:27); the intended false statement is C."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 27 (Stimulation of Release)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":71,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Glucagon catabolic: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"Glucagon → cAMP → PKA in liver: glycogen breakdown and gluconeogenesis to glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Glucagon side: increased glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, ketogenesis, lipolysis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Glucagon effects list: liver glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, ketogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-9-1","s":"Q4. Which of the following metabolic processes is increased due to glucagon?","o":["A. Lipolysis","B. Glycogen synthesis","C. Glucose uptake","D. Protein synthesis","E. Fatty acid synthesis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lipolysis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Lipolysis.</b> Glucagon is catabolic. It activates <b>HSL</b> (hormone-sensitive lipase) in fat, freeing fatty acids for β-oxidation and liver ketogenesis.<br>B. Glycogen synthesis: an insulin effect.<br>C. Glucose uptake into muscle and fat: an insulin effect.<br>D. Protein synthesis: an insulin effect.<br>E. Fatty acid synthesis: an insulin effect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":9,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F3881-53-1","s":"Which of the following describes the major effects of glucagon?","o":["A. Anabolic effects on glycogen and fat storage in liver and adipose tissue.","B. Catabolic effects, increasing liver glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and ketogenesis.","C. Inhibition of protein synthesis and glycogen breakdown in the liver.","D. Decreased fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis in the liver.","E. Stimulation of insulin release and glucose uptake in muscle cells."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Catabolic effects, increasing liver glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis, and ketogenesis.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Glucagon is <b>catabolic</b>, mainly in the liver: more glycogen breakdown, gluconeogenesis, lipolysis in fat, fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis.<br>A. Anabolic storage of glycogen and fat is insulin's job.<br>C. Glucagon increases, not inhibits, liver glycogen breakdown.<br>D. Glucagon increases fatty acid oxidation and ketogenesis.<br>E. Glucagon only potentiates insulin release at the beta cell, and it doesn't raise muscle glucose uptake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":53,"f":"F3881"}]},{"id":"F0088-3-1","s":"Which of the following is a consequence of glucagon binding to its receptor?","o":["A. Increase in muscle glycogenolysis","B. Activation of guanylate cyclase and protein kinase B","C. Activation of liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis","D. Inhibition of hormone-sensitive lipase in adipose tissue","E. Increase in glycolysis and ATP generation in pancreas"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Activation of liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Glucagon binds a Gs-coupled receptor on liver cells → cAMP (cyclic AMP) → PKA (protein kinase A) → <b>more liver gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis</b>.<br>A. Muscle has no glucagon receptors; epinephrine drives muscle glycogenolysis.<br>B. Guanylate cyclase isn't glucagon's pathway, and protein kinase B is insulin's.<br>D. Glucagon activates, not inhibits, hormone-sensitive lipase.<br>E. More glycolysis and ATP (adenosine triphosphate) in beta cells is the glucose signal for insulin release.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 29 (Signaling by Glucagon)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":3,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":8,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":2,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":3,"f":"F2030"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-74-1","s":"What is the effect of glucagon on: i.liver glycogen breakdown ii.liver gluconeogenesis iii.lipolysis in adipocytes iv.fatty acid oxidation v.ketogenesis in liver vi.Amino acid uptake in liver for gluconeogenesis","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"All increased (glucagon is catabolic, main target liver): ↑ liver glycogen breakdown, ↑ gluconeogenesis, ↑ adipocyte lipolysis, ↑ fatty acid oxidation, ↑ ketogenesis, ↑ hepatic amino acid uptake for gluconeogenesis","a":null,"why":"Glucagon is <b>catabolic</b> and its <b>main target is the liver</b>, so all six <b>increase</b>: liver glycogen breakdown, liver gluconeogenesis, lipolysis in fat cells, fatty acid oxidation, liver ketogenesis, and liver amino acid uptake to feed gluconeogenesis.<br>Common mix-up: glucagon does not act on muscle; epinephrine drives muscle glycogen breakdown.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":74,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Starvation: brain burns ketones, liver can't","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0577-69.jpg","cap":"Brain fasting state: ketone bodies become the main fuel in prolonged fast","where":"2022 TA review, slide 69"},{"src":"sl/F0577-57.jpg","cap":"Fasting liver: lacks thiophorase, so it exports ketone bodies unused","where":"2022 TA review, slide 57"},{"src":"sl/F0917-37.jpg","cap":"Liver makes ketone bodies; extrahepatic tissues burn them in the TCA cycle","where":", slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-80-1","s":"What is the significance of ketone bodies?","o":["A. Ketones are broken down for energy in the liver when there is no more glucose left.","B. Ketones are broken down for energy in the brain when there is no more glucose left.","C. Ketones are broken down in the liver but not used for energy there.","D. Ketones are by-products of fatty acid oxidation but do not provide substantial energy."],"g":["B"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ketones are broken down in the liver but not used for energy there.","a":"C","why":"<b>B.</b> In prolonged fasting, when glucose is scarce, tissues outside the liver such as the <b>brain break down ketone bodies for energy</b>.<br>A. The liver makes ketone bodies but can't break them down: it lacks <b>thiophorase</b> (succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase; CoA = coenzyme A).<br>C. False as written: the liver doesn't break ketone bodies down at all.<br>D. Ketone bodies give substantial energy; β-hydroxybutyrate is a major brain fuel in starvation.","ex":"","fl":["Keyed C is wrong as worded: the liver SYNTHESIZES ketone bodies but cannot break them down (no thiophorase) - the source's own note says so. The correct statement is B: extrahepatic tissues such as the brain oxidize ketone bodies for energy when glucose is scarce (prolonged fasting)."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":80,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0997-11-1","s":"The liver is an active metabolizing organ that is involved in carbohydrate, fat and protein anabolic and catabolic pathways. Which of the following important metabolic compounds is not used by hepatocytes?","o":["A. Mevalonic acid","B. Glyceraldehyde 3-P","C. Acetyl-CoA","D. Malonyl-CoA","E. β-Hydroxybutyrate"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"β-Hydroxybutyrate","a":"E","why":"<b>E. β-Hydroxybutyrate.</b> The liver <b>makes</b> ketone bodies but <b>can't use</b> them, because it lacks <b>thiophorase</b> (succinyl-CoA:acetoacetate CoA transferase; CoA = coenzyme A).<br>A. Mevalonic acid: used by the liver to make cholesterol.<br>B. Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate: a glycolysis and gluconeogenesis intermediate.<br>C. Acetyl-CoA: central to liver fat synthesis, ketogenesis and the TCA (tricarboxylic acid) cycle.<br>D. Malonyl-CoA: used for fatty acid synthesis in the fed liver.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":11,"f":"F0997"}]},{"id":"F0088-21-1","s":"A patient is brought to the emergency room after being found by search and rescue teams. He was mountain climbing, got caught in a sudden snowstorm, and had to survive in a cave. He had nothing to eat but snow for 12 days. In adapting to these conditions, which metabolic process has increased?","o":["A. The muscle’s uptake of glucose","B. The red blood cells’ use of fatty acids","C. The liver’s use of ketone bodies","D. The brain’s use of ketone bodies","E. The red blood cells’ use of ketone bodies"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"The brain’s use of ketone bodies","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> After about 12 days of starvation the <b>brain uses ketone bodies</b> for much of its fuel. This spares glucose and muscle protein.<br>A. Muscle glucose uptake falls: insulin is low, so GLUT4 stays inside the cell.<br>B. Red blood cells have no mitochondria, so they can't burn fatty acids.<br>C. The liver makes ketone bodies but <b>can't use them</b> (it lacks thiophorase).<br>E. Red blood cells can't use ketone bodies either; they rely only on glucose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 15 IS","sl":21,"f":"F0088"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":12,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 15 IA session","sl":19,"f":"F0531"},{"t":"2024 interactive","n":"interactive session week 15 Fall 2024","sl":27,"f":"F2030"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Two-day fast: pyruvate carboxylase, gluconeogenesis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-28.jpg","cap":"Glucagon raises liver gluconeogenesis and amino acid uptake for it","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/F0577-57.jpg","cap":"Fasting liver: acetyl-CoA from β-oxidation activates pyruvate carboxylase","where":"2022 TA review, slide 57"}],"q":[{"id":"F0997-13-1","s":"Which of the following hepatic enzymes would be most active after 2 days of fasting?","o":["A. Citrate synthase","B. Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","C. HMG-CoA synthase","D. Pyruvate carboxylase","E. Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1 (CPS1)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyruvate carboxylase","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pyruvate carboxylase.</b> At two days of fasting the liver's main job is <b>gluconeogenesis</b>. β-Oxidation floods the liver with acetyl-CoA (acetyl coenzyme A), which <b>activates pyruvate carboxylase</b>, the first gluconeogenic step (pyruvate → oxaloacetate).<br>A. Citrate synthase: the TCA (tricarboxylic acid) cycle slows in the fasting liver.<br>B. Acetyl-CoA carboxylase: fat synthesis, off in fasting.<br>C. HMG-CoA (3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl-CoA) synthase: ketogenesis is still building at two days and peaks after weeks.<br>E. CPS1 (carbamoyl phosphate synthetase 1): the urea cycle only clears the nitrogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":13,"f":"F0997"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Liver glycogen covers the first hours of fasting","slides":[{"src":"sl/G027-6.jpg","cap":"Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose between meals; muscle glycogen fuels muscle only","where":"2026 lecture: P14 Nutrition, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/F0577-60.jpg","cap":"Five phases of glucose homeostasis: glycogen first, then gluconeogenesis, then ketones","where":"2022 TA review, slide 60"}],"q":[{"id":"F0997-15-1","s":"During the first 16-18 hours of fasting, which of the following serves as the main source for maintaining blood glucose homeostasis?","o":["A. Hepatic glycogen","B. Adipose triglycerides","C. Muscle glycogen","D. Hepatic ketone bodies","E. Muscle alanine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatic glycogen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Hepatic glycogen.</b> Liver glycogen keeps blood glucose up <b>between meals and through about the first 12–18 hours of fasting</b>. Then it is nearly used up and gluconeogenesis takes over.<br>B. Adipose fat gives fatty acids, which can't become glucose.<br>C. Muscle glycogen fuels muscle only; muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase.<br>D. Ketone bodies rise after days, and they are a fuel, not glucose.<br>E. Muscle alanine feeds gluconeogenesis later in the fast.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["P14 Nutrition slide 6 (Fuel Stores in Humans)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 15 quiz","sl":15,"f":"F0997"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Epinephrine increases glycogenolysis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-33.jpg","cap":"Counter-regulation table: epinephrine +++ glycogenolysis; glucagon ++ glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 33"},{"src":"sl/G029-5.jpg","cap":"Epinephrine joins glucagon pulling toward glycogenolysis, lipolysis, ketogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-3-1","s":"Which of these hormones is NOT correlated with its proper action?","o":["A. Glucagon- Increased Lipolysis","B. Epinephrine- increased Ketogenesis","C. Epinephrine- Decrease Glycogenolysis","D. Insulin- Decreased Serum blood glucose"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Epinephrine- Decrease Glycogenolysis","a":"C","why":"<b>C</b> is the mismatch: <b>epinephrine increases glycogenolysis</b> in liver and muscle, through cAMP (cyclic AMP) and PKA (protein kinase A).<br>A. Glucagon → more lipolysis: a correct pair.<br>B. Epinephrine → more ketogenesis: correct; it drives lipolysis, sending fatty acids to the liver.<br>D. Insulin → lower blood glucose: a correct pair.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 28 (Major effects are catabolic)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 4 (Regulation of Blood)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":3,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":202,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Glucagon/PKA phosphorylates; insulin dephosphorylates","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-29.jpg","cap":"PKA phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase (on), glycogen synthase and pyruvate kinase (off)","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-76-1","s":"Choose the correct answer","o":["A. Every enzyme is phosphorylated to become activated.","B. PKB phosphorylates glycogen synthase to promote glycogen synthesis after a meal.","C. PKA phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase to promote glycogenolysis.","D. PKA is downstream of insulin release, and PKB is downstream of glucagon release."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"PKA phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase to promote glycogenolysis.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Glucagon → cAMP (cyclic AMP) → <b>PKA (protein kinase A) phosphorylates glycogen phosphorylase</b>, turning it on, so glycogen breaks down.<br>A. Phosphorylation turns some enzymes <b>off</b>, such as glycogen synthase and pyruvate kinase.<br>B. Insulin activates glycogen synthase by <b>dephosphorylating</b> it; PKB (protein kinase B) blocks the kinase that would phosphorylate it.<br>D. Reversed: PKB is downstream of insulin, PKA downstream of glucagon.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 29 (Signaling by Glucagon)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 18 (Mechanism of Insulin Action)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":76,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Unconscious hypoglycemia → IV glucose","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-32.jpg","cap":"Hypoglycemia: glucose under 50 mg/dL; symptoms resolve with glucose administration","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 32"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-53-1","s":"A diabetic patient presents with hypoglycemia and has fainted. What is the most appropriate intervention?","o":["A. Give them IV glucose STAT.","B. Give them orange juice and a jello cup STAT.","C. Give them insulin.","D. Give them glucagon."],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"IV glucose STAT","a":"A","why":"<b>A. IV (intravenous) glucose</b> right away. A fainted hypoglycemic patient needs glucose fast, and symptoms resolve with glucose.<br>B. Juice and food work only if the patient is awake and can swallow.<br>C. Insulin would drop glucose further.<br>D. A glucagon shot is the backup when there is no IV access. It is slower and needs liver glycogen, which may be used up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 32 (Plasma glucose level low enough to cause signs and symptoms of altered mental st)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":53,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Alcohol raises NADH, blocks gluconeogenesis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G029-35.jpg","cap":"Ethanol's NADH diverts pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate; less glucose","where":"2026 lecture: L33 Insulin & Glucagon, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F1218-53-1","s":"What is alcoholic hypoglycemia? Explain this process","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Alcohol ↑ NADH levels; the two major gluconeogenic precursors (pyruvate & OAA) are diverted to alternate pathways (lactate, malate), leading to ↓ gluconeogenesis and ↓ glucose","a":null,"why":"Ethanol metabolism (alcohol and aldehyde dehydrogenases) makes a large amount of <b>NADH</b> (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) in the liver. High NADH drives <b>pyruvate → lactate</b> and <b>oxaloacetate → malate</b>, removing the two key gluconeogenic precursors. So <b>gluconeogenesis and blood glucose fall</b>, worst after fasting when glycogen is gone.<br>Common mix-up: the cause is the high ratio of NADH to NAD+, not a hormone change.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":53,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F1013-26-1","s":"3. A homeless man was found to be severely intoxicated with alcohol and brought into the ER. His labs revealed he had alcoholic hypoglycemia. This is due to an increase in which of the following enzymes?","o":["A. Alcohol DH","B. NADH","C. FADH2","D. NAD+"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"NADH","a":"B","why":"<b>B. NADH</b> (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide). Alcohol dehydrogenase and aldehyde dehydrogenase both make NADH, which pulls pyruvate and oxaloacetate away from <b>gluconeogenesis</b>. NADH is a coenzyme, not an enzyme; the stem's word is loose.<br>A. Alcohol dehydrogenase makes the NADH, but the enzyme itself doesn't increase.<br>C. FADH2 (reduced flavin adenine dinucleotide) isn't made by ethanol metabolism.<br>D. NAD+ (the oxidized form) falls as it is used up.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 36 (Question)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":26,"f":"F1013"}]},{"id":"F2303-55-1","s":"A 35-year-old man is brought to the emergency department after excessive alcohol consumption. He is disoriented, sweating, and has a blood glucose level of 45 mg/dL. The patient's liver enzymes are elevated, and his past medical history includes occasional binge drinking. Upon further investigation, you recall that ethanol metabolism affects certain metabolic pathways. Given this context, which of the following ratios best explains the hypoglycemia observed in this patient?","o":["A. ATP/AMP","B. FADH2/FAD","C. Glucagon/Insulin","D. NADH/NAD+","E. Glucose/Glucose 6-P"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"NADH/NAD+","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Ethanol metabolism raises the <b>ratio of NADH to NAD+</b> (reduced to oxidized nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide). That diverts pyruvate to lactate and oxaloacetate to malate, so <b>gluconeogenesis fails</b> and glucose drops.<br>A. The ratio of ATP to AMP (adenosine tri- to monophosphate) reflects energy charge, not ethanol's effect.<br>B. Ethanol doesn't make FADH2, so the ratio of FADH2 to FAD doesn't explain it.<br>C. A higher glucagon-to-insulin ratio would raise glucose.<br>E. The ratio of glucose to glucose 6-phosphate isn't the problem.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 36 (Question)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":55,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F3881-55-1","s":"Which of the following explains why hypoglycemia may occur following alcohol consumption?","o":["A. Alcohol stimulates insulin release, increasing glucose uptake in peripheral tissues.","B. Alcohol increases NADH levels, diverting gluconeogenesis intermediates and decreasing gluconeogenesis.","C. Alcohol inhibits glucose uptake by the liver, reducing glucose production.","D. Alcohol enhances glycogenolysis in the liver, leading to an oversupply of glucose.","E. Alcohol directly stimulates the release of glucagon, increasing blood glucose levels."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Alcohol increases NADH levels, diverting gluconeogenesis intermediates and decreasing gluconeogenesis.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Alcohol raises liver <b>NADH</b> (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide), which diverts gluconeogenic intermediates (pyruvate → lactate, oxaloacetate → malate). So <b>gluconeogenesis drops</b>.<br>A. Alcohol doesn't trigger insulin release.<br>C. The problem is glucose production, not liver glucose uptake.<br>D. More glycogenolysis would raise glucose.<br>E. Glucagon would raise glucose; alcohol doesn't directly release it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 36 (Question)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":55,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0950-5-1","s":"A 24-year-old woman with Type 1 diabetes becomes hypoglycemic after consuming alcohol on an empty stomach. What is the mechanism of alcohol-induced hypoglycemia?","o":["A. Increased gluconeogenesis","B. Depletion of oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","C. Increased glycogenolysis","D. Decreased hepatic NADH levels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Depletion of oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Ethanol metabolism makes a large amount of <b>NADH</b> (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) in the liver. High NADH pushes <b>oxaloacetate to malate</b> and pyruvate to lactate, so <b>gluconeogenesis lacks substrate</b>. With no food, glucose falls.<br>A. Gluconeogenesis decreases, not increases.<br>C. Glycogenolysis would raise glucose, and on an empty stomach glycogen is low anyway.<br>D. Alcohol raises, not lowers, liver NADH.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 36 (Question)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":5,"f":"F0950"}]},{"id":"F0950-14-1","s":"A 25-year-old patient presents with confusion, lactic acidosis, and hypoglycemia after consuming alcohol. What is the primary biochemical mechanism behind the metabolic derangements?","o":["A. Increased glycolysis due to high NADH levels","B. Impaired gluconeogenesis due to depletion of pyruvate and oxaloacetate","C. Increased glycogenolysis from elevated glucagon levels","D. Impaired fatty acid oxidation due to reduced acetyl-CoA availability"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired gluconeogenesis due to depletion of pyruvate and oxaloacetate","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Alcohol raises liver <b>NADH</b> (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide). That turns <b>pyruvate into lactate</b> (lactic acidosis) and <b>oxaloacetate into malate</b>, starving gluconeogenesis (hypoglycemia).<br>A. High NADH doesn't speed glycolysis (glycolysis needs NAD+); the lactate comes from pyruvate being reduced.<br>C. Glucagon-driven glycogenolysis would raise glucose.<br>D. Fatty acid oxidation makes acetyl-CoA rather than needing it, and it doesn't explain lactate or low glucose.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 36 (Question)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":14,"f":"F0950"}]},{"id":"F0621-13-1","s":"Q6. A teenager was found to be confused after heavy drinking at a party. It was also discovered that he did not eat the whole day. What most likely explains his symptoms?","o":["A. Increased gluconeogenesis","B. Increased NAD+/NADH ratios","C. Decrease in gluconeogenic precursors","D. Decrease in lactic acid","E. Increased cytosolic [NADH] in the brain"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decrease in gluconeogenic precursors","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Heavy drinking without food: glycogen is low, so glucose depends on gluconeogenesis. Ethanol's <b>NADH</b> (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) turns pyruvate and oxaloacetate into lactate and malate, <b>removing gluconeogenic precursors</b> → hypoglycemia → confusion.<br>A. Gluconeogenesis decreases.<br>B. The ratio of NAD+ to NADH falls.<br>D. Lactic acid increases.<br>E. The extra NADH is in the liver, where most ethanol is metabolized, not the brain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 36 (Question)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":13,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F0028-74-1","s":"Choose the correct answer","o":["A. Glucagon is stimulated by glucagon-like peptides.","B. Glucagon has mostly anabolic activity in the body.","C. Glucagon directly inhibits insulin release.","D. Gluconeogenesis is inhibited by excess alcohol consumption."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Gluconeogenesis is inhibited by excess alcohol consumption.","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Excess alcohol <b>inhibits gluconeogenesis</b>: its NADH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) diverts pyruvate and oxaloacetate to lactate and malate.<br>A. GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) <b>suppresses</b> glucagon. Glucagon is stimulated by low glucose, amino acids and epinephrine.<br>B. Glucagon is mostly catabolic.<br>C. Glucagon is a <b>potentiator</b> of insulin release, not an inhibitor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 35 (Alcoholic Hypoglycemia)","L33 Insulin & Glucagon slide 27 (Stimulation of Release)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":74,"f":"F0028"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Ketoacidosis: acetone gives fruity breath","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0917-37.jpg","cap":"Acetone from ketone bodies leaves through the lungs, giving breath a fruity odor","where":", slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-82-1","s":"What is a hallmark of ketoacidosis in patients?","o":["A. Yellow sclera","B. Fruity breath","C. Sweet-smelling urine","D. Ascites & hepatomegaly","E. Hematuria","F. Slow respiratory rate"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fruity breath","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fruity breath.</b> Ketoacidosis makes <b>acetone</b>, which is breathed out and smells fruity.<br>A. Yellow sclera: jaundice from bilirubin.<br>C. Sweet-smelling urine: maple syrup urine disease (branched-chain ketoacids).<br>D. Ascites and hepatomegaly: liver disease or heart failure.<br>E. Hematuria: bleeding in the kidney or urinary tract.<br>F. Breathing is <b>deep and rapid</b> (Kussmaul) to blow off acid, not slow.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L32 Macronutrients slide 19 (Carbohydrates: Role, Energy Value, and Metabolic Relevance)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":82,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L33","label":"Diabetes: liver gluconeogenesis about 3× normal","slides":[],"q":[{"id":"F1218-46-4","s":"What is the rate of hepatic gluconeogenesis in diabetics?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"3-times the normal","a":null,"why":"In type 2 diabetes, <b>liver gluconeogenesis runs about three times the normal rate</b> after an overnight fast, and total liver glucose output is about double. Weak insulin action and high glucagon leave gluconeogenesis unchecked, which drives <b>fasting hyperglycemia</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the extra glucose comes mainly from gluconeogenesis, not glycogen breakdown.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":46,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamins: organic, essential, small amounts","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-4.jpg","cap":"Vitamins organic, from diet or gut bacteria; minerals inorganic","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 4"},{"src":"sl/G030-5.jpg","cap":"Vitamin classification tree; vitamin K listed as phylloquinones and menaquinones","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-178-1","s":"What are essential organic compounds that cannot be synthesized by the body and must be supplied exogenously in small amounts?","o":["A. Minerals","B. Vitamins","C. Cofactor","D. Coenzyme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamins","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamins</b> are <b>organic</b> compounds the body <b>cannot make</b>, so the diet (or gut bacteria) must supply them in <b>small amounts</b> (micrograms to milligrams).<br>A. Minerals: <b>inorganic</b> elements, such as iron and zinc, needed for enzyme and protein structure.<br>C. Cofactor: a non-protein helper an enzyme needs, often a metal ion such as zinc; a role, not a dietary class.<br>D. Coenzyme: an organic, non-protein enzyme helper, often made from a vitamin; also a role, not a dietary class.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 4 (Vitamins: Metabolically essential organic compounds that cannot be synthesized b)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":178,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-166-1","s":"Which of the following is classified a necessary organic chemical that must be consumed in at least small concentrations or produced by gut bacteria?","o":["A. Zinc","B. Calcium oxalate","C. Phylloquinone","D. Manganese phosphate","E. Monomagnesium phosphate"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Phylloquinone","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Phylloquinone</b> is <b>vitamin K</b> (K1, from green plants), an organic vitamin; <b>gut bacteria</b> make the related menaquinones (K2). It is needed in small amounts.<br>A. Zinc: an inorganic mineral, not a vitamin.<br>B. Calcium oxalate: the salt that forms kidney stones, not a nutrient.<br>D. Manganese phosphate: an inorganic mineral salt.<br>E. Monomagnesium phosphate: an inorganic mineral salt.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 4 (Vitamins: Metabolically essential organic compounds that cannot be synthesized b)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin K)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":166,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Fat-soluble vitamins need bile salt micelles","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-6.jpg","cap":"Vitamins A, D, E, K ride bile micelles into mucosal cells, then chylomicrons","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-134-1","s":"Which of the following vitamins requires bile salt micelles for its absorption from the small intestine?","o":["A. C","B. D","C. A","D. E"],"g":["B","C","D"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin A (source key)","a":"C","why":"<b>B, C and D are all correct.</b> Vitamins <b>D, A and E</b> are <b>fat-soluble</b>, so each is absorbed together with dietary lipids inside <b>bile salt micelles</b>, then packed into chylomicrons. Vitamin K works the same way.<br>A. Vitamin C: water-soluble (ascorbic acid); it is taken up by transporters and needs no micelles.","ex":"","fl":["Vitamins D, A and E are all fat-soluble and all need bile salt micelles for absorption (G030:6-7); only vitamin C (option A) does not. The question has three correct answers, so the single key C is incomplete."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":134,"f":"F0998"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-143-1","s":"Which of the following is true regarding vitamins?","o":["A. Fat soluble vitamins do not accumulate in the body to toxic amounts and are rapidly excreted","B. Vitamins are inorganic compounds that are essential for the structure and function of proteins","C. Vitamin D requires bile salt micelles for efficient absorption in the digestive tract","D. Vitamins are organic compounds that can be synthesized by the body","E. Large amounts of vitamins are required to maintain growth, health and reproduction of an organism"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D requires bile salt micelles for efficient absorption in the digestive tract","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Vitamin D is <b>fat-soluble</b>, so it is absorbed with dietary lipids and needs <b>bile salt micelles</b> to reach the intestinal cell.<br>A. False: fat-soluble vitamins are stored and <b>can build up to toxic levels</b>; water-soluble ones are excreted.<br>B. False: inorganic compounds are minerals; vitamins are organic.<br>D. False: vitamins <b>cannot</b> be made by the body.<br>E. False: vitamins are needed in <b>small</b> (microgram to milligram) amounts.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 4 (Vitamins: Metabolically essential organic compounds that cannot be synthesized b)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":143,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Fat-soluble vitamins are A, D, E, K","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-5.jpg","cap":"Fat-soluble branch: A, D, K, E; B-complex and C are water-soluble","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-108-1","s":"Which vitamins are fat-soluble?","o":["A. A, B, E, K","B. A, B, C, K","C. A, D, E, K","D. A, C, E, K","E. A, C, D, K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"A, D, E, K","a":"C","why":"<b>C. A, D, E, K</b> are the fat-soluble vitamins: retinol (A), cholecalciferol (D), tocopherols (E), phylloquinone and menaquinones (K). Every other choice slips in a <b>water-soluble</b> vitamin, B or C.<br>A. A, B, E, K: the B vitamins are water-soluble.<br>B. A, B, C, K: B and C are water-soluble.<br>D. A, C, E, K: vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is water-soluble.<br>E. A, C, D, K: vitamin C is water-soluble.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 5 (Fat Soluble vs Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":108,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":128,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":108,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":111,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":119,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":119,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Fat malabsorption → vitamin K deficiency, bruising","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-6.jpg","cap":"Fat-soluble vitamins absorbed with lipids via bile micelles; malabsorption blocks this","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/G030-31.jpg","cap":"Liver needs vitamin K to carboxylate factors II, VII, IX, X","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-129-1","s":"A patient presents to the office with a history of pancreatitis and steatorrhea. She reports problems with her night vision. What other finding may be expected in this patient?","o":["A. Easy bruising","B. Dermatitis","C. Nystagmus","D. Teeth loss","E. Alopecia"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Easy bruising","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Easy bruising.</b> Pancreatitis with <b>steatorrhea</b> (fatty stools) means fat is not absorbed, so all fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) fall. Poor night vision shows low vitamin A; low <b>vitamin K</b> means fewer active clotting factors, so she bruises easily.<br>B. Dermatitis: niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency, a water-soluble vitamin.<br>C. Nystagmus: thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency (Wernicke encephalopathy).<br>D. Teeth loss: vitamin C deficiency (scurvy).<br>E. Alopecia: biotin or zinc deficiency, not fat malabsorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":129,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":144,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":124,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":155,"f":"F0621"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":165,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":126,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":134,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":134,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2303-110-1","s":"A 52-year-old woman presents to her physician with fatigue, easy bruising, and difficulty seeing in low light. She has a history of celiac disease and reports non-compliance with her gluten-free diet. Physical examination reveals scattered ecchymoses on her extremities. Laboratory studies show a prolonged prothrombin time (PT). PT is a blood test that measures the time it takes for the liquid portion (plasma) of your blood to clot. Which of the following deficiencies is most likely contributing to her symptoms?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin D","C. Vitamin E","D. Vitamin K"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin K.</b> Untreated celiac disease damages the small intestine, so fat and fat-soluble vitamins are malabsorbed. Easy bruising, ecchymoses (skin bruises) and a <b>prolonged prothrombin time (PT)</b> show too little vitamin K for active clotting factors.<br>A. Vitamin A: also likely low (her poor night vision), but it doesn't explain the bruising or long PT.<br>B. Vitamin D: bone pain, osteomalacia.<br>C. Vitamin E: hemolysis, neurologic signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":110,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F0035-168-1","s":"Which of the following is TRUE about phylloquinones?","o":["A. Low risk for toxicity because excess excreted in urine","B. Require stomach acid for absorption from the gut","C. Necessary for the absorption of calcium, and thus bone formation","D. Likely deficient in patients with IBS and cystic fibrosis","E. Deficiency causes bleeding gums and poor wound healing"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Likely deficient in patients with IBS and cystic fibrosis","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Phylloquinone is <b>vitamin K</b>. Like every fat-soluble vitamin it needs bile salt micelles and normal fat absorption, so it runs low in <b>cystic fibrosis</b> (no pancreatic enzymes) and inflammatory bowel disease (\"IBS\" here should read IBD; irritable bowel syndrome does not cause malabsorption).<br>A. False: fat-soluble vitamins are stored, not flushed out in urine.<br>B. False: absorption needs bile salts, not stomach acid.<br>C. False: calcium absorption is vitamin D.<br>E. False: bleeding gums and poor wound healing are vitamin C (scurvy).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":168,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Chemical names of vitamins A, D, E, K","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-6.jpg","cap":"List: A retinal/retinol/retinoic acid, D calcitriol, E tocopherol, K phyllo/menaquinones","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"},{"src":"sl/G030-40.jpg","cap":"Summary table: other names and active forms of vitamins A, D, K, E","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 40"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-110-1","s":"KNOW NAMES FOR VITAMINS AS WELL AS LETTER: Vitamin A → _______; Vitamin D → _______; Vitamin E → _______; Vitamin K → ______/_______","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Vitamin A → Retinal, Retinol, Retinoic acid; Vitamin D → Calcitriol; Vitamin E → α/γ-Tocopherol; Vitamin K → Phyllo/Menaquinones","a":null,"why":"<b>A = retinol, retinal, retinoic acid. D = calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol), made from cholecalciferol (D3) or ergocalciferol (D2). E = α- and γ-tocopherol. K = phylloquinone (plants) and menaquinones (gut bacteria).</b><br>Common mix-up: <b>retinoic acid</b> is the vitamin A form that acts as a nuclear hormone; <b>retinal</b> is the form inside rhodopsin for vision.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (What are the different forms of vitamin A ?)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 37 (Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":110,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":170,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Kidney hydroxylation activates vitamin D, not A","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-9.jpg","cap":"Vitamin A forms: retinol alcohol, retinal aldehyde, retinoic acid nuclear hormone","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G030-11.jpg","cap":"Vitamin A absorbed as retinol and β-carotene, stored as retinyl palmitate","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Vitamin D: liver 25-hydroxylase, then kidney 1-α-hydroxylase makes calcitriol","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-145-1","s":"Which of the following is not true about Vitamin A?","o":["A. Vitamin A may be synthesized from the provitamin B-carotene","B. Vitamin A is stored in the body in the form of retinyl palmitate","C. Vitamin A is used therapeutically to treat severe acne and psoriasis","D. The different forms of Vitamin A include primary alcohols, aldehydes and nuclear hormones","E. Vitamin A must be converted into its active form by a hydroxylase enzyme in the kidney"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin A must be converted into its active form by a hydroxylase enzyme in the kidney","a":"E","why":"<b>E</b> is the false statement. A kidney hydroxylase (<b>1-α-hydroxylase</b>) activates <b>vitamin D</b>, not vitamin A. Vitamin A needs no kidney step.<br>A. True: β-carotene from plants is split into retinal by 15,15′-dioxygenase.<br>B. True: vitamin A is stored in the liver as retinyl palmitate.<br>C. True: retinoids treat severe acne (isotretinoin, Accutane) and psoriasis.<br>D. True: retinol is an alcohol, retinal an aldehyde, retinoic acid a nuclear hormone.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (What are the different forms of vitamin A ?)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Absorption, Transport & Storage of Vitamin A)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Therapeutic Actions of Vitamin A)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":145,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Clotting factors are vitamin K, not A","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-13.jpg","cap":"Retinoid actions: treat psoriasis, promyelocytic leukemia, acne; support vision, growth","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G030-12.jpg","cap":"Retinoic acid binds nuclear receptor RAR and switches on target genes","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-114-1","s":"Which of the following is not a function of vitamin A?","o":["A. Treat psoriasis","B. Treat promyelocytic leukemia","C. Maintain blood clotting factors","D. Maintain vision"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Maintain blood clotting factors","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Maintaining <b>blood clotting factors</b> is <b>vitamin K</b>, not vitamin A.<br>A. Vitamin A function: all-trans retinoic acid (tretinoin) treats psoriasis.<br>B. Vitamin A function: all-trans retinoic acid treats acute promyelocytic leukemia.<br>D. Vitamin A function: 11-cis-retinal is part of rhodopsin, the light pigment of rod cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Therapeutic Actions of Vitamin A)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":114,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":122,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":122,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F1013-112-1","s":"Which of the following statements about Vitamin A is false?","o":["A. Acts as a nuclear hormone","B. May be synthesized from a provitamin, such as β-carotene","C. Used as a treatment for psoriasis","D. Used as a treatment for promyelocytic leukemia","E. Maintains blood clotting factors"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Maintains blood clotting factors","a":"E","why":"<b>E</b> is false. Keeping clotting factors working is <b>vitamin K</b>'s job: it adds carboxyl groups to factors II, VII, IX and X. Vitamin A does not do this.<br>A. True: retinoic acid binds its nuclear receptor (RAR, retinoic acid receptor) and turns genes on.<br>B. True: β-carotene is a provitamin split into retinal.<br>C. True: all-trans retinoic acid treats psoriasis.<br>D. True: all-trans retinoic acid treats acute promyelocytic leukemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 12 (Retinoic Acid Is A Nuclear Hormone)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Therapeutic Actions of Vitamin A)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":112,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":131,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":111,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0028-152-1","s":"Which of the following statements about vitamin A is FALSE?","o":["A. Used as a treatment for psoriasis","B. Acts as a nuclear hormone","C. Absorbed as retinol and β-carotene in GI tract","D. Maintain blood clotting factors","E. Used to treat promyelocytic leukemia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Maintain blood clotting factors","a":"D","why":"<b>D</b> is false. Clotting factors depend on <b>vitamin K</b>, which adds carboxyl groups to factors II, VII, IX and X. Vitamin A has no role in clotting.<br>A. True: all-trans retinoic acid (tretinoin) treats psoriasis.<br>B. True: retinoic acid acts through its nuclear receptor, the retinoic acid receptor (RAR).<br>C. True: the gut absorbs vitamin A as retinol and β-carotene.<br>E. True: all-trans retinoic acid treats acute promyelocytic leukemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 12 (Retinoic Acid Is A Nuclear Hormone)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Therapeutic Actions of Vitamin A)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":152,"f":"F0028"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"β-carotene: provitamin A and antioxidant","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-10.jpg","cap":"β-carotene split to retinal by 15,15′-dioxygenase; lowers heart disease, macular degeneration","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-150-1","s":"What is the biochemical basis of β-carotene’s role in reducing the risk of heart disease and macular degeneration?","o":["A. It acts as a direct precursor for rhodopsin in the eye","B. It inhibits cholesterol synthesis in the liver","C. It is converted to retinol and functions as an antioxidant","D. It binds to retinoic acid receptors in endothelial cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It is converted to retinol and functions as an antioxidant","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Dietary <b>β-carotene</b> lowers the risk of heart disease, some cancers, cataracts and macular degeneration through its <b>antioxidant</b> action. It is also the provitamin that 15,15′-dioxygenase splits into retinal, which becomes retinol.<br>A. Rhodopsin uses 11-cis-retinal; that is vision, not heart protection.<br>B. β-carotene does not block liver cholesterol synthesis; statins do that.<br>D. Retinoic acid, not β-carotene, binds retinoic acid receptors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Therapeutic Actions of Vitamin A)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (15,15’-Dioxygenase)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":150,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"APL: ATRA restores promyelocyte differentiation","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-14.jpg","cap":"PML-RARα blocks differentiation; ATRA swaps corepressors for coactivators, cells mature","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 14"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-138-1","s":"Which of the following mechanisms underlies the therapeutic effect of all-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) in acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL)?","o":["A. Stimulation of apoptosis in leukemic promyelocytes","B. Induction of differentiation in leukemic promyelocytes","C. Inhibition of DNA synthesis","D. Activation of the immune system against leukemic cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Induction of differentiation in leukemic promyelocytes","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) binds the abnormal receptor <b>PML-RARα</b> (promyelocytic leukemia–retinoic acid receptor alpha), releases its gene repression, and lets leukemic promyelocytes <b>differentiate</b> into mature white cells. It was one of the first targeted cancer drugs.<br>A. Not apoptosis: the cells mature rather than being killed.<br>C. Not inhibition of DNA synthesis, which is how classic chemotherapy works.<br>D. Not immune activation against the leukemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":138,"f":"F0998"}]},{"id":"F3881-146-1","s":"How does retinoic acid contribute to its therapeutic use in acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL)?","o":["A. By promoting programmed cell death of leukemic cells","B. By inducing differentiation of promyelocytes into mature leukocytes","C. By stimulating proliferation of white blood cells","D. By repairing DNA damage in leukemic cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"By inducing differentiation of promyelocytes into mature leukocytes","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> In acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), the fusion protein <b>PML-RARα</b> (promyelocytic leukemia–retinoic acid receptor alpha) blocks maturation. All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA) <b>relieves the repression</b>, so promyelocytes <b>differentiate</b> into mature white cells.<br>A. It does not mainly kill leukemic cells; it makes them mature.<br>C. It does not drive white-cell proliferation; there are already too many immature cells.<br>D. It does not repair DNA; the fusion gene stays.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":146,"f":"F3881"}]},{"id":"F2296-180-1","s":"ATRA is one of the first known targeted small molecule cancer therapeutics. Explain how it works as a treatment for acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL).","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"ATRA (all-trans-retinoic acid) relieves the repression by PML-RARa and restores the differentiation of promyelocytes into mature leukocytes","a":null,"why":"<b>ATRA (all-trans retinoic acid) binds PML-RARα, relieves its gene repression, and restores differentiation of promyelocytes into mature white cells.</b> In acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), the PML gene (promyelocytic leukemia gene) fuses with the RARα gene (retinoic acid receptor alpha); the fusion protein blocks maturation.<br>Common mix-up: ATRA works by <b>maturation (differentiation)</b>, not by killing cells or blocking DNA synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":180,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F1013-119-1","s":"A patient presents to you with symptoms of anemia, paleness, nosebleeds, and frequent infections. You notice on blood smear a vast, abnormal number of immature white blood cells. What is the mechanism of the disease?","o":["A. Fusion of the retinoic acid receptor gene and PML gene, represses WBC maturation, and can be alleviated using ATRA treatment","B. Fusion of the PML gene with ATRA, which stimulates the overproduction of WBC’s and RAR","C. Fusion of RAR gene and PML gene to form ATRA, repressing differentiation of final stages of WBC maturation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Fusion of the retinoic acid receptor gene and PML gene, represses WBC maturation, and can be alleviated using ATRA treatment","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> In acute promyelocytic leukemia (APL), the <b>PML gene</b> (promyelocytic leukemia gene) fuses with the <b>retinoic acid receptor alpha (RARα) gene</b>. The fusion protein PML-RARα represses genes, so promyelocytes stop maturing and pile up. <b>All-trans retinoic acid (ATRA)</b> lifts the block.<br>B. ATRA is the drug, not a gene; the disease is a maturation block, not overproduction.<br>C. The fusion makes PML-RARα, not ATRA; ATRA is the treatment.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 14 (Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":119,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":178,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Bitot spots, night blindness → vitamin A deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-15.jpg","cap":"Xerophthalmia photos: xerosis, night blindness, Bitot spots, keratomalacia","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-117-1","s":"A 39 year old male presents to the office complaining of difficulty seeing at night. On examination, there is dryness of the sclera with patches of gray bubbles (Bitot’s spots). What is this patient most likely deficient in?","o":["A. Vitamin K","B. Vitamin C","C. Vitamin A","D. Biotin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin A","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin A.</b> Night blindness plus dry sclera with grey bubbly patches (<b>Bitot spots</b>) is xerophthalmia, the classic vitamin A deficiency.<br>A. Vitamin K deficiency: bleeding, bruising.<br>B. Vitamin C deficiency: bleeding gums, poor wound healing.<br>D. Biotin deficiency: hair loss and a scaly rash around eyes, nose and mouth; no Bitot spots.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":117,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":125,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":125,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F1013-116-1","s":"You are a rotating third year student, and you see a patient with limited past medical history. You see the following on physical exam. What is your top differential?","o":["A. Vitamin E excess","B. Vitamin K deficiency","C. Vitamin A deficiency","D. Vitamin A excess","E. Vitamin D excess","F. Vitamin E deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin A deficiency","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin A deficiency.</b> The photo shows a <b>Bitot spot</b>: a foamy grey-white patch on the white of the eye beside the cornea, a sign of xerophthalmia (dry eye). Night blindness and keratomalacia go with it.<br>A. Vitamin E excess: least toxic vitamin; no eye spots.<br>B. Vitamin K deficiency: bleeding.<br>D. Vitamin A excess: dry itchy skin, cheilitis, raised intracranial pressure.<br>E. Vitamin D excess: high blood calcium.<br>F. Vitamin E deficiency: hemolysis and neurologic deficits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F1013-116.jpg","in":"Photo of an eye with a foamy, white-gray conjunctival plaque beside the cornea (Bitot's spot) — the finding to identify","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":116,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":176,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F2296-182-1","s":"29 year-old female presents to her physician with complaints of dry eyes for the past 2 months. She states that she noticed her sclera look dry and wrinkly, and there are also patches of little gray bubbles on the sclera. She also adds that she is having difficulty seeing at night. She is concerned that she also has bulging corneal opacities. As her physician, you are suspicious of a vitamin deficiency. What vitamin deficiency would are you diagnosing the patient with?","o":["A. Vitamin E deficiency","B. Vitamin D deficiency","C. Vitamin A deficiency","D. Vitamin K deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin A deficiency","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin A deficiency.</b> All four signs of <b>xerophthalmia</b> are here: dry, wrinkled sclera (xerosis), night blindness, grey bubbly patches (<b>Bitot spots</b>), and a soft, bulging, clouded cornea (<b>keratomalacia</b>).<br>A. Vitamin E deficiency: hemolysis, lost reflexes, poor balance.<br>B. Vitamin D deficiency: rickets or osteomalacia.<br>D. Vitamin K deficiency: bleeding.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":182,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F2303-112-1","s":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the clinic by his parents due to difficulty seeing at night and dryness of his eyes. On examination, his conjunctivae appear dry, and triangular white spots are noted on the bulbar conjunctiva near the cornea. His diet consists primarily of rice and very few fruits or vegetables. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient’s symptoms?","o":["A. Deficiency of vitamin C","B. Deficiency of vitamin A","C. Deficiency of vitamin D","D. Deficiency of vitamin E","E. Deficiency of zinc"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Deficiency of vitamin A","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin A deficiency.</b> Night blindness, dry conjunctivae and triangular white <b>Bitot spots</b> are classic. A rice diet with few vegetables gives little vitamin A or β-carotene. Vitamin A (as retinal) is needed for rhodopsin and healthy eye epithelium.<br>A. Vitamin C: bleeding gums, poor wound healing (scurvy).<br>C. Vitamin D: rickets.<br>D. Vitamin E: hemolysis, neurologic signs.<br>E. Zinc: poor wound healing, rash, poor taste; no Bitot spots.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":112,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F0928-21-1","s":"A 45-year old male patient complains of dry sensation in his eyes. PE indicated grey patches on the sclerae. During questioning, he was bragging about his 60-lb. body weight loss following a very strict fat-free diet. Which of the following vitamins could be deficient in this patient leading to the symptoms described above?","o":["A. Tocopherol","B. Calcitriol","C. Ascorbic acid","D. Retinol","E. Phylloquinone"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Retinol","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Retinol</b> (vitamin A). Dry eyes (<b>xerophthalmia</b>) with grey scleral patches (<b>Bitot spots</b>) mean vitamin A deficiency; a very strict fat-free diet starves him of fat-soluble vitamins.<br>A. Tocopherol (vitamin E) deficiency: hemolysis and lost reflexes.<br>B. Calcitriol (vitamin D) deficiency: soft, bowed bones.<br>C. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) deficiency: bleeding gums, poor wound healing.<br>E. Phylloquinone (vitamin K) deficiency: bleeding and bruising.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":21,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":1,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":37,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":2,"f":"F2435"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Night blindness: no 11-cis-retinal for rhodopsin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-11.jpg","cap":"Retina: 11-cis-retinal joins opsin as rhodopsin; deficiency causes night blindness","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G030-9.jpg","cap":"11-cis-retinal, made by photoisomerization, is part of the visual pigment rhodopsin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-148-1","s":"Which of the following best explains how Vitamin A deficiency leads to night blindness?","o":["A. Reduced keratin production in epithelial tissues and retinal degeneration","B. Impaired rhodopsin regeneration and corneal epithelial breakdown","C. Accumulation of β-carotene in epithelial tissues and scleral opacities","D. Overexpression of retinoic acid receptors in visual cells"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired rhodopsin regeneration and corneal epithelial breakdown","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Rod cells need <b>11-cis-retinal</b> to rebuild <b>rhodopsin</b> after light bleaches it. Without vitamin A, rhodopsin is not regenerated, so dim-light vision fails (night blindness). Dry, breaking-down corneal epithelium (xerophthalmia, keratomalacia) comes with it.<br>A. Deficiency causes <b>more</b> keratin (keratin deposits), not less.<br>C. β-carotene is low, not building up.<br>D. Receptors are not overexpressed; there is too little retinoid to act on them.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (What are the different forms of vitamin A ?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":148,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin A deficiency → Bitot spots, keratomalacia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-15.jpg","cap":"Vitamin A deficiency signs with photos: Bitot spots, keratomalacia, night blindness","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G030-16.jpg","cap":"Vitamin A excess signs: dry itchy skin, cheilitis, hair loss, intracranial pressure","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-135-1","s":"A 36 year old woman presents to the office complaining of difficulty seeing at night. After bloodwork, the patient is found to be deficient in Vitamin A. What might you expect to see on physical examination?","o":["A. Bitot’s spots on the sclera","B. Osteomalacia","C. Increased bleeding tendency","D. Loss of deep tendon reflexes","E. Dry and itchy skin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Bitot’s spots on the sclera","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Bitot spots</b> (grey, foamy patches on the sclera) are a sign of vitamin A deficiency, together with night blindness, dry eyes and keratomalacia.<br>B. Osteomalacia: vitamin D deficiency in adults.<br>C. Increased bleeding tendency: vitamin K deficiency.<br>D. Loss of deep tendon reflexes: vitamin E deficiency.<br>E. Dry and itchy skin: vitamin A <b>excess</b> (hypervitaminosis A), not deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":135,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":115,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":156,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-148-1","s":"A 30 year old presents with difficulty seeing at night. Blood work reveals a vitamin A deficiency. What might you expect to see on the patient’s physical exam?","o":["A. Muscle fasciculations","B. Keratomalacia","C. Decreased bone density","D. Dry and itchy skin","E. Skin Purpura"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Keratomalacia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Keratomalacia</b> (soft, bulging, clouded cornea) is part of xerophthalmia from vitamin A deficiency, along with night blindness and Bitot spots.<br>A. Muscle fasciculations: low calcium or magnesium, not vitamin A.<br>C. Decreased bone density: vitamin D deficiency.<br>D. Dry and itchy skin: vitamin A <b>excess</b>.<br>E. Skin purpura: vitamin K or vitamin C deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (Xeropthalmia (Dry eye syndrome))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":148,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Active vitamin D = calcitriol","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-21.jpg","cap":"Active vitamin D is 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol); D2 plants, D3 animals/skin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Pathway: cholecalciferol → liver 25-hydroxylase → kidney 1-α-hydroxylase → calcitriol","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-120-1","s":"What is the active form of vitamin D?","o":["A. D3 (cholecalciferol)","B. D2 (ergocalciferol)","C. 25-hydroxycholecalciferol (calcidiol)","D. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol)</b> is active vitamin D, made when the kidney's 1-α-hydroxylase acts on 25-hydroxycholecalciferol.<br>A. D3 (cholecalciferol): inactive precursor from animal food or made in sunlit skin.<br>B. D2 (ergocalciferol): inactive precursor from plants.<br>C. 25-hydroxycholecalciferol (calcidiol): the liver product; still inactive until the kidney step.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":120,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":128,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":128,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1013-124-2","s":"Bonus Question: What is the name of the active form of Vitamin D?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","a":null,"why":"<b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol).</b> The liver adds a hydroxyl group at carbon 25 (25-hydroxylase), then the kidney adds one at carbon 1 (1-α-hydroxylase) to make the active form.<br>Common mix-up: <b>25-hydroxycholecalciferol</b> (calcidiol) is the liver product and is not yet active.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":124,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":138,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":118,"f":"F0180"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Kidney 1-α-hydroxylase makes active vitamin D","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Skin, liver, then kidney 1-α-hydroxylase makes calcitriol; PTH stimulates it","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/D039-16.jpg","cap":"Chronic kidney disease lowers renal 1α-hydroxylase, so calcitriol and calcium absorption fall","where":"2026 spring interactive, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-124-1","s":"Where is the active form of Vitamin D formed?","o":["A. Skin","B. Intestine","C. Liver","D. Kidney"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Kidney","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Kidney.</b> The final activating step is <b>1-α-hydroxylation</b> in the kidney, making 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol). Parathyroid hormone (PTH) and low phosphate switch this enzyme on.<br>A. Skin: ultraviolet (UV) light turns 7-dehydrocholesterol into cholecalciferol (D3), an inactive precursor.<br>B. Intestine: absorbs dietary D2 and D3; no activation.<br>C. Liver: 25-hydroxylase makes 25-hydroxycholecalciferol, still inactive.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Synthesis of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":124,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":138,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":118,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":159,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"D039-17-1","s":"A 43-year-old man with chronic renal failure, who requires hemodialysis complains of generalized bone pain. Physical examination shows bowing of legs and a sallow complexion. A radiograph of man shows generalized osteopenia. The patient most likely has a deficiency of which of the following vitamin?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin K","C. Vitamin D","D. Vitamin C","E. Vitamin E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin D.</b> The kidney's <b>1-α-hydroxylase</b> turns 25-hydroxyvitamin D into active <b>calcitriol</b>. In chronic renal failure too little kidney is left, so calcitriol falls, the gut absorbs less calcium, and bone mineralizes poorly: bone pain, osteopenia, bowed legs.<br>A. Vitamin A: eye and skin signs.<br>B. Vitamin K: bleeding.<br>D. Vitamin C: scurvy, bleeding gums.<br>E. Vitamin E: hemolysis, neurologic signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Synthesis of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":17,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":9,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":23,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":6,"f":"F0078"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Liver 25-hydroxylase; liver disease lowers vitamin D","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-22.jpg","cap":"Liver 25-hydroxylase turns cholecalciferol into 25-hydroxycholecalciferol before the kidney step","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G030-23.jpg","cap":"Skin makes cholecalciferol; liver adds 25-OH; kidney 1-hydroxylase makes calcitriol","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-152-1","s":"Why might a person with liver disease develop Vitamin D deficiency despite adequate sunlight exposure?","o":["A. The liver cannot produce cholecalciferol from 7-dehydrocholesterol","B. The liver is unable to hydroxylate Vitamin D to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol","C. The liver overproduces parathyroid hormone, depleting Vitamin D stores","D. The liver secretes bile acids that interfere with Vitamin D absorption"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The liver is unable to hydroxylate Vitamin D to 25-hydroxycholecalciferol","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Sunlight makes cholecalciferol in the skin, but the <b>liver's 25-hydroxylase</b> must turn it into 25-hydroxycholecalciferol before the kidney can activate it. A failing liver blocks this step.<br>A. Cholecalciferol is made from 7-dehydrocholesterol in the <b>skin</b>, not the liver.<br>C. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) comes from the parathyroid glands, not the liver.<br>D. Bile acids <b>help</b> vitamin D absorption; they don't block it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Synthesis of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 23 (Metabolism And Actions of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":152,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin D raises gut calcium and phosphate uptake","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-24.jpg","cap":"Calcitriol raises intestinal calcium uptake, kidney reabsorption and bone mobilization","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G030-23.jpg","cap":"Calcitriol induces calbindin in intestinal cells to absorb calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-151-1","s":"Which of the following is true about vitamin D?","o":["A. Vitamin D leads to the reabsorption of Calcium and Phosphate from the intestines","B. Vitamin D will lead to excess excretion of Calcium from the kidneys","C. Parathyroid hormone directly inhibits the synthesis of Vitamin D","D. The active form of Vitamin D is synthesized by the liver","E. A Vitamin D deficiency promotes osteoblast activity and bone strength"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D leads to the reabsorption of Calcium and Phosphate from the intestines","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Calcitriol (active vitamin D) raises <b>intestinal absorption of calcium and phosphate</b> (it makes calbindin, a calcium-binding protein) to keep blood levels normal.<br>B. False: it <b>reduces</b> calcium loss in urine by increasing kidney reabsorption.<br>C. False: parathyroid hormone (PTH) <b>stimulates</b> kidney 1-α-hydroxylase, so it raises calcitriol.<br>D. False: the kidney, not the liver, makes the active form.<br>E. False: deficiency weakens bone (rickets, osteomalacia).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 23 (Metabolism And Actions of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Functions of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":151,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F0928-23-1","s":"A 3-year old girl was brought to the clinic for consultation. She is well below the 5th percentile for both height and weight with multiple rachitic deformities of the skeleton to include severe bowing of the tibias bilaterally and pigeon chest. Which of the following can be the reason for her clinical symptoms?","o":["A. Increased reabsorption of calcium from kidney","B. Decreased intestinal uptake of calcium and phosphate","C. Increased intestinal uptake of calcium","D. Increased reabsorption of phosphate from kidney","E. Decreased resorption of calcium by bone"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased intestinal uptake of calcium and phosphate","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Bowed tibias, pigeon chest and poor growth are <b>rickets</b>, from too little vitamin D. Without calcitriol the gut absorbs <b>less calcium and phosphate</b>, so new bone can't mineralize.<br>A. More kidney calcium reabsorption would raise calcium, not cause rickets.<br>C. More intestinal calcium uptake is the opposite of the defect.<br>D. Low vitamin D with high parathyroid hormone (PTH) makes the kidney <b>lose</b> phosphate.<br>E. Bone resorption <b>rises</b> (PTH effect); it does not fall.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Functions of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":23,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":3,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":39,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":4,"f":"F2435"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Rickets = vitamin D (calcitriol) deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-25.jpg","cap":"Vitamin D deficiency causes rickets in children, osteomalacia in adults; bowed-leg X-ray","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-141-2","s":"Bonus Question: What is Rickets caused by?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Vitamin D Deficiency","a":null,"why":"<b>Vitamin D deficiency</b> (too little calcitriol) in a child. Low calcitriol means poor gut absorption of calcium and phosphate, so growing bone stays soft: bowed legs, poor growth.<br>Common mix-up: the same deficiency in <b>adults</b> is <b>osteomalacia</b> (fractures), not rickets.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":141,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":121,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F1201-123-1","s":"A 2 year old child presents to the pediatrician with bowed legs and stunted growth. He is diagnosed with rickets. What vitamin supplement should be prescribed to correct these problems?","o":["A. Vitamin K","B. Vitamin E","C. Vitamin D","D. Folic Acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin D.</b> Rickets is soft, poorly mineralized bone in a child from vitamin D deficiency; giving vitamin D restores calcium and phosphate absorption.<br>A. Vitamin K: for bleeding from low clotting factors.<br>B. Vitamin E: for hemolysis and neurologic deficits.<br>D. Folic acid: for megaloblastic anemia and to prevent neural tube defects.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":123,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":131,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":131,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F2303-114-1","s":"A 5-year-old boy presents with bowing of the legs, delayed growth, and frontal bossing. Physical examination reveals widened wrists and rachitic rosary (prominent costochondral junctions). Laboratory tests show decreased serum calcium and phosphate, and increased parathyroid hormone (PTH) levels. The parents mention that the boy spends very little time outdoors. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this condition?","o":["A. Deficiency of 1, 25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","B. Excess 1, 25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","C. Vitamin A deficiency","D. Excess calcium intake","E. Phosphate toxicity"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Deficiency of 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol (calcitriol)","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> This is <b>rickets</b>: bowed legs, frontal bossing, wide wrists, rachitic rosary, low calcium and phosphate, high parathyroid hormone (PTH). Little sunlight means little skin vitamin D, so too little active <b>1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol</b> (calcitriol).<br>B. Excess calcitriol gives <b>high</b> calcium and kidney stones.<br>C. Vitamin A deficiency: eye signs.<br>D. Excess calcium intake doesn't cause rickets.<br>E. Phosphate excess doesn't fit a low serum phosphate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Individuals who spend inadequate time in the sunlight, dietary sources of vitami)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":114,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin D deficiency → low calcium, high PTH","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-24.jpg","cap":"Low serum calcium raises PTH and calcitriol to pull calcium from gut, bone, kidney","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G030-23.jpg","cap":"Deficiency raises PTH, increases bone turnover and fracture risk","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-154-1","s":"What is the downstream effect of Vitamin D deficiency on bone health?","o":["A. Increased calcium reabsorption in the kidneys and bone mineralization","B. Decreased parathyroid hormone secretion and hypophosphatemia","C. Hypocalcemia, increased bone resorption, and impaired mineralization","D. Hypercalcemia, excessive osteoclast activation, and bone fragility"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypocalcemia, increased bone resorption, and impaired mineralization","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Without vitamin D the gut absorbs little calcium, so blood calcium falls (<b>hypocalcemia</b>). Parathyroid hormone (PTH) rises and pulls calcium out of bone (<b>more resorption</b>), and new bone <b>mineralizes poorly</b> (rickets, osteomalacia).<br>A. Mineralization falls in deficiency; this describes vitamin D working normally.<br>B. PTH goes <b>up</b>, not down.<br>D. High calcium with overactive osteoclasts fits vitamin D <b>excess</b>, not deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Functions of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":154,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Rickets → poor growth, skeletal deformities","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-25.jpg","cap":"Rickets in children: poor growth, skeletal deformities; X-ray of bowed legs","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-141-1","s":"A 3 year old child presents to the pediatrician with bowed legs and stunted growth. He is diagnosed with Rickets. What is this child at risk for?","o":["A. Keratomalacia","B. Hemolytic anemia and jaundice","C. Poor growth and skeletal deformities","D. Hypercalcemia","E. Visual impairment"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Poor growth and skeletal deformities","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Rickets is vitamin D deficiency in children; bone can't mineralize, so the child shows <b>poor growth and skeletal deformities</b> (bowed legs).<br>A. Keratomalacia: vitamin A deficiency.<br>B. Hemolytic anemia and jaundice: too much vitamin K given to infants.<br>D. Hypercalcemia: vitamin D <b>excess</b>; rickets has low or normal calcium.<br>E. Visual impairment: vitamin A deficiency (night blindness) or vitamin E deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":141,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":121,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":162,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin D excess → hypercalcemia, stones, confusion","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-26.jpg","cap":"Vitamin D toxicity: hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, dazed, nausea, thirst; sarcoidosis link","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G030-24.jpg","cap":"Calcitriol raises gut calcium absorption and bone mobilization, so excess raises calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-156-1","s":"Which of the following symptoms is most directly associated with vitamin D toxicity?","o":["A. Hyperphosphatemia and anemia","B. Hypercalcemia and renal calcification","C. Hypocalcemia and skeletal deformities","D. Hypermagnesemia and muscle weakness"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypercalcemia and renal calcification","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Excess calcitriol raises calcium absorption, so blood and urine calcium climb (<b>hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria</b>) and calcium deposits in the kidney (stones, calcification).<br>A. Anemia is not a feature of vitamin D excess; this pair fits chronic kidney failure.<br>C. Hypocalcemia and skeletal deformities: vitamin D <b>deficiency</b> (rickets).<br>D. Hypermagnesemia and weakness: magnesium excess, as in kidney failure or magnesium antacid overuse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (Vitamin D Toxicity)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":156,"f":"F3881"}]},{"id":"F0950-8-1","s":"A 62-year-old man presents with recurrent kidney stones. Labs reveal high serum calcium and hypercalciuria. Deficiency in which vitamin is most likely?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin C","C. Vitamin D","D. Vitamin K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin D</b>, but in <b>excess</b>: the stem's word \"deficiency\" is an error, since no vitamin deficiency causes this picture. Too much calcitriol raises gut calcium absorption and bone resorption, giving <b>hypercalcemia</b> (high blood calcium), <b>hypercalciuria</b> (high urine calcium) and kidney stones.<br>A. Vitamin A: eye signs (deficiency) or skin, liver and brain signs (excess).<br>B. Vitamin C: scurvy.<br>D. Vitamin K: bleeding.","ex":"","fl":["Stem error: hypercalcemia + hypercalciuria + kidney stones point to vitamin D EXCESS (toxicity, G030:26), not deficiency - the source's own justification says 'Excessive Vitamin D'. Keyed C is right only if 'Deficiency' is read as 'Excess'; as written, no option is a deficiency that causes this picture."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (Vitamin D Toxicity)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Functions of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":8,"f":"F0950"}]},{"id":"F0621-153-1","s":"Which of the following is not associated with Vitamin D Toxicity?","o":["A. Kidney Stones","B. Confusion and changes in mental status","C. Nausea","D. Osteomalacia","E. Sarcoidosis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Osteomalacia","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Osteomalacia</b> is soft adult bone from vitamin D <b>deficiency</b>, the opposite problem.<br>A. Kidney stones: yes; excess calcitriol causes hypercalciuria (high urine calcium).<br>B. Confusion and mental change: yes; high blood calcium causes a dazed state and stupor.<br>C. Nausea: yes, with loss of appetite and thirst.<br>E. Sarcoidosis: yes; its granulomas make calcitriol, so it can present with vitamin D toxicity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (Vitamin D Toxicity)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (Disorders of Vitamin D)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":153,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F1013-126-1","s":"42 yo F presents to your office complaining of loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, altered mental status, and weight loss. Her past medical history is significant for sarcoidosis. Which of the following is the cause of her presentation?","o":["A. Excess vitamin D intake","B. IBS","C. Vitamin E deficiency","D. Vitamin K deficiency","E. Pancreatic insufficiency"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Excess vitamin D intake","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Excess vitamin D.</b> Too much calcitriol causes <b>hypercalcemia</b> (high blood calcium): loss of appetite, nausea, vomiting, thirst, a dazed or confused state. <b>Sarcoidosis</b> fits, because its granuloma cells make calcitriol themselves.<br>B. IBS (irritable bowel syndrome): bowel symptoms, no confusion.<br>C. Vitamin E deficiency: hemolysis, lost reflexes.<br>D. Vitamin K deficiency: bleeding.<br>E. Pancreatic insufficiency: fatty stools, not high-calcium confusion and vomiting.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (Vitamin D Toxicity)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":126,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":174,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin D is the most toxic fat-soluble vitamin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-26.jpg","cap":"When in excess, vitamin D is the most toxic fat-soluble vitamin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G030-37.jpg","cap":"Vitamin E is the least toxic fat-soluble vitamin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-184-1","s":"Which of the following vitamins, when in excess, is the most toxic?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin D","C. Vitamin E","D. Vitamin K"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin D","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin D</b> is the <b>most toxic</b> fat-soluble vitamin in excess: high calcitriol causes hypercalcemia, hypercalciuria, kidney stones and confusion.<br>A. Vitamin A: also toxic in chronic excess (skin, liver, raised intracranial pressure, birth defects), but less than D.<br>C. Vitamin E: the <b>least</b> toxic fat-soluble vitamin.<br>D. Vitamin K: toxicity is rare; long dosing in infants can cause hemolytic anemia and jaundice.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (Vitamin D Toxicity)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":184,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin K: diet and gut bacteria; makes prothrombin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K from diet and gut bacteria; cycle carboxylates factors II, VII, IX, X","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G030-31.jpg","cap":"Liver γ-carboxylates prothrombin and factors VII, IX, X; Gla binds calcium","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-132-1","s":"Which vitamin is sourced from the diet AND gut bacteria, and what is its correct function?","o":["A. Vitamin K, RBC maturation","B. Vitamin E, antioxidant","C. Vitamin C, calcium absorption","D. Vitamin K, prothrombin production","E. Vitamin A, visual acuity"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K, prothrombin production","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> <b>Vitamin K</b> comes from green vegetables and from <b>gut bacteria</b>. The liver needs it to make active <b>prothrombin</b> (factor II) and factors VII, IX and X.<br>A. Vitamin K is right, but red-cell maturation needs folate and vitamin B12.<br>B. Vitamin E is an antioxidant, but gut bacteria don't make it.<br>C. Vitamin C doesn't drive calcium absorption; vitamin D does.<br>E. Vitamin A supports vision, but gut bacteria don't make it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin K)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":132,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":180,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin K deficiency → less active prothrombin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-31.jpg","cap":"Gla residues on prothrombin chelate calcium to bind platelet phospholipids","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G030-33.jpg","cap":"Deficiency: hypoprothrombinemia, less active prothrombin, longer clotting times","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-136-1","s":"What causes the bleeding tendency in vitamin K deficiency?","o":["A. Decreased thrombin","B. Increased platelet production","C. Failure of fibrin crosslinking","D. Inhibition of thrombin formation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased thrombin (hypoprothrombinemia: less active prothrombin/factor II)","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Decreased thrombin.</b> Without vitamin K the liver can't add γ-carboxyl groups (Gla, γ-carboxyglutamate) to prothrombin, so there is little <b>active prothrombin</b> (hypoprothrombinemia), too little thrombin forms, and clotting times lengthen.<br>B. Platelet production is normal.<br>C. Fibrin crosslinking is factor XIII, which needs no vitamin K.<br>D. Nothing actively inhibits thrombin formation; the factors are just made inactive. \"Inhibition\" describes anticoagulant drugs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (The -carboxyglutamates of prothrombin are good chelators of calcium. The prothr)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":136,"f":"F0998"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Warfarin blocks vitamin K reductases","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-32.jpg","cap":"Dicoumarol and warfarin block reductases that form and recycle active vitamin K","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K cycle: warfarin inhibits vitamin K reductase and epoxide reductase","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-160-1","s":"What is the mechanism of action of warfarin as an anticoagulant?","o":["A. It inhibits the synthesis of clotting factors directly in the liver","B. It blocks Vitamin K absorption in the small intestine","C. It prevents the recycling of active Vitamin K by inhibiting reductases","D. It chelates calcium, reducing clotting factor activation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"It prevents the recycling of active Vitamin K by inhibiting reductases","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Warfarin blocks the <b>reductases</b> (vitamin K epoxide reductase and vitamin K reductase) that recycle vitamin K to its active hydroquinone form. Without it, factors II, VII, IX and X are not γ-carboxylated and can't work.<br>A. The liver still makes the factor proteins; they just lack Gla (γ-carboxyglutamate) residues.<br>B. Warfarin doesn't affect vitamin K absorption.<br>D. Calcium chelation (citrate in blood-collection tubes) is a different anticoagulant mechanism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Dicoumarol And Warfarin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":160,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1013-137-1","s":"What two molecules inhibit the reductases involved in the formation and recycling of active Vitamin K?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Dicoumarol and warfarin (Coumadin)","a":null,"why":"<b>Dicoumarol and warfarin (Coumadin).</b> They block vitamin K reductase and vitamin K epoxide reductase, so active vitamin K (the hydroquinone) is not regenerated. Factors II, VII, IX and X then lack Gla residues (γ-carboxyglutamate) and can't bind calcium.<br>Common mix-up: warfarin does <b>not</b> block vitamin K absorption or chelate calcium; it stops vitamin K recycling.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Dicoumarol And Warfarin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":137,"f":"F1013"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Newborn bleeding: sterile gut, no vitamin K","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-33.jpg","cap":"Newborn deficiency from sterile intestines: hemorrhagic disease of the newborn","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"},{"src":"sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K source: diet and gut bacteria","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F0950-10-1","s":"A newborn has a prolonged bleeding time. Laboratory tests show normal platelet counts but increased prothrombin time. Which vitamin is most likely deficient?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin K","C. Vitamin D","D. Vitamin C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin K.</b> Newborns have a <b>sterile gut</b> (no vitamin K-making bacteria) and little stored vitamin K, so they make too few active factors II, VII, IX and X: bleeding with a long <b>prothrombin time</b> (PT) but normal platelets (hemorrhagic disease of the newborn).<br>A. Vitamin A: eye signs.<br>C. Vitamin D: rickets.<br>D. Vitamin C: scurvy; clotting tests are normal.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (A newborn does well with breast feeding. Two days later, the mother calls franti)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":10,"f":"F0950"}]},{"id":"F1013-134-1","s":"A distraught mother calls your office because her newborn is bleeding from the nostrils and umbilical cord. The most likely cause is:","o":["A. Vitamin K deficiency due to sterile intestines in newborns","B. Vitamin C deficiency due to citrus-poor diet during pregnancy","C. Vitamin E deficiency due to malabsorption during pregnancy","D. Biotin deficiency due to ingesting too many raw eggs during pregnancy"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K deficiency due to sterile intestines in newborns","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Newborns have <b>sterile intestines</b>, so no bacteria make vitamin K, and breast milk has little. Factors II, VII, IX and X stay inactive and the baby bleeds (hemorrhagic disease of the newborn); a vitamin K shot at birth prevents it.<br>B. Vitamin C deficiency is not a cause of newborn bleeding.<br>C. Vitamin E deficiency: hemolysis, not bleeding.<br>D. Biotin deficiency (raw-egg avidin binds biotin): rash, hair loss, not bleeding.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (A newborn does well with breast feeding. Two days later, the mother calls franti)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":134,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":147,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":127,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":130,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":138,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":138,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3881-158-1","s":"Why are newborns at risk of Vitamin K deficiency?","o":["A. They are unable to absorb Vitamin K due to immature bile production","B. Their intestines lack gut flora to synthesize Vitamin K","C. Maternal Vitamin K is not transferred through the placenta","D. Vitamin K is degraded rapidly in their immature liver"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Their intestines lack gut flora to synthesize Vitamin K","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> A newborn's gut is <b>sterile</b>, so no bacteria make vitamin K, and the baby bleeds (hemorrhagic disease of the newborn).<br>A. Bile production is not the limiting problem.<br>C. Only a little vitamin K crosses the placenta, which keeps stores low, but some does; the lecture's cause is the sterile gut.<br>D. The liver doesn't rapidly destroy vitamin K.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (A newborn does well with breast feeding. Two days later, the mother calls franti)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":158,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Long clotting time, normal platelets → vitamin K","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-33.jpg","cap":"Hypoprothrombinemia: increased coagulation times and bleeding tendency","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"},{"src":"sl/G030-31.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K needed to make active factors II, VII, IX, X in liver","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F0950-19-1","s":"A 62-year-old man presents with bleeding gums and poor wound healing. Laboratory studies reveal normal platelet counts but prolonged clotting times. Which vitamin deficiency is the most likely cause?","o":["A. Vitamin C","B. Vitamin D","C. Vitamin K","D. Vitamin E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin K.</b> The key is the lab: <b>prolonged clotting times</b> with normal platelets means too few active clotting factors (II, VII, IX, X), which need vitamin K.<br>A. Vitamin C: bleeding gums and poor wound healing come from weak collagen, but <b>clotting tests are normal</b> in scurvy.<br>B. Vitamin D: bone disease.<br>D. Vitamin E: hemolysis, neurologic deficits.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (The -carboxyglutamates of prothrombin are good chelators of calcium. The prothr)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":19,"f":"F0950"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Cephalosporins cause vitamin K deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-33.jpg","cap":"Cefoperazone, cefamandole, moxalactam cause deficiency by a warfarin-like mechanism","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 33"},{"src":"sl/G030-30.jpg","cap":"Vitamin K recycling reductases that warfarin-like drugs block","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-186-1","s":"16 year-old female presents to your office for a wellness visit. She states that for the past 3 months, she noticed that she has had an increased bleeding tendency. She states that 3 months ago, she was treated with a Cephalosporin for an infection. You are suspecting which of the following vitamin deficiencies?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin D","C. Vitamin K","D. Vitamin E"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin K","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin K.</b> Some cephalosporins block vitamin K recycling in a <b>warfarin-like</b> way, and antibiotics also kill the gut bacteria that make vitamin K. Fewer active clotting factors means a bleeding tendency.<br>A. Vitamin A: eye and skin signs.<br>B. Vitamin D: bone disease.<br>D. Vitamin E: hemolysis and neurologic deficits, not bleeding.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":186,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F3881-162-1","s":"Which of the following antibiotics may lead to Vitamin K deficiency and increased bleeding risk?","o":["A. Penicillin","B. Cephalosporins like cefoperazone","C. Macrolides like erythromycin","D. Fluoroquinolones like ciprofloxacin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cephalosporins like cefoperazone","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Certain cephalosporins (cefoperazone, cefamandole, moxalactam) cause vitamin K deficiency by a <b>warfarin-like mechanism</b>: they block vitamin K recycling, so clotting factors stay inactive and bleeding risk rises.<br>A. Penicillin: no warfarin-like action on vitamin K.<br>C. Erythromycin (a macrolide): no warfarin-like action on vitamin K.<br>D. Ciprofloxacin (a fluoroquinolone): no warfarin-like action on vitamin K.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Vitamin K Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":162,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin E = membrane antioxidant","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-37.jpg","cap":"α-tocopherol structure; antioxidant against free radicals; least toxic fat-soluble vitamin","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 37"},{"src":"sl/D039-8.jpg","cap":"Vitamin E protects membranes, preventing lipid peroxidation of polyunsaturated fatty acids","where":"2026 spring interactive, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-139-1","s":"Which fat soluble vitamin functions as an antioxidant?","o":["A. Vitamin C","B. Vitamin K","C. Zinc","D. Vitamin E","E. Vitamin A"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin E","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin E</b> is the fat-soluble <b>antioxidant</b>; it protects membrane lipids from free radicals.<br>A. Vitamin C: also an antioxidant, but <b>water-soluble</b>.<br>B. Vitamin K: carboxylates clotting factors.<br>C. Zinc: a mineral, not a vitamin.<br>E. Vitamin A: its roles are vision, epithelial upkeep and gene expression; the lecture's antioxidant vitamin is E.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 37 (Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":139,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":185,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"D039-9-1","s":"Which of the following vitamins acts as an antioxidant in the body?","o":["A. Vitamin K","B. Niacin","C. Thiamine","D. Pyridoxine","E. Vitamin E"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin E","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Vitamin E</b> (α-tocopherol) is the main <b>lipid antioxidant</b>: it sits in cell membranes and stops free radicals from oxidizing polyunsaturated fatty acids (lipid peroxidation).<br>A. Vitamin K: γ-carboxylates clotting factors.<br>B. Niacin (vitamin B3): makes NAD⁺ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) for energy reactions.<br>C. Thiamine (vitamin B1): makes thiamine pyrophosphate for pyruvate dehydrogenase.<br>D. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6): makes pyridoxal phosphate for amino acid reactions.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 37 (Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":9,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":5,"f":"F0186"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-157-1","s":"Which of the following is true regarding Vitamin E?","o":["A. Gamma Tocopherol is the most active form of vitamin E","B. Vitamin E is the most toxic of all fat soluble vitamins","C. Vitamin E deficiencies are most often seen in adults","D. Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the prevention of non enzymatic oxidation of cell components by free radicals"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin E acts as an antioxidant in the prevention of non enzymatic oxidation of cell components by free radicals","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Vitamin E is an <b>antioxidant</b> that prevents nonenzymatic oxidation of cell components, especially membrane lipids, by oxygen and free radicals.<br>A. False: α-tocopherol is most active; γ-tocopherol is only the main dietary form.<br>B. False: vitamin E is the <b>least</b> toxic; vitamin D is the most toxic.<br>C. False: deficiency is almost entirely in <b>premature infants</b>, or adults with fat malabsorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 37 (Vitamin E)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 38 (Deficiency of Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":157,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F3881-164-1","s":"How does Vitamin E protect erythrocytes from hemolysis?","o":["A. By stabilizing hemoglobin structure","B. By acting as an antioxidant to prevent membrane lipid peroxidation","C. By enhancing glucose uptake into erythrocytes","D. By increasing erythropoietin production in the kidneys"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"By acting as an antioxidant to prevent membrane lipid peroxidation","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Vitamin E sits in the red-cell membrane and traps free radicals, so membrane <b>polyunsaturated fatty acids are not peroxidized</b>. Without it, red cells become sensitive to peroxide, their membranes break, and they hemolyze.<br>A. It does not bind or stabilize hemoglobin.<br>C. It does not change glucose uptake.<br>D. It does not affect erythropoietin, the kidney hormone that drives red-cell production.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 37 (Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":164,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"α-Tocopherol is the most active vitamin E","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-37.jpg","cap":"α-tocopherol most active form; γ-tocopherol main US dietary form","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-132-1","s":"Which of the following is the active form of vitamin E in the body?","o":["A. Y-tocopherol","B. B-Tocopherol","C. a-Tocopherol","D. delta-Tocopherol"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"α-Tocopherol","a":"C","why":"<b>C. α-Tocopherol</b> is the <b>most active</b> form of vitamin E in humans.<br>A. γ-Tocopherol: the <b>main form in the US diet</b> and supplements, but less active.<br>B. β-Tocopherol: a minor form with low activity.<br>D. δ-Tocopherol: a minor form with low activity.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 37 (Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":132,"f":"F0998"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin E deficiency: hemolysis, lost reflexes","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-38.jpg","cap":"Vitamin E deficiency: hemolysis, abnormal membranes, lost reflexes, poor balance, vision","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/D039-8.jpg","cap":"Vitamin E prevents membrane lipid peroxidation, protecting red cells and nerves","where":"2026 spring interactive, slide 8"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-169-1","s":"Bloodwork done for a premature infant shows hemolytic anemia and abnormal cellular membranes. Which deficiency is most likely?","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Vitamin E","C. Vitamin B12","D. Folic acid"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin E","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin E.</b> Deficiency is almost entirely seen in <b>premature infants</b>. Without this membrane antioxidant, red-cell membranes are oxidized, look abnormal, and the cells burst (<b>hemolytic anemia</b>).<br>A. Vitamin D: rickets, not hemolysis.<br>C. Vitamin B12: megaloblastic (big-cell) anemia plus nerve damage.<br>D. Folic acid: megaloblastic anemia, no membrane defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 38 (Deficiency of Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":169,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F3881-168-1","s":"What neurological symptoms might arise from Vitamin E deficiency?","o":["A. Hyperactive reflexes and muscular hypertrophy","B. Loss of deep tendon reflexes and impaired balance","C. Increased proprioception and muscle weakness","D. Visual hallucinations and sensory neuropathy"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Loss of deep tendon reflexes and impaired balance","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Vitamin E deficiency damages nerve membranes, giving <b>loss of deep tendon reflexes</b>, muscle weakness, <b>impaired balance</b> and visual impairment.<br>A. Reflexes are lost, not hyperactive, and muscles weaken rather than grow.<br>C. Proprioception (sense of limb position) is <b>lost</b>, not increased.<br>D. Visual hallucinations are not a feature, so this pair is wrong.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 38 (Deficiency of Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":168,"f":"F3881"}]},{"id":"F1201-132-1","s":"A 12 year old girl is evaluated for complaint of muscle weakness. She states she finds it difficult to maintain her balance. On examination, there is decreased strength and decreased deep tendon reflexes in all extremities. Her blood work shows hemolytic anemia and abnormal cellular membranes. What deficiency is most likely causing her symptoms?","o":["A. Vitamin E","B. Vitamin D","C. Vitamin B12","D. Folic acid"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin E","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vitamin E.</b> Muscle weakness, poor balance and decreased deep tendon reflexes (<b>neurologic deficit</b>) plus <b>hemolytic anemia with abnormal cell membranes</b> are vitamin E deficiency.<br>B. Vitamin D: bone pain, rickets; no hemolysis.<br>C. Vitamin B12: neurologic signs too, but with megaloblastic (big-cell) anemia, not membrane-damage hemolysis.<br>D. Folic acid: megaloblastic anemia, no neurologic signs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 38 (Deficiency of Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":132,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":141,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":141,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F1013-122-1","s":"A 28 year old male presents to you complaining of vision problems. Physical exam shows absence of deep tendon reflexes and muscle weakness. CBC shows low hematocrit (RBCs) and blood smear shows atypical cell membranes. What is your most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Vitamin A deficiency","B. Vitamin E deficiency","C. Vitamin K excess","D. Vitamin E excess","E. Vitamin D deficiency"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin E deficiency","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin E deficiency.</b> It shows two things: <b>hemolysis</b> (low hematocrit, abnormal red-cell membranes from lipid peroxidation) and <b>neurologic deficits</b> (lost deep tendon reflexes, weakness, poor balance, vision problems).<br>A. Vitamin A deficiency: night blindness, Bitot spots; no hemolysis.<br>C. Vitamin K excess: hemolysis and jaundice in infants; no lost reflexes.<br>D. Vitamin E excess: least toxic vitamin; no such picture.<br>E. Vitamin D deficiency: bone disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 38 (Deficiency of Vitamin E)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":122,"f":"F1013"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":150,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":130,"f":"F0180"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":182,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Vitamin E deficiency needs fat malabsorption","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-38.jpg","cap":"Deficiency almost only in premature infants or adults with defective lipid absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 38"},{"src":"sl/G030-6.jpg","cap":"Fat-soluble vitamins need lipid absorption via bile micelles and chylomicrons","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-166-1","s":"Why is Vitamin E deficiency almost exclusively seen in premature infants and individuals with fat malabsorption disorders?","o":["A. It requires lipid absorption for transport and storage","B. Premature infants have higher metabolic demands for antioxidants","C. It is synthesized by gut bacteria, which are absent in these groups","D. These individuals have increased oxidative stress that depletes Vitamin E"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"It requires lipid absorption for transport and storage","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Vitamin E is fat-soluble, so it needs normal <b>fat absorption</b> (bile salt micelles, chylomicrons) to get in and be carried around. Premature infants have low stores and immature fat absorption; adults become deficient only with fat malabsorption or lipid transport defects.<br>B. Higher antioxidant demand alone does not explain it.<br>C. Gut bacteria make vitamin K, not vitamin E.<br>D. Oxidative stress alone rarely depletes it; poor absorption does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 38 (Deficiency of Vitamin E)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":166,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L34","label":"Match each fat-soluble vitamin to its role","slides":[{"src":"sl/G030-40.jpg","cap":"Summary table: names, active forms and functions of vitamins A, D, K, E","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 40"},{"src":"sl/G030-10.jpg","cap":"β-carotene lowers heart disease, lung and skin cancer, macular degeneration","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-172-1","s":"Match (Vitamin E, Vitamin A, Vitamin K, Vitamin D): Responsible for bleeding and clotting / Hemolytic anemia, neuro deficit / Reduces risk for heart disease, lung and skin cancer, and macular degeneration / Rickets / Controls expression of gene for keratin / Least toxic of all fat soluble vitamins / Deficiency increases risk of bone fractures","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"K - bleeding and clotting; E - hemolytic anemia, neuro deficit; A - reduces risk for heart disease, lung and skin cancer, macular degeneration; D - rickets; A - controls expression of gene for keratin; E - least toxic fat-soluble vitamin; D - deficiency increases risk of bone fractures","a":null,"why":"<b>K: bleeding and clotting. E: hemolytic anemia and neurologic deficit; least toxic. A: β-carotene lowers risk of heart disease, lung and skin cancer, and macular degeneration; retinoic acid controls the keratin gene. D: rickets; deficiency raises fracture risk.</b><br>Common mix-up: vitamin D, not E, is the <b>most</b> toxic fat-soluble vitamin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 40 (Summary Of Fat Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":172,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Water-soluble vitamins need frequent intake","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-5.jpg","cap":"Water-soluble vitamins are excreted rather than stored; only niacin and pyridoxine are toxic","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/G030-6.jpg","cap":"Fat-soluble vitamins need bile salt micelles and accumulate in tissues to toxic levels","where":"2026 lecture: L34 Fat-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-15-1","s":"Which of the following is a general property of water-soluble vitamins?","o":["A. Require protein for transport.","B. Need fat for absorption.","C. Less readily excreted by kidney","D. Required in frequent doses.","E. Deficiency is rare."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Required in frequent doses.","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Required in frequent doses.</b> Water-soluble vitamins are <b>not stored</b> (vitamin B12 is the exception) and the kidney <b>excretes</b> the excess, so they must be eaten regularly.<br>A. Require protein for transport: true of fat-soluble vitamins, which ride on carrier proteins and lipoproteins.<br>B. Need fat for absorption: fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) need bile salt micelles.<br>C. Less readily excreted: true of fat-soluble vitamins; water-soluble ones are readily excreted.<br>E. Deficiency is rare: false; it is common in alcoholics, pregnancy and malabsorption.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 5 (Heterogeneous group of compounds and they differ chemically)","L34 Fat-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (How are fat soluble vitamins absorbed ?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":15,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":7,"f":"F0186"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Toxic exceptions: niacin and pyridoxine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-5.jpg","cap":"Exceptions to non-toxicity: niacin causes flushing, itching, liver damage; pyridoxine causes neuropathy","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-189-1","s":"Water soluble vitamins are not prone to toxicity from excess since they are being excreted. However, there are two water soluble vitamins that actually do cause toxicities. Name these vitamins.","o":["A. Folic acid and Vitamin B1","B. Vitamin B3 and Vitamin B6","C. Biotin and Vitamin C","D. Vitamin B9 and Thiamine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B3 and Vitamin B6","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin B3 (niacin) and vitamin B6 (pyridoxine).</b> Most water-soluble vitamins are excreted, so excess is harmless. The two exceptions: <b>niacin causes flushing, itching and liver damage</b>; <b>pyridoxine causes sensory and peripheral neuropathy</b>.<br>A. Folic acid (B9) and B1 (thiamine): not toxic in excess.<br>C. Biotin (B7) and vitamin C: not toxic in excess.<br>D. B9 and thiamine: the same pair as A under other names.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 5 (Heterogeneous group of compounds and they differ chemically)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":189,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0577-154-1","s":"Q1 - Which of the following is false about general aspects of water soluble vitamins?","o":["A. Deficiency of water soluble vitamins often presents in tissues that rapidly turn over (Skin, Gut)","B. Water soluble vitamins includes Vitamins B and C","C. Water soluble vitamins have RDA values to eliminate deficiency symptoms, and not to optimize health.","D. Pyroxidine (Vitamin B6) cause cause flushing, itching and liver damage."],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyroxidine (Vitamin B6) cause cause flushing, itching and liver damage.","a":"D","why":"<b>D is false.</b> Flushing, itching and liver damage come from excess <b>niacin (B3)</b>. Excess <b>pyridoxine (B6)</b> causes <b>sensory and peripheral neuropathy</b> instead.<br>A. True: rapidly turning over tissues (skin, gut, immune system) show deficiency first.<br>B. True: the water-soluble group is vitamin C plus the B complex.<br>C. True: RDA (recommended dietary allowance) values are set to prevent deficiency, not to optimize health.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 5 (Heterogeneous group of compounds and they differ chemically)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":154,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":134,"f":"F0180"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Subpopulations differ in vitamin needs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-5.jpg","cap":"Rapid-turnover tissues most at risk; children, pregnancy, aging, alcoholics at risk; RDA prevents deficiency","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 5"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-189-1","s":"Which of the following is TRUE of water soluble vitamins?","o":["A. All tissues are at high risk of deficiency and rapidly show symptoms of deficiency","B. They have RDA values as guides for optimal health","C. Subpopulations have different needs and risks for deficiency","D. Require bile salts for absorption"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Subpopulations have different needs and risks for deficiency","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Subpopulations have different needs and risks.</b> Growing children, pregnancy, aging, malabsorption syndromes and alcoholics are at higher risk of deficiency.<br>A. False: not all tissues. <b>Rapidly turning over tissues</b> (skin, gut, immune system) are most at risk.<br>B. False: RDA (recommended dietary allowance) values only <b>prevent deficiency</b>; there are no criteria for optimal health.<br>D. False: bile salts are needed for <b>fat-soluble</b> vitamins (A, D, E, K).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 5 (Heterogeneous group of compounds and they differ chemically)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 4 (Fat Soluble vs Water Soluble Vitamins)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":189,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Match B vitamins to coenzyme roles","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-7.jpg","cap":"Review list pairing each B vitamin with its reaction or enzyme","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G031-6.jpg","cap":"Table of water-soluble vitamins with active forms: TPP, FMN/FAD, NAD+, coenzyme A, THF","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-194-1","s":"Mix and Match… TPP; Riboflavin; Niacin; Pantothenic Acid; Vitamin B7 — (1) Is a precursor for FMN (2) Is a cofactor for bicarbonate-dependent carboxylations (3) Is a precursor of coenzyme A (4) Is a precursor for NAD+ (5) Is a cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation reactions","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"TPP (B1) = 5; Riboflavin (B2) = 1; Niacin (B3) = 4; Pantothenic acid (B5) = 3; Vitamin B7 (biotin) = 2","a":null,"why":"<b>TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate, from B1) → 5</b>: oxidative decarboxylation. <b>Riboflavin (B2) → 1</b>: makes FMN (flavin mononucleotide) and FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide). <b>Niacin (B3) → 4</b>: makes NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide). <b>Pantothenic acid (B5) → 3</b>: makes coenzyme A. <b>Biotin (B7) → 2</b>: carries carbon dioxide for bicarbonate-dependent carboxylations.<br>Common mix-up: biotin <b>adds</b> carbon dioxide (carboxylation); thiamine <b>removes</b> it (decarboxylation).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":194,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F3881-171-1","s":"Match each vitamin to the following description: A. Thiamine (B1) B. Riboflavin (B2) C. Niacin (B3) D. Pantothenic acid (B5) E. Biotin (B7) F. Pyridoxine (B6) G. Folic acid (B9) H. Cobalamin (B12) — Oxidative decarboxylation; ALA synthase; Precursor of THF; Oxidoreduction reaction; Precursor of Coenzyme A; Precursor of NAD+ and NAD; Bicarbonate dependent carboxylation; Methylmalonyl CoA mutase and methionine synthase","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Oxidative decarboxylation - A (B1); ALA synthase - F (B6); Precursor of THF - G (B9); Oxidoreduction - B (B2); Precursor of CoA - D (B5); Precursor of NAD+/NADP+ - C (B3); Bicarbonate-dependent carboxylation - E (B7); Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase and methionine synthase - H (B12)","a":null,"why":"<b>Energy-releasing:</b> thiamine (B1) → oxidative decarboxylation; riboflavin (B2) → oxidoreduction; niacin (B3) → NAD+ and NADP+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide and its phosphate); pantothenic acid (B5) → coenzyme A; biotin (B7) → bicarbonate-dependent carboxylation. <b>Hematopoietic:</b> pyridoxine (B6) → ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase; folic acid (B9) → THF (tetrahydrofolate); cobalamin (B12) → methylmalonyl-CoA mutase and methionine synthase.<br>Common mix-up: <b>B6</b>, not B12, runs the first step of heme synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 6 (Sources and Functional Forms)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":171,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Vitamin C: dopamine to norepinephrine","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-9.jpg","cap":"Ascorbate drives dopamine-to-norepinephrine conversion; full list of vitamin C functions","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-102-1","s":"A malnourished elderly man presents with fatigue and easy bruising. In addition to defective collagen synthesis, which of the following additional biochemical processes is most likely impaired due to the same vitamin deficiency?","o":["A. Conversion of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA","B. Synthesis of norepinephrine from dopamine","C. Carboxylation of pyruvate to oxaloacetate","D. Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA","E. Formation of tetrahydrofolate from folic acid"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Synthesis of norepinephrine from dopamine","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Norepinephrine from dopamine.</b> Easy bruising and defective collagen mean <b>vitamin C deficiency</b>. Vitamin C is also the cofactor for <b>dopamine beta-hydroxylase</b>, which turns dopamine into norepinephrine.<br>A. Methylmalonyl-CoA (coenzyme A) to succinyl-CoA: needs vitamin B12.<br>C. Pyruvate to oxaloacetate (pyruvate carboxylase): needs biotin (B7).<br>D. Pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (pyruvate dehydrogenase): needs thiamine (B1).<br>E. Folic acid to tetrahydrofolate: dihydrofolate reductase, a folate-pathway step; no vitamin C.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (Vitamin C - Ascorbic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 13 (Review of Objective 2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":102,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Scurvy → collagen synthesis fails","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-11.jpg","cap":"Scurvy photos: petechiae on skin and swollen, bleeding gums","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11"},{"src":"sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Prolyl hydroxylase needs ascorbate to add OH to proline in collagen","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-117-1","s":"A 45-year-old man comes to the clinic with complaints of gum bleeding, fatigue, and poor wound healing. He admits to a diet lacking fruits and vegetables. Which of the following processes is most affected by his condition?","o":["A) Collagen synthesis","B) Protein digestion","C) Glucose metabolism","D) Fat absorption"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Collagen synthesis","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Collagen synthesis.</b> Bleeding gums, poor wound healing and no fruits or vegetables mean <b>scurvy (vitamin C deficiency)</b>. Vitamin C is needed to <b>hydroxylate proline and lysine</b> in collagen, so gums and vessels become weak.<br>B. Protein digestion: gastric and pancreatic proteases; no vitamin C role.<br>C. Glucose metabolism: relies on B vitamins such as thiamine and niacin.<br>D. Fat absorption: needs bile salts and lipase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (Vitamin C - Ascorbic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":117,"f":"F2303"}]},{"id":"F0928-25-1","s":"A 77-year-old male is brought to your clinic at the insistence of his daughter for a regular check up appointment. The patient lives alone and has not seen a physician in several years. According to the daughter, the patient has been reclusive ever since his wife passed away and has been caring for himself. On physical exam, the patient is very thin and disheveled. He has poor dentition with swollen gingivae. The rest of his exam is within normal limits. Which of the following is most likely affected in this patient?","o":["A. Coagulation factor synthesis","B. Bone metabolism","C. Amino acid and carbohydrate metabolism","D. Collagen synthesis","E. DNA synthesis"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Collagen synthesis","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Collagen synthesis.</b> An isolated elderly man eating poorly with <b>swollen gums</b> has <b>scurvy (vitamin C deficiency)</b>. Without vitamin C, proline and lysine in collagen are not hydroxylated.<br>A. Coagulation factor synthesis: vitamin K.<br>B. Bone metabolism: vitamin D.<br>C. Amino acid and carbohydrate metabolism: B vitamins such as B6 (pyridoxine) and B1 (thiamine).<br>E. DNA synthesis: folate and vitamin B12.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":25,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":7,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":43,"f":"D006"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2296-191-1","s":"A 42-year-old female patient rushes to the ER complaining of a few fallen out teeth. Before referring her to the dentist, you, as you definitely should, gather more information and take a thorough history. You find out that besides fallen teeth, she suffers from bleeding gums. Apparently, she participates in some experiment and has not been eating fruits and vegetables for 8 months. Upon the physical examination, you notice some red spots on her legs. This patient is suffering from the inability to synthetize…","o":["A. Hemoglobin","B. Glycogen","C. Fibrillin","D. Collagen","E. Insulin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Collagen","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Collagen.</b> Lost teeth, bleeding gums and red spots after eight months without fruit or vegetables mean <b>scurvy</b>. Vitamin C is needed to <b>hydroxylate proline and lysine</b>, so stable collagen cannot form.<br>A. Hemoglobin: needs iron, and B6 (pyridoxine) for heme.<br>B. Glycogen: no vitamin C role.<br>C. Fibrillin: defective in Marfan syndrome, a genetic disease.<br>E. Insulin: made by pancreatic beta cells; not vitamin C dependent.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":191,"f":"F2296"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Vitamin C hydroxylates proline, lysine in collagen","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Prolyl hydroxylase reaction: ascorbate needed to hydroxylate proline in pro-alpha collagen chains","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/F2296-193.jpg","cap":"Proline and lysyl hydroxylases cycle Fe2+ to Fe3+; ascorbic acid reduces it back","where":"2023 TA review, slide 193"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-194-1","s":"Which of the following vitamins are necessary for the hydroxylation of proline and lysine to make collagen?","o":["A. Vitamin C","B. Copper","C. Biotin","D. Iron","E. Vitamin B6"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin C","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Vitamin C.</b> Prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases hydroxylate <b>proline and lysine</b> in collagen; vitamin C keeps their iron in the reduced <b>Fe2+ (ferrous)</b> state.<br>B. Copper: a mineral; cofactor for lysyl oxidase, which cross-links collagen after hydroxylation.<br>C. Biotin: carboxylation reactions.<br>D. Iron: sits in the hydroxylase, but it is a mineral, not a vitamin.<br>E. Vitamin B6: pyridoxal phosphate for transamination and ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (Vitamin C - Ascorbic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":194,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F0998-147-1","s":"A deficiency of which of the following vitamins would most likely lead to impaired lysine hydroxylation in collagen?","o":["A. Thiamine","B. Riboflavin","C. Niacin","D. Ascorbic acid"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascorbic acid","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C).</b> Lysyl hydroxylase needs vitamin C to keep its iron as <b>Fe2+ (ferrous iron)</b>. Without it, <b>lysine and proline in collagen are not hydroxylated</b>.<br>A. Thiamine (B1): oxidative decarboxylation, e.g. pyruvate dehydrogenase.<br>B. Riboflavin (B2): FMN and FAD (flavin mononucleotide, flavin adenine dinucleotide) for oxidoreduction.<br>C. Niacin (B3): NAD+ and NADP+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide and its phosphate).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (Vitamin C - Ascorbic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":147,"f":"F0998"}]},{"id":"F3881-173-1","s":"Which of the following is true of the functions of Vitamin C?","o":["A. Involved in the hydroxylation of Pro and Lys in collagen","B. Involved in the synthesis of dopamine from norepinephrine","C. Does not have antioxidant properties","D. Involved in excretion of dietary iron from small intestine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Involved in the hydroxylation of Pro and Lys in collagen","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen.</b> Vitamin C keeps the hydroxylase's iron as <b>Fe2+ (ferrous iron)</b>. It also makes carnitine from trimethyllysine.<br>B. Reversed: vitamin C helps make <b>norepinephrine from dopamine</b>, not dopamine from norepinephrine.<br>C. False: vitamin C <b>is an antioxidant</b>.<br>D. False: vitamin C helps <b>absorb</b> dietary iron in the small intestine; it does not excrete it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (Vitamin C - Ascorbic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":173,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0028-180-1","s":"A 14 year old boy is brought in for evaluation by police after he is found living in the park. He states he has been eating very little other than food scraps. On examination, he has red, swollen gums, bruises all over his body, and small, purple spots on his skin. He states his teeth have become loose and some have fallen out recently. The vitamin deficiency that is causing his symptoms is most likely involved in which process?","o":["A. Calcium absorption from the GI tract","B. Cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation reactions","C. Hydroxylation of Pro and Lys in collagen","D. Precursor of THF"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hydroxylation of Pro and Lys in collagen","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen.</b> Swollen red gums, bruises, petechiae and loose teeth in a poorly fed boy mean <b>scurvy (vitamin C deficiency)</b>.<br>A. Calcium absorption from the gut: vitamin D.<br>B. Cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation: thiamine (B1), as thiamine pyrophosphate.<br>D. Precursor of THF (tetrahydrofolate): folic acid (B9).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (Vitamin C - Ascorbic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":180,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F1013-142-1","s":"A 65-year-old man presents with fatigue, bleeding gums, and easy bruising. Upon examination, he has purple spots on his skin (petechiae) and delayed wound healing. Laboratory tests reveal low Vitamin C levels. Based on the known functions of Vitamin C, which of the following underlying mechanisms is most likely responsible for his symptoms?","o":["A. Impaired hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen, leading to weakened connective tissue.","B. Inhibition of the absorption of dietary iron, causing iron deficiency anemia.","C. Reduced calcium absorption from the GI tract","D. Disruption of oxidative decarboxylation reactions, resulting in Beriberi"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen, leading to weakened connective tissue.","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Impaired hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen.</b> Weak collagen gives fragile capillaries (petechiae, bruising, bleeding gums) and slow wound healing: <b>scurvy</b>.<br>B. Low vitamin C does reduce iron absorption, but anemia does not explain bleeding gums or poor healing.<br>C. Calcium absorption from the gut: vitamin D.<br>D. Oxidative decarboxylation and beriberi: thiamine (B1).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":142,"f":"F1013"}]},{"id":"F2435-8-1","s":"A 75-year-old woman is seen in the emergency room with a fractured arm. Physical examination revealed multiple bruises and perifollicular hemorrhages, periodontitis, and bleeding gums. Her diet consists predominately of weak coffee, bouillon, rolls, and plain pasta. Lab results indicated mild microcytic anemia. Which of the following enzymes should be less active than normal in this patient?","o":["A. Homocysteine methyltransferase","B. γ-Glutamyl carboxylase","C. Dihydrofolate reductase","D. ALA synthase","E. Prolyl hydroxylase"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Prolyl hydroxylase","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Prolyl hydroxylase.</b> Perifollicular hemorrhages, bleeding gums and a diet with no fruit or vegetables mean <b>scurvy</b>. Prolyl hydroxylase needs vitamin C to keep its iron as <b>Fe2+</b>. Low vitamin C also cuts iron absorption, giving the microcytic anemia.<br>A. Homocysteine methyltransferase (methionine synthase): vitamin B12 and folate.<br>B. Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase: vitamin K.<br>C. Dihydrofolate reductase: folate pathway; no vitamin C.<br>D. ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase: vitamin B6.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 9 (Vitamin C - Ascorbic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":8,"f":"F2435"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Vitamin C keeps hydroxylase iron as Fe2+","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-10.jpg","cap":"Hydroxylase contains Fe2+; vitamin C keeps it reduced for proline and lysine hydroxylation","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 10"},{"src":"sl/F2296-193.jpg","cap":"Ascorbate regenerates Fe2+ in the hydroxylases, becoming semidehydroascorbic acid","where":"2023 TA review, slide 193"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-98-1","s":"A patient presents with perifollicular hemorrhages, poor wound healing, and gingival bleeding. Which of the following best describes the vitamin involved?","o":["A. Water-soluble vitamin that maintains iron in the reduced Fe²⁺ state during collagen hydroxylation","B. Fat-soluble vitamin that promotes γ-carboxylation of clotting factors","C. Water-soluble vitamin functioning as NADPH in redox reactions","D. Water-soluble vitamin functioning as PLP in aminotransferase reactions","E. Fat-soluble vitamin functioning as retinoic acid in epithelial differentiation"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Water-soluble vitamin that maintains iron in the reduced Fe²⁺ state during collagen hydroxylation","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Water-soluble vitamin that keeps iron as Fe2+ during collagen hydroxylation</b>, i.e. vitamin C. Perifollicular hemorrhages, poor wound healing and bleeding gums are <b>scurvy</b>.<br>B. Fat-soluble, gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors: vitamin K.<br>C. Water-soluble, NADPH (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate): niacin (B3).<br>D. Water-soluble, PLP (pyridoxal phosphate) in transamination: vitamin B6.<br>E. Fat-soluble, retinoic acid in epithelial differentiation: vitamin A.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":98,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3768-100-1","s":"A 6-year-old child presents with gingival swelling, perifollicular hemorrhages, and poor wound healing. Laboratory evaluation reveals normal platelet count and normal coagulation studies. Which of the following best explains the biochemical basis of this patient’s symptoms?","o":["A. Impaired γ-carboxylation of glutamate residues in clotting factors due to deficiency of a fat-soluble vitamin","B. Decreased activity of lysyl oxidase resulting in defective cross-linking of collagen fibrils","C. Inability to maintain iron in the ferrous (Fe²⁺) state required for hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen","D. Impaired synthesis of tetrahydrofolate leading to decreased thymidine production","E. Defective pyridoxal phosphate–dependent transamination reactions in connective tissue cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Inability to maintain iron in the ferrous (Fe²⁺) state required for hydroxylation of proline and lysine in collagen","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Iron cannot be kept as Fe2+ (ferrous) for proline and lysine hydroxylation.</b> This is <b>scurvy</b>. Normal platelets and clotting tests show the bleeding comes from weak collagen in vessel walls.<br>A. Vitamin K deficiency: would prolong clotting tests, which are normal here.<br>B. Lysyl oxidase: needs <b>copper</b>, not vitamin C.<br>D. Low THF (tetrahydrofolate): folate deficiency, megaloblastic anemia.<br>E. PLP (pyridoxal phosphate) defects: vitamin B6 deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":100,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Scurvy signs → vitamin C deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-11.jpg","cap":"Scurvy: sore spongy gums, petechiae, bruising, poor wound healing, with photos","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-164-1","s":"50 year old man comes in with spongy gums, petechiae, and bruising with poor wound healing. What vitamin deficiency does he have?","o":["A. Vitamin A","B. Vitamin B","C. Vitamin C","D. Vitamin D","E. Vitamin K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin C","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin C.</b> <b>Spongy gums, petechiae, bruising and poor wound healing</b> are the scurvy signs listed in lecture.<br>A. Vitamin A: night blindness, dry eyes and skin.<br>B. Vitamin B: a group, not one vitamin; B deficiencies give beriberi, pellagra, cheilosis or anemia.<br>D. Vitamin D: rickets, osteomalacia.<br>E. Vitamin K: bleeding from low clotting factors; no spongy gums or poor wound healing.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":164,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F1201-135-1","s":"A 14 year old boy is brought in for evaluation by police after he is found living in the park. He states he has been eating very little other than food scraps. On examination, he has red, swollen gums, bruises all over his body, and small, purple spots on his skin. He states his teeth have become loose and some have fallen out recently. What deficiency is causing his symptoms?","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Vitamin C","C. Cobalamin","D. Niacin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin C","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin C.</b> Red swollen gums, bruises, petechiae and loose teeth in a poorly fed teen are <b>scurvy</b>; weak collagen makes gums and capillaries fragile.<br>A. Vitamin D: rickets in children (bowed legs, bone pain).<br>C. Cobalamin (B12): megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy.<br>D. Niacin (B3): pellagra (diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 10 (Vit C)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":135,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":145,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":145,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0035-196-1","s":"A patient presents to you with the following purple spots on their extremities and flank. You also see larger bruises on their extensor surfaces. What deficiency do you most likely suspect?","o":["A. Pyridoxine","B. Cobalamin","C. Ascorbic acid","D. Pantothenic acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ascorbic acid","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ascorbic acid (vitamin C).</b> Purple spots (<b>petechiae</b>) and large bruises are <b>scurvy</b>: capillaries are fragile because collagen is poorly hydroxylated.<br>A. Pyridoxine (B6): neuropathy and sideroblastic anemia.<br>B. Cobalamin (B12): megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy.<br>D. Pantothenic acid (B5): fatigue, sleep disturbance, impaired coordination; rare.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Photo of petechiae on skin; stem describes them, so not required","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":196,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F0577-158-1","s":"Q3 - Manifestations of Scurvy include all of the following except:","o":["A. Spongy gums","B. Petechiae","C. Poor Wound Healing","D. Peripheral Edema","E. Alopecia","F. Both D and E"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"Both D and E","a":"F","why":"<b>F. Both D and E.</b> Scurvy causes <b>spongy gums, petechiae, bruising and poor wound healing</b>, so A, B and C are true features.<br>D. Peripheral edema: a sign of <b>wet beriberi</b> (thiamine, B1).<br>E. Alopecia (hair loss): a sign of <b>biotin (B7) deficiency</b>.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 11 (Deficiency:)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":158,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":138,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Thiamine (TPP) → oxidative decarboxylation, PDH","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-17.jpg","cap":"TPP is cofactor for PDH, alpha-KG dehydrogenase, branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, transketolase","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/G031-18.jpg","cap":"Map of TPP steps: pyruvate→acetyl-CoA, alpha-KG→succinyl-CoA, branched-chain keto acids, pentose shunt","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/G031-15.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex needs five cofactors: TPP, lipoic acid, CoA, FAD, NAD+","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-20-1","s":"Deficiency of thiamine in alcoholics may lead to Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. This syndrome is associated with energy deficit in the brain because thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP) is:","o":["A. Necessary for oxidation of alcohol","B. An essential cofactor for Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","C. Necessary for utilizing amino acids for energy via transamination","D. A cofactor for Transketolase","E. A major cofactor for Reduction reactions"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"An essential cofactor for Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","a":"B","why":"<b>B. TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) is an essential cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase.</b> Without it, pyruvate cannot become acetyl-coenzyme A, the citric acid cycle slows, and the glucose-dependent <b>brain runs short of energy</b>.<br>A. Alcohol oxidation: alcohol dehydrogenase uses NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide).<br>C. Transamination: uses pyridoxal phosphate (B6).<br>D. True that TPP serves transketolase, but that pentose phosphate step makes sugars, not energy.<br>E. Reduction reactions: use NADPH (from niacin), not TPP.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (PDH Complex (PDHC))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":20,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":10,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":24,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":8,"f":"F0078"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0028-174-1","s":"Beriberi is characterized by cardiovascular or neurologic symptoms. This disease is caused by a deficiency of a vitamin that can be described as:","o":["A. Cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation reactions","B. Not found in plants","C. Precursor for an electron carrier","D. Necessary for lysyl oxidase function"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation reactions","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cofactor for oxidative decarboxylation.</b> Beriberi is <b>thiamine (B1)</b> deficiency. Its active form, TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate), serves pyruvate dehydrogenase, alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase and branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase.<br>B. Not found in plants: vitamin B12.<br>C. Precursor for an electron carrier: niacin (NAD+, nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) or riboflavin (FAD, flavin adenine dinucleotide).<br>D. Lysyl oxidase: needs copper, a mineral.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":174,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F2296-198-1","s":"A 60-year-old female patient is brought to the ER by her son. He says that his mother seems to be confused, has poor coordination, and disrupted extremities movement. He also says that her eyes look “unusual”. After performing a physical exam, you notice your patient having horizontal nystagmus and ptosis; moreover, her muscle strength is ⅗ in upper and lower extremities. You find out that she has been drinking alcohol almost every day for 15 years, ever since her husband died. Considering your most likely diagnosis, what enzyme do you expect your patient to be deficient in?","o":["A. Pyruvate dehydrogenase","B. Biotinidase","C. Pyruvate carboxylase","D. Succinate Dehydrogenase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Pyruvate dehydrogenase.</b> Confusion, poor coordination, <b>horizontal nystagmus and ptosis</b> in a chronic drinker are <b>Wernicke encephalopathy</b> (thiamine, B1). TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) is the cofactor for this oxidative decarboxylation.<br>B. Biotinidase: recycles biotin; its deficiency is an inherited disorder.<br>C. Pyruvate carboxylase: needs biotin (B7).<br>D. Succinate dehydrogenase: uses FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide) from riboflavin (B2).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":198,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F3768-89-1","s":"A malnourished patient with chronic alcoholism develops confusion, ataxia, and horizontal nystagmus. Administration of glucose without prior vitamin supplementation worsens his symptoms. Which of the following best describes the functional form and metabolic classification of the deficient vitamin?","o":["A. Water-soluble vitamin functioning as tetrahydrofolate in nucleotide synthesis","B. Water-soluble vitamin functioning as thiamine pyrophosphate in oxidative decarboxylation","C. Fat-soluble vitamin functioning as retinoic acid in gene transcription","D. Water-soluble vitamin functioning as NAD+ in electron transport","E. Fat-soluble vitamin functioning as γ-carboxyglutamate in coagulation factors"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Water-soluble vitamin functioning as thiamine pyrophosphate in oxidative decarboxylation","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Water-soluble vitamin acting as TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) in oxidative decarboxylation.</b> Confusion, ataxia and nystagmus in an alcoholic, worsened by glucose, is <b>Wernicke encephalopathy</b>; glucose raises thiamine demand.<br>A. Tetrahydrofolate in nucleotide synthesis: folate (B9).<br>C. Retinoic acid in gene transcription: vitamin A.<br>D. NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide): niacin (B3); its deficiency is pellagra.<br>E. Gamma-carboxyglutamate in clotting factors: vitamin K.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":89,"f":"F3768"}]},{"id":"F3768-106-1","s":"A chronic alcoholic is brought to the emergency department with confusion, horizontal nystagmus, and ataxia. Laboratory studies show elevated lactate levels. Administration of IV glucose worsens his mental status. Which of the following biochemical reactions is most directly impaired due to this vitamin deficiency?","o":["A. Conversion of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA in mitochondria","B. Transfer of one-carbon units during thymidylate synthesis","C. Carboxylation of pyruvate to oxaloacetate during gluconeogenesis","D. Reduction of NADP⁺ to NADPH in the pentose phosphate pathway","E. Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA by a multienzyme complex"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA by a multienzyme complex","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (coenzyme A) by pyruvate dehydrogenase.</b> Confusion, nystagmus and ataxia in an alcoholic is <b>Wernicke encephalopathy</b>. Without TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate), pyruvate backs up as <b>lactate</b>, and glucose worsens it.<br>A. Methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA: vitamin B12.<br>B. One-carbon transfer for thymidylate: folate (B9).<br>C. Pyruvate carboxylation: biotin (B7).<br>D. Making NADPH in the pentose phosphate pathway: dehydrogenases using niacin-derived NADP+, not thiamine.","ex":"","fl":["Key E is correct; explanation-only error: the notes' 'Why others are wrong' list is mislabelled ('E → Folate deficiency'); it is option B (one-carbon transfer in thymidylate synthesis) that is folate — E (oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate by PDH) is the thiamine answer."],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (PDH Complex (PDHC))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":106,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Thiamine helps mild maple syrup urine disease","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-18.jpg","cap":"TPP-dependent breakdown of valine, isoleucine and leucine keto acids","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 18"},{"src":"sl/G031-17.jpg","cap":"TPP is the cofactor for branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, the defective enzyme","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-43-1","s":"Thiamine supplementation may help treat milder form of defect:","o":["A. Phenylketonuria","B. Albinism","C. Maple syrup urine disease","D. Alkaptonuria","E. Severe combined Immunodeficiency syndrome"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Maple syrup urine disease","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Maple syrup urine disease.</b> It is a defect of <b>branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase</b>, a TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate)-dependent enzyme that breaks down valine, isoleucine and leucine. Milder forms respond to thiamine.<br>A. Phenylketonuria: phenylalanine hydroxylase, which uses tetrahydrobiopterin.<br>B. Albinism: tyrosinase defect, no melanin.<br>D. Alkaptonuria: homogentisate oxidase defect.<br>E. Severe combined immunodeficiency: often adenosine deaminase deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 17 (Vitamin B1 - Thiamine)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":43,"f":"F0319"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Beriberi → thiamine deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-19.jpg","cap":"Wet beriberi: leg edema photo, tachycardia, cardiomegaly, heart failure; infantile beriberi","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/G031-20.jpg","cap":"Dry beriberi: symmetrical peripheral neuropathy of the legs; Wernicke–Korsakoff in alcoholics","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-156-1","s":"Q2 - Deficiency of Vitamin B1 causes what syndrome?","o":["A. Beriberi","B. Scurvy","C. Megaloblastic Anemia","D. Homocystinuria","E. Achlorhydria"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Beriberi","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Beriberi.</b> Thiamine (B1) deficiency gives <b>wet beriberi</b> (heart failure, edema) and <b>dry beriberi</b> (neuropathy), plus Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome.<br>B. Scurvy: vitamin C.<br>C. Megaloblastic anemia: folate (B9) or B12.<br>D. Homocystinuria: B6, B12 or folate pathway defects.<br>E. Achlorhydria: no stomach acid; it causes B12 and iron malabsorption, not B1 deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":156,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":136,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F1201-137-1","s":"Beriberi is characterized by cardiovascular or neurologic symptoms. This disease is caused by a deficiency of:","o":["A. Thiamine","B. Niacin","C. Serine","D. Pyridoxine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Thiamine","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Thiamine (B1).</b> Beriberi has a <b>cardiovascular form (wet)</b> and a <b>nervous form (dry, plus Wernicke–Korsakoff)</b>; both come from low TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) and poor energy production.<br>B. Niacin (B3): pellagra.<br>C. Serine: an amino acid, not a vitamin.<br>D. Pyridoxine (B6): neuropathy, sideroblastic anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":137,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":148,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":148,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F1013-144-1","s":"A 60-year-old man presents with fatigue, muscle weakness, and swelling in his legs and congestive heart failure. Which of the following is most likely responsible for his condition?","o":["A. Vitamin B3 (Niacin) deficiency","B. Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic Acid) deficiency","C. Vitamin B1 (Thiamine) deficiency","D. Vitamin B7 (Biotin) deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B1 (Thiamine) deficiency","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Thiamine (B1) deficiency.</b> Leg swelling with <b>congestive heart failure</b> is <b>wet beriberi</b>: edema, tachycardia, cardiomegaly, cardiomyopathy.<br>A. Niacin (B3): pellagra (diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia).<br>B. Pantothenic acid (B5): fatigue, sleep disturbance, impaired coordination; no heart failure.<br>D. Biotin (B7): dermatitis, alopecia, enteritis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":144,"f":"F1013"}]},{"id":"F0035-199-1","s":"A 43 yo patient presents to you complaining of palpitations, vomiting, and swollen extremities. Past medical history significant for alcoholism and CHF. What is your top differential?","o":["A. B1 deficiency- wet beriberi","B. B1 deficiency- dry beriberi","C. B6 deficiency- peripheral edema","D. B12 deficiency- peripheral neuropathy"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"B1 deficiency- wet beriberi","a":"A","why":"<b>A. B1 deficiency, wet beriberi.</b> Palpitations, <b>swollen extremities and heart failure</b> in an alcoholic fit wet beriberi (edema, tachycardia, cardiomegaly).<br>B. Dry beriberi: symmetrical peripheral neuropathy, no edema.<br>C. B6 deficiency does not cause peripheral edema; it causes neuropathy and sideroblastic anemia.<br>D. B12 deficiency does cause neuropathy, but not edema or heart failure.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":199,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F3768-109-1","s":"A homeless woman presents with peripheral edema, tachycardia, and cardiomegaly. She also reports numbness and burning pain in her feet. Which vitamin deficiency best explains both the cardiovascular and neurologic findings?","o":["A. Riboflavin deficiency impairing flavoprotein-dependent electron transport","B. Niacin deficiency leading to decreased NAD⁺ synthesis","C. Thiamine deficiency impairing oxidative decarboxylation reactions","D. Biotin deficiency affecting bicarbonate-dependent carboxylations","E. Pantothenic acid deficiency impairing coenzyme A synthesis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Thiamine deficiency impairing oxidative decarboxylation reactions","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Thiamine deficiency impairing oxidative decarboxylation.</b> Edema, tachycardia and cardiomegaly are <b>wet beriberi</b>; numb, burning feet are <b>dry beriberi</b>. Heart and nerves fail because energy (ATP, adenosine triphosphate) production falls.<br>A. Riboflavin (B2): angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis.<br>B. Niacin (B3): pellagra.<br>D. Biotin (B7): dermatitis, alopecia.<br>E. Pantothenic acid (B5): fatigue and sleep problems; rare.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":109,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Korsakoff psychosis = chronic thiamine deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-21.jpg","cap":"Wernicke acute (nystagmus, ptosis, ataxia) vs Korsakoff chronic (confabulation); treat with thiamine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G031-20.jpg","cap":"Wernicke encephalopathy and Korsakoff psychosis are the acute and chronic phases","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 20"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-139-1","s":"A patient presents to the ED complaining of hallucinations and memory loss. When you question him about his symptoms, he begins making up wild stories. You notice from his chart that he suffers from chronic alcohol abuse. What should you administer to treat his vitamin deficiency?","o":["A. Riboflavin","B. Vitamin A","C. Folic Acid","D. Thiamine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Thiamine","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Thiamine.</b> Memory loss, hallucinations and <b>confabulation</b> (making up stories) in a chronic drinker are <b>Korsakoff psychosis</b>, the chronic phase of thiamine deficiency. Treat with thiamine, intravenous then oral; full recovery is unlikely.<br>A. Riboflavin (B2): cheilosis, glossitis, angular stomatitis.<br>B. Vitamin A: night blindness, dry eyes.<br>C. Folic acid: megaloblastic anemia; it does not fix thiamine deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 21 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":139,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":151,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":151,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0035-202-1","s":"39 yo patient is brought to the ER by his family, with incoherent speech. His family states he has been telling extravagant stories recently, but none can affirm that any are true. The patient is disoriented, not knowing his name or where he is. What do you suspect?","o":["A. Wernicke’s encephalopathy, chronic deficiency","B. Korsakoff’s psychosis, chronic deficiency","C. Thiamine excess","D. Korsakoff’s psychosis, acute deficiency","E. Wernicke’s encephalopathy, acute deficiency"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Korsakoff’s psychosis, chronic deficiency","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Korsakoff psychosis, chronic deficiency.</b> Telling made-up stories (<b>confabulation</b>), memory loss and disorientation are Korsakoff, the <b>chronic</b> phase of thiamine deficiency.<br>A. Wernicke encephalopathy is the acute phase, not chronic, and lacks confabulation.<br>C. Thiamine excess: not toxic; it is excreted.<br>D. Korsakoff psychosis is chronic, not acute.<br>E. Wernicke encephalopathy (acute): nystagmus, ptosis, ataxia, confusion; no confabulation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 19 (Deficiency of Vitamin B1)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 20 (Dry Beriberi)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":202,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Riboflavin (FMN, FAD) → energy-yielding pathways","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-22.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin converted to FMN then FAD, cofactors for oxidoreduction reactions","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"},{"src":"sl/G031-23.jpg","cap":"FAD and FMN carry electrons from fatty acyl-CoA, succinate and pyruvate to CoQ","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-7-1","s":"The dietary riboflavin molecule is converted into active forms in the body and is essential for:","o":["A. Hemoglobin synthesis.","B. Energy yielding pathways.","C. Prevent megaloblastic anemia.","D. Absorption of Fe+2","E. B12 absorption."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Energy yielding pathways.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Energy-yielding pathways.</b> Riboflavin (B2) becomes <b>FMN and FAD</b> (flavin mononucleotide, flavin adenine dinucleotide), prosthetic groups for <b>oxidoreduction</b> in pyruvate dehydrogenase, the citric acid cycle, fatty acid oxidation and the electron transport chain.<br>A. Hemoglobin synthesis: B6 for ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase.<br>C. Preventing megaloblastic anemia: folate and B12.<br>D. Iron absorption: vitamin C.<br>E. B12 absorption: intrinsic factor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":7,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":4,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":21,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":2,"f":"F0078"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Riboflavin deficiency → low FAD","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-24.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin deficiency photos: angular stomatitis, cheilosis, magenta glossitis, corneal vascularization","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G031-22.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin is the precursor of FMN and FAD","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-119-1","s":"A patient presents with angular stomatitis, cheilosis, and a smooth magenta-colored tongue. Laboratory analysis shows decreased activity of several mitochondrial oxidoreductase enzymes. Which functional coenzyme is deficient?","o":["A. Flavin adenine dinucleotide derived from riboflavin","B. Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide derived from niacin","C. Coenzyme A derived from pantothenic acid","D. Thiamine pyrophosphate derived from thiamine","E. Pyridoxal phosphate derived from pyridoxine"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Flavin adenine dinucleotide derived from riboflavin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide) from riboflavin.</b> Angular stomatitis, cheilosis and a <b>magenta tongue</b> are riboflavin (B2) deficiency. FAD and FMN (flavin mononucleotide) are prosthetic groups of many mitochondrial oxidoreductases.<br>B. NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) from niacin: pellagra.<br>C. Coenzyme A from pantothenic acid: fatigue, sleep problems.<br>D. TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate): beriberi.<br>E. PLP (pyridoxal phosphate) from B6: neuropathy, sideroblastic anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":119,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2296-196-1","s":"A 50-year-old male patient presents to your office. Upon the physical examination, you notice him having fissuring at the angles of the mouth, redness and swelling of the lips, swollen tongue of the unusually purplish-red color. After performing the eye exam, you noticed the invasion of multiple blood vessels into the cornea. You quickly identify his vitamin deficiency, and based on that make an assumption that in this patient, the concentration of ___ is mostly affected.","o":["A. NAD+","B. Coenzyme A","C. NADP+","D. Coenzyme Q","E. FAD"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"FAD","a":"E","why":"<b>E. FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide).</b> Mouth-corner fissures, swollen lips, a magenta tongue and <b>corneal vascularization</b> are riboflavin (B2) deficiency. Riboflavin is the precursor of FAD and FMN (flavin mononucleotide).<br>A. NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide): from niacin (B3).<br>B. Coenzyme A: from pantothenic acid (B5).<br>C. NADP+ (its phosphate form): also from niacin.<br>D. Coenzyme Q: made in the body, not from a vitamin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":196,"f":"F2296"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"FMN, FAD in ETC Complexes I and II","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-23.jpg","cap":"FMN passes NADH electrons to CoQ (Complex I); succinate's FAD feeds CoQ (Complex II)","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-145-1","s":"Which of the following components of the mitochondrial electron transport chain requires riboflavin (vitamin B2) as a key cofactor?","o":["A. Complex I","B. Complex II","C. Complex III","D. Complex IV"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Complex III (source key)","a":"C","why":"<b>A and B.</b> Riboflavin (B2) makes <b>FMN (flavin mononucleotide), used in Complex I</b>, and <b>FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide), used in Complex II</b> (succinate dehydrogenase). Both pass electrons to coenzyme Q. The old key, C, was wrong.<br>C. Complex III (cytochrome bc1): heme and iron–sulfur centers, no flavin.<br>D. Complex IV (cytochrome c oxidase): heme and copper, no flavin.","ex":"","fl":["Riboflavin forms FMN and FAD (G031:22-23). FMN is in Complex I (NADH dehydrogenase) and FAD is in Complex II (succinate dehydrogenase). Complex III (cytochrome bc1) has no flavin. So A is correct (B is also correct); the key C is wrong."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 23 (Fatty acyl CoA’s)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":145,"f":"F0998"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Light-sensitive redox vitamin = riboflavin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-22.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin: FMN/FAD precursor, oxidoreduction cofactor, prosthetic group, light sensitive","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 22"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-204-1","s":"Which water soluble vitamin is light sensitive and is a cofactor for oxidation-reduction reactions?","o":["A. B12","B. B9","C. B6","D. B3","E. B2","F. B1"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"B2","a":"E","why":"<b>E. B2 (riboflavin).</b> Riboflavin is <b>light sensitive</b> and, as FMN and FAD (flavin mononucleotide, flavin adenine dinucleotide), a cofactor for <b>oxidoreduction</b> reactions.<br>A. B12 (cobalamin): methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase, methionine synthase.<br>B. B9 (folate): one-carbon transfer as tetrahydrofolate.<br>C. B6 (pyridoxine): transamination, heme synthesis.<br>D. B3 (niacin): its NAD+ also carries electrons, but niacin is not the light-sensitive one.<br>F. B1 (thiamine): oxidative decarboxylation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 22 (Vitamin B2 - Riboflavin)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":204,"f":"F0035"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Riboflavin deficiency: mouth, tongue, cornea signs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-24.jpg","cap":"Four riboflavin deficiency signs with photos; 2 C's: cheilosis and corneal vascularization","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-206-1","s":"Recall: What are the four major symptoms of riboflavin deficiency?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis, corneal vascularization","a":null,"why":"<b>Angular stomatitis</b> (fissures at the mouth corners), <b>cheilosis</b> (vertical lip fissures, then red swollen lips), <b>glossitis</b> (smooth, swollen magenta tongue) and <b>corneal vascularization</b>. Memory hook: the 2 C's, cheilosis and corneal vascularization.<br>Common mix-up: glossitis also occurs in pellagra (niacin), but the cornea finding points to riboflavin (B2).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":206,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F3881-176-1","s":"What type of disorder/clinical manifestations would a deficiency of Vitamin B2 (riboflavin) lead to?","o":["A. Beriberi (Wet Beriberi, Dry Beriberi) and Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome","B. Pellagra","C. Angular Stomatitis, Cheilosis, Glossitis, Corneal Vascularization","D. Microcytic Anemia and Sideroblastic Anemia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Angular Stomatitis, Cheilosis, Glossitis, Corneal Vascularization","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis, corneal vascularization.</b> These are riboflavin (B2) deficiency; remember the <b>2 C's: cheilosis and corneal vascularization</b>.<br>A. Beriberi and Wernicke–Korsakoff: thiamine (B1).<br>B. Pellagra: niacin (B3).<br>D. Microcytic, sideroblastic anemia: pyridoxine (B6), needed by ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase for heme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":176,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1022-11-1","s":"29-year-old man presents to the free clinic with multiple complaints. He has a sore throat, fatigue, rash, and sensitivity to light. He states he has never seen a doctor and his past medical history is not known. Physical examination reveals the finding in the figure, glossitis, stomatitis, and a dry rash diffusely. Which of the following vitamins is most likely deficient in this patient?","o":["A. Vitamin C","B. Vitamin A","C. Vitamin B6","D. Vitamin B2","E. Vitamin B9"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B2","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Vitamin B2 (riboflavin).</b> <b>Glossitis, stomatitis</b>, sore throat, a dry rash and light sensitivity fit riboflavin deficiency: angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis, corneal vascularization.<br>A. Vitamin C: spongy bleeding gums, petechiae, poor wound healing.<br>B. Vitamin A: night blindness, dry eyes.<br>C. Vitamin B6: neuropathy and sideroblastic anemia; light sensitivity from corneal vessels points to B2.<br>E. Vitamin B9 (folate): megaloblastic anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Photo of a red, inflamed tongue with angular cheilitis; the stem already states glossitis/stomatitis/photosensitivity, so the picture is not required.","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":11,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":47,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":27,"f":"F0928"}]},{"id":"F1201-141-1","s":"Which of the following is not caused by a deficiency in riboflavin?","o":["A. Cheilosis","B. Angular stomatitis","C. Corneal Vascularization","D. Dementia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Dementia","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Dementia.</b> Dementia is one of the 3 D's of <b>pellagra (niacin, B3)</b>, not riboflavin deficiency.<br>A. Cheilosis: lip fissures, a riboflavin (B2) sign.<br>B. Angular stomatitis: cracks at the mouth corners, a riboflavin sign.<br>C. Corneal vascularization: vessels growing into the cornea, a riboflavin sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":141,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":154,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":154,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Brown syndrome: riboflavin transporter neuronopathy","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-26.jpg","cap":"Brown syndrome: motor, sensory and cranial neuronopathy; improves with riboflavin supplements","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 26"},{"src":"sl/G031-25.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin transporters RFVT2 (SLC52A2) and RFVT3 (SLC52A3) move B2 from gut to tissue","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-208-1","s":"Recall: ________ disease/syndrome is caused by mutations in which gene(s), associated with motor and sensory neuronopathy, and can be improved with B2 supplementation?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Brown(-Vialetto-Van Laere) syndrome; SLC52A2 or SLC52A3 (riboflavin transporter deficiency neuronopathy)","a":null,"why":"<b>Brown syndrome</b> (riboflavin transporter deficiency neuronopathy) comes from mutations in <b>SLC52A2 or SLC52A3</b>, the genes for riboflavin transporters 2 and 3. It causes motor, sensory and cranial neuronopathy and improves with <b>riboflavin (B2) supplements</b>.<br>Common mix-up: it is often misdiagnosed as an autoimmune disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (RFVT2 (SLC52A2) and RFVT3 (SLC52A3))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (A motor/sensory neuronopathy that is usually misdiagnosed as an autoimmune disea)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":208,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F0028-178-1","s":"Which of the following is not characteristic of Brown syndrome?","o":["A. Proximal and distal limb weakness","B. Associated with mutations in SLC52A2","C. Sensory neuronopathy","D. Associated with consumption of raw eggs","E. Treat with B2 (riboflavin) supplements"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Associated with consumption of raw eggs","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Raw egg consumption</b> is not part of Brown syndrome. Raw egg avidin blocks <b>biotin (B7)</b>; Brown syndrome is a riboflavin transporter defect.<br>A. Proximal and distal limb weakness: its motor neuronopathy, a true feature.<br>B. SLC52A2 mutations: true; the gene codes riboflavin transporter 2.<br>C. Sensory neuronopathy (gait ataxia): true.<br>E. Riboflavin (B2) supplements: true; they improve signs and symptoms.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (RFVT2 (SLC52A2) and RFVT3 (SLC52A3))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (A motor/sensory neuronopathy that is usually misdiagnosed as an autoimmune disea)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":178,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2435-12-1","s":"A 35-year old female presents with a 6-week history of progressive dysphagia, dyspnea, dysphonia (troubled speaking), gait disturbance, arm weakness and weight loss. She also had progressive hearing loss over many years and observed some reduction in sharpness of her vision. The physician deduces her symptoms may be due to a cranial neuronopathy. Initial work-up ruled out infections, autoimmune disorders and toxic lead exposure as possible causes. A nutritional cause is suspected since she has been too depressed to eat a healthy diet the past two months since she and her husband separated. A diet deficient in which of the following vitamins would best account for her symptoms?","o":["A. Ascorbic Acid","B. Cobalamin","C. Riboflavin","D. Retinal","E. Tocopherol"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Riboflavin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Riboflavin (B2).</b> Dysphagia, dysphonia, hearing loss, failing vision and arm weakness are the <b>cranial and motor neuronopathy</b> of riboflavin transporter deficiency (Brown syndrome), which riboflavin improves.<br>A. Ascorbic acid (C): scurvy (gums, petechiae).<br>B. Cobalamin (B12): neuropathy with megaloblastic anemia, not deafness and bulbar palsy.<br>D. Retinal (A): night blindness.<br>E. Tocopherol (E): hemolysis and spinocerebellar ataxia, not deafness or bulbar palsy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 25 (RFVT2 (SLC52A2) and RFVT3 (SLC52A3))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 26 (A motor/sensory neuronopathy that is usually misdiagnosed as an autoimmune disea)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 24 (Deficiency of Vitamin B2)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":12,"f":"F2435"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Niacin can be made from tryptophan","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-27.jpg","cap":"Tryptophan → kynurenine → quinolinic acid → NAD+; Hartnup disease blocks tryptophan transport","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/D039-4.jpg","cap":"Tryptophan is an important endogenous source of niacin equivalents; Hartnup causes deficiency","where":"2026 spring interactive, slide 4"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-5-1","s":"Which amino acid is an important endogenous precursor of niacin equivalents?","o":["A. Arginine","B. Tryptophan","C. Isoleucine","D. Leucine","E. Tyrosine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Tryptophan","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Tryptophan.</b> The body turns tryptophan (via kynurenine and quinolinic acid) into <b>niacin equivalents</b> that make NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide). So defective tryptophan uptake (<b>Hartnup disease</b>) can cause pellagra.<br>A. Arginine: urea cycle and nitric oxide.<br>C. Isoleucine: branched-chain amino acid; no niacin.<br>D. Leucine: branched-chain, ketogenic; no niacin.<br>E. Tyrosine: catecholamines, thyroid hormone, melanin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":5,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":3,"f":"F0186"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3768-91-1","s":"A 32-year-old man presents with photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea, and progressive cognitive decline. Laboratory testing reveals decreased NAD+ levels. Which of the following statements correctly describes this vitamin?","o":["A. It is a fat-soluble vitamin stored in adipose tissue and rarely deficient","B. It is synthesized exclusively from dietary leafy greens","C. It primarily functions as a bicarbonate-dependent carboxylation cofactor","D. Its active form is pyridoxal phosphate","E. It is a water-soluble vitamin that can be synthesized from tryptophan"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"It is a water-soluble vitamin that can be synthesized from tryptophan","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Water-soluble vitamin that can be made from tryptophan.</b> Photosensitive dermatitis, diarrhea and dementia with low NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) is <b>pellagra (niacin, B3)</b>.<br>A. Niacin is water-soluble, not stored in fat.<br>B. Leafy greens are the classic folate source; niacin comes from meat, yeast and tryptophan.<br>C. Bicarbonate-dependent carboxylation: biotin (B7).<br>D. Pyridoxal phosphate: vitamin B6.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 39 (Review of Objective 3)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":91,"f":"F3768"}]},{"id":"F3768-112-1","s":"A patient with Hartnup disease develops photosensitive dermatitis and diarrhea. Laboratory testing reveals decreased levels of NAD⁺. Which of the following best describes the biochemical role of the deficient vitamin?","o":["A. Acts as a prosthetic group in oxidoreduction reactions as FMN and FAD","B. Serves as a precursor of NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ and can be synthesized from tryptophan","C. Functions as a carrier of activated two-carbon fragments in TPP-dependent reactions","D. Provides activated acetyl groups as part of coenzyme A","E. Covalently binds CO₂ for carboxylation reactions"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Serves as a precursor of NAD⁺ and NADP⁺ and can be synthesized from tryptophan","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Precursor of NAD+ and NADP+, made from tryptophan.</b> NAD+ is nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide. In <b>Hartnup disease</b>, tryptophan transport is defective, so niacin synthesis falls and pellagra appears.<br>A. FMN and FAD (flavin mononucleotide, flavin adenine dinucleotide): riboflavin (B2).<br>C. Two-carbon transfer with TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate): thiamine (B1).<br>D. Coenzyme A: pantothenic acid (B5).<br>E. Binding carbon dioxide for carboxylation: biotin (B7).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":112,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"NADPH drives reductive synthesis (cholesterol)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-28.jpg","cap":"NADH and NADPH act as reducing agents; NADPH protects against reactive oxygen species","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-42-1","s":"NADPH dependent enzyme are primarily essential for body  :","o":["A. heme synthesis.","B. energy yielding reactions.","C. synthesis methionine .","D. synthesis of histamine.","E. cholesterol synthesis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"cholesterol synthesis","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Cholesterol synthesis.</b> NADPH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate) is the <b>reducing agent for building molecules</b>: cholesterol (at HMG-CoA, hydroxymethylglutaryl-coenzyme A, reductase), fatty acids and steroids.<br>A. Heme synthesis: needs B6 for ALA (aminolevulinic acid) synthase, not NADPH.<br>B. Energy-yielding reactions: use NAD+ and FAD, not NADPH.<br>C. Methionine synthesis: vitamin B12 and folate.<br>D. Histamine synthesis: histidine decarboxylase uses B6.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 28 (Pyruvate)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":42,"f":"F0319"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Niacin → NAD+, the main electron carrier","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-28.jpg","cap":"NAD+ carries electrons from pyruvate, isocitrate, alpha-KG, malate to CoQ and oxygen","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G031-27.jpg","cap":"Niacin is the precursor of NAD+ and NADP+","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 27"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-143-1","s":"Which vitamin is a precursor to a major electron carrier that controls metabolic respiration?","o":["A. cobalamin","B. niacin","C. folic acid","D. thiamine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"niacin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Niacin (B3).</b> Niacin makes <b>NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide)</b>, the electron carrier that feeds the electron transport chain; the lecture says NAD+ controls metabolic respiration.<br>A. Cobalamin (B12): methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase and methionine synthase.<br>C. Folic acid (B9): one-carbon carrier as tetrahydrofolate.<br>D. Thiamine (B1): TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) for oxidative decarboxylation; not an electron carrier.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":143,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":157,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":157,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Pellagra (3 D's) → niacin deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-29.jpg","cap":"Pellagra: diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia, later death; scaly rash on sun-exposed skin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/F2296-201.jpg","cap":"Four D's of niacin deficiency beside photos of sun-exposed leg dermatitis","where":"2023 TA review, slide 201"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-210-1","s":"Recall: What are the 3 D’s of pellagra (niacin deficiency)?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia (death is the later fourth D)","a":null,"why":"<b>Diarrhea, dermatitis and dementia</b> are the 3 D's of pellagra (niacin, B3 deficiency). <b>Death</b> comes later if untreated, making it the fourth D. The dermatitis is on <b>sun-exposed skin</b>, thick and scaly.<br>Common mix-up: the source answer listed death in place of dementia.","ex":"","fl":["Source answer lists 'Diarrhea, Dermatitis, Death'; the 3 D's of pellagra are diarrhea, dermatitis, DEMENTIA, with death as the later outcome (G031:29).","The source keyed diarrhea, dermatitis, death; the third D is dementia, and death is the later fourth D."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":210,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F2296-200-1","s":"Recall 4 D’s associated with Niacin Deficiency…","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Diarrhea, Dementia, Dermatitis, Death (if untreated)","a":null,"why":"<b>Diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia</b>, and later <b>death</b> if untreated: pellagra from niacin (B3) deficiency. Glossitis may also occur. The rash is on <b>sun-exposed skin</b>, thick and scaly.<br>Common mix-up: dementia (pellagra, niacin) vs confabulation (Korsakoff psychosis, thiamine).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":200,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F0621-167-1","s":"Patient comes in with diarrhea, dermatitis, and dementia. What disease is this related with and what is the vitamin deficiency?","o":["A. Brown syndrome - Riboflavin","B. Wernicke-korsakoff syndrome - Thiamine","C. Sleep Disturbances - Pantothenic Acid","D. Pellagra - Niacin","E. Alopecia - Biotin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pellagra - Niacin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pellagra, niacin.</b> <b>Diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia</b> are the 3 D's of niacin (B3) deficiency; death is the late fourth D.<br>A. Brown syndrome, riboflavin: motor, sensory and cranial neuronopathy.<br>B. Wernicke–Korsakoff, thiamine: nystagmus, ataxia, confusion, confabulation.<br>C. Pantothenic acid deficiency causes sleep disturbances, but no dermatitis or diarrhea triad.<br>E. Biotin deficiency: alopecia, dermatitis, enteritis; the named 3 D's triad is pellagra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":167,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F0028-182-1","s":"A 40-year-old woman presents to the clinic complaining of diarrhea and a “skin rash” in sun-exposed areas (see picture). Which vitamin is the patient likely deficient in?","o":["A. cobalamin","B. niacin","C. folic acid","D. thiamine"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"niacin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Niacin (B3).</b> Diarrhea plus a <b>rash on sun-exposed skin</b> (thick, scaly) is <b>pellagra</b>; dementia and later death complete the D's.<br>A. Cobalamin (B12): megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy.<br>C. Folic acid (B9): megaloblastic anemia, neural tube defects.<br>D. Thiamine (B1): beriberi and Wernicke–Korsakoff.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Photos of pigmented, scaly dermatitis on sun-exposed feet/legs (pellagra); stem already gives diarrhea + photosensitive rash","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":182,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F1013-146-1","s":"A 45-year-old man with a history of alcoholism presents with diarrhea, a red, scaly rash on sun-exposed areas of his body, and confusion. What condition is most likely responsible for his symptoms?","o":["A. Wet beriberi due to thiamine deficiency","B. Pellagra due to niacin deficiency","C. Biotin deficiency leading to dermatitis","D. Riboflavin deficiency causing angular stomatitis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Pellagra due to niacin deficiency","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Pellagra due to niacin deficiency.</b> Diarrhea, a <b>red scaly rash on sun-exposed skin</b> and confusion (dementia) are the 3 D's. Alcoholism is a common cause.<br>A. Wet beriberi: edema and heart failure (thiamine, B1).<br>C. Biotin (B7) deficiency: dermatitis and alopecia, but the rash is not limited to sun-exposed skin.<br>D. Riboflavin (B2): angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":146,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Maize (corn) diet → pellagra","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-27.jpg","cap":"Niacin is synthesized from tryptophan, so low tryptophan intake limits niacin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 27"},{"src":"sl/G031-29.jpg","cap":"Pellagra signs: the 3 D's and photos of sun-exposed dermatitis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-39-1","s":"Which among the following may predispose to Pellagra?","o":["A. methotrexate intake.","B. maize supplemented diet occasionally with a little vegetable oil.","C. Glucose -6-phosphatase deficiency.","D. an inherited defect where painful skin excoriations (pruritis) provoked by porphyrin-mediated generation of superoxide .","E. Cystic fibrosis."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"maize supplemented diet occasionally with a little vegetable oil.","a":"B","why":"<b>B. A maize (corn) diet.</b> Corn is <b>low in tryptophan</b> and its niacin is tightly bound unless the corn is treated with lime, so a corn-based diet causes <b>pellagra</b>.<br>A. Methotrexate: blocks dihydrofolate reductase, causing folate deficiency.<br>C. Glucose-6-phosphatase deficiency: von Gierke glycogen storage disease.<br>D. Porphyrin-driven painful skin photosensitivity: a porphyria, a heme synthesis defect.<br>E. Cystic fibrosis: fat malabsorption, so fat-soluble vitamin deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 29 (Deficiency of Niacin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 27 (Vitamin B3 - Niacin)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":39,"f":"F0319"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Pantothenic acid → coenzyme A","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-30.jpg","cap":"Pantothenic acid built into coenzyme A, carrier of acetyl and acyl groups","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G031-15.jpg","cap":"Coenzyme A from pantothenic acid is one of five PDH complex cofactors","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-122-1","s":"A patient with severe fatigue and sleep disturbances is found to have decreased synthesis of acetyl-CoA from multiple metabolic substrates. Which vitamin deficiency would most directly impair formation of acetyl-CoA through its role in coenzyme A synthesis?","o":["A. Thiamine","B. Niacin","C. Riboflavin","D. Pantothenic acid","E. Biotin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pantothenic acid","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pantothenic acid (B5).</b> It is the <b>precursor of coenzyme A</b>, the carrier of acetyl (two-carbon) groups from carbohydrate, fat and amino acids. Deficiency gives fatigue, <b>sleep disturbances</b>, impaired coordination and nausea.<br>A. Thiamine (B1): TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate) for oxidative decarboxylation.<br>B. Niacin (B3): NAD+ and NADP+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide and its phosphate).<br>C. Riboflavin (B2): FMN and FAD (flavin mononucleotide, flavin adenine dinucleotide).<br>E. Biotin (B7): carboxylations.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin B5 – Pantothenic Acid)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":122,"f":"F3768"}]},{"id":"D039-30-1","s":"Pantothenic acid (vitamin B₅) is a precursor of coenzyme A. Which enzyme requires this coenzyme?","o":["A. Pyruvate Carboxylase","B. Transketolase","C. Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","D. Pyruvate Kinase","E. Alanine Transaminase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyruvate dehydrogenase.</b> Pantothenic acid (B5) makes <b>coenzyme A</b>, which accepts the acetyl group when pyruvate dehydrogenase turns pyruvate into <b>acetyl-coenzyme A</b>. It is one of the complex's five vitamin-derived cofactors.<br>A. Pyruvate carboxylase: uses biotin (B7); acetyl-coenzyme A is only its activator.<br>B. Transketolase: uses TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate).<br>D. Pyruvate kinase: needs no vitamin coenzyme.<br>E. Alanine transaminase: uses PLP (pyridoxal phosphate, B6).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin B5 – Pantothenic Acid)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 15 (PDH Complex (PDHC))"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":30,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":13,"f":"F0186"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Pantothenic acid (B5) → coenzyme A","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-30.jpg","cap":"Pantothenic acid structure built into coenzyme A; deficiency signs","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G031-15.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase's five vitamin cofactors: coenzyme A comes from pantothenic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-176-1","s":"Pantothenic acid is a precursor of what coenzyme?","o":["A. FAD","B. CoA","C. NAD+","D. methylcobalamin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"CoA","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Coenzyme A (CoA).</b> Pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) is built into <b>coenzyme A</b>, the carrier of acetyl and fatty acyl groups.<br>A. FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide): made from riboflavin (B2).<br>C. NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide): made from niacin (B3).<br>D. Methylcobalamin: an active form of vitamin B12, not of B5.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin B5 – Pantothenic Acid)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":176,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":145,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":160,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":160,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F2297-27-1","s":"7.Your mother sent you a bottle of multivitamins (Given Below). Looking at its label, you know that it is important source of coenzyme in your body for the enzyme:","o":["A. Pyruvate Carboxylase","B. Transketolase","C. Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","D. Pyruvate Kinase","E. Alanine Transaminase"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyruvate Dehydrogenase","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyruvate dehydrogenase.</b> The bottle is <b>pantothenic acid (B5)</b>, which becomes <b>coenzyme A (CoA)</b>. CoA is one of the five vitamin cofactors of the <b>pyruvate dehydrogenase complex</b> (pyruvate → acetyl-CoA).<br>A. Pyruvate carboxylase: needs biotin (B7).<br>B. Transketolase: needs thiamine pyrophosphate (from B1).<br>D. Pyruvate kinase: needs no vitamin coenzyme.<br>E. Alanine transaminase: needs pyridoxal phosphate (from B6).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F2297-27.jpg","in":"Photo of a bottle labelled 'TWINLAB Pantothenic Acid (B-5) Caps' - the vitamin is named only in the picture.","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 30 (Vitamin B5 – Pantothenic Acid)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":27,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":14,"f":"F0078"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Biotin → pyruvate carboxylase → hypoglycemia, lactate","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-31.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate carboxylase needs biotin to make oxaloacetate for gluconeogenesis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G031-32.jpg","cap":"Biotin bound to carboxylases; needed for liver gluconeogenesis and lipid synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/F0319-41.jpg","cap":"Pyruvate carboxylase blocked: pyruvate becomes lactate, which competes with urate excretion","where":"2022 faculty review, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-26-1","s":"A deficiency of biotin may be associated with:","o":["A. Coarse facial features","B. Hypoglycemia","C. Methylmalonic acidosis","D. Bleeding gums","E. Hyperammonemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hypoglycemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hypoglycemia.</b> Biotin (B7) carries carbon dioxide for <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b> (pyruvate → oxaloacetate), the first step of liver <b>gluconeogenesis</b>. Without it, the fasting liver can't make enough glucose.<br>A. Coarse facial features: storage diseases such as the mucopolysaccharidoses.<br>C. Methylmalonic acidosis: vitamin B12 deficiency (methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase).<br>D. Bleeding gums: vitamin C deficiency (scurvy).<br>E. Hyperammonemia: urea cycle defects or liver failure, not the classic biotin sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (Pyruvate Carboxylase: in mitochondriaFormation of OAA from Pyruvate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":26,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":12,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":26,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":12,"f":"F0078"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0319-40-1","s":"Biotin defect may be associated with:","o":["A. rusty brown ring around cornea","B. hyperglycemia","C. hyperuricemia","D. darkening of helix","E. sweet sickly urine smell"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"hyperuricemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hyperuricemia.</b> Biotin is needed by <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b>. When that step fails, pyruvate backs up and is turned into <b>lactate</b>. Lactate <b>competes with urate</b> for excretion by the kidney, so uric acid rises.<br>A. Rusty-brown ring around the cornea (Kayser-Fleischer ring): Wilson disease.<br>B. Hyperglycemia: wrong direction; blocked gluconeogenesis gives low glucose.<br>D. Darkened ear helix (ochronosis): alkaptonuria.<br>E. Sweet urine smell: maple syrup urine disease (branched-chain keto acid dehydrogenase, a thiamine enzyme).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 31 (Pyruvate Carboxylase: in mitochondriaFormation of OAA from Pyruvate)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":40,"f":"F0319"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Biotin = CO₂ carrier; multiple carboxylase deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-32.jpg","cap":"Biotin covalently attached to carboxylases at the carbon dioxide site","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G031-34.jpg","cap":"Multiple carboxylase deficiency: holocarboxylase synthetase (early) or biotinidase (late) defect","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-93-1","s":"A newborn develops metabolic acidosis and decreased activity of pyruvate carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase, and acetyl-CoA carboxylase. Genetic testing reveals deficiency of an enzyme responsible for recycling a water-soluble vitamin. Which vitamin and functional characteristic best describe this disorder?","o":["A. Riboflavin; precursor of FMN and FAD used in oxidoreduction","B. Biotin; covalently attached carrier of CO₂ in carboxylation reactions","C. Pantothenic acid; precursor of coenzyme A","D. Niacin; electron carrier derived from tryptophan","E. Pyridoxine; cofactor in heme synthesis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Biotin; covalently attached carrier of CO₂ in carboxylation reactions","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Biotin; covalently attached carbon dioxide carrier.</b> Pyruvate, propionyl-CoA (coenzyme A) and acetyl-CoA carboxylase all use <b>biotin bound to the enzyme</b> to carry carbon dioxide. A defect in recycling it (<b>biotinidase</b> or <b>holocarboxylase synthetase</b>) causes <b>multiple carboxylase deficiency</b>.<br>A. Riboflavin: makes flavin coenzymes (FMN, FAD) for redox reactions.<br>C. Pantothenic acid: makes coenzyme A.<br>D. Niacin: makes NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide).<br>E. Pyridoxine: makes pyridoxal phosphate for transamination and heme synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 33 (Free biotin is formed from dietary sources or from the cleavage of biocytin by t)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":93,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Raw eggs (avidin) → biotin deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-32.jpg","cap":"Raw egg avidin binds biotin and blocks its absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 32"},{"src":"sl/G031-35.jpg","cap":"Biotin deficiency photos: facial dermatitis and alopecia","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-186-1","s":"A patient presents to the ER complaining of dermatitis, alopecia, and depression. He states he has been consuming large amounts of raw eggs in preparation for an upcoming bodybuilding competition. What vitamin deficiency is most likely causing his symptoms?","o":["A. Thiamine","B. Folic acid","C. Niacin","D. Biotin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Biotin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Biotin.</b> Raw egg white contains <b>avidin</b>, a heat-labile protein that binds biotin tightly and blocks its absorption. Biotin deficiency causes <b>dermatitis, alopecia (hair loss), depression</b>, enteritis and hallucinations.<br>A. Thiamine (B1): beriberi and Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.<br>B. Folic acid: megaloblastic anemia, neural tube defects.<br>C. Niacin (B3): pellagra (diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 35 (Biotin deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":186,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":149,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":166,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":166,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-213-1","s":"Your New Year resolution for 2021 is to compete in a bodybuilding competition. You begin exercising six hours a day, and add large amounts of protein and raw eggs to your diet. After a few months, you notice hair loss, visual disturbances, and scaly, scratchy skin. Which vitamin are you most likely deficient in?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Biotin, vitamin B7","a":null,"why":"<b>Biotin (vitamin B7).</b> Eating many <b>raw eggs</b> supplies <b>avidin</b>, a protein that binds biotin in the gut so it can't be absorbed (cooking destroys avidin). The result is biotin deficiency: <b>hair loss (alopecia), scaly dermatitis</b>, depression and hallucinations.<br>Common mix-up: niacin deficiency (pellagra) also gives dermatitis, but with diarrhea and dementia, and has no link to raw eggs.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 32 (Biotin : Vitamin B7)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 35 (Biotin deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":213,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Inherited biotin recycling defect → dermatitis, alopecia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-35.jpg","cap":"Biotin deficiency: dermatitis, enteritis, alopecia, depression, hallucinations, with photos","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 35"},{"src":"sl/G031-34.jpg","cap":"Biotinidase and holocarboxylase synthetase defects cause multiple carboxylase deficiency","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 34"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-26-1","s":"A 2-year old boy born to parents who are second cousins is referred to the Dermatology clinic for severe rashes all over the body of one year duration. The parents also observed increased restlessness and mental deterioration. Relevant findings on PE include alopecia and diffused eczematous rash all over the body especially in the neck, trunk and genital areas. Neurologic exam marked irritability and ataxia (wide-based gait). A genetic defect leading to the deficiency of which of the following vitamins could best explain the patient’s symptoms described above?","o":["A. Riboflavin","B. Folic acid","C. Biotin","D. Cobalamin","E. Thiamine"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Biotin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Biotin.</b> A toddler of related parents with <b>alopecia, eczema-like rash</b>, irritability and ataxia has inherited <b>biotinidase deficiency</b> (late-onset multiple carboxylase deficiency). Biotin can't be recycled, so the child becomes biotin-deficient; biotin supplements fix it.<br>A. Riboflavin: cracks at mouth corners, glossitis, corneal vascularization.<br>B. Folic acid: megaloblastic anemia, neural tube defects.<br>D. Cobalamin (B12): megaloblastic anemia with neuropathy.<br>E. Thiamine: beriberi, Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 34 (Early-onset)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 35 (Biotin deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":26,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":9,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":45,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":10,"f":"F2435"}]},{"id":"F3768-115-1","s":"A newborn presents with metabolic acidosis and decreased activity of pyruvate carboxylase and propionyl-CoA carboxylase. Genetic testing reveals impaired recycling of a vitamin cofactor. Which of the following clinical features is most consistent with this deficiency?","o":["A. Dermatitis, alopecia, and depression","B. Cheilosis and corneal vascularization","C. Peripheral neuropathy and cardiomyopathy","D. Photosensitive rash and diarrhea","E. Macrocytic anemia and elevated methylmalonic acid"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Dermatitis, alopecia, and depression","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Dermatitis, alopecia and depression.</b> Low pyruvate and propionyl-CoA (coenzyme A) carboxylase activity plus a recycling defect means <b>biotin deficiency</b> (multiple carboxylase deficiency). Biotin deficiency causes <b>dermatitis, enteritis, alopecia, depression and hallucinations</b>.<br>B. Cheilosis and corneal vascularization: riboflavin (B2).<br>C. Neuropathy and cardiomyopathy: thiamine (B1), beriberi.<br>D. Sun-exposed rash and diarrhea: niacin (B3), pellagra.<br>E. Macrocytic anemia with high methylmalonic acid: vitamin B12.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 35 (Biotin deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 39 (Review of Objective 3)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":115,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Match B1, B2, B3 deficiency signs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-21.jpg","cap":"Thiamine deficiency: Wernicke (nystagmus, ptosis, ataxia) and Korsakoff signs","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G031-24.jpg","cap":"Riboflavin deficiency photos: angular stomatitis, cheilosis, glossitis, corneal vascularization","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G031-29.jpg","cap":"Niacin deficiency: pellagra's three Ds with sun-exposed dermatitis photos","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-119-1","s":"Match the Following! Deficiency of B2; Deficiency of B1; Deficiency of B3","o":["A. Neurological damage, Horizontal nystagmus, ptosis, ataxia, confusion, hallucinations, confabulations","B. Pellagra (3Ds: Diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia), Glossitis","C. Fissures at corner of mouth, tongue is swollen, corneal vascularization"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"B2 -> fissures at mouth corners, swollen tongue, corneal vascularization; B1 -> neurological damage (Wernicke-Korsakoff); B3 -> pellagra","a":"B2-C, B1-A, B3-B","why":"<b>B1 (thiamine) → A:</b> Wernicke encephalopathy (nystagmus, ptosis, ataxia, confusion); Korsakoff psychosis adds hallucinations and confabulation.<br><b>B2 (riboflavin) → C:</b> angular stomatitis (cracks at mouth corners), cheilosis, glossitis, <b>corneal vascularization</b>.<br><b>B3 (niacin) → B:</b> <b>pellagra</b>, the three Ds (diarrhea, dermatitis, dementia), plus glossitis.<br>Common mix-up: B2 and B3 both give glossitis; corneal vascularization points to B2, the three Ds to B3.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 38 (Review of Objective 3)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 39 (Review of Objective 3)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":119,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Hematopoietic vitamins = B6, folate, B12","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-7.jpg","cap":"Vitamin classes: hematopoietic group is pyridoxine, folic acid and cobalamin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 7"},{"src":"sl/G031-41.jpg","cap":"Nutritional anemias by cell size: pyridoxine microcytic; B12 and folate macrocytic","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 41"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-161-1","s":"What are the hematopoietic vitamins?","o":["A. A. Folic Acid (B9)","B. B. Cobalamin (B12)","C. C. Niacin (B3)","D. D. Pyridoxine (B6)","E. E. Biotin (B7)","F. F. A and B","G. G. B and D","H. H. A, B, and D","I. I. All of the Above"],"g":["H"],"k":["H"],"rv":false,"at":"A, B, and D","a":"H","why":"<b>H. A, B and D.</b> The <b>hematopoietic (blood-forming) vitamins</b> are <b>folic acid (B9), cobalamin (B12) and pyridoxine (B6)</b>. Folate and B12 are needed for DNA synthesis; B6 for heme synthesis.<br>A, B, D alone: each is only one of the three.<br>C. Niacin (B3): an energy-releasing vitamin.<br>E. Biotin (B7): an energy-releasing vitamin.<br>F. A and B: leaves out pyridoxine.<br>G. B and D: leaves out folic acid.<br>I. All of the above: wrongly adds niacin and biotin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 40 (Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":161,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Match B6, folate, B12 to anemia type","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-41.jpg","cap":"Anemia by cell size: pyridoxine microcytic; B12 and folate macrocytic","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G031-43.jpg","cap":"Pyridoxine deficiency: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia with ringed sideroblast images","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 43"},{"src":"sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"B12 versus folate table: neuro signs and methylmalonic acid only in B12","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F2303-121-1","s":"Match the Following! Deficiency of B12; Deficiency of B9; Deficiency of B6","o":["A. Sideroblastic anemia","B. Megaloblastic anemia with an accumulation of only homocysteine, and no excess methymalonic acid","C. Megaloblastic anemia w/ peripheral tingling, and loss of memory"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"B12 -> megaloblastic anemia with tingling and memory loss; B9 -> megaloblastic anemia with only homocysteine raised; B6 -> sideroblastic anemia","a":"B12-C, B9-B, B6-A","why":"<b>B12 → C:</b> megaloblastic anemia <b>plus nerve signs</b> (tingling, memory loss).<br><b>B9 (folate) → B:</b> megaloblastic anemia with <b>only homocysteine</b> raised; methylmalonic acid stays normal.<br><b>B6 (pyridoxine) → A:</b> <b>sideroblastic</b> (microcytic) anemia, because pyridoxal phosphate is needed by aminolevulinic acid synthase to make heme.<br>Common mix-up: B12 and folate both cause megaloblastic anemia; only B12 adds neuropathy and high methylmalonic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 60 (Review of Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":121,"f":"F2303"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-170-1","s":"Match the B vitamin deficiency with the type of anemia it causes. Folate; Cobalamin; Pyridoxine — A. Megaloblastic anemia; B. Microcytic anemia; C. Sideroblastic anemia; D. Pernicious anemia; E. Both A and B; F. Both A and D; G. Both B and C; H. Both B and D","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Folate = A (megaloblastic); Cobalamin = F (megaloblastic + pernicious); Pyridoxine = G (microcytic + sideroblastic)","a":null,"why":"<b>Folate → A (megaloblastic).</b> Without folate, purines and thymidylate run short, so DNA can't be made.<br><b>Cobalamin → F (megaloblastic + pernicious).</b> B12 deficiency is megaloblastic; when it comes from loss of intrinsic factor it is called <b>pernicious anemia</b>.<br><b>Pyridoxine → G (microcytic + sideroblastic).</b> Pyridoxal phosphate runs aminolevulinic acid synthase; less heme gives small cells and iron-ringed sideroblasts.<br>Common mix-up: pernicious anemia belongs to B12 only, not folate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 41 (Classification of nutritional anemias by RBC cell size)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":170,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Deficiencies that cause anemia (incl. copper)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-41.jpg","cap":"Anemia by cell size: iron and pyridoxine microcytic; B12 and folate macrocytic","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 41"},{"src":"sl/G031-63.jpg","cap":"Mineral table: copper deficiency causes anemia and skeletal defects","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 63"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-157-1","s":"In which of the following deficiencies would you observe some form of anemia?","o":["A. Iron, Pyridoxine, Folic Acid","B. Iron, Pyridoxine, Copper","C. Zn, Biotin, Cobalamin","D. Iron, Thiamine, Folic Acid"],"g":["A","B"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron, Pyridoxine, Folic Acid","a":"A","why":"<b>A and B are both correct.</b> Iron, pyridoxine (B6) and folic acid deficiencies all cause anemia (iron and B6 microcytic, folate megaloblastic). <b>Copper</b> deficiency also causes <b>anemia</b> (lecture mineral table: anemia and skeletal defects), so B fits too.<br>C. Zinc, biotin, cobalamin: zinc lack gives skin lesions and poor healing; biotin lack gives dermatitis and alopecia; neither causes anemia.<br>D. Iron, thiamine, folic acid: thiamine lack causes beriberi, not anemia.","ex":"","fl":["Copper deficiency also causes anemia (2026 G031:63 mineral table: copper deficiency → 'Anemia, skeletal defects'), so B (iron, pyridoxine, copper) is as correct as the keyed A; the item has two defensible answers","Option B is also fully correct: copper deficiency causes anemia (G031:63 lists 'Anemia, skeletal defects' as the copper deficiency sign; copper is needed for the ferroxidases hephaestin and ceruloplasmin). The question has two correct answers, so key A is not the only right one."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 41 (Classification of nutritional anemias by RBC cell size)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 63 (Skin lesions, poor wound healing)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":157,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":178,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":178,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Pyridoxine (B6) → ALA synthase → heme","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-42.jpg","cap":"Pyridoxal phosphate reactions, incl. glycine + succinyl-CoA → delta-aminolevulinic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 42"},{"src":"sl/G031-43.jpg","cap":"B6 deficiency: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia; ringed sideroblast images","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 43"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-23-1","s":"Hemoglobin synthesis in the body essentially requires Vitamin:","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Tocopherol","C. Pyridoxine","D. Vitamin E","E. Vitamin K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyridoxine (B6).</b> Its active form, <b>pyridoxal phosphate</b>, is the cofactor for <b>aminolevulinic acid synthase</b> (glycine + succinyl-coenzyme A → delta-aminolevulinic acid), the first step of heme synthesis. No heme, no hemoglobin.<br>A. Vitamin D: calcium and bone.<br>B. Tocopherol: this is vitamin E, a lipid antioxidant.<br>D. Vitamin E: antioxidant; not needed for heme.<br>E. Vitamin K: gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 42 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":23,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":11,"f":"F0186"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2297-25-1","s":"5. Hemoglobin synthesis in the body essentially requires vitamin:","o":["A. Vitamin D","B. Niacin","C. Pyridoxine","D. Vitamin E","E. Vitamin K"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxine","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Pyridoxine (B6).</b> Its active form, <b>pyridoxal phosphate</b>, is the cofactor for <b>aminolevulinic acid synthase</b> (glycine + succinyl-coenzyme A), the first step of heme synthesis. No heme, no hemoglobin.<br>A. Vitamin D: calcium and bone.<br>B. Niacin (B3): makes NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) for redox reactions; deficiency is pellagra.<br>D. Vitamin E: lipid antioxidant.<br>E. Vitamin K: gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 42 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":25,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":10,"f":"F0078"}]},{"id":"F0028-190-1","s":"A deficiency in which of the following vitamins will lead to sideroblastic anemia?","o":["A. Vitamin C","B. Thiamine","C. Niacin","D. Pyridoxine"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Pyridoxine","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Pyridoxine (B6).</b> <b>Pyridoxal phosphate</b> is the coenzyme for <b>aminolevulinic acid synthase</b>, the first step of heme synthesis. Iron that can't enter heme piles up in the mitochondria of red-cell precursors, forming <b>ringed sideroblasts</b>.<br>A. Vitamin C: scurvy (bleeding gums, petechiae).<br>B. Thiamine (B1): beriberi, Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.<br>C. Niacin (B3): pellagra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 57 (Sideroblastic anemia is observed in the deficiency of which of the following?)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 42 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":190,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":147,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":163,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":163,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F3768-131-1","s":"A patient presents with microcytic anemia and ringed sideroblasts on bone marrow biopsy. Which of the following enzymatic reactions is most directly impaired?","o":["A. Conversion of N⁵-methyl-THF to THF","B. Isomerization of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA","C. Decarboxylation of branched-chain ketoacids","D. Synthesis of δ-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) in heme production","E. Reduction of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Synthesis of δ-aminolevulinic acid (ALA) in heme production","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Synthesis of delta-aminolevulinic acid.</b> Microcytic anemia with <b>ringed sideroblasts</b> means poor heme synthesis. <b>Aminolevulinic acid synthase</b> needs <b>pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)</b>; iron that can't enter heme collects in mitochondria around the nucleus.<br>A. N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate → tetrahydrofolate: methionine synthase, needs B12.<br>B. Methylmalonyl-coenzyme A → succinyl-coenzyme A: needs B12.<br>C. Branched-chain keto acid decarboxylation: thiamine (B1).<br>E. Dihydrofolate → tetrahydrofolate: folate pathway; gives megaloblastic, not sideroblastic, anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 42 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 43 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 57 (Sideroblastic anemia is observed in the deficiency of which of the following?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":131,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Neural tube defects ← folate one-carbon transfers","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"Tetrahydrofolate one-carbon forms feeding purine and thymidylate synthesis","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"},{"src":"sl/G031-46.jpg","cap":"Folate deficiency: anencephaly and spina bifida, days seventeen to thirty","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-128-1","s":"A pregnant woman who did not take prenatal vitamins delivers a newborn with spina bifida. Which biochemical process was most directly impaired during early embryogenesis?","o":["A. Oxidative decarboxylation of α-ketoglutarate","B. Conversion of homocysteine to methionine via methylcobalamin","C. One-carbon transfer reactions required for purine and thymidylate synthesis","D. Carboxylation of pyruvate to oxaloacetate","E. γ-carboxylation of glutamate residues in clotting factors"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"One-carbon transfer reactions required for purine and thymidylate synthesis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. One-carbon transfers for purine and thymidylate synthesis.</b> This is <b>folate (B9)</b> deficiency. Folate becomes <b>tetrahydrofolate</b>, which carries one-carbon units to build purines and thymidylate for DNA. Too little DNA synthesis in days seventeen to thirty after conception leaves the neural tube open.<br>A. Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase: needs thiamine (B1).<br>B. Homocysteine → methionine via methylcobalamin: the B12 step.<br>D. Pyruvate carboxylation: biotin.<br>E. Gamma-carboxylation of clotting factors: vitamin K.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 46 (Neural tube defects)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":128,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3768-145-1","s":"A 28-year-old pregnant woman with poor nutrition presents with macrocytic anemia. Methylmalonic acid levels are normal. The fetus is at increased risk for neural tube defects due to impaired:","o":["A. Myelin lipid synthesis in the developing spinal cord","B. One-carbon transfer reactions necessary for nucleotide biosynthesis","C. Oxidative decarboxylation in embryonic mitochondria","D. Heme synthesis in fetal erythroblasts","E. Transamination reactions required for amino acid synthesis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"One-carbon transfer reactions necessary for nucleotide biosynthesis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. One-carbon transfers for nucleotide synthesis.</b> Macrocytic anemia with <b>normal methylmalonic acid</b> means <b>folate</b> deficiency. Folate (as tetrahydrofolate) supplies one-carbon units for <b>purines and thymidylate</b>; without them embryonic cells can't make DNA and the neural tube fails to close.<br>A. Myelin lipid synthesis: disturbed in B12, not folate, deficiency.<br>C. Oxidative decarboxylation: thiamine (B1).<br>D. Heme synthesis: pyridoxine (B6).<br>E. Transamination: pyridoxine (B6).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 46 (Neural tube defects)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":145,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Homocysteine needs B6, folate, B12","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Homocysteine to methionine (B12, folate) and to cysteine via transsulfuration (B6)","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"},{"src":"sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"Methionine synthase: homocysteine + N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, with methylcobalamin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"D039-12-1","s":"The vitamins essential for Homocysteine metabolism in the body are:","o":["A. Vitamin A, Vitamin D and Vitamin E","B. Folate, Vitamin B12 and Vitamin B6","C. Vitamin C and Vitamin K","D. Vitamin B12 and Vitamin K","E. Vitamin E and Vitamin C"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate, Vitamin B12 and Vitamin B6","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Folate, vitamin B12 and vitamin B6.</b> Homocysteine has two exits. Back to <b>methionine</b> by methionine synthase, which needs <b>N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate (folate)</b> and <b>methylcobalamin (B12)</b>. Or on to cystathionine by cystathionine beta-synthase, which needs <b>pyridoxal phosphate (B6)</b>.<br>A. Vitamins A, D, E: fat-soluble; no role here.<br>C. Vitamins C and K: collagen and clotting.<br>D. B12 and K: misses folate and B6; K is for clotting.<br>E. Vitamins E and C: antioxidants.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring interactive","n":"water & fat-soluble vitamins IS (May 2026)","sl":12,"f":"D039"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 class clickers week 17 IS","sl":6,"f":"F0186"},{"t":"2024 clicker","n":"Exam 4 compiled clickers","sl":22,"f":"F2297"},{"t":"2023 interactive","n":"week 17 IA session","sl":4,"f":"F0078"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0319-49-1","s":"[Fill-in diagram] (1) Homocysteine → Cystathionine → Cysteine, with blank boxes 'Enzyme' and 'Coenzyme/cofactor' on the homocysteine → cystathionine step; (2) Homocysteine → Methionine, with blank boxes 'Enzyme' and 'Coenzyme/cofactor'.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"(1) Cystathionine synthase, cofactor pyridoxine (B6); (2) Methyltransferase/methionine synthase, cofactors methyl-folate (N5-methyl-THF) and methyl-cobalamin (B12)","a":null,"why":"<b>Step 1 (homocysteine → cystathionine):</b> <b>cystathionine beta-synthase</b>, using <b>pyridoxal phosphate (vitamin B6)</b>.<br><b>Step 2 (homocysteine → methionine):</b> <b>methionine synthase</b>, which takes the methyl group from <b>N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate (folate)</b> and passes it on through <b>methylcobalamin (B12)</b>.<br>Common mix-up: lack of B6, folate or B12 can each raise homocysteine; only B12 lack also raises methylmalonic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 42 (Vitamin B6 - Pyridoxine)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":49,"f":"F0319"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Tetrahydrofolate → de novo purine ring","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"N10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate feeds purine synthesis; methylene form makes thymidylate","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-55-1","s":"Vegetarian consume green leafy vegetables , a source rich in vitamin folic acid. Folic acid in the form of Tetrahydrofolate (THF) is essential for the:","o":["A. synthesis of AMP from IMP","B. the synthesis of deoxynucleotide diphosphates","C. the denovo synthesis of the purine ring","D. the salvage of pyrimidine bases","E. synthesis of CTP from UTP"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"the denovo synthesis of the purine ring","a":"C","why":"<b>C. De novo synthesis of the purine ring.</b> <b>Tetrahydrofolate</b> carries one-carbon units; <b>N10-formyl-tetrahydrofolate</b> donates two carbons of the purine ring. (Another form makes thymidylate.)<br>A. AMP (adenosine monophosphate) from IMP (inosine monophosphate): uses aspartate, no folate.<br>B. Deoxynucleotide diphosphates: made by ribonucleotide reductase, no folate.<br>D. Pyrimidine base salvage: no folate needed.<br>E. CTP (cytidine triphosphate) from UTP (uridine triphosphate): uses glutamine's nitrogen, no folate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":55,"f":"F0319"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Folate deficiency → megaloblastic anemia (slow DNA)","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-45.jpg","cap":"Megaloblastic anemia: too few purines and thymidine, so cells can't make DNA","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 45"},{"src":"sl/G031-44.jpg","cap":"Folate forms feeding purine and thymidylate synthesis; deficiency gives megaloblastic anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 44"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-194-2","s":"Bonus question: what disease is caused by folic acid deficiency","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Megaloblastic anemia with only homocysteine accumulation","a":null,"why":"<b>Megaloblastic anemia.</b> Without folate, cells can't make enough <b>purines and thymidylate</b>, so DNA synthesis lags. Red-cell precursors keep growing but can't divide, giving large cells. <b>Homocysteine</b> rises; methylmalonic acid stays normal. In early pregnancy, folate lack also causes <b>neural tube defects</b>.<br>Common mix-up: B12 deficiency gives the same anemia but adds nerve signs and high methylmalonic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":194,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F0319-57-1","s":"Megaloblastic anemia is caused by abnormally slow synthesis of :","o":["A. S-adenosylmethionine.","B. dUMP.","C. folic acid.","D. hemoglobin.","E. DNA."],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"DNA.","a":"E","why":"<b>E. DNA.</b> Megaloblastic anemia comes from <b>slow DNA synthesis</b>: too little folate or B12 means too few purines and thymidylate. Cells keep growing but can't divide, so they become large.<br>A. S-adenosylmethionine: a methyl donor; its lack is not what enlarges red cells.<br>B. dUMP (deoxyuridine monophosphate): it builds up; it is thymidylate made from it that is slow.<br>C. Folic acid: a dietary vitamin; the body doesn't make it.<br>D. Hemoglobin: slow heme or hemoglobin synthesis gives microcytic anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 44 (Folic Acid (B9))"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":57,"f":"F0319"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Low folate in pregnancy → neural tube defects","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-46.jpg","cap":"Anencephaly and spina bifida photos; folate needed in the first month","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 46"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-151-1","s":"A 24 year old woman presents to the office concerned about her diet. She is thinking about becoming pregnant and already has one child born with spina bifida. What additional supplementation can she add that will be beneficial for her pregnancy?","o":["A. Iron","B. B12","C. Biotin","D. Folate"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Folate.</b> A prior child with <b>spina bifida</b> means a high risk of another <b>neural tube defect</b>. The tube closes days seventeen to thirty after conception, so folate must be taken <b>before and during the first month</b> of pregnancy.<br>A. Iron: prevents anemia, not neural tube defects.<br>B. B12: needed for blood and nerves; folate is the vitamin that prevents neural tube defects.<br>C. Biotin: carboxylase cofactor; no link to neural tube defects.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 46 (Neural tube defects)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":151,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":169,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":169,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0028-184-1","s":"A 23-year-old woman gives birth to an infant with birth defects pictured below. The mother did not receive prenatal care. Which vitamin was the mother likely deficient in?","o":["A. Iron","B. B12","C. Biotin","D. Folate"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Folate.</b> The photos show <b>anencephaly</b> (missing skull vault and upper brain) and <b>lumbosacral spina bifida</b>, the two main <b>neural tube defects</b>. They follow too little folate in the first month after conception, when the tube closes.<br>A. Iron: maternal lack causes anemia, not neural tube defects.<br>B. B12: megaloblastic anemia and neuropathy.<br>C. Biotin: dermatitis, alopecia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":"img/F0028-184.jpg","in":"Two infant photos showing neural tube defects (anencephaly; lumbosacral spina bifida/myelomeningocele) - the defect is shown only in the pictures","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 46 (Neural tube defects)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":184,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0621-172-1","s":"A woman who did not know she was pregnant until 5 months gestation presents to the OBGYN clinic. What birth defect is the fetus at risk of and what vitamin deficiency caused it?","o":["A. Achlorhydria; B9","B. Atrial septal defect; B12","C. Anencephaly and spina bifida; B9","D. Sideroblastic anemia; B6","E. DiGeorge Syndrome; B12"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Anencephaly and spina bifida; B9","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Anencephaly and spina bifida; B9 (folate).</b> With too little folate early in pregnancy, the <b>neural tube</b> fails to close (days seventeen to thirty). The lecture advises zero point four milligrams of folate in the first month.<br>A. Achlorhydria: low stomach acid; not a birth defect.<br>B. Atrial septal defect; B12: not a vitamin-deficiency defect.<br>D. Sideroblastic anemia; B6: not a birth defect.<br>E. DiGeorge syndrome: a chromosome 22 deletion, not a vitamin deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 46 (Neural tube defects)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":172,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F2296-205-1","s":"A new patient presents to the OB/GYN office. She is pregnant for the first time and is at 38 weeks gestation. She has immigrated to the U.S. 5 months ago, has not been taking any vitamins or supplements, and did not go to any prenatal visits because of lack of insurance. What disorder do you expect her baby to have?","o":["A. Menkes disease","B. Wilson’s Disease","C. Biotin deficiency","D. Pellagra","E. Spina Bifida","F. Microcytic Anemia"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Spina Bifida","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Spina bifida.</b> No vitamins and no prenatal care through the first trimester means likely <b>low folate</b> while the neural tube closed (days seventeen to thirty), so a <b>neural tube defect</b> is the expected risk.<br>A. Menkes disease: inherited ATP7A copper-pump gene defect.<br>B. Wilson disease: inherited ATP7B copper-pump gene defect.<br>C. Biotin deficiency: raw eggs or biotinidase defects.<br>D. Pellagra: niacin deficiency.<br>F. Microcytic anemia: iron or B6 deficiency; not the classic risk here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 46 (Neural tube defects)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":205,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"B12 made only by microbes; two active forms","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-47.jpg","cap":"Cobalamin structure; made only by microorganisms, absent in plants, vegans need supplements","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 47"},{"src":"sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"Methylcobalamin and deoxyadenosylcobalamin reactions","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-87-1","s":"A 24-year-old vegan presents with progressive fatigue and numbness in her feet. Laboratory studies show macrocytosis and elevated methylmalonic acid. She is started on intramuscular supplementation of the deficient vitamin. Which of the following best describes the functional forms and source classification of this vitamin?","o":["A. Pyridoxal phosphate derived from plant-based foods and required for transamination reactions","B. Tetrahydrofolate derived from leafy vegetables and required for one-carbon transfers","C. Methylcobalamin and deoxyadenosylcobalamin synthesized by microorganisms and absent in plants","D. Thiamine pyrophosphate derived from whole grains and required for oxidative decarboxylation","E. Flavin adenine dinucleotide synthesized from tryptophan in the liver"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Methylcobalamin and deoxyadenosylcobalamin synthesized by microorganisms and absent in plants","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Methylcobalamin and deoxyadenosylcobalamin; made only by microorganisms, absent in plants.</b> Vegan + numb feet + macrocytosis + high methylmalonic acid = <b>B12 deficiency</b>. B12 comes only from microbes, so vegans need supplements.<br>A. Pyridoxal phosphate: vitamin B6.<br>B. Tetrahydrofolate: folate; doesn't raise methylmalonic acid.<br>D. Thiamine pyrophosphate: vitamin B1.<br>E. FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide): from riboflavin; the body makes niacin, not FAD, from tryptophan.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 7 (Review of Objective 1)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 60 (Review of Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":87,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Lost acid or intrinsic factor → B12 deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-48.jpg","cap":"Stomach acid frees B12 from food; B12 binds haptocorrin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 48"},{"src":"sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells; cubilin uptake in ileum; transcobalamin in blood","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"},{"src":"sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"B12 deficiency causes: achlorhydria, proton pump inhibitors, autoimmune parietal-cell loss","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-215-1","s":"Which vitamin relies on intrinsic factor for absorption from the gut, and can cause megaloblastic anemia if deficient?","o":["A. B6","B. B7","C. B2","D. B9","E. B12"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"B12","a":"E","why":"<b>E. B12 (cobalamin).</b> B12 binds <b>intrinsic factor</b>, made by gastric <b>parietal cells</b>; the complex is taken up by <b>cubilin</b> receptors in the <b>ileum</b>. B12 lack causes megaloblastic anemia.<br>A. B6: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia; no intrinsic factor.<br>B. B7 (biotin): dermatitis, alopecia.<br>C. B2 (riboflavin): cheilosis, corneal vascularization.<br>D. B9 (folate): megaloblastic anemia too, but absorbed without intrinsic factor.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 48 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":215,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F0621-174-1","s":"A patient with a history of gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) presents with fatigue, tingling, loss of memory, and high levels of methylmalonyl CoA. What vitamin deficiency may have caused these symptoms?","o":["A. Folate","B. Cobalamin","C. Pyridoxine","D. Thiamine","E. Riboflavin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Cobalamin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Cobalamin (B12).</b> GERD (gastroesophageal reflux disease) is treated with <b>proton pump inhibitors</b>, which cut stomach acid. <b>Acid frees B12</b> from food proteins, so less B12 is absorbed. B12 lack causes tingling, memory loss and high <b>methylmalonyl-coenzyme A</b>.<br>A. Folate: megaloblastic anemia, but no nerve signs and normal methylmalonic acid.<br>C. Pyridoxine: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia.<br>D. Thiamine: beriberi, Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.<br>E. Riboflavin: mouth cracks, corneal vascularization.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 48 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 54 (B12 is needed for conversion of methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":174,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2435-6-1","s":"A 45-year-old female patient presents to her primary care physician 4 years after gastric bypass surgery complaining of numbness and tingling in her feet and fingertips. She has also been feeling fatigued over the last few months and cannot walk far before becoming winded. Physical exam shows conjunctival pallor and pale skin. Lab tests are conducted and demonstrate a hematocrit of 31% (normal range 35%-45%) and Mean Corpuscular Volume (MCV) of 112 fl (normal range 80 fl - 100 fl). A peripheral blood smear is obtained and is shown in figure below. What is the most likely cause of this patient's clinical syndrome?","o":["A. Cobalamin deficiency","B. Folate deficiency","C. Hypothyroidism","D. Iron deficiency","E. Lead poisoning"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Cobalamin deficiency","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Cobalamin deficiency.</b> <b>Gastric bypass</b> bypasses most of the stomach's acid and <b>intrinsic factor</b>, so B12 is poorly absorbed. Macrocytic anemia (mean corpuscular volume over one hundred) <b>plus numbness and tingling</b> points to B12.<br>B. Folate deficiency: macrocytic anemia too, but no neuropathy.<br>C. Hypothyroidism: can be mildly macrocytic, but doesn't explain the neuropathy.<br>D. Iron deficiency: microcytic anemia.<br>E. Lead poisoning: microcytic anemia with basophilic stippling.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"Blood smear (patient vs normal) showing oval macrocytes and a hypersegmented neutrophil; not needed to answer (MCV 112 + neuropathy after gastric bypass).","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":6,"f":"F2435"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Anti-intrinsic factor antibodies → pernicious anemia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-50.jpg","cap":"Autoimmune parietal-cell loss removes intrinsic factor: pernicious anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 50"},{"src":"sl/G031-49.jpg","cap":"Intrinsic factor from parietal cells is required for ileal B12 uptake","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 49"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-56-1","s":"Which among the following rules out megaloblastic anemia due to malabsorption of B12 ?","o":["A. High red cell width","B. Hyper segmented neutrophil","C. No history of medication","D. Current symptoms more than a year","E. Undetectable Anti Intrinsic factor antibodies"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Undetectable Anti Intrinsic factor antibodies","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Undetectable anti-intrinsic factor antibodies.</b> The main cause of B12 malabsorption is <b>pernicious anemia</b>: autoimmune loss of parietal cells and antibodies against <b>intrinsic factor</b>. Of these choices, only a negative antibody test points away from that cause.<br>A. High red cell distribution width: any megaloblastic anemia.<br>B. Hypersegmented neutrophils: both B12 and folate deficiency.<br>C. No medication history: doesn't exclude autoimmune or gastric causes.<br>D. Symptoms over a year: duration doesn't identify the cause.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 49 (vitamin B12 absorption)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 50 (Deficiency of Vitamin B12)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":56,"f":"F0319"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"High methylmalonic acid + homocysteine → B12","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-51.jpg","cap":"B12's two reactions: methionine synthase and methylmalonyl-CoA mutase","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 51"},{"src":"sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"B12 versus folate table: B12 raises homocysteine and methylmalonic acid","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-203-1","s":"Which one of the following is associated with accumulation of both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid?","o":["A. Vitamin B6","B. Vitamin B9","C. Vitamin B12","D. Folic Acid"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin B12.</b> B12 has two jobs: <b>methylcobalamin</b> turns homocysteine into methionine, and <b>deoxyadenosylcobalamin</b> turns methylmalonyl-coenzyme A into succinyl-coenzyme A. Lack of B12 raises <b>both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid</b>.<br>A. B6: can raise homocysteine (cystathionine step), not methylmalonic acid.<br>B. B9: folate; raises only homocysteine.<br>D. Folic acid: same as B9; raises only homocysteine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 54 (B12 is needed for conversion of methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":203,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F0950-3-1","s":"A 35-year-old woman presents with progressive fatigue and pallor. Labs reveal a macrocytic anemia with elevated methylmalonic acid and homocysteine levels. Which deficiency is the most likely cause?","o":["A. Folate","B. Vitamin B12","C. Iron","D. Vitamin B6"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Vitamin B12.</b> Macrocytic anemia with <b>both methylmalonic acid and homocysteine</b> raised is B12 deficiency, because B12 is needed by both methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase and methionine synthase.<br>A. Folate: macrocytic anemia with high homocysteine, but methylmalonic acid stays normal.<br>C. Iron: microcytic anemia.<br>D. Vitamin B6: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia from poor heme synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":3,"f":"F0950"}]},{"id":"F0928-24-1","s":"A 29-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with worsening fatigue and lightheadedness over the past several months. Laboratory studies are ordered as seen below. Hemoglobin: 9.0 g/dL (normal range 12.0 to 15.5 g/dL); Hematocrit: 29% (normal range 35%-45%); Methylmalonic acid: 1.0 mmol/L (normal < 0.40 mmol/L); Homocysteine: 20 mmol/L (normal 5-15 mmol/L). Which of the following is the most likely etiology of this patient's symptoms?","o":["A. Pyridoxine deficiency","B. Iron deficiency","C. Vitamin B12 deficiency","D. Ascorbate deficiency","E. Folate deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Vitamin B12 deficiency.</b> Anemia with <b>high methylmalonic acid</b> (one point zero; normal under zero point four) <b>and high homocysteine</b> (twenty; normal five to fifteen) means B12 deficiency, since only B12 serves both pathways.<br>A. Pyridoxine: microcytic, sideroblastic anemia.<br>B. Iron: microcytic anemia; no metabolite rise.<br>D. Ascorbate (vitamin C): scurvy.<br>E. Folate: raises homocysteine only.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":24,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":5,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":41,"f":"D006"}]},{"id":"F0028-188-1","s":"A 52 year old woman presents to the office complaining of feeling tired and lethargic. She states she been constantly forgetting things and also notes a tingling sensation in her hands and feet. Blood work shows megaloblastic anemia and elevated levels of homocysteine. What other metabolite level is expected to be elevated?","o":["A. methylmalonic acid","B. ketone bodies","C. folate","D. acetic acid"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"methylmalonic acid","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Methylmalonic acid.</b> Megaloblastic anemia with <b>memory loss and tingling</b> is <b>B12 deficiency</b>. B12 (as deoxyadenosylcobalamin) turns methylmalonyl-coenzyme A into succinyl-coenzyme A, so methylmalonic acid builds up along with homocysteine.<br>B. Ketone bodies: fasting or diabetic ketoacidosis.<br>C. Folate: may be normal or high (folate trap), but it is not the marker that defines B12 deficiency.<br>D. Acetic acid: not a marker of any vitamin deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 54 (B12 is needed for conversion of methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":188,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":155,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":175,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":175,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F3768-137-1","s":"A strict vegan presents with anemia. Lab studies show:\nMCV: 112 fL\nHypersegmented neutrophils\nElevated methylmalonic acid\nElevated homocysteine\nWhich of the following coenzyme functions is deficient?","o":["A. Pyridoxal phosphate functioning in aminotransferase reactions","B. Methylcobalamin functioning in the conversion of homocysteine to methionine","C. Tetrahydrofolate functioning in purine synthesis","D. Flavin adenine dinucleotide functioning in electron transport","E. Coenzyme A functioning in acetyl group transfer"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Methylcobalamin functioning in the conversion of homocysteine to methionine","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Methylcobalamin in homocysteine → methionine.</b> Vegan + macrocytosis + hypersegmented neutrophils + <b>both methylmalonic acid and homocysteine raised</b> = <b>B12 deficiency</b>. Methylcobalamin is the B12 form used by methionine synthase.<br>A. Pyridoxal phosphate (B6): transamination; deficiency is microcytic.<br>C. Tetrahydrofolate (folate): its lack doesn't raise methylmalonic acid.<br>D. FAD (flavin adenine dinucleotide): riboflavin.<br>E. Coenzyme A: pantothenic acid.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 47 (Vitamin B12 - Cobalamin)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":137,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Folate trap: B12 lack → functional folate deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-52.jpg","cap":"Folate trap diagram: folate stuck as N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate without B12","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 52"}],"q":[{"id":"F1201-153-1","s":"Which vitamin deficiency will lead to a functional folate deficiency?","o":["A. Thiamine","B. Riboflavin","C. B12","D. Niacin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"B12","a":"C","why":"<b>C. B12.</b> Methionine synthase, which needs <b>B12</b>, is the only enzyme that removes the methyl group from <b>N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate</b>. Without B12, folate is <b>trapped</b> in that form, so cells act folate-deficient: the <b>folate trap</b>.<br>A. Thiamine (B1): oxidative decarboxylation; beriberi.<br>B. Riboflavin (B2): flavin coenzymes (FAD, flavin adenine dinucleotide) for redox reactions.<br>D. Niacin (B3): NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide); pellagra.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 52 (http://lpi.oregonstate.edu/mic/vitamins/folate)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":153,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":172,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":172,"f":"F1218"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0035-218-1","s":"Recall: Describe Folate Trap","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Folate equilibrium lies toward N5-methyl-THF; methionine synthase (needs B12) is the only way to remove that methyl group, so in B12 deficiency folate is trapped as N5-methyl-THF → functional folate deficiency (megaloblastic anemia)","a":null,"why":"<b>Folate trap:</b> folate's reactions run toward <b>N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate</b>, and this step can't be reversed. Only <b>methionine synthase</b>, which needs <b>B12</b>, removes that methyl group (handing it to homocysteine to make methionine). Without B12, most folate is <b>stuck</b> as N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate, so there's too little usable folate for DNA: a <b>functional folate deficiency</b> with megaloblastic anemia.<br>Common mix-up: folate levels can be normal; the folate is just unusable.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":218,"f":"F0035"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Folate deficiency: homocysteine only, no neuropathy","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"B12 versus folate table: folate gives anemia only, homocysteine only","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-160-1","s":"Q4 - A Folic Acid deficiency leads to which of the following?","o":["A. Loss of memory","B. Tingling","C. Accumulation of Methylmalonic Acid","D. Accumulation of Homocystine","E. Ataxia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Accumulation of Homocystine","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Accumulation of homocysteine.</b> Folate (as N5-methyl-tetrahydrofolate) gives the methyl group that turns homocysteine into methionine, so folate lack raises <b>homocysteine only</b>. Folate deficiency causes <b>only megaloblastic anemia</b>, no nerve signs.<br>A. Loss of memory: a B12 nerve sign.<br>B. Tingling: a B12 nerve sign.<br>C. Methylmalonic acid: builds up only in B12 deficiency.<br>E. Ataxia: a B12 nerve sign.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":160,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":140,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F3881-179-1","s":"Which of the following is true for a folate (B9) deficiency? (Hint: there are 2 correct answers)","o":["A. Deficiency of B9 causes megaloblastic anemia + neurological symptoms (tingling, loss of memory)","B. Deficiency of B9 causes ONLY megaloblastic anemia","C. There is an accumulation of ONLY homocysteine","D. There is an accumulation of both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid"],"g":["B","C"],"k":["B","C"],"rv":false,"at":"Deficiency of B9 causes ONLY megaloblastic anemia + There is an accumulation of ONLY homocysteine","a":"B,C","why":"<b>B and C.</b> Folate deficiency causes <b>only megaloblastic anemia</b> (no nerve damage) and raises <b>only homocysteine</b>.<br>A. Anemia plus tingling and memory loss: <b>B12</b> deficiency.<br>D. Both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid: <b>B12</b> deficiency, since B12 also runs methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":179,"f":"F3881"}]},{"id":"F3768-134-1","s":"A 70-year-old woman on long-term proton pump inhibitors presents with macrocytic anemia. She has normal methylmalonic acid but elevated homocysteine levels. Which vitamin deficiency best explains this pattern?","o":["A. Vitamin B6 deficiency impairing transamination reactions","B. Vitamin B12 deficiency impairing methionine synthase","C. Niacin deficiency impairing NAD⁺ production","D. Riboflavin deficiency impairing oxidoreduction","E. Folate deficiency impairing thymidylate synthesis"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate deficiency impairing thymidylate synthesis","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Folate deficiency impairing thymidylate synthesis.</b> Macrocytic anemia + high homocysteine + <b>normal methylmalonic acid</b> = <b>folate</b> deficiency. The proton pump inhibitor hints at B12, but the labs decide.<br>A. B6: microcytic, not macrocytic, anemia.<br>B. B12: would also raise methylmalonic acid.<br>C. Niacin (NAD+, nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide): pellagra, not anemia.<br>D. Riboflavin: mouth cracks, corneal vascularization; not anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 45 (Megaloblastic anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":134,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3768-147-1","s":"A patient presents with megaloblastic anemia. Homocysteine is elevated, but methylmalonic acid is normal. Neurologic exam is normal. Which of the following laboratory findings would most strongly distinguish this condition from B12 deficiency?","o":["A. Elevated methylmalonic acid levels","B. Elevated propionyl-CoA levels","C. Increased serum homocysteine levels","D. Hypersegmented neutrophils","E. Decreased thymidylate synthesis"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Elevated methylmalonic acid levels","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Elevated methylmalonic acid.</b> High homocysteine, normal methylmalonic acid and normal nerves mean <b>folate</b> deficiency. The test that separates it from B12 deficiency is <b>methylmalonic acid</b>: high only when B12 is lacking.<br>B. Propionyl-coenzyme A: not a routine test or the standard discriminator.<br>C. High homocysteine: seen in both.<br>D. Hypersegmented neutrophils: seen in both.<br>E. Decreased thymidylate synthesis: happens in both.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":147,"f":"F3768"}]},{"id":"F0621-196-1","s":"Bonus question 2: What’s the differences between Folic Acid deficiency vs B12 deficiency?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia AND neurological symptoms (tingling, neuromuscular incoordination, memory loss) with accumulation of both homocysteine and methylmalonic acid; folate deficiency causes only megaloblastic anemia with only homocysteine accumulation. Folic acid can reverse the hematologic abnormalities of B12 deficiency and mask it, so therapy starts with both B12 and folate until the cause is known.","a":null,"why":"<b>B12 deficiency:</b> megaloblastic anemia <b>plus nerve signs</b> (tingling, poor coordination, memory loss); <b>homocysteine and methylmalonic acid</b> both rise.<br><b>Folate deficiency:</b> <b>megaloblastic anemia only</b>; <b>only homocysteine</b> rises.<br>Folic acid alone can fix the anemia of B12 deficiency and <b>mask</b> it while nerve damage goes on, so treatment starts with both until the cause is known.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":196,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Folate masks B12 deficiency","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-53.jpg","cap":"Folic acid reverses B12's anemia and can mask it; treat with both","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 53"},{"src":"sl/G031-54.jpg","cap":"Methylmalonyl-CoA buildup makes abnormal fatty acids that damage myelin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 54"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-140-1","s":"A patient with B12 deficiency is treated with folate supplementation alone. Hemoglobin levels improve, but neurologic symptoms progress. Which biochemical phenomenon explains this outcome?","o":["A. Excess folate competitively inhibits B12 absorption","B. Folate corrects methylmalonyl-CoA accumulation but not homocysteine levels","C. Folate supplementation bypasses the need for methionine synthase in myelin synthesis","D. Folate reverses impaired DNA synthesis but does not correct methylmalonyl-CoA accumulation","E. Folate enhances transamination reactions independent of B12"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Folate reverses impaired DNA synthesis but does not correct methylmalonyl-CoA accumulation","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Folate restores DNA synthesis but not methylmalonyl-coenzyme A handling.</b> Extra folate gives enough tetrahydrofolate for purines and thymidylate, so the <b>anemia improves</b>. Methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase still needs B12, so abnormal fatty acids keep damaging <b>myelin</b>. Folate <b>masks</b> B12 deficiency.<br>A. Folate doesn't block B12 absorption.<br>B. Backwards: folate can't clear methylmalonyl-coenzyme A.<br>C. Folate doesn't bypass methionine synthase.<br>E. Transamination uses B6, not folate.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 53 (B12  Vs. Folate deficiency)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 54 (B12 is needed for conversion of methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":140,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"B12 neuropathy: methylmalonyl-CoA, methylcitrate","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-54.jpg","cap":"Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase needs B12; abnormal fatty acids enter myelin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 54"},{"src":"sl/G031-55.jpg","cap":"Propionyl-CoA + oxaloacetate → 2-methylcitrate, a brain mitochondrial toxin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 55"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-143-1","s":"A 63-year-old woman presents with fatigue and difficulty walking. Examination reveals decreased vibration sense in the lower extremities and a positive Romberg sign. Labs show:\nHb: 9.1 g/dL\nMCV: 116 fL\nHypersegmented neutrophils\nElevated homocysteine\nElevated methylmalonic acid\nWhich of the following metabolic reactions is directly impaired and contributes to neurologic injury?","o":["A. Conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine","B. Reduction of dihydrofolate to tetrahydrofolate","C. Isomerization of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA","D. Formation of δ-aminolevulinic acid from glycine and succinyl-CoA","E. Synthesis of thymidylate from deoxyuridylate"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Isomerization of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Methylmalonyl-coenzyme A → succinyl-coenzyme A.</b> Neuropathy (lost vibration sense, positive Romberg) with high methylmalonic acid = <b>B12 deficiency</b>. <b>Deoxyadenosylcobalamin</b> is needed by methylmalonyl-coenzyme A mutase; the backup makes <b>abnormal fatty acids</b> that enter myelin.<br>A. Homocysteine → cystathionine: uses B6.<br>B. Dihydrofolate → tetrahydrofolate: folate pathway.<br>D. Delta-aminolevulinic acid formation: uses B6.<br>E. Thymidylate from deoxyuridylate: folate-dependent; causes the anemia, not the neuropathy.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 54 (B12 is needed for conversion of methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 51 (Biochemical basis of B12 deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":143,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0998-141-1","s":"Which of the following statements best describes the biochemical mechanism underlying the neurologic deficits observed in vitamin B12 deficiency?","o":["A. Impaired fatty acid oxidation secondary to methylmalonyl-CoA accumulation","B. Diminished myelin production due to reduced methionine synthesis","C. Axonal degeneration resulting from methylcitrate toxicity","D. Cerebral edema caused by homocysteine-induced oxidative stress"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Axonal degeneration resulting from methylcitrate toxicity","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Axonal damage from methylcitrate toxicity.</b> In B12 deficiency, propionyl-coenzyme A backs up. Citrate synthase joins it with oxaloacetate to make <b>2-methylcitrate</b>, a <b>mitochondrial toxin</b> that opens the permeability transition pore in <b>brain mitochondria</b>.<br>A. Methylmalonyl-coenzyme A blocks fatty acid <b>synthesis</b> (making abnormal fatty acids), not oxidation.<br>B. The lecture ties nerve damage to the methylmalonyl-coenzyme A branch, not to low methionine.<br>D. Homocysteine is a cardiovascular risk factor; cerebral edema isn't the B12 mechanism.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 54 (B12 is needed for conversion of methylmalonyl CoA into succinyl CoA)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 55 (Propionyl-CoA and oxaloacetate is condensed by the enzyme citrate synthase to fo)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 60 (Review of Objective 4)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":141,"f":"F0998"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Macroelements (grams/day) vs microelements","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-64.jpg","cap":"Macroelements (grams per day) versus microelements (micrograms to milligrams per day)","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 64"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-156-1","s":"Which of the following minerals is classified as a macroelements due to its daily requirement in gram quantities?","o":["A. Iron","B. Zinc","C. Selenium","D. Calcium","E. Copper"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Calcium","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Calcium.</b> <b>Macroelements</b> are needed in <b>grams per day</b>: calcium, phosphorus, magnesium, sodium, potassium and chloride. <b>Microelements</b> (trace elements) are needed in micrograms to milligrams per day: iron, copper, zinc, iodine, selenium, chromium, manganese, molybdenum, fluorine.<br>A. Iron: microelement.<br>B. Zinc: microelement.<br>C. Selenium: microelement.<br>E. Copper: microelement.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 64 (Macroelements and)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":156,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Wilson: ATP7B fails to excrete copper into bile","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-66.jpg","cap":"ATP7B in liver trans-Golgi sends copper into bile and ceruloplasmin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 66"},{"src":"sl/G031-67.jpg","cap":"Wilson disease features, incl. Kayser-Fleischer ring photo","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 67"}],"q":[{"id":"F1013-148-1","s":"A 15-year-old girl presents with neurological symptoms, including tremors and difficulty speaking, along with a brown ring around the iris and hepatomegaly. Laboratory tests show elevated liver enzymes, and a slit-lamp examination reveals Kayser-Fleischer rings. Genetic testing confirms a mutation in the ATP7B gene. Based on her clinical presentation, which of the following biochemical processes is most likely impaired in this patient?","o":["A. Copper absorption and transport to tissues for use in enzymatic processes","B. Excretion of excess copper through bile from the liver","C. Copper incorporation into ceruloplasmin for antioxidant defense","D. Conversion of copper to a non-toxic form for safe storage in tissues"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Excretion of excess copper through bile from the liver","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Excretion of excess copper into bile.</b> <b>ATP7B</b> is a copper pump in the liver's trans-Golgi network that sends excess copper into <b>bile</b>. When it fails, copper builds up in liver, brain and cornea (<b>Kayser-Fleischer rings</b>).<br>A. Faulty absorption and transport to tissues: Menkes disease (ATP7A).<br>C. ATP7B also loads copper into ceruloplasmin (so ceruloplasmin is low), but the toxic build-up comes from failed bile excretion.<br>D. Safe storage (metallothionein binding): not the defect.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 66 (Mutations in ATP7B gene)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":148,"f":"F1013"}]},{"id":"F3768-150-1","s":"A 19-year-old male presents with tremors, dysarthria, and personality changes. Slit-lamp examination reveals pigmented rings at the corneal margin. Laboratory testing shows decreased serum ceruloplasmin and elevated hepatic copper concentration. Which of the following best describes the underlying defect?","o":["A. Mutation in a copper transporter required for intestinal absorption leading to systemic copper deficiency","B. Excess dietary intake of a macroelement required in gram quantities per day","C. Zinc deficiency resulting in impaired metallothionein synthesis","D. Defective iron incorporation into hemoglobin due to copper deficiency","E. Impaired excretion of copper into bile due to dysfunction of a trans-Golgi copper-transporting ATPase"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired excretion of copper into bile due to dysfunction of a trans-Golgi copper-transporting ATPase","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Poor copper excretion into bile from a faulty trans-Golgi copper pump.</b> Tremor, dysarthria, personality change, <b>Kayser-Fleischer rings</b>, low ceruloplasmin and high liver copper = <b>Wilson disease</b>, an ATP7B gene mutation.<br>A. Faulty absorption causing copper deficiency: Menkes disease (ATP7A).<br>B. Copper is a <b>microelement</b> (milligrams a day); excess intake isn't the cause.<br>C. Zinc deficiency doesn't cause copper overload.<br>D. Copper deficiency doesn't fit high liver copper.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 66 (Mutations in ATP7B gene)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":150,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Wilson: copper in tissues, low ceruloplasmin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-67.jpg","cap":"Wilson disease: copper accumulates in tissues; liver, neuropsychiatric, Kayser-Fleischer rings","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 67"},{"src":"sl/G031-66.jpg","cap":"Defective ATP7B: copper builds up in liver, free copper spreads to organs","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"F0319-60-1","s":"Wilsons disease may be associated with:","o":["A. ophthalmoplegia ,ataxia and encephalopathy","B. low Ferritin, Iron and % Transferrin saturation","C. Impaired DNA synthesis and megaloblasts","D. Hepatolenticular degeneration and  low plasma ceruloplasmin","E. Photosensitive dermatitis and gross aminoaciduria"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepatolenticular degeneration and  low plasma ceruloplasmin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hepatolenticular degeneration and low plasma ceruloplasmin.</b> Wilson disease damages the <b>liver</b> and the <b>lenticular nucleus</b> of the basal ganglia. <b>Ceruloplasmin</b> is low because ATP7B can't load copper into it.<br>A. Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, encephalopathy: Wernicke encephalopathy (thiamine).<br>B. Low ferritin, iron and transferrin saturation: iron deficiency.<br>C. Impaired DNA synthesis, megaloblasts: folate or B12 deficiency.<br>E. Photosensitive dermatitis with gross aminoaciduria: Hartnup disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 66 (Mutations in ATP7B gene)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 faculty review","n":"Dr. Bagh Exam 4 review Fall 2022","sl":60,"f":"F0319"}]},{"id":"F0577-162-1","s":"Q5 - A 25 year old male presents to the Emergency Department with movement disorders, drooling, dysarthria, migraines and insomnia and a recent seizure. The patient is known to have a mutation in the ATP7B gene. Which of the following laboratory findings if the patient likely to have?","o":["A. Low serum selenium","B. Low serum zinc","C. High serum copper","D. High levels of copper in tissues","E. Low levels of copper"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"High levels of copper in tissues","a":"D","why":"<b>D. High copper in tissues.</b> ATP7B mutation = <b>Wilson disease</b>: copper can't be excreted into bile, so it <b>accumulates in liver, brain</b>, cornea and other organs.<br>A. Low selenium: cardiomyopathy.<br>B. Low zinc: rash, poor healing, weak immunity.<br>C. High serum copper: total serum copper is usually <b>low</b>, because little copper is bound to ceruloplasmin; only free copper is high.<br>E. Low copper: Menkes disease (ATP7A).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 66 (Mutations in ATP7B gene)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":162,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":142,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Wilson = ATP7B; Menkes = ATP7A","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-72.jpg","cap":"Review: Wilson (copper accumulation) versus Menkes (ATP7A, kinky hair)","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 72"},{"src":"sl/G031-68.jpg","cap":"Menkes: ATP7A mutation, poor copper distribution, lysyl oxidase, kinky hair","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 68"},{"src":"sl/G031-66.jpg","cap":"Wilson: ATP7B liver copper pump into bile","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-220-1","s":"True or False? Wilson’s Disease is characterized by poor distribution of copper in the body, and is due to a mutation in ATP7A?","o":["A. True","B. False"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"False","a":"B","why":"<b>False.</b> Wilson disease is copper <b>accumulation</b> in tissues from an <b>ATP7B</b> mutation (the liver pump that sends copper into bile). <b>Poor copper distribution</b> from an <b>ATP7A</b> mutation is <b>Menkes disease</b>.<br>Common mix-up: the two genes. Wilson = ATP7B, too much copper in liver and brain; Menkes = ATP7A, too little copper reaching tissues.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 66 (Mutations in ATP7B gene)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":220,"f":"F0035"}]},{"id":"F3881-182-1","s":"Which of the following is true for Menkes disease?","o":["A. A common clinical presentation would be Kayser-Fleischer rings","B. Caused by a mutation in the ATP7B gene","C. Caused by a mutation in the ATP7A gene","D. Associated with a deficiency in zinc"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Caused by a mutation in the ATP7A gene","a":"C","why":"<b>C. ATP7A mutation.</b> In Menkes disease a faulty <b>ATP7A</b> copper pump leaves copper poorly distributed, so copper enzymes such as lysyl oxidase fail: <b>kinky hair, loose skin and joints</b>.<br>A. Kayser-Fleischer rings: Wilson disease.<br>B. ATP7B mutation: Wilson disease.<br>D. Zinc deficiency: Menkes is a copper disorder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":182,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0028-192-1","s":"Which of the following pairings is correct?","o":["A. Copper deficiency – impaired immune functioning","B. Wilson’s Disease – ATP7A mutation","C. Coarse, kinky hair; loose skin and joints – ATP7A mutation","D. Menkes disease – ATP7B mutation"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Coarse, kinky hair; loose skin and joints – ATP7A mutation","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Coarse kinky hair, loose skin and joints – ATP7A.</b> That is <b>Menkes disease</b>: a faulty ATP7A copper pump leaves tissues copper-poor, so copper-containing <b>lysyl oxidase</b> can't cross-link collagen.<br>A. Impaired immune function is the hallmark of <b>zinc</b> deficiency (zinc is needed for interleukin-2 and T cells); copper deficiency is taught as anemia and skeletal defects.<br>B. Wilson disease is ATP7B, not ATP7A.<br>D. Menkes disease is ATP7A, not ATP7B.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":192,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0621-176-1","s":"Wilsons or Menkes? Kayser-Fleischer rings; ATP7B; Loose skin and joints; Cardiomyopathy; Poor distribution of copper in the body; Copper cation pump in the liver; Aminoaciduria; ATP7A; Coarse, kinky, colorless hair; Neuropsychiatric symptoms; Accumulation of copper in the tissues; Pancreatitis; Accumulation of copper in intestinal mucosa, muscl, spleen, kidney; Lysyl oxidase deficiency; Liver disease","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Wilson: Kayser-Fleischer rings, ATP7B, cardiomyopathy, copper cation pump in the liver, aminoaciduria, neuropsychiatric symptoms, accumulation of copper in the tissues, pancreatitis, liver disease | Menkes: loose skin and joints, poor distribution of copper in the body, ATP7A, coarse kinky colorless hair, accumulation of copper in intestinal mucosa/muscle/spleen/kidney, lysyl oxidase deficiency","a":null,"why":"<b>Wilson (ATP7B):</b> the liver copper pump fails and copper <b>accumulates</b>: liver disease, neuropsychiatric signs, <b>Kayser-Fleischer rings</b>, cardiomyopathy, pancreatitis, aminoaciduria.<br><b>Menkes (ATP7A):</b> copper is <b>poorly distributed</b>, stuck in intestinal mucosa, muscle, spleen and kidney; <b>lysyl oxidase</b> fails, giving coarse kinky colorless hair and loose skin and joints.<br>Common mix-up: Wilson = too much copper in tissues; Menkes = too little copper where it's needed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":176,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Wilson: free copper → oxidative organ damage","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-66.jpg","cap":"Defective ATP7B: excess free copper carried to other organs in blood","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 66"},{"src":"sl/G031-65.jpg","cap":"Pro-oxidant versus antioxidant micronutrients: copper ions are pro-oxidant","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 65"},{"src":"sl/G031-67.jpg","cap":"Wilson disease: cardiomyopathy and pancreatitis among systemic signs","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 67"}],"q":[{"id":"F3768-160-1","s":"A patient with Wilson’s disease develops cardiomyopathy and pancreatitis. Which mechanism best explains systemic manifestations?","o":["A. Copper deficiency impairing collagen cross-linking","B. Excess free copper generating oxidative damage in multiple tissues","C. Zinc deficiency causing impaired immune regulation","D. Decreased ceruloplasmin synthesis due to folate deficiency","E. Increased biliary excretion of copper leading to systemic depletion"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Excess free copper generating oxidative damage in multiple tissues","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Excess free copper causes oxidative damage.</b> With ATP7B broken, copper fills the liver, then <b>free copper</b> spills into blood and lodges in brain, <b>heart and pancreas</b>. Copper ions are <b>pro-oxidants</b> that drive free-radical damage.<br>A. Copper deficiency with poor collagen cross-linking: Menkes disease.<br>C. Zinc deficiency: rash and weak immunity, not organ copper damage.<br>D. Ceruloplasmin is low from the ATP7B defect, not from folate deficiency.<br>E. Biliary copper excretion is decreased, not increased.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 67 (Copper – Wilson’s Disease)","L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 65 (| Pro-Oxidant | Anti-Oxidant |)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":160,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Menkes: kinky hair, lax skin → lysyl oxidase","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-68.jpg","cap":"Menkes: copper-containing lysyl oxidase cross-links collagen; kinky hair, loose skin","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 68"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-143-1","s":"A 3-year-old girl presents with abnormal brittle hair and skin laxity. Serum analysis shows low copper and ceruloplasmin levels. These clinical and laboratory findings are most consistent with which of the following diagnoses?","o":["A. Menkes disease","B. Wilson disease","C. Vitamin C deficiency","D. Zinc toxicity"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Menkes disease","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Menkes disease.</b> A young child with <b>brittle kinky hair</b>, <b>skin laxity</b> and <b>low serum copper and ceruloplasmin</b> has Menkes: ATP7A mutation, poor copper distribution, weak lysyl oxidase.<br>B. Wilson disease: ceruloplasmin is low too, but copper builds up in liver and brain; no kinky hair or lax skin.<br>C. Vitamin C deficiency: corkscrew hairs and bleeding gums; copper is normal.<br>D. Zinc toxicity: can lower copper, but doesn't explain this congenital picture.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":143,"f":"F0998"}]},{"id":"F3768-153-1","s":"A 2-year-old child presents with poor growth, hypotonia, brittle “kinky” hair, and joint laxity. Laboratory testing reveals low serum copper. Which enzyme activity is most directly reduced in this condition?","o":["A. Lysyl oxidase required for collagen cross-linking","B. γ-carboxylase required for clotting factor activation","C. ALA dehydratase involved in heme synthesis","D. Pyruvate carboxylase involved in gluconeogenesis","E. Transketolase in the pentose phosphate pathway"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Lysyl oxidase required for collagen cross-linking","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Lysyl oxidase.</b> Kinky hair, joint laxity, hypotonia and low copper in a toddler = <b>Menkes disease</b>. <b>Lysyl oxidase</b> contains copper and cross-links collagen and elastin; without it, skin, joints, vessels and hair are weak.<br>B. Gamma-carboxylase: vitamin K.<br>C. Aminolevulinic acid dehydratase: a zinc enzyme (lead displaces the zinc).<br>D. Pyruvate carboxylase: biotin.<br>E. Transketolase: thiamine.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 68 (Menkes disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":153,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L35","label":"Zinc deficiency: periorificial rash, taste, immunity","slides":[{"src":"sl/G031-69.jpg","cap":"Zinc deficiency: periorificial rash, poor healing, interleukin-2 immunity, taste, penicillamine","where":"2026 lecture: L35 Water-Soluble Vitamins, slide 69"}],"q":[{"id":"F2296-208-1","s":"Your new pediatric patient presents with the the sharply-demarcated red rash around his mouth and decreased taste sensations. Upon the physical examination, you notice a few stretches that, according to patient’s mom, had been there for a while. His height is below the 3rd percentile for his age. Your findings together with the history provided by patient’s mom make you suggest that this patient has ___.","o":["A. Copper Deficiency","B. Zinc Deficiency","C. Folic Acid Deficiency","D. Beriberi","E. Iron Deficiency"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Zinc Deficiency","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Zinc deficiency.</b> A sharply bordered <b>rash around the mouth</b> (periorificial dermatitis), <b>reduced taste</b> (gustin, a salivary taste protein, contains zinc) and <b>short stature</b> are classic for zinc deficiency in children.<br>A. Copper deficiency: anemia, kinky hair, lax skin.<br>C. Folic acid deficiency: megaloblastic anemia.<br>D. Beriberi: thiamine; neuropathy, heart failure.<br>E. Iron deficiency: microcytic anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 69 (Dermatitis, poor wound healing, periorificial lesions)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":208,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F3768-158-1","s":"A patient being treated for Wilson’s disease with penicillamine develops dermatitis and impaired wound healing. Which mineral deficiency most likely explains these findings?","o":["A. Selenium deficiency impairing glutathione peroxidase","B. Iron deficiency impairing hemoglobin synthesis","C. Zinc deficiency due to chelation therapy","D. Magnesium deficiency affecting ATP stability","E. Manganese deficiency impairing mitochondrial enzymes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Zinc deficiency due to chelation therapy","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Zinc deficiency from chelation.</b> <b>Penicillamine</b> chelates copper in Wilson disease but also removes <b>zinc</b>. Zinc deficiency gives <b>dermatitis, poor wound healing</b>, periorificial lesions and weak immunity.<br>A. Selenium (glutathione peroxidase): cardiomyopathy.<br>B. Iron: microcytic anemia.<br>D. Magnesium: cramps and arrhythmias, not dermatitis.<br>E. Manganese: growth retardation.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 69 (Dermatitis, poor wound healing, periorificial lesions)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":158,"f":"F3768"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3768-162-1","s":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the clinic for frequent infections and poor wound healing. His parents report that he has a limited diet and is a very picky eater. On examination, he has a rash around his mouth and nose (periorificial dermatitis) and decreased sense of taste. Laboratory testing reveals low levels of a trace mineral required in small (milligram) amounts in the diet. Which of the following best explains this child’s increased susceptibility to infections?","o":["A. Decreased regulation and activation of T lymphocytes due to deficiency of a trace mineral cofactor","B. Impaired collagen cross-linking caused by reduced lysyl oxidase activity","C. Accumulation of copper in tissues due to impaired biliary excretion","D. Decreased production of NAD⁺ leading to impaired cellular energy metabolism","E. Impaired heme synthesis due to reduced δ-aminolevulinic acid synthase activity"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased regulation and activation of T lymphocytes due to deficiency of a trace mineral cofactor","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Poor T-cell regulation from lack of a trace-mineral cofactor.</b> Periorificial rash, poor healing and low taste = <b>zinc deficiency</b>. Zinc is needed for <b>interleukin-2</b> to regulate T lymphocytes, helper T cells and natural killer cells, so infections are frequent.<br>B. Lysyl oxidase: copper (Menkes disease).<br>C. Copper build-up: Wilson disease.<br>D. NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide): niacin.<br>E. Aminolevulinic acid synthase: vitamin B6.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L35 Water-Sol. Vitamins slide 69 (Dermatitis, poor wound healing, periorificial lesions)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 spring Montana TA review","n":"MoD Exam 3 TA review: vitamin part only","sl":162,"f":"F3768"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Heme vs non-heme iron proteins","slides":[{"src":"sl/G026-5.jpg","cap":"Heme proteins: hemoglobin, myoglobin, cytochromes, catalase carry iron in protoporphyrin","where":"2026 lecture: L31 Heme & Bilirubin, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/F0257-66.jpg","cap":"Heme iron examples vs non-heme iron-sulfur proteins like Complexes I and II","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-226-1","s":"Iron has an important role in metabolism. Cytochrome P450 is an example of what kind of iron in the body?","o":["A. Heme Iron","B. Non-Heme Iron"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme Iron","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Heme iron.</b> Cytochrome P450 enzymes carry a <b>heme</b> group; the name \"cytochrome\" means a colored heme protein.<br>B. Non-heme iron: iron-sulfur or bare-iron proteins, such as succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II), NADH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) dehydrogenase (Complex I), lipoxygenases and ribonucleotide reductase.<br>Rule: every cytochrome, plus hemoglobin and myoglobin, is heme iron.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 5 (Heme)","L36 Iron slide 4 (Iron: What is it and Why It Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":226,"f":"F0035"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":160,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":183,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":183,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0257-62-1","s":"Which protein listed below contains heme iron?","o":["A. Ribonucleotide reductase","B. Phenylalanine hydroxylase","C. Lipoxygenases","D. Myoglobin","E. NADH dehydrogenase (Complex I)"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Myoglobin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Myoglobin</b> carries a <b>heme</b> group (iron inside a porphyrin ring) that binds oxygen in muscle.<br>A. Ribonucleotide reductase: non-heme iron; makes DNA building blocks.<br>B. Phenylalanine hydroxylase: non-heme iron; turns phenylalanine into tyrosine.<br>C. Lipoxygenases: non-heme iron; make leukotrienes from arachidonic acid.<br>E. NADH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) dehydrogenase, Complex I: iron-sulfur clusters, not heme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 4 (Iron: What is it and Why It Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":62,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":62,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0257-60-1","s":"Which of the following is an example of a non-heme iron protein (iron-sulfur protein) involved in metabolism?","o":["A. Hemoglobin","B. Myoglobin","C. Cytochrome P450 enzymes","D. Succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II)","E. Cytochrome c oxidase"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II)","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II)</b> holds its iron in <b>iron-sulfur clusters</b>, not in heme, so it is a non-heme iron protein.<br>A. Hemoglobin: heme iron; carries oxygen in red cells.<br>B. Myoglobin: heme iron; stores oxygen in muscle.<br>C. Cytochrome P450 enzymes: heme iron; liver drug-oxidizing enzymes.<br>E. Cytochrome c oxidase (Complex IV): heme iron (hemes a and a3) in the electron transport chain.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 4 (Iron: What is it and Why It Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":60,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":60,"f":"F0938"},{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":195,"f":"F0028"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3881-185-1","s":"Knowledge Check: List examples of heme iron and non-heme iron proteins.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Heme iron: hemoglobin, myoglobin, cytochrome P450, cytochrome c oxidase. Non-heme iron: succinate dehydrogenase (complex II), NADH dehydrogenase (complex I), lipoxygenases, phenylalanine hydroxylase, ribonucleotide reductase","a":null,"why":"<b>Heme iron</b> (iron inside a porphyrin ring): <b>hemoglobin, myoglobin, cytochrome P450 enzymes, cytochrome c oxidase</b> and other cytochromes.<br><b>Non-heme iron</b>: the iron-sulfur proteins <b>succinate dehydrogenase (Complex II)</b> and <b>NADH (reduced nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide) dehydrogenase (Complex I)</b>, plus enzymes with bare active-site iron: <b>lipoxygenases, phenylalanine hydroxylase, ribonucleotide reductase</b>.<br>Mix-up: anything named \"cytochrome\" is heme; Complexes I and II are iron-sulfur.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L31 Heme slide 5 (Heme)","L36 Iron slide 4 (Iron: What is it and Why It Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":185,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron's job: oxygen and energy metabolism","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-4.jpg","cap":"Iron supports oxygen transport, electron transport, DNA synthesis, redox enzymes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 4"},{"src":"sl/F0257-66.jpg","cap":"Iron is a cofactor for oxygen and energy metabolism proteins","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 66"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-64-1","s":"Iron is essential in metabolism because it serves as a cofactor for proteins and enzymes involved in which primary processes?","o":["A. Glucose transport and glycogen synthesis","B. Oxygen and energy metabolism","C. Amino acid transamination","D. Lipid storage and fatty acid elongation","E. Purine and pyrimidine salvage pathways"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Oxygen and energy metabolism","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Iron is a cofactor mainly for <b>oxygen handling</b> (hemoglobin, myoglobin) and <b>energy metabolism</b> (electron transport chain cytochromes and iron-sulfur proteins).<br>A. Glucose transporters and glycogen synthase need no iron.<br>C. Transamination needs vitamin B6 (pyridoxal phosphate), not iron.<br>D. Lipid storage and fatty acid elongation are not iron-driven.<br>E. Purine and pyrimidine salvage uses phosphoribosyl pyrophosphate; iron's DNA role is ribonucleotide reductase.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 4 (Iron: What is it and Why It Matters)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":64,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":64,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Fenton reaction → hydroxyl radical","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-5.jpg","cap":"Fenton reaction makes hydroxyl radical, which no enzyme can detoxify","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 5"},{"src":"sl/F0257-69.jpg","cap":"Reactive oxygen species chain with the Fenton reaction circled","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 69"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-67-1","s":"Free iron can be very toxic because:","o":["A. The body cannot detoxify the HO• radical it helps generate","B. It increases water production in mitochondria","C. It prevents formation of reactive oxygen species","D. It is rapidly converted into harmless Fe³⁺","E. It neutralizes hydrogen peroxide"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The body cannot detoxify the HO• radical it helps generate","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Free ferrous iron (Fe²⁺) turns hydrogen peroxide into the <b>hydroxyl radical (•OH)</b> (Fenton reaction), and <b>no enzyme can detoxify •OH</b>.<br>B. Water production: iron toxicity is about radicals, not water.<br>C. It creates reactive oxygen species; it does not prevent them.<br>D. Fe³⁺ is made, but •OH is released in the same step.<br>E. It consumes hydrogen peroxide only to make the worse •OH.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 5 (Clinical consequences:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":67,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":67,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0144-35-1","s":"A 45-year-old woman presents with bronze skin, fatigue, and right upper quadrant discomfort. Her labs show elevated free Fe2+ in her tissues. Build-up of free Fe2+ in tissues increases the formation of which highly reactive oxygen species?","o":["A. Superoxide (O₂•–)","B. Hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂)","C. Hydroxyl radical (•OH)","D. Nitric oxide (NO)","E. All of the above"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hydroxyl radical (•OH)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Hydroxyl radical (•OH).</b> Free ferrous iron drives the <b>Fenton reaction</b>: ferrous iron plus hydrogen peroxide gives ferric iron, hydroxide and the hydroxyl radical. No enzyme removes •OH, so it damages liver, pancreas and heart.<br>A. Superoxide: leaks from mitochondria; not the Fenton product.<br>B. Hydrogen peroxide: the Fenton reaction uses it up.<br>D. Nitric oxide: made from arginine by nitric oxide synthase.<br>E. All of the above: iron makes only •OH here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 5 (Clinical consequences:)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":35,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":35,"f":"F1048"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron leaves only by sloughing and bleeding","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-6.jpg","cap":"Iron balance: 1–2 mg/day lost by sloughing, desquamation, bleeding; liver ferritin stores","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-73-1","s":"A patient develops a condition that causes increased sloughing of intestinal mucosal cells and skin cells. Based on iron homeostasis, what is the most likely consequence?","o":["A. A significant increase in the 3–4 mg of non-heme iron transported in the blood bound to transferrin","B. A decrease in liver iron storage, where non-heme iron is normally bound to ferritin","C. A large rise in total-body iron because 3.5–4 g are present in the body","D. A reduction in the amount of iron absorbed daily (1–2 mg/day)","E. No change in iron balance because iron is not lost through mucosal or skin cell shedding"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"A decrease in liver iron storage, where non-heme iron is normally bound to ferritin","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The body has <b>no excretion route</b> for iron; it loses about 1–2 mg a day only through <b>sloughed gut cells, shed skin, bleeding and menstruation</b>. More shedding means more loss, so stores (liver ferritin) fall.<br>A. Plasma transferrin iron (3–4 mg) would not rise from losing iron.<br>C. Total body iron (3–4 g) falls, not rises.<br>D. Absorption goes up, not down: lower stores lower hepcidin.<br>E. Shed gut and skin cells are a real iron loss.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 6 (Systemic Iron Balance Depends on Iron Export)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":73,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":73,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Hepcidin's target is ferroportin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"Hepcidin binds ferroportin; ferroportin internalized and degraded in lysosomes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G032-12.jpg","cap":"Liver hepcidin removes ferroportin from macrophages and enterocytes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-39-1","s":"Hepcidin decreases serum iron by acting on which protein?","o":["A. DMT1","B. HCP1","C. Ferroportin (FPN)","D. Transferrin receptor"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferroportin (FPN)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ferroportin (FPN).</b> Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the only iron exporter; ferroportin is <b>internalized and degraded</b>, so iron stays in gut cells and macrophages and <b>serum iron falls</b>.<br>A. DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1): apical Fe²⁺ importer in the duodenum.<br>B. HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1): apical importer of dietary heme.<br>D. Transferrin receptor: brings transferrin-bound iron into cells by endocytosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)","L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":39,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":39,"f":"F1048"}]},{"id":"F0928-29-1","s":"Anemia of chronic disease is the result of increased hepcidin synthesis induced by inflammatory cytokines such as IL-6. Hepcidin inhibits iron absorption by enterocytes as well as iron release from storage in liver and macrophages. Which of the following proteins is the target of hepcidin?","o":["A. Divalent metal ion transporter 1 (DMT1)","B. Ferri reductase (Dcytb)","C. Heme oxygenase (HOX1)","D. Transferrin","E. Ferroportin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferroportin","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Ferroportin.</b> Hepcidin binds ferroportin, the only cellular iron exporter, and causes its <b>internalization and lysosomal degradation</b>; iron stays inside enterocytes, macrophages and liver cells.<br>A. DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1): apical Fe²⁺ importer.<br>B. Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b, a ferrireductase): reduces Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺.<br>C. HOX1 (heme oxygenase 1): frees iron from heme.<br>D. Transferrin: carries Fe³⁺ in plasma.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":29,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":49,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":193,"f":"F3881"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":13,"f":"F1022"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Ferroportin loss traps iron in cells","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Basolateral ferroportin exports Fe²⁺; hephaestin then oxidizes it for transferrin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"Open ferroportin lets iron into plasma; blocked ferroportin keeps it in","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-80-1","s":"A mutation reduces the activity of ferroportin (FPN) in enterocytes. Based on the information shown, what is the most likely consequence?","o":["A. Less Fe²⁺ reaches the bloodstream for oxidation by hephaestin (ferroxidase)","B. Increased transport of Fe²⁺ across the apical membrane by DMT1","C. Increased release of Fe²⁺ from heme by heme oxygenase (HOX1)","D. Greater conversion of Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ for transport by transferrin (TF)","E. Decreased uptake of dietary heme iron by HCP1"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Less Fe²⁺ reaches the bloodstream for oxidation by hephaestin (ferroxidase)","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Ferroportin (FPN) pumps Fe²⁺ out the basolateral side; <b>hephaestin</b> then oxidizes it to Fe³⁺ for transferrin. Weak ferroportin means <b>less Fe²⁺ reaches the blood</b>.<br>B. DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) apical uptake does not rise.<br>C. Heme oxygenase (HOX1) freeing Fe²⁺ from heme is unaffected.<br>D. Less, not more, Fe²⁺ is oxidized for transferrin (TF).<br>E. Apical heme uptake by HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1) is unaffected.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)","L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":80,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":80,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-180-1","s":"If a patient had a rare genetic disorder which resulted in the production of a mutated non-functional ferroportin protein what would the likely result be?","o":["A. Decreased transport of Fe3 in the blood","B. Decreased stored Fe3 in the cell","C. Decreased Fe2 in the blood and accumulation of Fe2 in the cell","D. Decreased Fe3 absorption into the duodenal mucosal cells"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased Fe2 in the blood and accumulation of Fe2 in the cell","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Ferroportin is the only iron exporter: it moves <b>Fe²⁺ out of duodenal cells (and macrophages) into the blood</b>. If it fails, less iron enters the blood and iron <b>builds up inside the cell</b> (held in ferritin).<br>A. Transferrin itself still works; it just receives less iron. A transport defect would be a transferrin problem.<br>B. Stored iron in the cell rises, not falls.<br>D. Apical uptake is Fe²⁺ through DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1); ferroportin is not involved.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)","L36 Iron slide 9 (Iron Export is the Key Regulatory Step)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":180,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Low hepcidin → more absorption and release","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"Low hepcidin: ferroportin open, iron enters blood; high hepcidin: degraded","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G032-13.jpg","cap":"Signals raising or lowering hepcidin and the resulting ferroportin activity","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-41-1","s":"Repression of hepcidin synthesis leads to:","o":["A. Decreased iron absorption in the gut","B. Increased iron release via ferroportin","C. Increased degradation of transferrin","D. Iron sequestration in macrophages"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased iron release via ferroportin","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Less hepcidin leaves more <b>ferroportin</b> on gut cells and macrophages, so <b>more iron is released</b> into blood and more is absorbed.<br>A. Absorption goes up, not down.<br>C. Hepcidin does not act on transferrin.<br>D. Iron sequestration in macrophages happens when hepcidin is high (anemia of chronic disease).<br>Hepcidin is repressed by iron deficiency, hypoxia and increased or ineffective red cell production.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 9 (Iron Export is the Key Regulatory Step)","L36 Iron slide 13 (Signals That Regulate Hepcidin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":41,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":41,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0112-17-1","s":"Which of the following is most likely to occur in a patient with hepcidin deficiency?","o":["A. Decreased iron absorption from the intestine, leading to iron deficiency anemia","B. Increased iron absorption from the intestine, potentially causing iron overload","C. Reduced iron release from macrophages, leading to iron deficiency","D. Decreased iron uptake in enterocytes, resulting in low serum iron levels"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased iron absorption from the intestine, potentially causing iron overload","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Low hepcidin leaves ferroportin active, so <b>gut absorption rises</b> and macrophages release more iron → <b>iron overload</b> (hemochromatosis pattern).<br>A. Less absorption with iron deficiency anemia: that is high hepcidin.<br>C. Reduced macrophage release: also high hepcidin.<br>D. Low enterocyte uptake and low serum iron: the opposite of hepcidin deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 37 (| Hepcidin High | Hepcidin Low |)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":17,"f":"F0112"},{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":153,"f":"F1013"}]},{"id":"F0065-23-1","s":"A mouse model lacks the HAMP gene. Which of the following patterns is most likely?","o":["A. Decreased intestinal iron absorption and low serum iron","B. Increased ferroportin activity and systemic iron overload","C. Normal ferroportin function but low ferritin levels","D. Increased hepcidin levels and iron-restricted erythropoiesis","E. Impaired iron export from macrophages with anemia of chronic disease"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased ferroportin activity and systemic iron overload","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> HAMP (hepcidin antimicrobial peptide gene) encodes <b>hepcidin</b>. With no hepcidin, ferroportin is never degraded, so gut absorption and macrophage release run unchecked → <b>iron overload</b>, like hemochromatosis.<br>A. Absorption rises and serum iron is high.<br>C. Ferroportin is overactive and ferritin is high.<br>D. Hepcidin is absent, not increased.<br>E. Trapped macrophage iron (anemia of chronic disease) comes from too much hepcidin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":23,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F3881-191-1","s":"Which of the following is INCORRECT regarding hepcidin synthesis?","o":["A. Repression of hepcidin synthesis is associated with hypoxia, iron deficiency and Thalassemias","B. Hepcidin synthesis involves the HAMP gene","C. Induction of hepcidin synthesis is associated with anemia of chronic disease","D. Repression of hepcidin synthesis leads to decreased iron absorption and release via FPN"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Repression of hepcidin synthesis leads to decreased iron absorption and release via FPN","a":"D","why":"<b>D is incorrect.</b> <b>Repression</b> of hepcidin leaves ferroportin (FPN) active, so iron absorption and release <b>increase</b>, not decrease.<br>A. True: hypoxia, iron deficiency and thalassemias (ineffective erythropoiesis) repress hepcidin.<br>B. True: hepcidin is made from the HAMP gene.<br>C. True: inflammation induces hepcidin, causing anemia of chronic disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 37 (| Hepcidin High | Hepcidin Low |)","L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":191,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"High hepcidin → less absorption and release","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"High hepcidin degrades ferroportin; iron stays in enterocyte, less plasma iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"},{"src":"sl/G032-12.jpg","cap":"Hepcidin acts on macrophages, enterocytes and hepatocytes to block iron export","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 12"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-154-1","s":"Induction of Hepcidin synthesis leads to what?","o":["A. Increased iron absorption and release via FPN","B. Increase iron absorption and held by FPN","C. Decreased iron absorption and release via FPN","D. Iron deficiency","E. Hypoxia"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased iron absorption and release via FPN","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> More hepcidin → ferroportin (FPN) is internalized and degraded → <b>less iron absorbed</b> from gut cells and <b>less released</b> from macrophages and liver.<br>A. Increased absorption and release: that is low hepcidin.<br>B. Hepcidin never increases absorption; iron is held because ferroportin is destroyed.<br>D. Iron deficiency lowers hepcidin; it is a repressor, not a result.<br>E. Hypoxia also lowers hepcidin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 9 (Iron Export is the Key Regulatory Step)","L36 Iron slide 12 (Hepcidin Controls Systemic Iron Export)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":154,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Lecture 40 (iron) TA review","sl":7,"f":"F2432"}]},{"id":"F0208-35-1","s":"Which statement accurately describes the role of hepcidin in iron homeostasis?","o":["A. Hepcidin promotes the absorption of iron in the small intestine by enhancing the activity of divalent metal ion transporter (DMT1).","B. Hepcidin acts as a positive regulator of iron release from macrophages and the liver by stabilizing ferroportin (FPN).","C. Hepcidin inhibits iron export mediated by ferroportin (FPN) in enterocytes, macrophages, and the liver.","D. Hepcidin has no influence on iron metabolism as it primarily regulates calcium absorption in the intestines."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepcidin inhibits iron export mediated by ferroportin (FPN) in enterocytes, macrophages, and the liver.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Hepcidin <b>blocks iron export</b> by ferroportin (FPN) in <b>enterocytes, macrophages and liver cells</b>; it binds ferroportin and makes the cell degrade it.<br>A. It does not boost DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1); it lowers iron absorption.<br>B. It is a negative regulator; it destroys ferroportin rather than stabilizing it.<br>D. It is the master regulator of iron, not calcium.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":35,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":215,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F0257-83-1","s":"A patient has excess hepcidin. What is the most likely effect on serum iron?","o":["A. Increased iron release from intestinal cells, macrophages, and liver","B. Decreased degradation of ferroportin (FPN) in lysosomes","C. Decreased serum iron by inhibiting its release from intestinal cells, macrophages, and liver","D. Increased recycling of heme iron from RBC","E. Increased iron uptake from intestine due to hepcidin deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased serum iron by inhibiting its release from intestinal cells, macrophages, and liver","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Hepcidin makes ferroportin internalize and degrade, so <b>iron cannot leave</b> gut cells, macrophages or liver cells → <b>serum iron falls</b>.<br>A. Iron release goes down, not up.<br>B. Lysosomal degradation of ferroportin (FPN) increases.<br>D. Macrophage recycling of red-cell iron is blocked, not increased.<br>E. Increased gut uptake happens with hepcidin deficiency, the opposite state.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 8 (The only known iron exporter in humans)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":83,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":83,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"True statement: transferrin on chromosome 3","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-97.jpg","cap":"Transferrin, chromosome 3, binds two Fe³⁺ in serum","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 97"},{"src":"sl/G032-33.jpg","cap":"HFE, HJV and TFR2 mutations all lower hepcidin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 33"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-33-1","s":"You are on your rotations and your attending gives you a couple of statements for you to verify as a means to test your knowledge. Of the following statements, which one would you confirm is true?","o":["A. Excess Hepcidin increases serum iron","B. Mutations in only the HJV gene can decrease hepcidin synthesis","C. The protein transferrin is encoded by chromosome 3","D. Hepcidin is not needed in iron metabolism","E. Both A and C"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"The protein transferrin is encoded by chromosome 3","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>transferrin gene is on chromosome 3</b> (as is the transferrin receptor gene).<br>A. Excess hepcidin lowers serum iron by degrading ferroportin.<br>B. Several genes lower hepcidin: HFE (hemochromatosis gene), HJV (hemojuvelin), TFR2 (transferrin receptor 2) and HAMP (the hepcidin gene).<br>D. Hepcidin is the master regulator of body iron.<br>E. Both A and C: A is false.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":33,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":33,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Obesity inflammation → anemia of chronic disease","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-31.jpg","cap":"Anemia of chronic disease: iron present but trapped in macrophages","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 31"},{"src":"sl/G032-13.jpg","cap":"Inflammation via interleukin-6 raises hepcidin, cutting plasma iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-182-1","s":"A 35 year old patient with a BMI of 42 presents for her annual checkup. As part of your plan, you recommend a strict diet and plenty of exercise. The patient asks you what the exact mechanism of obesity is. You respond that it is an inflammatory condition which has many complex factors including hormonal and immunological aspects. Based on this, what other condition would you expect to find in this patient?","o":["A. Anemia","B. Tay Sachs","C. Hereditary hemochromatosis","D. Von-Hippel Lindau Syndrome"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Anemia","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Anemia.</b> Obesity is a chronic <b>inflammatory</b> state: inflammation → IL-6 (interleukin-6) → <b>more hepcidin</b> → ferroportin blocked → iron trapped in macrophages → less iron for red cells (anemia of chronic disease).<br>B. Tay-Sachs: inherited hexosaminidase A deficiency.<br>C. Hereditary hemochromatosis: low hepcidin from gene mutations; inflammation does the opposite.<br>D. Von Hippel-Lindau: inherited tumor syndrome.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)","L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":182,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Hepcidin repressors: iron need, hypoxia, erythropoiesis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-13.jpg","cap":"Hepcidin falls with iron deficiency, hypoxia, increased erythropoiesis, erythroferrone","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/F0257-88.jpg","cap":"Hepcidin repressors (hypoxia, iron deficiency, ineffective erythropoiesis) vs inducers","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 88"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-31-1","s":"Which of the following is responsible for repressed hepcidin synthesis?","o":["A. Hypoxia","B. Iron deficiency","C. Ineffective erythropoiesis","D. All of the above"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above","a":"D","why":"<b>D. All of the above.</b> Hepcidin falls when the body <b>needs iron</b>: <b>iron deficiency</b>, <b>hypoxia</b>, and <b>increased or ineffective red cell production</b> (signaled by erythropoietin and erythroferrone). Low hepcidin lets ferroportin export more iron.<br>A, B and C are each true repressors.<br>Mix-up: hepcidin rises with high iron stores, inflammation (interleukin-6) and infection.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 12 (Hepcidin Controls Systemic Iron Export)","L36 Iron slide 13 (Signals That Regulate Hepcidin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":31,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":31,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron absorbed as Fe²⁺ through DMT1","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Apical ferrireductase makes Fe²⁺; DMT1 carries Fe²⁺ into the duodenal cell","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-7-1","s":"Which form of iron is primarily used by the body for absorption and incorporation into hemoglobin?","o":["A. Ferric iron (Fe³⁺)","B. Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺)","C. Metallic iron (Fe⁰)","D. Heme iron only","E. Stored iron in ferritin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Ferrous iron (Fe²⁺).</b> Dietary Fe³⁺ must be reduced to <b>Fe²⁺</b> before DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) can take it in, and heme holds iron as Fe²⁺.<br>A. Ferric iron (Fe³⁺): the transport and storage form; not absorbed as is.<br>C. Metallic iron (Fe⁰): not a biological form.<br>D. Heme iron only: non-heme iron is absorbed too, as Fe²⁺.<br>E. Ferritin iron: storage, not the absorbed form.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)","L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":7,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":18,"f":"F0131"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0028-199-1","s":"Which of the following is correct regarding dietary iron absorption at the apical surface of the duodenum:","o":["A. Absorbed as Fe3+ via divalent metal ion transporter (DMT1)","B. Absorbed as Fe2+ via divalent metal ion transporter (DMT1)","C. Absorbed as Fe3+ via ferroportin (FPN)","D. Absorbed as Fe2+ via ferroportin (FPN)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Absorbed as Fe2+ via divalent metal ion transporter (DMT1)","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> At the apical surface iron enters as <b>Fe²⁺ through DMT1</b> (divalent metal transporter 1), after Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b) reduces Fe³⁺.<br>A. DMT1 carries Fe²⁺, not Fe³⁺.<br>C. FPN (ferroportin) is basolateral and exports; it does not absorb.<br>D. Ferroportin carries Fe²⁺, but out of the cell at the basolateral side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":199,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0133-35-1","s":"A patient’s duodenal biopsy demonstrates reduced expression of a transporter responsible for moving Fe²⁺ across the apical membrane of enterocytes. Which of the following proteins is most likely deficient in this patient?","o":["A. DMT1","B. Hephaestin","C. Ferroportin","D. Ceruloplasmin","E. Transferrin receptor (TFR)"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"DMT1","a":"A","why":"<b>A. DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1)</b> carries <b>Fe²⁺ across the apical</b> brush border of duodenal cells, coupled to protons.<br>B. Hephaestin: basolateral ferroxidase, Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺.<br>C. Ferroportin: basolateral exporter, the only iron exporter.<br>D. Ceruloplasmin: plasma ferroxidase, Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺.<br>E. Transferrin receptor (TFR): brings transferrin-bound Fe³⁺ into cells by endocytosis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":35,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":35,"f":"F0890"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"HCP1 imports dietary heme","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"HCP1 imports dietary heme; heme oxygenase frees Fe²⁺ inside the cell","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-13-1","s":"A researcher blocks the apical membrane protein HCP1 in duodenal enterocytes of a lab animal. Which dietary component will be least efficiently absorbed?","o":["A. Non-heme iron from spinach","B. Heme iron from meat","C. Vitamin C","D. Folate","E. Copper"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Heme iron from meat","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Heme iron from meat</b> enters duodenal cells through <b>HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1)</b>; heme oxygenase then frees the iron.<br>A. Non-heme iron: reduced by Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b), taken up by DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1).<br>C. Vitamin C: sodium-dependent vitamin C transporters.<br>D. Folate: this lecture tests HCP1 as the heme carrier (the same protein also carries folate, as the proton-coupled folate transporter).<br>E. Copper: its own copper transporter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":13,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Dcytb reduces Fe³⁺→Fe²⁺ first","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Step 1: brush-border ferrireductase (Dcytb) reduces Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺ before DMT1","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-14-1","s":"Non-heme dietary iron is absorbed after an initial step catalyzed by a brush-border enzyme. Which reaction does this enzyme perform?","o":["A. Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺","B. Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺","C. Heme → biliverdin","D. Biliverdin → bilirubin","E. Fe²⁺ → heme"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺.</b> The brush-border <b>ferrireductase Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b)</b> reduces dietary ferric iron to ferrous iron so <b>DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1)</b> can carry it in.<br>A. Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺: the opposite, done by hephaestin and ceruloplasmin on the blood side.<br>C. Heme → biliverdin: heme oxygenase.<br>D. Biliverdin → bilirubin: biliverdin reductase.<br>E. Fe²⁺ → heme: ferrochelatase, in heme synthesis.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":14,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1},{"id":"F1201-164-1","s":"What is the first step of iron absorption in the duodenum?","o":["A. Hephaestin (Ferrooxidase) converts Fe2+ to Fe3+ for transferrin transporting","B. Feri-reductase on the brush border reduces iron for transport into mucosal cells by divalent metal ion transporter","C. Fe2+ is just absorbed freely coupled to a sodium co-transport system"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Feri-reductase on the brush border reduces iron for transport into mucosal cells by divalent metal ion transporter","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Step one is <b>reduction</b>: the brush-border <b>ferrireductase Dcytb</b> (duodenal cytochrome b) turns Fe³⁺ into Fe²⁺ so DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) can take it in.<br>A. Hephaestin oxidizing Fe²⁺ → Fe³⁺ for transferrin is the <b>last</b> step, on the blood side.<br>C. Iron is not absorbed freely with sodium; DMT1 is proton-coupled.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":164,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":232,"f":"F0035"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":189,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":189,"f":"F1218"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Enterocyte iron: ferritin stores, ferroportin exports","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Enterocyte iron stored in ferritin or exported by basolateral ferroportin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-15-1","s":"Inside duodenal enterocytes, iron released from dietary heme can either be stored or exported. Which combination correctly pairs the storage form and the exporter protein?","o":["A. Storage in hemosiderin; export via DMT1","B. Storage in ferritin; export via ferroportin","C. Storage in transferrin; export via hephaestin","D. Storage in albumin; export via HCP1","E. Storage in ferritin; export via transferrin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Storage in ferritin; export via ferroportin","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Inside the enterocyte, iron is either <b>stored in ferritin</b> (lost when the cell sloughs) or <b>exported by ferroportin</b> across the basolateral membrane.<br>A. Hemosiderin is an overload store; DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) imports.<br>C. Transferrin carries iron in plasma; hephaestin oxidizes iron, it does not pump it.<br>D. Albumin does not store iron; HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1) imports heme.<br>E. Transferrin is a plasma carrier, not an exporter.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)","L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":15,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Hephaestin oxidizes Fe²⁺ for transferrin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Basolateral hephaestin (ferroxidase) oxidizes exported Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ for transferrin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0359-14-1","s":"What role does ferroxidase play ?","o":["A. absorb Fe2+ via the apical surface of the duodenum","B. Fe2+ is just absorbed freely coupled to a sodium co-transport system","C. Converts Fe2+ to Fe3+ for transferrin transporting","D. Converts Fe3+ to Fe2+ for transferrin transporting"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Converts Fe2+ to Fe3+ for transferrin transporting","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> A <b>ferroxidase</b> (hephaestin on gut cells, ceruloplasmin in plasma) <b>oxidizes Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺</b> so transferrin can carry it.<br>A. Apical Fe²⁺ uptake: DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1).<br>B. Iron is not absorbed freely with sodium; DMT1 is proton-coupled.<br>D. Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺ is reduction, done by Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b); transferrin binds Fe³⁺, not Fe²⁺.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":14,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":126,"f":"F2303"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 40 iron TA review","sl":4,"f":"F2436"}]},{"id":"F0065-16-1","s":"A mutation in hephaestin that abolishes its ferroxidase activity would most directly impair which process?","o":["A. Reduction of Fe³⁺ to Fe²⁺ in the intestinal lumen","B. Release of Fe²⁺ from heme inside enterocytes","C. Loading of Fe³⁺ onto transferrin in the plasma","D. Uptake of iron into enterocytes via DMT1","E. Binding of iron to ferritin in hepatocytes"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Loading of Fe³⁺ onto transferrin in the plasma","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Hephaestin</b> is the enterocyte <b>ferroxidase</b>: it oxidizes Fe²⁺ leaving ferroportin to <b>Fe³⁺</b>, the only form <b>transferrin</b> can bind.<br>A. Lumen Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺: Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b).<br>B. Releasing Fe²⁺ from heme: heme oxygenase.<br>D. Apical uptake: DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1).<br>E. Loading ferritin: ferritin's own ferroxidase (H subunit).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":16,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Enterocyte iron pathway, step by step","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Numbered enterocyte steps: reductase, DMT1, HCP1, heme oxygenase, ferritin, ferroportin, hephaestin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G036-9.jpg","cap":"Whole-body iron map: absorption, hepcidin, transferrin, macrophage recycling","where":"2026 iron-deficiency case, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-37-1","s":"Match the protein to its function. Proteins: Hephaestin (ferroxidase); Ferroportin; Heme oxygenase (HOX1); HCP1; Feri-reductase (Dcytb); DMT1; Transferrin","o":["A. Converts Fe2+ to Fe3+ for transport by transferrin in the blood","B. Reduces Fe3+ to Fe2+ at the apical surface of duodenal mucosal cells","C. Uptakes Fe2+ at the apical surface of the duodenum","D. Transports dietary heme iron into enterocytes","E. Releases Fe2+ from protoporphyrin inside enterocytes","F. Transports Fe2+ out of cells into the bloodstream","G. Binds Fe3+ for circulation in the bloodstream"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Hephaestin → converts Fe2+ to Fe3+ for transferrin; Ferroportin → transports Fe2+ out of cells into the bloodstream; Heme oxygenase (HOX1) → releases Fe2+ from protoporphyrin inside enterocytes; HCP1 → transports dietary heme iron into enterocytes; Dcytb → reduces Fe3+ to Fe2+ at the apical surface; DMT1 → uptakes Fe2+ at the apical surface; Transferrin → binds Fe3+ for circulation","a":null,"why":"Follow the duodenal cell from lumen to blood:<br><b>Dcytb</b> (duodenal cytochrome b, ferrireductase) reduces Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺ → <b>DMT1</b> (divalent metal transporter 1) takes Fe²⁺ in. <b>HCP1</b> (heme carrier protein 1) takes heme in → <b>heme oxygenase (HOX1)</b> frees Fe²⁺. <b>Ferroportin</b> exports Fe²⁺ → <b>hephaestin</b> oxidizes it to Fe³⁺ → <b>transferrin</b> carries Fe³⁺ in blood.<br>Mix-up: reduce at the apical side, oxidize at the basolateral side.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":37,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":37,"f":"F1048"}]},{"id":"F0208-33-1","s":"Arrange the steps in the absorption and transport of iron in the gastrointestinal system in the correct order: 1. Iron is absorbed as Fe2+ via the apical surface of mucosal cells in the duodenum by divalent metal ion transporter (DMT1) after reduction by ferrireductase (Dcytb). 2. Some of the absorbed iron is stored as ferritin (Fe3+). 3. Heme iron is absorbed via a specific transporter (HCP1), and Fe2+ is released by heme oxygenase (HOX1). 4. Hephaestin (ferroxidase) converts Fe2+ to Fe3+ for transport by transferrin (TF) in the blood. 5. The rest of the iron (Fe2+) is transported to the bloodstream via ferroportin (FPN).","o":["A. 1, 2, 3, 4, 5","B. 1, 3, 4, 5, 2","C. 2, 1, 3, 5, 4","D. 1, 3, 2, 5, 4"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"1, 3, 2, 5, 4","a":"D","why":"<b>D. 1, 3, 2, 5, 4.</b> Uptake first: <b>1</b> non-heme iron via Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b) and DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1), <b>3</b> heme via HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1) and heme oxygenase. Then <b>2</b> some is stored in ferritin, <b>5</b> the rest leaves by ferroportin, and last <b>4</b> hephaestin oxidizes it for transferrin.<br>A. Stores before heme uptake; oxidizes before export.<br>B. Oxidation must follow export.<br>C. Iron can't be stored before it is absorbed.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":33,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":213,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Phytate, polyphenols, calcium block non-heme iron","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-79.jpg","cap":"Non-heme iron: 85%, Fe³⁺, inhibited by phytate, polyphenols, calcium","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 79"},{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"Separate apical routes: non-heme iron via reductase and DMT1, heme via HCP1","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-77-1","s":"A patient increases their intake of phytate, polyphenols, and calcium. Based on the information shown, absorption of which form of dietary iron will be most inhibited?","o":["A. Heme iron (Fe²⁺)","B. Heme iron (absorbed without major dietary inhibitors)","C. Non-heme iron (Fe³⁺)","D. Heme iron (stimulated by ascorbate)","E. Neither form"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Non-heme iron (Fe³⁺)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Non-heme iron (Fe³⁺).</b> <b>Phytate, polyphenols (tea, coffee) and calcium</b> bind or block non-heme iron; ascorbate (vitamin C) enhances it.<br>A. Heme iron (Fe²⁺) is taken up intact by HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1) and is little affected.<br>B. Heme iron escapes these inhibitors, so it is not the one blocked.<br>D. Ascorbate mainly helps non-heme, not heme, iron.<br>E. Neither: non-heme absorption clearly falls.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":77,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":77,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"DMT1 imports Fe²⁺; hephaestin oxidizes it","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-15.jpg","cap":"DMT1 imports Fe²⁺ apically; hephaestin oxidizes it basolaterally","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 15"},{"src":"sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"High hepcidin degrades ferroportin, so absorbed iron never reaches plasma","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-152-1","s":"Iron is absorbed in the duodenum as Fe2+ by _____ and Fe2+ is converted to Fe3+ by _____","o":["A. DMT1 and Dcytb","B. DMT1 and ferroxidase","C. FPN and TF","D. HCP1 and ferroportin","E. Hephaestin and HOX1"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"DMT1 and ferroxidase (hephaestin)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. DMT1 and ferroxidase.</b> <b>DMT1</b> (divalent metal transporter 1) brings Fe²⁺ into duodenal cells; the ferroxidase <b>hephaestin</b> turns exported Fe²⁺ into Fe³⁺.<br>A. Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b) reduces Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺, the opposite.<br>C. FPN (ferroportin) exports and TF (transferrin) carries; neither oxidizes.<br>D. HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1) imports heme; ferroportin exports.<br>E. Hephaestin doesn't import; HOX1 (heme oxygenase 1) frees iron from heme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":152,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Lecture 40 (iron) TA review","sl":5,"f":"F2432"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3881-189-1","s":"Knowledge Check: Iron is absorbed in the duodenum as Fe2+ by _____________. Fe2+ is converted to Fe3+ by _____________. Induction of hepcidin synthesis leads to _____________ iron absorption and release via _____________.","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"DMT1; ferroxidase (hephaestin); decreased; FPN (ferroportin)","a":null,"why":"Iron enters duodenal cells as <b>Fe²⁺ through DMT1</b> (divalent metal transporter 1). On the blood side, <b>hephaestin</b> (a ferroxidase) turns Fe²⁺ into Fe³⁺ for transferrin. Hepcidin induction <b>decreases</b> absorption and release because it degrades <b>ferroportin (FPN)</b>, the exporter.<br>Mix-up: Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b) does the opposite reaction, Fe³⁺ → Fe²⁺, before uptake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)","L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":189,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Dietary heme iron: 15%, Fe²⁺","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-79.jpg","cap":"Heme iron: 15% of diet, Fe²⁺; non-heme: 85%, Fe³⁺","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 79"}],"q":[{"id":"F0998-150-1","s":"Heme Iron constitutes what % in a mixed diet and presents in which form?","o":["A. 15% and Fe2+","B. 25% and Fe2+","C. 5% and Fe3+","D. 15% and Fe2+","E. 85% and Fe3+"],"g":["A","D"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"15% and Fe2+","a":"A","why":"<b>A / D (identical): 15%, as Fe²⁺.</b> <b>Heme iron</b> (from meat) is about <b>15%</b> of dietary iron and is <b>ferrous (Fe²⁺)</b>; it is absorbed better than non-heme iron (about 5–25%).<br>B. 25%: too high.<br>C. 5% and Fe³⁺: wrong share and wrong charge.<br>E. 85% and Fe³⁺: that is <b>non-heme</b> iron, which must be reduced before uptake.","ex":"","fl":["Stem mismatch: the question slide asks about NON-heme iron, which is ~85% of dietary iron and mostly ferric (Fe3+) = E. The key A (15% and Fe2+) is the answer for HEME iron, which is how the answer slide re-worded the stem. Options A and D are identical."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":150,"f":"F0998"},{"t":"2023 Harlem TA review","n":"Lecture 40 (iron) TA review","sl":3,"f":"F2432"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Ceruloplasmin oxidizes Fe²⁺→Fe³⁺ in plasma","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-16.jpg","cap":"Macrophage ferroportin exports Fe²⁺; ceruloplasmin oxidizes it to Fe³⁺","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 16"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-72-1","s":"Of all the many many enzymes and proteins involved with iron, what does ceruloplasmin do?","o":["A. Binds  iron in the serum for transport","B. Regulates ferroportin and iron absorption","C. Converts Fe2+ to Fe3+","D. Absorbs Heme","E. Absorbs Fe2+ at the intestinal cell apex"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Converts Fe2+ to Fe3+","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Ceruloplasmin</b> is a plasma <b>ferroxidase</b>: it converts Fe²⁺ (leaving cells through ferroportin) to Fe³⁺ for transferrin. Hephaestin does the same job on gut cells.<br>A. Binding serum iron for transport: transferrin.<br>B. Regulating ferroportin: hepcidin.<br>D. Absorbing heme: HCP1 (heme carrier protein 1).<br>E. Apical Fe²⁺ uptake: DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 16 (90% of iron used for erythropoiesis comes from macrophage recycling)","L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":72,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":171,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":151,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F0133-29-1","s":"A 85 year old man with a past medical history of seizures undergoes genetic analysis. It’s ascertained that he has a deficiency in a enzyme that oxidizes Fe2+ to Fe3+. Which enzyme deficiency does he have?","o":["A. Ceruloplasmin","B. Albumin","C. Glucokinase","D. Transferrin","E. Alpha glucosidase"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Ceruloplasmin","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Ceruloplasmin</b> is the copper-containing plasma <b>ferroxidase</b> that oxidizes Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺. Its absence (aceruloplasminemia) lets iron build up in brain and liver, causing neurologic disease.<br>B. Albumin: plasma carrier protein, not an enzyme.<br>C. Glucokinase: phosphorylates glucose in liver and pancreas.<br>D. Transferrin: carries Fe³⁺; does not oxidize it.<br>E. Alpha-glucosidase: digests starch and glycogen.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 16 (90% of iron used for erythropoiesis comes from macrophage recycling)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":29,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":29,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0065-17-1","s":"Which pair correctly matches an iron-binding protein with its main site of action?","o":["A. Transferrin – intracellular storage in hepatocytes","B. Ferritin – plasma transport of Fe³⁺","C. Ceruloplasmin – oxidation of Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ in plasma","D. Heme oxygenase – apical brush-border transport of Fe²⁺","E. Hemosiderin – active, readily mobilizable iron storage"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ceruloplasmin – oxidation of Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺ in plasma","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Ceruloplasmin</b> is a copper-containing <b>ferroxidase</b>: it oxidizes Fe²⁺ leaving macrophages and liver into <b>Fe³⁺ in plasma</b> so transferrin can bind it.<br>A. Transferrin carries iron in plasma; it doesn't store it.<br>B. Ferritin stores iron inside cells; it doesn't carry it in plasma.<br>D. Heme oxygenase frees iron from heme inside cells.<br>E. Hemosiderin is the slow, poorly mobilized store.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 16 (90% of iron used for erythropoiesis comes from macrophage recycling)","L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":17,"f":"F0065"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Macrophage recycling supplies most iron","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-16.jpg","cap":"Macrophages recycle old red cells: 90% of iron for erythropoiesis","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 16"},{"src":"sl/G032-6.jpg","cap":"Spleen salvage 30 mg/day vs absorption 1–2 mg/day; liver stores 1 g","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 6"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-18-1","s":"Most of the body’s daily iron requirement is supplied by which process?","o":["A. Absorption of 10–20 mg/day of dietary iron","B. Hepatic synthesis of iron from amino acids","C. Recycling of iron from senescent RBCs by splenic macrophages","D. Renal reabsorption of iron from primary urine","E. Release of iron from muscle myoglobin breakdown"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Recycling of iron from senescent RBCs by splenic macrophages","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> About <b>90%</b> of the iron for new red cells (20–30 mg/day) comes from <b>macrophages</b> recycling old red cells: heme oxygenase frees the iron and ferroportin exports it.<br>A. Absorption is only 1–2 mg/day.<br>B. Iron is an element; it can't be synthesized.<br>D. Little iron is filtered; the kidney is not a supply route.<br>E. Myoglobin breakdown is a minor source.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 16 (90% of iron used for erythropoiesis comes from macrophage recycling)","L36 Iron slide 6 (Systemic Iron Balance Depends on Iron Export)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":18,"f":"F0065"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0065-24-1","s":"Which tissue combination is the major site of iron recycling and the major iron storage organ, respectively?","o":["A. Bone marrow; kidney","B. Liver; skeletal muscle","C. Spleen macrophages; liver","D. Intestinal mucosa; spleen","E. Pancreas; bone marrow"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Spleen macrophages; liver","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Spleen macrophages; liver.</b> <b>Splenic macrophages</b> recycle iron from old red cells, and the <b>liver</b> is the main storage organ (ferritin, hemosiderin).<br>A. Bone marrow uses iron; the kidney does not store it.<br>B. Skeletal muscle holds myoglobin, not the main store.<br>D. Intestinal mucosa absorbs; the spleen recycles more than it stores.<br>E. The pancreas is a site of overload damage, not storage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 16 (90% of iron used for erythropoiesis comes from macrophage recycling)","L36 Iron slide 6 (Systemic Iron Balance Depends on Iron Export)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":24,"f":"F0065"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Low endosomal pH frees iron from transferrin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-17.jpg","cap":"Transferrin cycle: endosomal H⁺ frees iron; apotransferrin recycles on receptor","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/F0257-100.jpg","cap":"Transferrin cycle: clathrin pit, pH 5 endosome releases Fe³⁺, receptor recycles","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 100"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-203-1","s":"What causes the release of iron from transferrin during the transferrin cycle?","o":["A. The Fe3+-TF complex is endocytosed and the receptor is cleaved by clathrin","B. The low pH in the late endosome causes iron to release while TF remains bound to the TFR for recycling","C. The TFR is broken down by the low pH to release the iron","D. The high pH in the late endosome causes iron to release while TF remains bound to the TFR for recycling"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The low pH in the late endosome causes iron to release while TF remains bound to the TFR for recycling","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Acid releases iron: the late endosome's <b>low pH</b> (about 5) frees <b>Fe³⁺ from transferrin</b>, while transferrin stays on its receptor (TFR) and both return to the surface.<br>A. Clathrin coats the pit; it does not cleave the receptor.<br>C. The receptor is recycled, not broken down.<br>D. The endosome is acidic; high pH would keep iron bound.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":203,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":19,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":131,"f":"F2303"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 40 iron TA review","sl":9,"f":"F2436"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":238,"f":"F0035"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":168,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":195,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":195,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0133-41-1","s":"A researcher studies the transferrin cycle. After endocytosis and acidification of the endosome, which step occurs next?","o":["A. Transferrin degraded in lysosomes","B. Iron dissociates; transferrin stays bound to TFR","C. Iron + transferrin released together into cytosol","D. Iron remains bound and TF recycles to membrane","E. Apotransferrin remains bound to iron while exocytosed"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron dissociates; transferrin stays bound to TFR","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> The endosome's <b>low pH</b> makes <b>Fe³⁺ fall off transferrin</b>; apotransferrin stays on its receptor (TFR) and both recycle to the surface.<br>A. Transferrin is recycled, not degraded.<br>C. Iron leaves the endosome alone (as Fe²⁺ through DMT1, divalent metal transporter 1); transferrin stays put.<br>D. Iron does not stay bound; that is why acid is needed.<br>E. Apotransferrin means iron-free transferrin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":41,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":41,"f":"F0890"}]},{"id":"F0257-98-1","s":"A researcher blocks the pH drop inside late endosomes. Which step in the transferrin cycle will be directly impaired?","o":["A. Binding of Fe³⁺-transferrin to the transferrin receptor on the plasma membrane","B. Endocytosis of the (Fe³⁺-TF)–TFR complex from clathrin-coated pits","C. Release of Fe³⁺ from transferrin while TF remains bound to the receptor","D. Recycling of the TF-TFR complex back to the plasma membrane","E. Dissociation of apotransferrin from the transferrin receptor at neutral pH"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Release of Fe³⁺ from transferrin while TF remains bound to the receptor","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> The <b>pH drop</b> in the late endosome is what <b>releases Fe³⁺ from transferrin</b>; without it, iron stays bound.<br>A. Receptor binding happens at the cell surface at neutral pH.<br>B. Endocytosis from clathrin-coated pits comes before acidification.<br>D. Recycling to the membrane follows iron release; acid doesn't drive it.<br>E. Apotransferrin leaves the receptor at the surface's neutral pH.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":98,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":98,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Transferrin–receptor enters by clathrin endocytosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-100.jpg","cap":"Ferrotransferrin–receptor complex internalized through clathrin-coated pits","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 100"},{"src":"sl/G032-17.jpg","cap":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis of iron-loaded transferrin into endosomes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-43-1","s":"The ferrotransferrin-TFR complex is internalized into the cell through:","o":["A. Phagocytosis","B. Pinocytosis","C. Clathrin-mediated endocytosis","D. Passive diffusion"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Clathrin-mediated endocytosis","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Clathrin-mediated endocytosis.</b> Iron-loaded transferrin binds its receptor (TFR) and the complex gathers in <b>clathrin-coated pits</b> that pinch off as vesicles.<br>A. Phagocytosis: how macrophages eat old red cells.<br>B. Pinocytosis: non-specific fluid uptake, not receptor-driven.<br>D. Passive diffusion: proteins like transferrin can't diffuse through membranes.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":43,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":43,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron-binding proteins and their roles","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-72.jpg","cap":"Roles of transferrin, lactoferrin, receptor, ferritin, hemosiderin, haptoglobin, hemopexin","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 72"},{"src":"sl/G032-19.jpg","cap":"Ferritin: main soluble store; hemosiderin: insoluble overload store","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 19"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-31-1","s":"Which protein is used for active storage and for transport in blood respectively","o":["A. Transferrin; haptoglobin","B. Hemopexin; lactoferrin","C. Ferritin; transferrin","D. Haptoglobin; ferritin","E. Lactoferrin; hemosiderin"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferritin; transferrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Ferritin; transferrin.</b> <b>Ferritin</b> is the active, quick-release iron store; <b>transferrin</b> carries Fe³⁺ in blood.<br>A. Transferrin transports, it does not store; haptoglobin binds free hemoglobin.<br>B. Hemopexin binds free heme; lactoferrin carries iron in milk and tears.<br>D. Haptoglobin binds hemoglobin; ferritin stores, it does not transport.<br>E. Lactoferrin is in milk and tears; hemosiderin is inactive storage.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)","L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":31,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":211,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1},{"id":"F2303-124-1","s":"Match the correct statements: 1. Ferritin; 2. Transferrin; 3. Hemosiderin; 4. Hemopexin","o":["A. A plasma protein with high affinity for Heme","B. Active storage","C. Transport in blood","D. Inactive Storage"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Ferritin = active storage; Transferrin = transport in blood; Hemosiderin = inactive storage; Hemopexin = plasma protein that binds heme","a":"1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A","why":"<b>Ferritin → B, active storage</b> (soluble, quickly released). <b>Transferrin → C, transport in blood</b> (carries Fe³⁺). <b>Hemosiderin → D, inactive storage</b> (insoluble, slow release, builds up in overload). <b>Hemopexin → A</b>, plasma protein that binds <b>free heme</b>.<br>Mix-up: hemopexin binds heme and haptoglobin binds hemoglobin; neither is a storage protein.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)","L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":124,"f":"F2303"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":12,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 40 iron TA review","sl":2,"f":"F2436"}]},{"id":"F0257-70-1","s":"Match each protein with its correct role: 1. Transferrin 2. Lactoferrin 3. Transferrin receptor 4. Ferritin 5. Hemosiderin 6. Haptoglobin 7. Hemopexin","o":["A. Active storage","B. Transport in milk, tears","C. Cellular uptake","D. Transport in blood","E. Inactive storage","F. An immunoglobulin-like plasma protein that binds hemoglobin","G. A plasma protein with high affinity for heme"],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"1–D 2–B 3–C 4–A 5–E 6–F 7–G","a":null,"why":"<b>Transferrin</b> = transport in blood; <b>lactoferrin</b> = iron in milk and tears; <b>transferrin receptor</b> = cellular uptake; <b>ferritin</b> = active storage; <b>hemosiderin</b> = inactive storage; <b>haptoglobin</b> = binds free hemoglobin; <b>hemopexin</b> = binds free heme.<br>Mix-up: ferritin is soluble and quick to release; hemosiderin is insoluble, slow, and builds up in overload. Haptoglobin grabs hemoglobin; hemopexin grabs heme.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)","L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":70,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":70,"f":"F0938"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":187,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Transferrin carries Fe³⁺ in blood","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-17.jpg","cap":"Circulating iron is bound to transferrin as Fe³⁺","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 17"},{"src":"sl/F0257-97.jpg","cap":"Transferrin: chromosome 3, carbohydrate-rich, binds two Fe³⁺ in serum","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 97"}],"q":[{"id":"F0035-229-1","s":"Which Iron binding protein is responsible for carrying Iron in the blood?","o":["A. Hemosiderin","B. Ferritin","C. Transferrin","D. Transferrin Receptor"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Transferrin","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Transferrin</b> carries iron in the blood as <b>Fe³⁺</b> (two per molecule).<br>A. Hemosiderin: inactive, insoluble storage inside cells.<br>B. Ferritin: active storage inside cells (serum traces are used only as a marker of stores).<br>D. Transferrin receptor: on cell surfaces; brings transferrin-iron into cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)","L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":229,"f":"F0035"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":162,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":186,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":186,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0257-95-1","s":"A serum protein located on chromosome 3 binds two Fe³⁺ ions and helps maintain iron in a transportable form. Which protein is being described?","o":["A. Ceruloplasmin, a ferroxidase that converts Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺","B. Ferritin, a cytosolic protein that stores excess Fe³⁺","C. Transferrin, a carbohydrate-rich globular protein that binds circulating Fe³⁺","D. Haptoglobin, an immunoglobulin-like protein that binds hemoglobin","E. Lactoferrin, a protein involved in iron binding in milk and tears"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Transferrin, a carbohydrate-rich globular protein that binds circulating Fe³⁺","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Transferrin</b> is the serum iron carrier: gene on <b>chromosome 3</b>, a carbohydrate-rich globular protein that binds <b>two Fe³⁺</b> ions.<br>A. Ceruloplasmin: oxidizes Fe²⁺ to Fe³⁺; does not carry iron.<br>B. Ferritin: stores iron inside cells.<br>D. Haptoglobin: binds free hemoglobin.<br>E. Lactoferrin: binds iron in milk and tears.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":95,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":95,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Transferrin receptor = cellular iron uptake","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-17.jpg","cap":"Surface transferrin receptor binds Fe³⁺-transferrin and is endocytosed","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 17"}],"q":[{"id":"F0112-15-1","s":"Which of the following proteins is primarily responsible for the cellular uptake of iron and is located on the surface of cells?","o":["A. Ferritin","B. Transferrin","C. Lactoferrin","D. Transferrin Receptor"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Transferrin Receptor","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Transferrin receptor</b> sits on the <b>cell surface</b>, binds iron-loaded transferrin and brings it in by endocytosis.<br>A. Ferritin: storage protein inside cells.<br>B. Transferrin: carries iron in blood; it is not anchored on the cell surface.<br>C. Lactoferrin: binds iron in milk and tears.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 17 (Cell Biochemistry and Biophysics (2018) 76:329–337)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":15,"f":"F0112"},{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":151,"f":"F1013"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron stored as Fe³⁺ in ferritin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-18.jpg","cap":"Fe²⁺ enters ferritin, is oxidized, and stored as an Fe³⁺ mineral core","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-45-1","s":"After being released from transferrin, iron is primarily stored as:","o":["A. Fe²⁺ in hemoglobin","B. Fe³⁺ bound to ferritin","C. Free Fe²⁺ in cytoplasm","D. Fe³⁺ bound to transferrin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Fe³⁺ bound to ferritin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Fe³⁺ bound to ferritin.</b> Iron leaves the endosome as Fe²⁺; ferritin's ferroxidase oxidizes it and stores it as an <b>Fe³⁺ mineral core</b>, safe from radical reactions.<br>A. Hemoglobin uses iron; it is not a store.<br>C. Free Fe²⁺ is toxic (Fenton reaction); cells keep very little.<br>D. Transferrin carries Fe³⁺ in plasma, not inside cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)","L36 Iron slide 18 (Ferritin safely stores Iron inside cells and releases it in a controlled manner )"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":45,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":45,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Hemosiderin: insoluble, inactive overload store","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-19.jpg","cap":"Hemosiderin: insoluble, slowly mobilized, accumulates in overload; ferritin: main store","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 19"},{"src":"sl/F0257-107.jpg","cap":"Liver hemosiderin: golden-brown on H&E, blue with Prussian blue","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 107"}],"q":[{"id":"F0028-197-1","s":"Which iron binding protein is responsible for inactive iron storage?","o":["A. Transferrin","B. Ferritin","C. Hemoglobin","D. Hemosiderin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemosiderin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hemosiderin</b> is the <b>inactive</b> store: insoluble, partly degraded ferritin that releases iron slowly and builds up in iron overload.<br>A. Transferrin: transport in blood.<br>B. Ferritin: active, quickly mobilized storage.<br>C. Hemoglobin: uses iron to carry oxygen; not a store.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":197,"f":"F0028"},{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":68,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":167,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":147,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F0112-19-1","s":"Which of the following accurately describes the role of hemosiderin in the body?","o":["A. Hemosiderin stores iron in a soluble, readily available form for quick release when needed.","B. Hemosiderin is formed when there is excess iron in the body and stores it in an insoluble form, releasing iron slowly.","C. Hemosiderin is the primary protein involved in iron transport in the blood.","D. Hemosiderin is the main form of iron storage in tissues when iron levels are low or normal."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemosiderin is formed when there is excess iron in the body and stores it in an insoluble form, releasing iron slowly.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Hemosiderin</b> forms when iron is in <b>excess</b>: aggregated, partly degraded ferritin that is <b>insoluble</b> and releases iron slowly.<br>A. Soluble, quick-release storage is ferritin.<br>C. Transport in blood is transferrin.<br>D. With low or normal iron, the main store is ferritin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":19,"f":"F0112"},{"t":"2024 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":155,"f":"F1013"}]},{"id":"F0257-104-1","s":"A biopsy from a patient with iron overload shows golden-brown granules on H&E and blue staining on Prussian blue, indicating an insoluble iron storage complex that releases iron very slowly. Which iron-storage form is this, and how does it differ from ferritin?","o":["A. Hemosiderin — it is an inactive, insoluble iron store that releases iron more slowly than ferritin","B. Hemosiderin — it is a rapidly mobilized iron store found mainly in the heart","C. Ferritin — it is insoluble and contains ~30% iron by weight","D. Ferritin — it only forms as a degradation product of transferrin","E. Ferritin — it stains blue on Prussian blue and brown on H&E"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemosiderin — it is an inactive, insoluble iron store that releases iron more slowly than ferritin","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> Golden-brown granules on H&amp;amp;E (hematoxylin and eosin) that stain <b>blue with Prussian blue</b> are <b>hemosiderin</b>: insoluble, partly degraded ferritin that releases iron slowly.<br>B. Hemosiderin is mobilized slowly, not rapidly, and sits mostly in liver and macrophages.<br>C. Ferritin is soluble; the insoluble store (about 30% iron) is hemosiderin.<br>D. Ferritin is not made from transferrin.<br>E. That staining pattern is hemosiderin's.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":104,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":104,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0257-101-1","s":"A patient with long-standing transfusion-dependent anemia begins developing signs of iron overload. Which change in iron-storage pattern would most likely be observed first?","o":["A. Decrease in ferritin levels due to rapid iron mobilization","B. Decreased Fe³⁺ storage in liver and spleen","C. Loss of iron from ferritin during mucosal sloughing","D. Increase in hemosiderin relative to ferritin as iron accumulates"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Increase in hemosiderin relative to ferritin as iron accumulates","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> As transfused iron builds up, ferritin aggregates and partly breaks down into <b>hemosiderin</b>, so hemosiderin <b>rises relative to ferritin</b>; hemosiderin is the overload marker.<br>A. Ferritin rises with overload; it does not fall.<br>B. Liver and spleen Fe³⁺ stores increase.<br>C. Mucosal sloughing loss is tiny and isn't changed by transfusions.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":101,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":101,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Lung hemosiderin macrophages → chronic bleeding","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-107.jpg","cap":"Hemosiderin in lung interstitial macrophages indicates chronic bleeding","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 107"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-39-1","s":"Hemosiderin deposits in interstitial macrophages indicate what?","o":["A. Acute inflammation in the lung tissue.","B. Normal iron metabolism in the respiratory system.","C. Chronic bleeding or hemorrhage in the lung.","D. Increased oxygen saturation in the blood."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Chronic bleeding or hemorrhage in the lung.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Lung <b>macrophages full of hemosiderin</b> mean red cells leaked into the alveoli and were eaten: <b>chronic bleeding</b> (or congestion, as in heart failure) in the lung.<br>A. Acute inflammation shows neutrophils, not hemosiderin.<br>B. Normal lungs hold very little hemosiderin.<br>D. Blood oxygen saturation has nothing to do with iron storage in macrophages.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":39,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":219,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Transferrin saturation = serum iron ÷ TIBC","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-21.jpg","cap":"Transferrin saturation equals serum iron divided by TIBC, times 100","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/F0257-114.jpg","cap":"TIBC equals serum iron plus UIBC; saturation formula and normal values","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 114"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-88-1","s":"Looping back to the 25 year old patient in the previous question, it has now been three months since she presented to the Emergency Department. Since then she has been consuming iron pills (ferrous sulfate) and including meat and vitamin C into her diet. She reports feeling much better. Her labs today show the following: serum iron = 100 ug/dL, UIBC = 200. With this information, what is her % transferrin saturation?","o":["A. 36.6%","B. 29.9%","C. 45%","D. 26.6%","E. 33%"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"33%","a":"E","why":"<b>E. 33%.</b> Step 1: TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) equals serum iron plus UIBC (unsaturated iron-binding capacity): 100 + 200 = 300. Step 2: transferrin saturation equals serum iron divided by TIBC, times 100: 100 ÷ 300 = <b>one third, about 33%</b> (normal 20–50%).<br>Mix-up: dividing serum iron by UIBC (100 ÷ 200 = 50%) instead of by TIBC. Options A, B, C and D match no correct setup.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 21 (Iron Panel Markers)","L36 Iron slide 22 (Iron Panel Markers: What’s Normal?)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":88,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":187,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":167,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Low TIBC: iron overload or chronic disease","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-24.jpg","cap":"Low or normal TIBC: no iron hunger, seen in chronic disease and overload","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 24"},{"src":"sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Panel table: TIBC low in chronic disease, normal-low in overload, high in deficiency","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-185-1","s":"A patient comes into your practice complaining of fatigue and weakness. You suspect an iron disorder and order a blood panel that includes TIBC levels. The results of the blood test are a TIBC of 215 ug/dL (Normal 240-450 ug/dL). What is the diagnosis?","o":["A. Iron overload","B. Iron deficiency","C. Ferroportin deficiency","D. Overexpression of hepcidin"],"g":["A","D"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron overload","a":"A","why":"<b>A or D.</b> A low TIBC (total iron-binding capacity, i.e. little transferrin) means the body is <b>not trying to capture iron</b>. The lecture names two causes: <b>iron overload</b> and <b>anemia of chronic disease</b>, which is driven by <b>too much hepcidin</b>.<br>B. Iron deficiency: TIBC is high (\"iron hunger\").<br>C. Ferroportin deficiency traps iron in macrophages and raises ferritin; it is not a lecture cause of low TIBC.<br>Ferritin, saturation and serum iron separate A from D.","ex":"","fl":["The source keyed only A (iron overload), but a low TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) is also the pattern of anemia of chronic disease, which is caused by hepcidin overexpression, so D is equally right."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 22 (Iron Panel Markers: What’s Normal?)","L36 Iron slide 24 (Interpreting the Iron panel: The 3-Question Method)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":185,"f":"F0621"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Ferritin = stored iron marker","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-21.jpg","cap":"Ferritin reflects total body iron stores; serum iron reflects circulating iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/G032-23.jpg","cap":"Marker table: ferritin = stored iron, TIBC = transferrin level","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 23"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-47-1","s":"Which iron-binding protein reflects stored iron, and which reflects the body’s transport capacity?","o":["A. Ferritin = transport; Transferrin = storage","B. Ferritin = storage; Transferrin = transport","C. Both ferritin and transferrin = storage","D. Both ferritin and transferrin = transport"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferritin = storage; Transferrin = transport","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> <b>Ferritin = storage</b>: it reflects body iron stores. <b>Transferrin = transport</b>: it carries Fe³⁺ in blood, and its amount is measured as TIBC (total iron-binding capacity).<br>A. Roles reversed.<br>C. Transferrin does not store iron.<br>D. Ferritin does not carry iron in blood.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 23 (| Marker | What it reflects |)","L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":47,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":47,"f":"F0890"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0065-25-1","s":"A 45-year-old woman has fatigue. Her physician orders tests to evaluate her iron status. Which single parameter, if markedly increased, most reliably indicates increased body iron stores rather than transient dietary intake?","o":["A. Serum iron concentration","B. Transferrin concentration","C. Transferrin saturation measured after a meal","D. Ferritin concentration","E. Urinary urobilinogen"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Ferritin concentration","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Ferritin</b> reflects <b>stored iron</b>: it rises and falls with total body iron stores, not with the last meal (it also rises with inflammation).<br>A. Serum iron: circulating iron; swings with diet and time of day.<br>B. Transferrin (measured as TIBC, total iron-binding capacity): reflects demand; it rises in deficiency.<br>C. Post-meal saturation: reflects recent intake.<br>E. Urinary urobilinogen: a bilirubin product, not an iron test.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 23 (| Marker | What it reflects |)","L36 Iron slide 19 (Iron Storage)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":25,"f":"F0065"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"TIBC reflects how much transferrin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-21.jpg","cap":"TIBC reflects transferrin level: how strongly the body tries to capture iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 21"},{"src":"sl/F0257-114.jpg","cap":"Serum iron = bound sites, UIBC = free sites, TIBC = total sites","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 114"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-112-1","s":"A patient’s lab report notes that their TIBC is elevated, while serum iron is low. Based on the information, which of the following best explains what an elevated TIBC represents?","o":["A. Increased number of transferrin molecules already carrying iron","B. Increased number of free binding sites on transferrin","C. Decreased number of total transferrin binding sites","D. High saturation of transferrin with iron"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased number of free binding sites on transferrin","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) counts <b>all transferrin binding sites</b>. When TIBC rises while serum iron falls, the extra sites are <b>empty</b>: the liver makes more transferrin to catch scarce iron (\"iron hunger\"), so free sites increase.<br>A. Iron-carrying transferrin is low, since serum iron is low.<br>C. Total sites increase, not decrease.<br>D. Saturation (serum iron divided by TIBC) is low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 23 (| Marker | What it reflects |)","L36 Iron slide 22 (Iron Panel Markers: What’s Normal?)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":112,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":112,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0257-117-1","s":"A 55-year-old man is found to have bronze skin pigmentation and mildly elevated liver enzymes during a routine exam. Further testing shows high serum iron, high serum ferritin, and transferrin saturation >50%. His TIBC is low-normal. Based on the information, which finding best explains why TIBC becomes low or normal in iron excess?","o":["A. The liver decreases transferrin production when iron levels are high","B. Excess iron increases the number of free transferrin binding sites","C. Transferrin molecules are destroyed by iron overload","D. Bone marrow stops using transferrin in high iron states"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"The liver decreases transferrin production when iron levels are high","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) measures <b>transferrin</b>, and the <b>liver makes less transferrin when iron stores are high</b>. Fewer transferrin molecules → low or normal TIBC.<br>B. Excess iron fills sites, so free sites decrease.<br>C. Iron does not destroy transferrin.<br>D. Marrow use doesn't set TIBC; liver synthesis does.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":117,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":117,"f":"F0938"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron deficiency labs: ↓iron, ↑TIBC, ↓ferritin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Iron deficiency row: low iron, high TIBC, low ferritin, saturation, MCV","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/F0257-119.jpg","cap":"Iron deficiency markers incl. negative marrow iron stain and low hemoglobin","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 119"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-5-1","s":"Which laboratory finding pattern is most consistent with Iron Deficiency Anemia?","o":["A. Low ferritin, High TIBC","B. High ferritin, High TIBC","C. Low ferritin, Low TIBC","D. High ferritin, Low TIBC","E. Normal ferritin, Normal TIBC"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Low ferritin, High TIBC","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Low ferritin, high TIBC.</b> Iron deficiency empties stores (<b>low ferritin</b>), and the liver makes more transferrin to catch iron (<b>high TIBC</b>, total iron-binding capacity).<br>B. Full stores with iron hunger don't occur together.<br>C. Low TIBC means no iron hunger.<br>D. High ferritin, low TIBC: anemia of chronic disease or overload.<br>E. Both normal: no iron disorder.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 22 (Iron Panel Markers: What’s Normal?)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":5,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":14,"f":"F0131"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0144-47-1","s":"Which of the following lab patterns would you expect from a patient with iron deficiency anemia?","o":["A. High serum iron, low TIBC, high ferritin","B. Low serum iron, high TIBC, low ferritin","C. Low serum iron, low TIBC, high ferritin","D. High serum iron, high TIBC, high ferritin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Low serum iron, high TIBC, low ferritin","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Low serum iron, high TIBC, low ferritin.</b> Iron deficiency: little circulating iron, more transferrin (high TIBC, total iron-binding capacity) and empty stores.<br>A. High iron, low TIBC, high ferritin: iron overload.<br>C. Low iron, low TIBC, high ferritin: anemia of chronic disease.<br>D. Everything high: no single iron disorder gives this.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":47,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":47,"f":"F1048"}]},{"id":"F0028-201-1","s":"Which of the following is indicative of iron deficiency?","o":["A. Low serum iron, low TIBC, high % saturation of TF","B. High serum iron, low TIBC, high % saturation of TF","C. Low serum iron, high TIBC, low % saturation of TF","D. High serum iron, high TIBC, low % saturation of TF"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Low serum iron, high TIBC, low % saturation of TF","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Low serum iron, high TIBC, low saturation.</b> Little iron and more transferrin (high TIBC, total iron-binding capacity) make saturation (serum iron divided by TIBC) low.<br>A. Low TIBC and high saturation don't fit deficiency.<br>B. High iron, low TIBC, high saturation: iron overload.<br>D. High serum iron rules out deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":201,"f":"F0028"}]},{"id":"F0208-41-1","s":"What is seen in Iron Deficiency","o":["A. Normal TIBC","B. High serum ferritin","C. Live Fe > 2mg/g","D. Low Hb"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Low Hb","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Low Hb (hemoglobin).</b> Without iron, red cells can't make enough hemoglobin, so hemoglobin falls.<br>A. TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) is high in iron deficiency, not normal.<br>B. Ferritin is low because stores are empty.<br>C. Liver iron above 2 mg/g means iron overload.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":41,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":221,"f":"F2296"}]},{"id":"F0208-83-1","s":"A 25 year old female patient presents to the Emergency Department complaining of palpitations and SOB on exertion, in addition to profound fatigue. On exam you note conjunctival and lip pallor. She endorses a history of very heavy menses, she runs endurance distances, and is on a vegetarian diet. With all the signs pointing to an iron deficiency anemia, what lab values would you expect?","o":["A. Low serum iron, low TIBC, low % saturation of transferrin, low ferritin","B. Low serum iron, high TIBC, high %saturation of transferrin, high ferritin","C. High serum iron, high TIBC,  low % saturation of transferrin, low ferritin","D. Low serum iron, high TIBC,  low % saturation of transferrin, low ferritin","E. High serum iron, low TIBC, high % saturation of transferrin, high ferritin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Low serum iron, high TIBC,  low % saturation of transferrin, low ferritin","a":"D","why":"<b>D.</b> Heavy menses, endurance running and a vegetarian diet drain iron: stores empty (<b>low ferritin</b>), transferrin rises (<b>high TIBC</b>, total iron-binding capacity), so <b>saturation is low</b> along with serum iron.<br>A. Low TIBC: no iron hunger.<br>B. High saturation and ferritin: overload.<br>C. High serum iron: wrong for deficiency.<br>E. All high: iron overload.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":83,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":182,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":162,"f":"F0180"}]},{"id":"F0257-115-1","s":"A patient presents with fatigue and pallor. Lab results show high TIBC, low % transferrin saturation, and negative iron staining of bone-marrow macrophages. Based on these findings, which additional result would most strongly support the diagnosis?","o":["A. High serum ferritin","B. Low serum iron","C. High serum iron","D. Low or normal TIBC","E. Liver iron >2 mg/g"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Low serum iron","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Low serum iron.</b> High TIBC (total iron-binding capacity), low saturation and <b>no stainable iron in marrow macrophages</b> mean <b>iron deficiency</b>; low serum iron completes the pattern.<br>A. High ferritin: chronic disease or overload.<br>C. High serum iron: overload.<br>D. Low or normal TIBC: overload or chronic disease; here TIBC is already high.<br>E. Liver iron above 2 mg/g: iron overload.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":115,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":115,"f":"F0938"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Low ferritin labs → iron deficiency anemia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Pattern table: low ferritin = true iron deficiency, unlike other disorders","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"},{"src":"sl/G032-24.jpg","cap":"Three-question method: serum iron, then TIBC, then ferritin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 24"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-44-1","s":"A 32-year-old woman presents with fatigue and pica. Laboratory evaluation shows the following: DECREASED serum iron, DECREASED ferritin, INCREASED TIBC, DECREASED transferrin saturation. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Anemia of chronic disease","B. Iron deficiency anemia","C. Hemochromatosis","D. Sideroblastic anemia"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron deficiency anemia","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Iron deficiency anemia.</b> Low serum iron, <b>low ferritin</b>, <b>high TIBC</b> (total iron-binding capacity) and low saturation is the classic pattern; pica supports it.<br>A. Anemia of chronic disease: TIBC low, ferritin normal or high.<br>C. Hemochromatosis: serum iron, ferritin and saturation all high.<br>D. Sideroblastic anemia: iron high, stores full.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":44,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":44,"f":"F0890"}]},{"id":"F0295-24-1","s":"A 25-year-old mother of two who is consulting her primary care physician for fatigue, dizziness, thinning of nails and hair loss. She has pale conjunctivae, pale gums, swollen tongue, several chipped teeth and pale nailbeds. No yellowing of the sclera observed. Blood samples were drawn and the results of initial laboratory work-up are shown below: MCV 61 (80–98 fL); RBC 3.5 (4.2–5.9 x 10^6 cells/µL); Serum iron 25 (50–150 µg/dL); Serum ferritin (female) 5 (11–307 ng/mL); Total iron binding capacity 450 (250–310 µg/dL); % transferrin saturation 5 (20–50 %). What is your probable diagnosis?","o":["A. Obstructive Jaundice","B. B6 deficiency","C. Lead poisoning","D. Iron deficiency","E. Iron overload"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron deficiency","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Iron deficiency.</b> Small cells (MCV, mean corpuscular volume, 61), low serum iron, <b>ferritin 5</b>, <b>high TIBC</b> (total iron-binding capacity) and 5% saturation, with pale nails and conjunctivae.<br>A. Obstructive jaundice: yellow sclera and high conjugated bilirubin; none here.<br>B. Vitamin B6 deficiency: sideroblastic anemia with high iron and ferritin.<br>C. Lead poisoning: microcytic, but ferritin normal or high.<br>E. Iron overload: iron and ferritin high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":24,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":24,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":26,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":30,"f":"F2421"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0065-21-1","s":"A 50-year-old man presents with fatigue. Labs show anemia with low hemoglobin and low MCV. Serum ferritin is markedly decreased. Which is the most likely explanation for his findings?","o":["A. Anemia of chronic disease with high hepcidin","B. Iron deficiency anemia due to chronic blood loss","C. Thalassemia minor with normal iron stores","D. Lead poisoning with ferrochelatase inhibition","E. Anemia from transfusional iron overload"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron deficiency anemia due to chronic blood loss","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Microcytic anemia plus <b>very low ferritin</b> = <b>true iron deficiency</b>; in a 50-year-old man the usual cause is <b>chronic blood loss</b>, often from the gut.<br>A. Anemia of chronic disease: ferritin normal or high.<br>C. Thalassemia minor: iron stores and ferritin normal.<br>D. Lead poisoning blocks ferrochelatase; ferritin normal or high.<br>E. Transfusion overload: ferritin very high.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)","L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":21,"f":"F0065"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Hemochromatosis labs: ↑iron, ↓TIBC, ↑ferritin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-25.jpg","cap":"Hemochromatosis row: high iron, normal-low TIBC, high ferritin and saturation","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 25"}],"q":[{"id":"F0928-30-1","s":"Which of the following serum values is most consistent with the diagnosis of hemochromatosis in a patient?","o":["A. Low iron, low TIBC (total iron-binding capacity), and low ferritin","B. Increased iron, normal TIBC, and low ferritin","C. Low iron, increased TIBC, and decreased ferritin","D. Low iron, low TIBC, and increased ferritin","E. Increased iron, low TIBC, and increased ferritin"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased iron, low TIBC, and increased ferritin","a":"E","why":"<b>E. Increased iron, low TIBC, increased ferritin.</b> In hemochromatosis, low hepcidin lets extra iron in: <b>serum iron and ferritin rise</b>, and the liver makes less transferrin, so <b>TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) is low or normal</b>.<br>A. All low: not iron excess.<br>B. Low ferritin contradicts overload.<br>C. Low iron, high TIBC, low ferritin: iron deficiency.<br>D. Low iron, low TIBC, high ferritin: anemia of chronic disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 self-assessment","n":"Biochem GI self-assessment (Canvas)","sl":30,"f":"F0928"},{"t":"2023 quiz","n":"week 17 quiz","sl":15,"f":"F1022"},{"t":"2021 Harlem quiz","n":"Canvas quizzes 15-17 (image slides)","sl":51,"f":"D006"},{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":16,"f":"F2435"},{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":195,"f":"F3881"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"MCV separates microcytic from macrocytic","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-26.jpg","cap":"MCV classes: microcytic below 80 fL, macrocytic above 100 fL, with causes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-6-1","s":"Which finding best differentiates microcytic anemia from macrocytic anemia?","o":["A. Microcytic: Low MCV, Low ferritin; Macrocytic: High MCV, Normal/High ferritin","B. Microcytic: High MCV; Macrocytic: Low MCV","C. Both have High MCV","D. Microcytic: Low MCV; Macrocytic: Normal MCV","E. Both have Low ferritin"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic: Low MCV, Low ferritin; Macrocytic: High MCV, Normal/High ferritin","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>MCV</b> (mean corpuscular volume, red cell size) separates them: <b>microcytic is below 80 fL</b> (femtoliters), here from iron deficiency (low ferritin); <b>macrocytic is above 100 fL</b>, from B12 or folate deficiency, with normal or high ferritin.<br>B. Reversed.<br>C. Microcytic cells are small, so MCV is low.<br>D. Macrocytic means a high MCV, not normal.<br>E. Macrocytic anemia does not lower ferritin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":6,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":16,"f":"F0131"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron deficiency → microcytic, hypochromic RBCs","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-28.jpg","cap":"Smear of small, pale red cells in iron deficiency anemia","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G032-26.jpg","cap":"Microcytic anemia from reduced hemoglobin synthesis; red cell size drawings","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 26"}],"q":[{"id":"F0131-12-1","s":"What type of anemia is characteristic of iron deficiency?","o":["A. Macrocytic","B. Microcytic, hypochromic","C. Normocytic","D. Hemolytic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic, hypochromic","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Microcytic, hypochromic.</b> Iron shortage cuts <b>hemoglobin synthesis</b>, so red cells are <b>small</b> (mean corpuscular volume under 80 fL, femtoliters) and <b>pale</b>.<br>A. Macrocytic: B12 or folate deficiency (impaired DNA synthesis).<br>C. Normocytic: acute blood loss, early chronic disease, hemolysis.<br>D. Hemolytic: red cells destroyed early; not the iron-deficiency picture.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":12,"f":"F0131"}]},{"id":"G036-4-1","s":"A 25-year-old female presents to the outpatient clinic complaining of progressive fatigue, generalized weakness, and shortness of breath over the past several weeks. She reports that even light physical activity, such as climbing stairs or walking quickly, makes her unusually tired. She has also noticed that her skin looks paler than usual, and her fingernails have become thin and spoon-shaped. Laboratory findings: Serum iron 30 µg/dL (normal 60–170); TIBC 450 µg/dL (normal 250–370); Ferritin 10 ng/mL (normal 12–150); MCV 70 fL (normal 80–100). What is the most likely diagnosis for this patient?","o":["A. Macrocytic","B. Microcytic, hypochromic","C. Normocytic","D. Hemolytic"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic, hypochromic","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Microcytic, hypochromic.</b> MCV (mean corpuscular volume) 70 fL (femtoliters) is below 80, and low iron, high TIBC (total iron-binding capacity) and low ferritin show <b>iron deficiency</b>: less hemoglobin is made, so cells are small and pale. Spoon nails fit.<br>A. Macrocytic: MCV over 100 (B12 or folate deficiency).<br>C. Normocytic: MCV 80–100.<br>D. Hemolytic: red cells destroyed; iron would not be low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)","L36 Iron slide 25 (Iron panel interpretation is usually combined with MCV classification when evalu)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":4,"f":"G036"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-194-1","s":"Amala P. is a 39-year old mother of three who is consulting her primary care physician for fatigue, dizziness, thinning of nails and hair loss. She is a native of Mumbai, India whose family migrated to the U.S. 3 years ago. Amala is a vegetarian and mentions that her menstrual periods over the past six months have been heavier than usual (menorrhagia). In addition, she divulges that during these periods of heavy menstrual flow and while pregnant, she compulsively eats (pica) uncooked milled basmati rice which often resulted in abdominal pain. Eating uncooked rice was something her mother, grandmother and other women in Mumbai also did during pregnancy and periods of heavy menstrual flow. Amala also observed that in the last three months she gets tired more easily and has increasingly not been able to do as much housework as before. She also becomes breathless and has palpitations after climbing a flight of stairs. On physical examination, Amala’s vital signs are as follows: HR= 110/min, RR= 16/min, BP = 90/60 mm Hg. She has pale conjunctivae, pale gums, swollen tongue, several chipped teeth and pale nailbeds. No yellowing of the sclerae was observed. Blood samples were drawn and the results of initial laboratory work-up are shown below Peripheral blood smear result: Presence of red blood cells with increased zone of central pallor that are microcytic (thick arrows), hypochromic red blood cells (thin arrows) and have irregular shapes (arrowhead) Question 2: Based on Amala’s Sx, PE, lab and histo findings, what’s her most likely differential diagnosis?","o":["A. Cobalamin Deficiency","B. Hereditary Hemochromatosis","C. Microcytic hypochromic anemia","D. Folic acid deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Microcytic hypochromic anemia","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Microcytic hypochromic anemia.</b> The smear shows small (microcytic), pale cells with a wide central pallor (hypochromic). With menorrhagia, pica and a vegetarian diet, this is classic <b>iron deficiency</b>.<br>A. Cobalamin (vitamin B12) deficiency: large (macrocytic) cells.<br>B. Hereditary hemochromatosis: iron overload; no hypochromic anemia.<br>D. Folic acid deficiency: large (macrocytic) cells.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 26 (How Iron Disorders Fit into MCV Classification)","L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":194,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Pallor: less hemoglobin made","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-28.jpg","cap":"Low iron cuts hemoglobin synthesis, giving small, pale red cells","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28"}],"q":[{"id":"G036-8-1","s":"Why do patients with iron deficiency anemia often appear pale?","o":["A. Increased breakdown of hemoglobin","B. Decreased hemoglobin production reduces oxygen-carrying capacity","C. Increased melanin synthesis in the skin","D. Excess iron stored in tissues","E. Increased red blood cell size"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased hemoglobin production reduces oxygen-carrying capacity","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Without iron, red cells make <b>less hemoglobin</b>, the red pigment. Less hemoglobin means paler skin and conjunctivae and <b>less oxygen-carrying capacity</b>.<br>A. Faster hemoglobin breakdown (hemolysis) causes jaundice, not this.<br>C. More melanin would darken skin.<br>D. Iron is depleted, not in excess.<br>E. Cells get smaller (microcytic), not larger.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2026 clinical case","n":"Iron deficiency clinical case Fall 2026","sl":8,"f":"G036"},{"t":"2025 TBL","n":"TBL iron preparation","sl":20,"f":"F0131"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Causes of iron deficiency anemia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-29.jpg","cap":"Causes: blood loss (most common), demand, malabsorption like celiac, poor intake","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-49-1","s":"A 25-year-old female patient presents with fatigue, pallor, hair loss, and brittle nails. When asked about her menses, she reports that she’s always had heavy menses. What is her most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Anemia of chronic disease","B. Transfusion overload","C. Hereditary hemochromatosis","D. Iron deficiency anemia"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron deficiency anemia","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Iron deficiency anemia.</b> Fatigue, pallor, hair loss and brittle nails in a young woman with <b>heavy menses</b>: <b>blood loss</b> is the most common cause of iron deficiency.<br>A. Anemia of chronic disease: needs chronic inflammation, infection or cancer.<br>B. Transfusion overload: needs repeated transfusions.<br>C. Hereditary hemochromatosis: iron excess, mostly in middle-aged men; menstruation protects women.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":49,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":49,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0621-197-1","s":"(Amala vignette, slides 190-191) What are the most common causes of Amala’s condition?","o":["A. Pregnancy","B. Menorrhagia","C. Inadequate dietary intake","D. Malabsorption of iron","E. All of the above"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"All of the above","a":"E","why":"<b>E. All of the above.</b> Iron deficiency comes from <b>blood loss</b> (menorrhagia, gastrointestinal bleeding; the most common cause), <b>increased demand</b> (pregnancy, growth), <b>malabsorption</b> (celiac disease, bariatric surgery) and <b>poor intake</b> (a vegetarian diet).<br>A, B, C and D are each true causes, and Amala has all four.<br>Mix-up: in a man or older woman, think gastrointestinal bleeding first.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":197,"f":"F0621"}]},{"id":"F0950-18-1","s":"A 45-year-old woman is found to have low serum ferritin, microcytic anemia, and elevated total iron-binding capacity (TIBC). A subsequent biopsy reveals intestinal villous atrophy. Which of the following best explains her anemia?","o":["A. Impaired dietary iron absorption","B. Chronic blood loss","C. Excess hepcidin production","D. Decreased transferrin production"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Impaired dietary iron absorption","a":"A","why":"<b>A.</b> <b>Villous atrophy</b> means <b>celiac disease</b>, which damages the duodenum, where iron is absorbed. Result: iron deficiency (low ferritin, high TIBC, total iron-binding capacity, small cells).<br>B. Chronic blood loss also causes iron deficiency, but the biopsy points to malabsorption.<br>C. Excess hepcidin: ferritin normal or high, TIBC low.<br>D. Less transferrin would lower TIBC, not raise it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 15 (Iron absorption occurs primarily in the duodenum)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":18,"f":"F0950"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Signs that suggest anemia","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-28.jpg","cap":"Small, pale red cells: the anemia behind pallor and fatigue","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 28"},{"src":"sl/G032-29.jpg","cap":"Heavy menstrual bleeding and poor intake listed as iron deficiency causes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 29"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-192-1","s":"Amala P. is a 39-year old mother of three who is consulting her primary care physician for fatigue, dizziness, thinning of nails and hair loss. She is a native of Mumbai, India whose family migrated to the U.S. 3 years ago. Amala is a vegetarian and mentions that her menstrual periods over the past six months have been heavier than usual (menorrhagia). In addition, she divulges that during these periods of heavy menstrual flow and while pregnant, she compulsively eats (pica) uncooked milled basmati rice which often resulted in abdominal pain. Eating uncooked rice was something her mother, grandmother and other women in Mumbai also did during pregnancy and periods of heavy menstrual flow. Amala also observed that in the last three months she gets tired more easily and has increasingly not been able to do as much housework as before. She also becomes breathless and has palpitations after climbing a flight of stairs. On physical examination, Amala’s vital signs are as follows: HR= 110/min, RR= 16/min, BP = 90/60 mm Hg. She has pale conjunctivae, pale gums, swollen tongue, several chipped teeth and pale nailbeds. No yellowing of the sclerae was observed. Blood samples were drawn and the results of initial laboratory work-up are shown below Peripheral blood smear result: Presence of red blood cells with increased zone of central pallor that are microcytic (thick arrows), hypochromic red blood cells (thin arrows) and have irregular shapes (arrowhead) Question 1: Based on Amala’s MHx and physical exam findings, what makes you suspect patient might be anemic?","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Menorrhagia; fatigue, dizziness, thinning of nails and hair loss; pica (compulsively eats uncooked milled basmati rice); pale conjunctivae, pale gums, swollen tongue, several chipped teeth and pale nailbeds; combined with lab results and blood smear","a":null,"why":"Clues to anemia: <b>menorrhagia</b> (chronic blood loss), <b>fatigue, dizziness, breathlessness and palpitations</b> on exertion, <b>pallor</b> of conjunctivae, gums and nail beds, thinning nails and hair, a swollen tongue, and <b>pica</b> (eating raw rice, a classic iron-deficiency sign). Labs and the smear (small, pale red cells) confirm it.<br>Mix-up: no yellow sclera, so hemolysis and liver disease are unlikely.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)","L36 Iron slide 29 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":192,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Iron deficiency treatment: oral iron plus cause","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-29.jpg","cap":"Treatment: oral iron supplementation and fixing the cause of blood loss","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 29"},{"src":"sl/G036-11.jpg","cap":"Management: oral ferrous sulfate, parenteral iron if needed, address causes","where":"2026 iron-deficiency case, slide 11"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-199-1","s":"(Amala vignette, slides 190-191) What would be one of the recommended treatment for Amala?","o":["A. Blood transfusion","B. Iron replacement therapy","C. Folate and B12 supplement","D. Diet changes","E. C & D","F. B & D"],"g":["F"],"k":["F"],"rv":false,"at":"B & D","a":"F","why":"<b>F. B &amp;amp; D.</b> Iron deficiency is treated with <b>oral iron replacement</b> (ferrous sulfate) plus fixing the cause; for a vegetarian with heavy menses, <b>diet changes</b> (iron-rich foods with vitamin C) help.<br>A. Transfusion: only for severe or unstable anemia.<br>B. Iron alone: right but incomplete.<br>C. Folate and B12: for macrocytic anemia; hers is microcytic.<br>D. Diet alone: too slow by itself.<br>E. C &amp;amp; D: folate and B12 are wrong here.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 28 (Iron Deficiency Anemia)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":199,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Inflammation + microcytic anemia → high hepcidin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-30.jpg","cap":"Anemia of chronic disease: inflammation, interleukin-6, hepcidin, less ferroportin, trapped iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 30"},{"src":"sl/G032-31.jpg","cap":"Iron trapped in macrophages; low iron, low TIBC, normal-high ferritin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 31"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-121-1","s":"A patient develops hypochromic, microcytic RBCs due to overexpression of hepcidin. Which condition is the most likely cause of this finding?","o":["A. Heavy menstrual bleeding","B. Pregnancy with increased iron demand","C. Malabsorption of iron in the small intestine","D. Inflammation leading to anemia of chronic disease","E. Inadequate dietary iron intake"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Inflammation leading to anemia of chronic disease","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Inflammation → anemia of chronic disease.</b> Inflammatory cytokines (interleukin-6) <b>raise hepcidin</b>; iron is trapped, so red cells become small and pale.<br>A. Heavy menstrual bleeding: iron loss lowers hepcidin.<br>B. Pregnancy: high demand lowers hepcidin.<br>C. Malabsorption: iron deficiency lowers hepcidin.<br>E. Poor intake: iron deficiency lowers hepcidin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 31 (Lab Pattern)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":121,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":121,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0065-19-1","s":"A patient with long-standing rheumatoid arthritis develops microcytic anemia. Hemoglobin is low, MCV is decreased, and serum ferritin is normal-high. Which change in iron regulatory hormones best explains these findings?","o":["A. Decreased hepatic hepcidin synthesis, increasing ferroportin activity","B. Increased hepatic hepcidin synthesis, decreasing ferroportin activity","C. Decreased IL-6 production, decreasing hepcidin","D. Increased erythropoietin, decreasing hepcidin","E. Decreased hepcidin with increased intestinal iron absorption"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased hepatic hepcidin synthesis, decreasing ferroportin activity","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Rheumatoid arthritis → interleukin-6 → <b>liver makes more hepcidin</b> → ferroportin degraded → iron trapped in macrophages. Stores are full (<b>ferritin normal-high</b>) but the marrow is starved: anemia of chronic disease.<br>A. Less hepcidin would release iron.<br>C. Inflammation raises interleukin-6.<br>D. Erythropoietin lowers hepcidin, freeing iron.<br>E. Low hepcidin with high absorption: hemochromatosis pattern.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 31 (Lab Pattern)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":19,"f":"F0065"}]},{"id":"F2435-14-1","s":"A 31-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with a 4 month history of fatigue and shortness of breath. Her past medical history is significant for rheumatoid arthritis for which she takes methotrexate. She is vegan, drinks 2 glasses of wine per night, and lives in an old house. Physical exam reveals a thin, disheveled woman with conjunctival pallor. Selected laboratory tests showed: Hemoglobin: 9.5 g/dL (12 to 16 g/dL); Mean corpuscular volume: 73 fl (81 to 99 fl); Ferritin: 100 ng/ml (10 to 120 ng/mL). (Normal range given in the parenthesis). Which of the following is the best explanation of this patient’s presentation?","o":["A. Ferrochelatase inhibition","B. Folate deficiency","C. Increased hepcidin expression","D. Inherited hemoglobinopathy","E. Iron deficiency"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased hepcidin expression","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Increased hepcidin expression.</b> Rheumatoid arthritis → interleukin-6 → <b>hepcidin up</b> → iron trapped → microcytic anemia (MCV, mean corpuscular volume, 73) with <b>normal ferritin (100)</b> = anemia of chronic disease.<br>A. Lead (old house) blocks ferrochelatase, but there are no lead signs; the arthritis explains it.<br>B. Folate deficiency (methotrexate, alcohol): macrocytic.<br>D. Hemoglobinopathy: lifelong, usually mild.<br>E. Iron deficiency: ferritin would be low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 31 (Lab Pattern)"],"src":[{"t":"2020 Harlem clicker","n":"class clickers Fall '20 (Biochem Exam 4)","sl":14,"f":"F2435"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Interleukin-6 → less iron absorbed and released","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-13.jpg","cap":"Inflammation and infection induce hepcidin, lowering ferroportin activity and plasma iron","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G032-9.jpg","cap":"High hepcidin degrades ferroportin; iron stays inside cells","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 9"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-86-1","s":"A patient has inflammation mediated by IL-6. What effect does this have on iron handling?","o":["A. Increased iron absorption and release via FPN","B. Decreased iron absorption and release via FPN","C. Increased hepcidin suppression due to hypoxia","D. Increased iron release caused by ineffective erythropoiesis","E. Increased iron deficiency due to low hepcidin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased iron absorption and release via FPN","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Interleukin-6 → <b>more hepcidin</b> → ferroportin (FPN) degraded → <b>less iron absorbed</b> from the gut and <b>less released</b> from macrophages.<br>A. More absorption and release: low hepcidin.<br>C. Hypoxia lowers hepcidin; interleukin-6 raises it.<br>D. Ineffective erythropoiesis lowers hepcidin; interleukin-6 doesn't cause it.<br>E. Inflammation raises hepcidin, not lowers it.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 32 (Anemia of Chronic Disease and Functional Iron Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":86,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":86,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0359-17-1","s":"What is an outcome of chronic inflammation mediated by IL-6 on Iron storage?","o":["A. Repression of Hepcidin synthesis leading to increased iron absorption and release via FPN","B. Destruction of brush border enzyme feri-reductase leading to decreased intake and anemia.","C. Induction of Hepcidin leading to decreased iron absorption in intestines and decreased release from macrophages and release by FPN causing anemia of chronic disease.","D. Increased iron loss in the stool due to decreased intestinal absorption."],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Induction of Hepcidin leading to decreased iron absorption in intestines and decreased release from macrophages and release by FPN causing anemia of chronic disease.","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Interleukin-6 <b>induces hepcidin</b>; hepcidin degrades ferroportin (FPN), so <b>less iron is absorbed</b> and <b>macrophages hold their iron</b>: anemia of chronic disease.<br>A. Repressed hepcidin with more absorption: iron deficiency or hemochromatosis.<br>B. Inflammation does not destroy the ferrireductase Dcytb (duodenal cytochrome b).<br>D. The key effect is iron held in macrophages, not loss in stool.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 13 (Signals That Regulate Hepcidin)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2024","sl":17,"f":"F0359"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Lecture 40 iron TA review","sl":7,"f":"F2436"},{"t":"2019 TA review","n":"Biochem 4 TA review","sl":166,"f":"F1201"},{"t":"2024 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":129,"f":"F2303"},{"t":"2020 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam IV TA review","sl":235,"f":"F0035"},{"t":"2018 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 (L31-41) TA review","sl":192,"f":"F1200"},{"t":"2018-19 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA reviews merged","sl":192,"f":"F1218"}]},{"id":"F0065-22-1","s":"In which situation would systemic iron levels be expected to decrease the most due to a hepcidin-mediated mechanism?","o":["A. Acute hemorrhage with hypoxia","B. Iron deficiency from poor diet","C. Acute bacterial infection with high IL-6","D. High altitude exposure with increased erythropoiesis","E. Treatment with an iron chelator"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Acute bacterial infection with high IL-6","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Infection raises <b>interleukin-6</b>, which induces <b>hepcidin</b>; hepcidin degrades ferroportin, so gut cells and macrophages stop releasing iron and <b>serum iron falls fast</b>.<br>A. Bleeding and hypoxia lower hepcidin.<br>B. Iron deficiency lowers hepcidin.<br>D. High altitude and active red cell production lower hepcidin.<br>E. A chelator removes iron directly, not through hepcidin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 13 (Signals That Regulate Hepcidin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":22,"f":"F0065"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Interleukin-6 directly raises liver hepcidin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-13.jpg","cap":"Interleukin-6/STAT3 signaling raises hepcidin gene transcription in hepatocytes","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 13"},{"src":"sl/G032-30.jpg","cap":"Interleukin-6 raises hepcidin, lowers ferroportin: anemia of chronic disease","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 30"}],"q":[{"id":"F0382-17-1","s":"Patients with chronic rheumatoid arthritis have elevated serum interleukin-6 (IL-6) and decreased Hb levels. Which of the following proteins would also be expected to increase in serum in such patients?","o":["A. Ferritin","B. Ferroportin","C. Transferrin receptor","D. Hepcidin","E. Ceruloplasmin"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Hepcidin","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Hepcidin.</b> Interleukin-6 directly drives the liver to make <b>hepcidin</b>, which traps iron and lowers hemoglobin (anemia of chronic disease).<br>A. Ferritin: normal or high in this anemia, so not a sure rise; it isn't the protein interleukin-6 targets.<br>B. Ferroportin: degraded by hepcidin, so it falls.<br>C. Transferrin receptor: rises in iron deficiency.<br>E. Ceruloplasmin: copper-carrying ferroxidase; the iron change interleukin-6 drives is hepcidin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 31 (Lab Pattern)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh recap 2 heme & iron","sl":17,"f":"F0382"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"Bagh exam review heme & iron","sl":23,"f":"F0438"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":26,"f":"F2421"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0295-22-1","s":"Chronic inflammation can lead to microcytic anemia by increased signaling via interleukin-6 (IL-6) leading to decreased acquisition of dietary iron. Which of the following describes the direct effect of IL-6 signaling?","o":["A. Decreased translation of ferritin mRNA by the liver","B. Increased translation of the transferrin receptor mRNA by the liver","C. Increased synthesis of ferroportin by the enterocytes","D. Decreased synthesis of transferrin by the liver","E. Increased synthesis of hepcidin by the liver"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Increased synthesis of hepcidin by the liver","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Interleukin-6's direct iron effect is making <b>liver cells synthesize more hepcidin</b>, which degrades ferroportin and cuts dietary iron entry.<br>A. Ferritin translation follows cell iron through IRP/IRE (iron regulatory protein / iron-responsive element), not interleukin-6.<br>B. Transferrin receptor rises with low cell iron, not interleukin-6.<br>C. Ferroportin is degraded, not increased.<br>D. Transferrin does fall in inflammation (low TIBC, total iron-binding capacity), but that doesn't block dietary uptake.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 32 (Anemia of Chronic Disease and Functional Iron Deficiency)","L36 Iron slide 13 (Signals That Regulate Hepcidin)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh interactive slides heme, iron, IDA","sl":22,"f":"F0295"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Bagh supplemental heme & iron PE IS","sl":22,"f":"F0348"},{"t":"2025 faculty review","n":"compiled recap heme & iron","sl":27,"f":"F2421"}]},{"id":"F0133-38-1","s":"A patient on IL-6 therapy for rheumatoid arthritis develops anemia. Labs show low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, normal to high ferritin, and low TIBC. What factor explains these findings?","o":["A. Hypoxia","B. HAMP gene mutation","C. IL-6 activating JAK/STAT pathway","D. HFE gene mutation","E. Ineffective Erythropoiesis"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"IL-6 activating JAK/STAT pathway","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> <b>Interleukin-6</b> signals through <b>JAK/STAT3</b> (Janus kinase / signal transducer and activator of transcription 3) to switch on the hepcidin gene. More hepcidin traps iron: low serum iron, low TIBC (total iron-binding capacity), normal-high ferritin.<br>A. Hypoxia lowers hepcidin.<br>B. HAMP (hepcidin gene) mutation lowers hepcidin.<br>D. HFE (hemochromatosis gene) mutation lowers hepcidin.<br>E. Ineffective erythropoiesis lowers hepcidin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 30 (Anemia of Chronic Disease)","L36 Iron slide 32 (Anemia of Chronic Disease and Functional Iron Deficiency)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":38,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":38,"f":"F0890"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Hemochromatosis: low hepcidin, unchecked ferroportin","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-33.jpg","cap":"HFE, HJV, TFR2 mutations lower hepcidin, raising ferroportin activity and absorption","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 33"},{"src":"sl/F0257-93.jpg","cap":"HAMP, HFE, TFR2, HJV mutations decrease hepcidin synthesis","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 93"}],"q":[{"id":"F0133-49-1","s":"A 55-year-old man presents with skin hyperpigmentation, diabetes, arthropathy, and elevated liver enzymes. His iron studies show: INCREASED serum iron, INCREASED ferritin, INCREASED TF saturation. Which of the following mechanisms best explains his condition?","o":["A. Decreased intestinal iron absorption due to low hepcidin","B. Increased hepcidin activity leading to macrophage iron trapping","C. Mutation causing reduced hepcidin levels and unregulated ferroportin activity","D. Autoimmune destruction of enterocytes leading to iron loss"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Mutation causing reduced hepcidin levels and unregulated ferroportin activity","a":"C","why":"<b>C.</b> Hereditary hemochromatosis: gene mutations (most often HFE, the hemochromatosis gene) cause <b>low hepcidin</b>, so <b>ferroportin works unchecked</b> and too much iron is absorbed. Iron deposits in skin (bronze), pancreas (diabetes), joints and liver.<br>A. Low hepcidin increases, not decreases, absorption.<br>B. High hepcidin with trapped macrophage iron: anemia of chronic disease (low serum iron).<br>D. Destroyed enterocytes cause iron loss, not overload.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":49,"f":"F0133"},{"t":"2025 Harlem TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":49,"f":"F0890"}]},{"id":"F0257-91-1","s":"A patient is found to have a mutation in the HFE gene (Chr 6). Which outcome is most likely?","o":["A. Increased hepcidin synthesis leading to decreased iron absorption","B. Decreased hepcidin synthesis causing excessive iron absorption and release via FPN","C. Normal hepcidin synthesis with reduced iron export from enterocytes","D. Increased SMAD signaling resulting in hereditary iron deficiency","E. Increased transcription of the HAMP gene leading to anemia of chronic disease"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased hepcidin synthesis causing excessive iron absorption and release via FPN","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> HFE (hemochromatosis gene) mutation → the liver makes <b>too little hepcidin</b> → ferroportin (FPN) stays active → <b>excess iron absorption and release</b> → hereditary hemochromatosis.<br>A. Hepcidin falls, not rises.<br>C. Hepcidin is low and iron export increases.<br>D. SMAD (the hepcidin-inducing signal) falls, and the result is overload, not deficiency.<br>E. HAMP (hepcidin gene) transcription falls; high hepcidin is anemia of chronic disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":91,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":91,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-77-1","s":"A patient is admitted from the ER to the floor for further evaluation of abdominal pain, gray/bronze skin color, and joint pain. The attending physician you are shadowing notes that the patient has hereditary hemochromatosis, or excessive iron absorption leading to iron toxicity. The physician turns to you and asks “What is the cause of this patient’s disorder?” You answer:","o":["A. Increased hepcidin from a Chr 6 HFE mutation","B. Induction of hepcidin synthesis due to hypoxia","C. Hepcidin repression from ineffective erythropoiesis (RBC production)","D. Induction of hepcidin synthesis due to increased inflammation","E. Repression of hepcidin synthesis due to Chr 19 HAMP mutation"],"g":["E"],"k":["E"],"rv":false,"at":"Repression of hepcidin synthesis due to Chr 19 HAMP mutation","a":"E","why":"<b>E.</b> Hereditary hemochromatosis = <b>too little hepcidin</b>. A HAMP (hepcidin gene, chromosome 19) mutation removes hepcidin, so ferroportin stays active and iron absorption runs unchecked.<br>A. An HFE (chromosome 6) mutation lowers hepcidin; it doesn't raise it.<br>B. Hypoxia represses hepcidin.<br>C. Repression from ineffective erythropoiesis is the iron loading of thalassemia, not hereditary hemochromatosis.<br>D. Inflammation raising hepcidin causes anemia of chronic disease.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)","L36 Iron slide 11 (Hepcidin: The Master Regulator)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":77,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":176,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":156,"f":"F0180"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"HFE (chromosome 6) = commonest hemochromatosis gene","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-33.jpg","cap":"Most common form: Type 1, HFE mutation C282Y","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 33"},{"src":"sl/F0257-93.jpg","cap":"Gene chromosomes: HAMP 19, HFE 6, TFR2 7, HJV 1","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 93"}],"q":[{"id":"F0144-51-1","s":"A 48-year-old diabetic man presents with fatigue, weakness, and hyperpigmentation of his skin. After thorough work-up, his genetic tests show that he has a mutated HFE gene. What is his most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Hereditary Hemochromatosis Type 1","B. Hereditary Hemochromatosis Type 2","C. Transfusion overload","D. Anemia of chronic disease"],"g":["A"],"k":["A"],"rv":false,"at":"Hereditary Hemochromatosis Type 1","a":"A","why":"<b>A. Hereditary hemochromatosis Type 1</b> is the <b>HFE</b> (hemochromatosis gene) form, the most common (often the C282Y mutation). Diabetes, fatigue and bronze skin come from iron deposits.<br>B. Type 2 (juvenile): HJV (hemojuvelin) or HAMP (hepcidin gene) mutations.<br>C. Transfusion overload: acquired from repeated transfusions, not a gene.<br>D. Anemia of chronic disease: high hepcidin and low serum iron.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":51,"f":"F0144"},{"t":"2025 Middletown TA review","n":"TA review Fall 2025 GI","sl":51,"f":"F1048"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0257-89-1","s":"A 42-year-old man presents with chronic fatigue, arthralgia, and elevated transferrin saturation. Genetic testing reveals a mutation that decreases hepcidin synthesis. Which of the following mutations is most commonly associated with hereditary hemochromatosis due to reduced hepcidin production?","o":["A. HAMP (chromosome 19)","B. HFE (chromosome 6)","C. TFR2 (chromosome 7)","D. HJV (chromosome 1)"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HFE (chromosome 6)","a":"B","why":"<b>B. HFE (chromosome 6)</b> is the <b>most common</b> hemochromatosis gene (Type 1, usually C282Y). HFE normally helps the liver sense iron and make hepcidin.<br>A. HAMP (hepcidin gene, chromosome 19): rare juvenile form.<br>C. TFR2 (transferrin receptor 2, chromosome 7): rare Type 3.<br>D. HJV (hemojuvelin, chromosome 1): rare juvenile form.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":89,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":89,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0257-124-1","s":"A 38-year-old man presents with fatigue, joint pain, and elevated liver enzymes. Labs show very high transferrin saturation and high ferritin. Genetic testing reveals an autosomal recessive mutation that reduces hepcidin synthesis. Based on the slide, which mutation is most consistent with this patient’s condition?","o":["A. FPN (autosomal dominant)","B. HFE","C. Transferrin (Tf)","D. Ceruloplasmin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"HFE","a":"B","why":"<b>B. HFE</b> (hemochromatosis gene): <b>autosomal recessive</b>, the most common hereditary hemochromatosis, and it <b>lowers hepcidin</b> production.<br>A. FPN (ferroportin) disease is autosomal dominant, and the defect is in the exporter, not hepcidin synthesis.<br>C. Transferrin (Tf): its loss is a rare cause of anemia, not the common hemochromatosis gene.<br>D. Ceruloplasmin loss causes brain and liver iron build-up, not low hepcidin.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":124,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":124,"f":"F0938"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Organ iron damage → hemochromatosis","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-34.jpg","cap":"Iron damage: liver cirrhosis, pancreas diabetes, heart cardiomyopathy, bronze skin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/G032-35.jpg","cap":"Hemochromatosis picture: excess iron deposits in liver, pancreas, heart, skin","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0950-9-1","s":"A patient presents with progressive dyspnea, diabetes mellitus, and hepatomegaly. Iron studies reveal elevated ferritin and transferrin saturation. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["A. Wilson’s disease","B. Hemochromatosis","C. Iron deficiency anemia","D. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Hemochromatosis","a":"B","why":"<b>B. Hemochromatosis.</b> High ferritin and transferrin saturation plus <b>iron damage to organs</b>: liver (hepatomegaly), pancreas (diabetes), heart (cardiomyopathy → dyspnea).<br>A. Wilson's disease: copper overload; low ceruloplasmin, normal iron studies.<br>C. Iron deficiency anemia: ferritin and saturation low.<br>D. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency: emphysema and liver disease with normal iron studies.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)","L36 Iron slide 34 (Clinical Manifestations)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 faculty practice","n":"Exam 4 review questions & answers","sl":9,"f":"F0950"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Transfusion overload is acquired, not hereditary","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-35.jpg","cap":"Hemochromatosis (gut absorption) vs transfusion overload (repeated red cell iron)","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F3881-202-1","s":"Which of the following is INCORRECT regarding hereditary hemochromatosis?","o":["A. A mutation in HFE2 gene leads to HH Type 2 and has an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern","B. A mutation in ferroportin leads to HH Type 4 and has an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern","C. Can be caused by frequent transfusions secondary to chronic hemolytic anemia (e.g. sickle cell disease)","D. A mutation in TFR2 gene leads to HH Type 3 and has an autosomal recessive pattern"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Can be caused by frequent transfusions secondary to chronic hemolytic anemia (e.g. sickle cell disease)","a":"C","why":"<b>C is incorrect.</b> Iron overload from <b>frequent transfusions</b> (sickle cell, thalassemia) is <b>transfusion overload</b>, an acquired condition, not hereditary hemochromatosis.<br>A. True: HFE2 (the hemojuvelin gene) → Type 2 (juvenile), autosomal recessive.<br>B. True: ferroportin → Type 4, autosomal dominant.<br>D. True: TFR2 (transferrin receptor 2) → Type 3, autosomal recessive.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 33 (Mechanism)","L36 Iron slide 35 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":202,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Transfusion overload: hepcidin too low","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-35.jpg","cap":"Transfusion overload: repeated transfusions, no regulated iron excretion","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 35"},{"src":"sl/G032-37.jpg","cap":"Hepcidin low with blood loss and ineffective erythropoiesis","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 37"}],"q":[{"id":"F0065-20-1","s":"A teenager with transfusion-dependent chronic hemolytic anemia has high serum ferritin and evidence of organ iron deposition. Which underlying regulatory abnormality most directly contributes to his iron overload?","o":["A. Persistent overexpression of HAMP gene","B. Decreased hepcidin activity relative to iron burden","C. Decreased expression of ferroportin","D. Increased intestinal DMT1 activity","E. Excessive binding of iron to ferritin"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"Decreased hepcidin activity relative to iron burden","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Transfused iron has <b>no excretion route</b>. In chronic hemolytic anemia, busy or ineffective red cell production keeps <b>hepcidin too low for the iron load</b>, so the gut keeps absorbing and macrophages keep releasing iron.<br>A. HAMP (hepcidin gene) overexpression would limit iron entry.<br>C. Less ferroportin would trap iron in cells.<br>D. Higher DMT1 (divalent metal transporter 1) uptake is downstream, not the regulatory fault.<br>E. Ferritin binding stores iron safely.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 35 (Mechanism)","L36 Iron slide 37 (| Hepcidin High | Hepcidin Low |)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 faculty practice","n":"practice questions heme & iron","sl":20,"f":"F0065"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Transfusion overload → iron chelation","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-36.jpg","cap":"Chelator deferasirox binds excess iron; complex excreted in urine and feces","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-126-1","s":"A 17-year-old patient with sickle cell disease has received monthly blood transfusions for several years. He now develops signs of iron overload. According to the slide, what is the most appropriate treatment for transfusion-induced iron overload?","o":["A. Weekly phlebotomy","B. High-dose vitamin C","C. IV iron chelation therapy","D. Oral hepcidin supplements"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"IV iron chelation therapy","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Iron chelation therapy</b> (deferoxamine by IV, intravenous, infusion; or oral deferasirox) removes iron in transfusion-dependent sickle cell patients.<br>A. Weekly phlebotomy: for hereditary hemochromatosis; it would worsen his anemia.<br>B. Vitamin C: boosts iron absorption.<br>D. Oral hepcidin: not a therapy, and it can't remove iron already in tissues.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 36 (Drug Metabolism and Disposition: the Biological Fate of Chemicals 36(12):2523-38)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":126,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":126,"f":"F0938"}]},{"id":"F0179-26-1","s":"A teenager with a chronic hemolytic anemia has received frequent transfusions for years. He now has biochemical evidence of iron overload. The most appropriate therapy is","o":["A. Phlebotomy","B. Increase dietary calcium","C. Iron chelation (e.g., deferoxamine or deferasirox)","D. Erythropoietin injections","E. High-dose vitamin C to enhance absorption"],"g":["C"],"k":["C"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron chelation (e.g., deferoxamine or deferasirox)","a":"C","why":"<b>C. Iron chelation</b> (deferoxamine or deferasirox) binds excess iron so it is excreted. It is used for <b>transfusion overload</b> because these patients are anemic.<br>A. Phlebotomy: for hereditary hemochromatosis; it would worsen this anemia.<br>B. Dietary calcium: barely changes iron balance.<br>D. Erythropoietin: raises red cell production; doesn't remove iron.<br>E. Vitamin C: increases iron absorption, making it worse.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 36 (Drug Metabolism and Disposition: the Biological Fate of Chemicals 36(12):2523-38)","L36 Iron slide 35 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron after-IS with explanations","sl":26,"f":"F0179"},{"t":"2025 interactive","n":"Heme & iron before-IS question stems","sl":5,"f":"F0293"}],"t":1},{"id":"F0208-43-1","s":"For patients experiencing transfusion overload due to frequent transfusions secondary to chronic hemolytic anemia, which treatment is commonly employed to manage excess iron accumulation?","o":["A. Oral iron supplementation","B. Intravenous administration of iron","C. Bloodletting therapy","D. IV administration of iron-chelator"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"IV administration of iron-chelator","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Iron chelator</b> (deferoxamine by IV, intravenous, infusion; or oral deferasirox). Transfused iron can't be excreted, and these patients are anemic, so a chelator binds iron for removal in urine and stool.<br>A. Oral iron: adds iron.<br>B. Intravenous iron: adds iron.<br>C. Bloodletting (phlebotomy): used in hereditary hemochromatosis; it would worsen this anemia.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 35 (Mechanism)","L36 Iron slide 36 (Drug Metabolism and Disposition: the Biological Fate of Chemicals 36(12):2523-38)"],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":43,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":223,"f":"F2296"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Repeated transfusions → iron overload","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-35.jpg","cap":"Chronic transfusions for sickle cell or thalassemia load iron with no excretion","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 35"}],"q":[{"id":"F0621-187-1","s":"A 32 year old patient with a history of sickle cell anemia presents to your office with worsening fatigue and abdominal pain. They were seen in the emergency department last week for a worsening dyspnea requiring a transfusion, the third one in six months. Which of the following conditions is the patient most likely at risk for developing?","o":["A. Hereditary hemochromatosis","B. Microcytic RBCs","C. Hypochromic RBCs","D. Iron overload"],"g":["D"],"k":["D"],"rv":false,"at":"Iron overload","a":"D","why":"<b>D. Iron overload.</b> Each transfusion adds iron, and the body has <b>no regulated way to excrete it</b>. Repeated transfusions for sickle cell disease → transfusion iron overload.<br>A. Hereditary hemochromatosis: a genetic low-hepcidin disease, not caused by transfusions.<br>B. Microcytic red cells: iron deficiency, the opposite.<br>C. Hypochromic red cells: also iron deficiency.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 35 (Mechanism)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem TA review 22 (Exam 4)","sl":187,"f":"F0621"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Hemochromatosis treated by phlebotomy","slides":[{"src":"sl/G032-34.jpg","cap":"Hereditary hemochromatosis treatment: therapeutic phlebotomy","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 34"},{"src":"sl/G032-36.jpg","cap":"Chelators deferasirox and deferoxamine: treatment for transfusion overload","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 36"}],"q":[{"id":"F0577-176-2","s":"Gunner point: How would you treat this patient’s iron overload???","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Therapeutic phlebotomy (this patient has hereditary hemochromatosis)","a":null,"why":"<b>Therapeutic phlebotomy.</b> This gunner point follows the <b>hereditary hemochromatosis</b> case (bronze skin, joint pain, HAMP gene mutation). Those patients are not anemic, so regular <b>blood removal</b> pulls iron out of the body.<br>Mix-up: <b>iron chelators</b> (deferoxamine, deferasirox) are for <b>transfusion overload</b>, where patients are anemic and can't be bled.","ex":"","fl":["The source answered iron chelation, but this gunner point follows a hereditary hemochromatosis case, which is treated with therapeutic phlebotomy; chelation is for transfusion overload."],"ch":true,"img":null,"in":"","tg":["L36 Iron slide 36 (Drug Metabolism and Disposition: the Biological Fate of Chemicals 36(12):2523-38)"],"src":[{"t":"2022 TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":176,"f":"F0577"},{"t":"2021 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":156,"f":"F0180"}],"t":1}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"IRP–IRE: low iron → ↑TfR, ↓ferritin","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-110.jpg","cap":"IRP–IRE control: low iron raises TFR, lowers ferritin translation","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 110"}],"q":[{"id":"F0257-108-1","s":"A patient has very low intracellular iron levels. How will this affect the translation of transferrin receptor (TFR) and ferritin?","o":["A. IRP cannot bind IRE → ↑ ferritin synthesis and ↓ TFR synthesis","B. IRP binds IRE → ↑ TFR synthesis and ↓ ferritin synthesis","C. IRP binds IRE → ↑ ferritin synthesis and ↑ TFR synthesis","D. IRP cannot bind IRE → ↓ ferritin synthesis and ↑ TFR synthesis","E. IRP is degraded → ↑ TFR synthesis and ↓ ferritin synthesis"],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"IRP binds IRE → ↑ TFR synthesis and ↓ ferritin synthesis","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Low cell iron: <b>IRP</b> (iron regulatory protein) <b>binds IRE</b> (iron-responsive element) stem-loops. On transferrin receptor (TFR) mRNA (3′ end) it <b>stabilizes</b> the message → ↑TFR, more uptake. On ferritin mRNA (5′ end) it <b>blocks translation</b> → ↓ferritin.<br>A. That is high iron.<br>C. Ferritin is not increased.<br>D. IRP does bind when iron is low.<br>E. IRP2 is degraded in high iron, not low.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"2025 TA review lectures 36-37","sl":108,"f":"F0257"},{"t":"2025 Montana TA review","n":"GI exam TA review 2025","sl":108,"f":"F0938"}],"t":1},{"id":"F3881-198-1","s":"With regards to iron storage, choose the correct description for each statement: A. Under LOW cellular levels of iron: Binding of IRP (iron regulated proteins) to IRE (iron responsive element) stabilizes TFR mRNA (increased/decreased synthesis of TFR), therefore promoting/inhibiting ribosomal reading of ferritin mRNA (leading to increased/decreased synthesis of ferritin) B. Under HIGH cellular levels of iron: Iron binds to IRP, preventing IRP from binding to IRE; this leads to increased/decreased synthesis of TFR and increased/decreased synthesis of ferritin)","o":[],"g":[],"k":[],"rv":true,"at":"Low iron: increased TfR synthesis; inhibiting ribosomal reading of ferritin mRNA → decreased ferritin. High iron: decreased TfR synthesis and increased ferritin synthesis.","a":null,"why":"<b>Low iron:</b> IRP (iron regulatory protein) binds IRE (iron-responsive element) → stabilizes transferrin receptor (TFR) mRNA (<b>↑TFR</b>) and blocks ribosomes on ferritin mRNA (<b>↓ferritin</b>): the cell takes in more iron and stores less.<br><b>High iron:</b> iron binds IRP, IRP lets go of the IRE → <b>↓TFR, ↑ferritin</b>.<br>Mix-up: the IRE is at the 3′ end of TFR mRNA (protects it) but the 5′ end of ferritin mRNA (blocks it).","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2024 Middletown TA review","n":"Biochem Exam 4 TA review","sl":198,"f":"F3881"}]}]},{"l":"BIO-L36","label":"Ferritin H = heart, L = liver","slides":[{"src":"sl/F0257-106.jpg","cap":"Ferritin isoforms: H subunit chromosome 11 heart; L subunit chromosome 19 liver","where":"2025 Montana TA review, slide 106"},{"src":"sl/G032-18.jpg","cap":"Ferritin shell of H and L subunits; H subunit has ferroxidase activity","where":"2026 lecture: L36 Iron Metabolism, slide 18"}],"q":[{"id":"F0208-37-1","s":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the two isoforms of ferritin?","o":["A. The H subunit is predominant in the liver, encoded on Chromosome 19, and the L subunit is predominant in the heart, encoded on Chromosome 11.","B. The H subunit is predominant in the heart, encoded on Chromosome 11, and the L subunit is predominant in the liver, encoded on Chromosome 19.","C. Both the H and L subunits are encoded on Chromosome 11, with the H subunit predominant in the liver and the L subunit predominant in the heart.","D. The H subunit is predominant in the liver, encoded on Chromosome 11, and the L subunit is predominant in the heart, encoded on Chromosome 19."],"g":["B"],"k":["B"],"rv":false,"at":"The H subunit is predominant in the heart, encoded on Chromosome 11, and the L subunit is predominant in the liver, encoded on Chromosome 19.","a":"B","why":"<b>B.</b> Ferritin has two subunits: <b>H (heavy) — chromosome 11, mostly in heart</b> (carries the ferroxidase activity); <b>L (light) — chromosome 19, mostly in liver</b>.<br>A. Tissues and chromosomes are both swapped.<br>C. L is on chromosome 19, and the tissues are swapped.<br>D. Chromosomes right, tissues swapped: H is heart, L is liver.<br>Memory: H = Heart.","ex":"","fl":[],"ch":false,"img":null,"in":"","tg":[],"src":[{"t":"2023+ mixed TA review","n":"GI exam TA reviews 2023+","sl":37,"f":"F0208"},{"t":"2023 TA review","n":"Biochem TA Exam 4 review","sl":217,"f":"F2296"}],"t":1}]}]};